window.CS = {"lessons":{"w1l1":{"id":"w1l1","name":"Always true: facts, science and habits (if ≈ when)","cefr":"A2","ctype":"0","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If / When / Whenever + present simple","result":"present simple"},"uses":[{"use":"Science facts","ex":"If you heat ice, it melts."},{"use":"Habits and routines","ex":"If I miss the 7.15 train, I take a motorbike taxi."},{"use":"How apps, games and machines work","ex":"If your health bar hits zero, you go back to the start."},{"use":"What people and pets always do","ex":"My cat purrs if you stroke her ears."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Use if and the present simple in both halves for things that are always true.","body":["A zero conditional talks about things that are always true. It has two halves. The <b>if</b> half is the condition (the thing that happens first). The other half is the result (what happens next). Both halves use the present simple. We use it for science facts, for habits, and for how things work.","In these sentences, <b>if</b> means almost the same as <b>when</b> or <b>whenever</b>. All three say 'every time this happens'. So you can change <b>if</b> to <b>when</b>, and the meaning stays the same. This only works for facts and habits. It does not work for one day in the future. It does not work for a dream.","Do not put <b>will</b> after <b>if</b> in these sentences. Think of it like this: <b>if</b> takes a photo of a situation. A photo shows the present, so the verb is in the present simple. The same is true after <b>when</b> and <b>whenever</b>.","Remember the s. After he, she, it or one thing, the present simple verb ends in s. So we say metal expand<b>s</b> and my cat purr<b>s</b>. After I, you, we, they or many things, there is no s. The verb is one word. Do not put <b>is</b> in front of it, and do not use an -ing word alone.","A zero conditional is real. Nothing in it is imagined, so there is no hidden truth to find. It is not about one special day in the past or the future. It is about every time. If the result has <b>will</b>, the sentence is usually about one future time. That is the first conditional, and you will meet it in the next world."],"examples":[{"s":"If you heat ice, it melts.","g":"A science fact. Both halves use the present simple."},{"s":"Whenever it rains, the traffic on our road gets worse.","g":"Whenever means every time. You can also say if or when here."},{"s":"I feel sick if I read in the car.","g":"A habit. The if half can come second."},{"s":"If your health bar hits zero, you go back to the start.","g":"How a game works. The bar is one thing, so hits has an s. After you, there is no s."},{"s":"<s>If you will heat ice, it melts.</s>","g":"Wrong: no will after if. Say: If you heat ice, it melts."}]},"B2":{"key":"The zero conditional uses the present simple in both halves to state facts, habits and routines that hold every time.","body":["The zero conditional describes what always happens: scientific facts, the way machines and apps behave, and personal habits or routines. It has a condition half, introduced by <b>if</b>, and a result half. Because the sentence is about every occasion and not one particular day, both halves take the present simple.","In this pattern <b>if</b> is very close to <b>when</b> and <b>whenever</b>: each one means 'on every occasion that this happens'. You can swap them without changing the message. That swap only works for facts and habits. In a sentence about one future day, <b>when</b> would say the speaker is sure the thing will happen, and in an imagined sentence it would not fit at all.","Do not put <b>will</b> in the condition. A useful picture: <b>if</b> takes a photo of a situation, and a photo is always in the present. The same is true after <b>when</b> and <b>whenever</b>. If the result contains <b>will</b>, the sentence is normally about one future occasion. That is the first conditional, which comes in the next world.","Agreement still matters. With he, she, it or any single thing as the subject, the present simple verb ends in s: metal expand<b>s</b>, my cat purr<b>s</b>. With I, you, we, they or a plural subject there is no s. The verb is the present simple on its own, with no <b>is</b> in front of it and no -ing ending.","Finally, a zero conditional is real. It denies nothing, so there is no hidden Truth line to look for, unlike the imagined conditionals you will meet later. It also describes no single day, past or future: it is true every time."],"examples":[{"s":"If you heat ice, it melts.","g":"A fact: present simple in both halves, and it takes the s."},{"s":"Whenever it rains, the traffic on our road gets worse.","g":"Whenever means on every occasion; if or when would say the same."},{"s":"I feel sick if I read in the car.","g":"A habit, with the if half second."},{"s":"If your health bar hits zero, you go back to the start.","g":"A game rule: the bar is singular, so hits takes s; you takes none."},{"s":"<s>If you will heat ice, it melts.</s>","g":"Wrong: will cannot follow if here. Correct: If you heat ice, it melts."}]},"C1":{"key":"The zero conditional is the real, timeless conditional: present forms in both clauses, with if close in meaning to when or whenever.","body":["What coursebooks call the zero conditional is a real condition with timeless reference. The speaker treats the condition as something that genuinely occurs and states what follows on every occasion, which is why both clauses carry the present simple: the present here means 'always', not 'at this moment'. Scientific laws, the behaviour of devices and apps, game rules and personal routines all belong here.","Because the reference is general, <b>if</b>, <b>when</b> and <b>whenever</b> become near-equivalents: all three mean 'on any occasion that'. The difference returns as soon as the sentence concerns a single future occasion, where <b>when</b> assumes the event will happen and <b>if</b> leaves it open, and in imagined sentences, where <b>when</b> is impossible.","The condition clause does not take <b>will</b> for plain future or general meaning. The if-clause does not predict; it takes a photo of a situation, and the result says what goes with it. The same restriction holds after <b>when</b> and <b>whenever</b>. A result containing <b>will</b> normally turns the sentence towards one future occasion, the first conditional, although the two types differ only in time and not in reality.","Corpus studies show that this family, a present condition with a present, modal or imperative result, is by far the most common conditional in real English, well ahead of the textbook pattern with <b>will</b>. So the present simple result is a default, not a law: any sensible present form, modal verb or imperative can follow, as the next lesson shows. Agreement is the main accuracy risk: a third person singular subject needs its s, and the verb stands alone, without <b>is</b> and without -ing.","Since nothing is denied, a zero conditional carries no Truth receipt. That is the quickest way to separate it from an imagined conditional, whose past form signals distance from reality. A past-time version also exists for old habits, with the past simple in both clauses; it is just as real, but it describes a past period and not a single past day."],"examples":[{"s":"If you heat ice, it melts.","g":"Timeless fact: present simple in both clauses; no receipt, because nothing is denied."},{"s":"Whenever it rains, the traffic on our road gets worse.","g":"Whenever makes the 'every occasion' meaning explicit; if and when are equivalent here."},{"s":"I feel sick if I read in the car.","g":"A personal habit with the if-clause in final position."},{"s":"If your health bar hits zero, you go back to the start.","g":"A game rule: agreement applies clause by clause, hits against go."},{"s":"<s>If you will heat ice, it melts.</s>","g":"Wrong: the if-clause frames the situation and cannot take plain will. Correct: If you heat ice, it melts."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w1l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"My cat hides under the bed if she hears thunder."}],"stem":"What does Fah mean?","options":["Her cat hid under the bed once, last night.","Her cat does this every time there is thunder.","Her cat will do this tonight, but not usually.","Her cat never really hides; Fah is imagining it."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["The verbs are present simple, not past. Check the When dial: this is not about last night.","Yes: present simple in both halves means it is true every time.","One future night would need will in the result. Both verbs here are present simple, so it is a habit.","Nothing is imagined here. Present simple in both halves is real and always true."],"hint":"Look at the two verbs. One time, or every time?","why":"Hides and hears are both present simple, so the sentence describes what always happens when there is thunder: a real habit."},{"id":"w1l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"<b>If</b> I drink bubble tea late at night, I stay awake for hours. Which word can replace <b>If</b> and keep the same meaning?","options":["Unless","When","Before","Although"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Unless means 'except if', so it turns the meaning upside down.","Yes: in a habit, if means 'every time', and this word says the same.","Before changes the story: now the speaker is awake first and drinks the tea later.","Although shows a surprise, not a condition."],"hint":"This is a habit. Which word also says 'every time'?","why":"The sentence is a habit, so if means 'every time'. When says the same thing in facts and habits."},{"id":"w1l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"Which sentence is a zero conditional, a fact that is always true?","options":["If it rains this afternoon, we'll play badminton indoors.","If I had wings, I'd fly over the traffic to school.","If you mix blue and yellow paint, you get green.","It rained all morning, so we played badminton indoors."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This is about one afternoon in the future, and the result has will. That is a first conditional.","Had and I'd show an imagined world. Truth: I don't have wings.","Yes: present simple in both halves, and it is true every time.","This tells a story about one past morning. There is no condition in it."],"hint":"Check the When dial for each one: one day, or always?","why":"Mix and get are both present simple and the result is the same every time, so it is a general fact."},{"id":"w1l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If the temperature drops below zero, water ___.","options":["freeze","freezes","is freeze","freezing"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Water is one thing (it), so the present simple verb needs an s.","Yes: a fact needs the present simple, and water takes the s.","Is and a plain verb together are not an English form. Use the present simple on its own.","An -ing word alone cannot be the main verb. A fact needs the present simple."],"hint":"What is the subject of the gap? Does it need an s?","why":"This is a science fact, so the result is present simple; water is a singular subject, so the verb ends in s."},{"id":"w1l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If you ___ ice to a drink, it gets colder.","options":["will add","adds","add","adding"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No will after if. If takes the photo, so the verb stays in the present simple.","The subject is you, and you never takes the s.","Yes: present simple after if, and no s after you.","An -ing word alone cannot be the verb after if."],"hint":"Which tense comes after if in a fact? Who is the subject?","why":"After if in a fact we use the present simple, and the subject you takes the plain verb with no s."},{"id":"w1l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"[[If]] you [[will tap]] your card on the gate, it [[opens]] and a green light [[comes]] on.","answer":1,"fix":"tap","fb":["If is the right word for something that happens every time.","No will after if. If takes the photo, so use the present simple: tap.","Fine: the gate is one thing, so opens has its s.","Fine: a light is one thing, so comes has its s."],"hint":"Which half takes the photo? Check its verb.","why":"The if half of a fact uses the present simple, so will tap must be tap."},{"id":"w1l1-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"My cat runs to the kitchen whenever she ___ the fridge door.","options":["hear","hearing","will hear","hears"],"answer":3,"near":2,"fb":["One cat is she, so the verb needs an s.","An -ing word alone cannot be the verb here.","No will after whenever. It works like if: present simple.","Yes: present simple after whenever, with the s for she."],"hint":"Whenever works like if here. Which tense, and who is the subject?","why":"Whenever means every time, so it takes the present simple like if, and she needs the s form."},{"id":"w1l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"When the sun ___ (go) down, the lights on our street come on.","accept":["goes","has gone"],"model":"goes","hint":"A daily fact. Which tense, and does the sun need an s?","why":"This happens every evening, so the verb is present simple, and the sun is singular: goes."}],"world":"w1"},"w1l2":{"id":"w1l2","name":"Rules, instructions and advice: imperative and can / must / should results","cefr":"A2","ctype":"0","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + present simple","result":"imperative, or can / must / should / may + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Instructions","ex":"If the app freezes, close it and open it again."},{"use":"Rules","ex":"If you borrow a ball, you must return it after PE."},{"use":"Permission","ex":"If you are in M2, you can join the robot club."},{"use":"Advice","ex":"If you have a sore throat, you should rest your voice."},{"use":"Warnings","ex":"If you touch that pan, you may burn your hand."},{"use":"Requests","ex":"If you see Mook, can you give her this book?"}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"After an if half in the present simple, the result can be an order or a modal verb like can, must, should or may.","body":["Real life is full of rules, instructions and advice. Many of them start with an <b>if</b> half. The <b>if</b> half stays the same: present simple, and no <b>will</b>. Only the result changes.","For an instruction, use the imperative (the order form). It is the plain verb, with no subject, no s, no to and no -ing. Signs, apps and teachers use it all the time. You can give two instructions with <b>and</b>. Both are plain verbs. For a negative instruction, put <b>don't</b> before the plain verb. Use <b>don't</b> for everyone.","You can also use a modal verb (a small helping verb) in the result. Each one has its own meaning. <b>Can</b> means it is allowed. <b>Must</b> means it is necessary: a rule or a law, with no choice. <b>Should</b> means it is a good idea: advice, and you can choose. <b>May</b> and <b>might</b> mean it is possible, so they are good for warnings.","After a modal verb, always use the plain verb. Do not add to, s or -ing. This is true for every subject: she must wait, they must wait.","So read the result to find the job of the sentence. A plain verb gives an order. <b>Must</b> gives a rule. <b>Should</b> gives advice. <b>Can</b> gives permission. A present simple result gives a fact. None of these is a promise: a promise needs <b>will</b>. All of them are real, so nothing is imagined and nothing is about the past."],"examples":[{"s":"If the app freezes, close it and open it again.","g":"Two instructions. Close and open are plain verbs with no subject."},{"s":"If the haze is bad, don't exercise outside.","g":"A negative instruction: don't and the plain verb."},{"s":"If you are in M2, you can join the robot club.","g":"Can means it is allowed. You choose."},{"s":"If you borrow a ball, you must return it after PE.","g":"Must means a rule. The plain verb follows: <s>must to return</s> is wrong."},{"s":"If you have a sore throat, you should rest your voice.","g":"Should means advice: a good idea, but you choose."}]},"B2":{"key":"A real condition can be followed by an imperative or a modal verb, and the choice of modal carries the meaning.","body":["Most of the conditionals you read every day are not statements of fact at all. They are instructions on signs and in apps, school rules, safety warnings and friendly advice. The condition half is the same as before, the present simple with no <b>will</b>, but the result does a different job.","For an instruction the result is an imperative: the base form of the verb with no subject, no s, no <b>to</b> and no -ing ending. The negative is <b>don't</b> followed by the base form, whoever you are speaking to. Two instructions can be joined with <b>and</b>, and both stay in the base form.","A modal verb in the result adds a precise meaning. <b>Can</b> gives permission: the action is allowed, not required. <b>Must</b> states a rule or law: it is necessary and there is no choice. <b>Should</b> gives advice: it is a good idea, but the listener decides. <b>May</b> and <b>might</b> say that something is possible, which makes them natural in warnings.","Whatever the modal, the verb after it is the base form, for every subject. There is never <b>to</b> between the modal and the verb, never an s and never -ing.","To name the use of a sentence, read its result. A base verb is an instruction, <b>must</b> a rule, <b>should</b> advice, <b>can</b> permission, <b>may</b> a warning, and a present simple result a plain fact. A promise would need <b>will</b>. Nothing here is imagined or past, so no Truth line is hidden behind these sentences."],"examples":[{"s":"If the app freezes, close it and open it again.","g":"Two imperatives joined with and; both are base forms with no subject."},{"s":"If the haze is bad, don't exercise outside.","g":"Negative imperative: don't and the base form, for any listener."},{"s":"If you are in M2, you can join the robot club.","g":"Can gives permission: allowed, not required."},{"s":"If you borrow a ball, you must return it after PE.","g":"Must states a rule; the base form follows, so <s>must to return</s> is wrong."},{"s":"If you have a sore throat, you should rest your voice.","g":"Should gives advice and leaves the choice to the listener."}]},"C1":{"key":"Real conditions most often lead to imperatives and modals, and each result performs its own act: instruction, rule, permission, advice or warning.","body":["In authentic English the commonest job of an if-clause is to say when an instruction, rule or piece of advice applies. Help pages, medicine leaflets, transport notices and exam instructions are built on this pattern, which is far more frequent than the coursebook sequence with <b>will</b>. The condition clause is unchanged, a present form with no <b>will</b>, because it only frames the situation; the result clause performs the act.","An imperative result is the bare base form, with no subject and no inflection, so it cannot take s, <b>to</b> or -ing; its negative is <b>don't</b> with the base form for any listener. Imperatives joined by <b>and</b> each keep the base form. A normal verb elsewhere in the sentence still agrees with its own subject as usual.","A modal result keeps the modal's ordinary meaning. <b>Can</b> grants permission without obliging anyone. <b>Must</b> imposes necessity, typically a rule or law. <b>Should</b> recommends and leaves the decision to the listener. <b>May</b> and <b>might</b> mark possibility and so suit warnings; because those two overlap, choosing between them is a matter of tone, not correctness.","Modals are followed by the base form for every subject, with no <b>to</b>, no agreement s and no -ing. Errors here usually come from copying the pattern of <b>have to</b> or <b>want to</b>, which do take <b>to</b>.","The result therefore identifies the function. A <b>will</b> result would predict or promise; a past form would report the past or step away from reality. Neither is present here: these sentences are real, deny nothing and carry no Truth receipt. Any sensible present form, modal or imperative is acceptable after a real condition, so do not force such sentences into a single textbook formula."],"examples":[{"s":"If the app freezes, close it and open it again.","g":"Coordinated imperatives: bare base forms, no subject, no inflection."},{"s":"If the haze is bad, don't exercise outside.","g":"Negative imperative with don't, whoever is addressed."},{"s":"If you are in M2, you can join the robot club.","g":"Can grants permission without obligation."},{"s":"If you borrow a ball, you must return it after PE.","g":"Must imposes necessity; a bare base form follows, so <s>must to return</s> is wrong."},{"s":"If you have a sore throat, you should rest your voice.","g":"Should recommends; the listener remains free to decide."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w1l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","lines":[{"who":"Library sign","text":"If you finish a book, put it back on the right shelf."}],"stem":"What is the sign doing?","options":["Making a promise about the future","Describing an imagined situation","Giving an instruction","Describing one day in the past"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["A promise would use will, as in 'we will help you'. Here the result is an order: put.","Nothing is imagined. Students really finish books, and the sign tells them what to do.","Yes: the result starts with a plain verb, put, so it tells readers what to do.","The verbs are present, not past, and the sign speaks to every reader, every day."],"hint":"Look at the first word of the result. What kind of verb form is it?","why":"The result begins with the plain verb put, with no subject. That is an imperative, so the sign gives an instruction."},{"id":"w1l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Librarian","text":"If you have a library card, you can borrow three books."}],"stem":"What does <b>can</b> tell us here?","options":["Borrowing three books is allowed.","Borrowing three books is necessary.","Borrowing three books is a good idea.","Borrowing three books is against the rules."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: can in a rule gives permission. You are free to do it.","Necessary is must. Can only opens the door; you choose.","A good idea is should. Can says it is allowed, not that it is wise.","Against the rules would be can't or mustn't. Can says yes."],"hint":"Is the student forced, advised or free to do it?","why":"Can in the result gives permission: with a card, a student is allowed to borrow three books, but does not have to."},{"id":"w1l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"Which sentence gives advice, not a rule, an order or a fact?","options":["If you bring a phone to school, you must keep it in your bag.","If the bell rings, go straight to your classroom.","If you press this switch, the fan starts.","If you feel sleepy in class, you should drink some water."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Must makes this a rule: there is no choice.","The result is a plain verb, go, so this is an order.","This only says how the fan works. It is a fact, not advice.","Yes: should means it is a good idea, and the listener can choose."],"hint":"Which result recommends something but leaves the listener free to choose?","why":"Should in the result means 'this is a good idea'. Must is a rule, a plain verb is an order, and a present simple result is a fact."},{"id":"w1l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If the fire alarm rings, ___ the building by the nearest stairs.","options":["leaves","leave","leaving","to leave"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["An instruction uses the plain verb with no s. There is no he or she here.","Yes: an instruction is the plain verb, with no subject and no s.","An -ing word cannot give an instruction on its own.","Drop the to. An instruction starts with the plain verb."],"hint":"The result tells people what to do. Which verb form gives an order?","why":"The result is an instruction, so it needs the imperative: the plain verb with no subject, no s and no to."},{"id":"w1l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If you arrive after eight o'clock, you ___ the late book at the office.","options":["must to sign","must signs","must sign","must signing"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["After must there is no to. Must goes straight to the plain verb.","No s after a modal verb, for any subject.","Yes: must and the plain verb, for a school rule.","No -ing after must. Use the plain verb."],"hint":"What form of the verb follows a modal like must?","why":"Modal verbs such as must are followed by the plain verb, with no to, no s and no -ing."},{"id":"w1l2-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"If you ride on a motorbike taxi, you ___ wear a helmet. It's the law.","options":["can","may","might","must"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Can only says it is allowed. A law makes it necessary.","May says it is allowed or possible. A law is stronger than that.","Might says it is possible, like a guess. A law is not a guess.","Yes: the law leaves no choice, so the result needs the modal for 'necessary'."],"hint":"It's the law. Is the helmet allowed, possible or necessary?","why":"A law makes the helmet necessary, and must is the modal for a rule with no choice."},{"id":"w1l2-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If you [[finish]] the test early, [[checks]] your answers and [[stay]] in your seat until the teacher [[collects]] the papers.","answer":1,"fix":"check","fb":["Fine: present simple after if, and you takes no s.","An instruction is the plain verb: check, with no s.","Fine: stay is a plain verb, a second instruction.","Fine: the teacher is one person, so collects has its s."],"hint":"Two parts give instructions. Are both plain verbs?","why":"Instructions use the plain verb, so checks must be check; collects is right because its subject is the teacher."},{"id":"w1l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If the corridor floor is wet, ___ (not run). Walk slowly.","accept":["don't run","do not run"],"model":"don't run","hint":"A negative instruction. Which helping word goes before the plain verb?","why":"A negative instruction is don't and the plain verb, for everyone: don't run."}],"world":"w1"},"w1l3":{"id":"w1l3","name":"Building it right: clause order, the comma, he/she + -s, negatives and questions","cefr":"B1","ctype":"0","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + present simple, (comma)","result":"present simple (result first: no comma)"},"uses":[{"use":"Setting the scene first (with a comma)","ex":"If the bell rings, everyone stands up."},{"use":"Giving the result first (no comma)","ex":"Everyone stands up if the bell rings."},{"use":"Saying what follows when something doesn't happen","ex":"If Mum doesn't set an alarm, she oversleeps."},{"use":"Asking about a general result","ex":"What happens if you press this button?"},{"use":"Checking a fact with a yes or no question","ex":"Does your cat come if you call her name?"}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Put a comma after the if half only when it comes first, and check the s, the negative and the question form.","body":["You can start with the <b>if</b> half or with the result. The meaning is the same. But keep <b>if</b> with the same action. If you move <b>if</b> to the other action, the condition changes, and the meaning changes too.","The comma rule is simple. When the <b>if</b> half comes first, put a comma at the end of it. When the result comes first, use no comma. The comma never goes straight after the word <b>if</b>.","Do not add <b>so</b> after the <b>if</b> half. The word <b>if</b> already joins the two halves, and that is enough. The result starts with its subject.","Check every verb. After he, she, it or one person or thing, the present simple verb ends in s. Both halves can need it. For a negative, use <b>doesn't</b> for he, she or it, and <b>don't</b> for the others. After <b>doesn't</b> and <b>don't</b>, use the plain verb with no s. Do not use <b>won't</b> after <b>if</b> here. A negative sentence like this still talks about every time, not one day. It does not say the thing always happens.","To ask about a fact, use <b>What happens if</b>. <b>What</b> is the subject, so the verb has an s. It asks for the usual result, not for one day. For a yes or no question, start with <b>Does</b> for one thing. Use <b>Do</b> for you, we, they or many things. Then use the plain verb. The short answer uses the same word: Yes, it does."],"examples":[{"s":"If my sister misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"The if half is first, so there is a comma. My sister and she both take the s."},{"s":"My sister walks to school if she misses the bus.","g":"The result is first, so there is no comma. The meaning is the same."},{"s":"If it doesn't rain, the grass turns brown.","g":"A negative: doesn't and the plain verb rain, with no s."},{"s":"What happens if you press this button? Does it make a noise?","g":"Two questions. Happens has an s. After Does, make has no s."},{"s":"<s>If I'm tired, so I go to bed early.</s>","g":"Wrong: no so after the if half. Say: If I'm tired, I go to bed early."}]},"B2":{"key":"Either half can come first; the comma follows only an if half in first position, and agreement, negatives and questions use ordinary present simple grammar.","body":["The two halves can come in either order without any change of meaning, provided that <b>if</b> stays attached to the same action. Swap the actions instead of the halves and you have a different condition, and so a different message.","Punctuation follows the order. When the <b>if</b> half comes first, a comma closes it; when the result comes first, there is normally no comma before <b>if</b>. The comma belongs at the end of the whole condition, never directly after the word <b>if</b>.","Nothing else is needed to link the halves. Thai uses a small linking word before the result, and learners often translate it as <b>so</b>, but in English <b>so</b> after an if half is an error. The result simply begins with its subject.","Each verb agrees with its own subject: a third person singular subject takes s in either half. Negatives use <b>doesn't</b> for he, she or it and <b>don't</b> elsewhere, followed by the base form, because the s has already moved onto <b>doesn't</b>. <b>Won't</b>, like <b>will</b>, stays out of the if half here. A negative zero conditional still describes what follows on every such occasion; it does not claim that the situation always occurs, and it is not about one day.","Questions about general results use <b>What happens if</b>, where <b>what</b> is the singular subject, so the verb takes s. Yes or no questions begin with <b>Does</b> or <b>Do</b>, then the subject and the base form, and the short answer repeats the same helping word. Both kinds ask what usually follows, not what happened once."],"examples":[{"s":"If my sister misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"If half first, so a comma; both verbs take s for a singular subject."},{"s":"My sister walks to school if she misses the bus.","g":"Result first, so no comma; the meaning is unchanged."},{"s":"If it doesn't rain, the grass turns brown.","g":"Negative: doesn't carries the s, and rain is the base form."},{"s":"What happens if you press this button? Does it make a noise?","g":"What is the subject, so happens takes s; after Does, make is the base form."},{"s":"<s>If I'm tired, so I go to bed early.</s>","g":"Wrong: so cannot follow an if half. Correct: If I'm tired, I go to bed early."}]},"C1":{"key":"Clause order is a choice of focus, the comma is a convention tied to that order, and the rest is ordinary present simple grammar applied to each clause.","body":["Either clause may come first with no change in truth: an if-clause in first position sets the scene, while a final one adds the condition as new information. What must not move is the word <b>if</b> itself. Attach it to the other action and you reverse condition and result, which changes the meaning entirely.","The comma is a writing convention and not a rule of grammar, but examiners expect it: a comma after an if-clause that comes first, and normally none before a final one. The one regular exception is an afterthought added in speech, which you do not need yet. A comma directly after <b>if</b> is never right.","English needs no linking word before the result. Thai typically places a small linker there, which tempts learners to write <b>so</b>; standard English rejects it because <b>if</b> has already made one clause depend on the other. An emphatic <b>then</b> is possible, but nothing at all is the usual choice.","Agreement applies clause by clause, so a third person singular subject takes s wherever it stands. In negatives the helping verb carries the agreement, <b>doesn't</b> against <b>don't</b>, and the main verb returns to the base form; <b>won't</b> in its plain future sense is excluded from the if-clause for the same reason as <b>will</b>. Note the logic of a negative condition: it states what follows whenever the situation is absent, without claiming that it is always absent.","In <b>What happens if</b>, <b>what</b> is itself the subject, hence the singular verb with s and no helping verb. Yes or no questions take <b>Does</b> or <b>Do</b> with the base form, and the short answer echoes that helping verb. Both ask about the general result, not about a single past or future event."],"examples":[{"s":"If my sister misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"Scene-setting if-clause with its comma; agreement in both clauses."},{"s":"My sister walks to school if she misses the bus.","g":"Final if-clause, no comma; identical truth, different focus."},{"s":"If it doesn't rain, the grass turns brown.","g":"The helping verb carries agreement; the main verb is the base form."},{"s":"What happens if you press this button? Does it make a noise?","g":"Subject question with happens, then a Does question with the base form make."},{"s":"<s>If I'm tired, so I go to bed early.</s>","g":"Wrong: if already links the clauses, so so is redundant. Correct: If I'm tired, I go to bed early."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w1l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"Fern sneezes if she smells pepper. Which sentence means the same?","options":["If Fern sneezes, she smells pepper.","If Fern smells pepper, she sneezes.","Fern smells pepper if she sneezes.","Fern doesn't sneeze if she smells pepper."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The halves have swapped jobs: now sneezing is the condition. Keep 'smells pepper' with if.","Yes: the if half is still 'smells pepper'. Only the order changed, and a comma came in.","Here if sits on 'sneezes', so sneezing has become the condition.","The negative turns the meaning upside down."],"hint":"Which action is the condition? Keep if with that action.","why":"Moving the if half to the front does not change the meaning, as long as if stays with the same action: smells pepper."},{"id":"w1l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Jane's mum","text":"If Jane doesn't sleep eight hours, she can't concentrate in the first lesson."}],"stem":"What is true?","options":["Jane never manages to sleep for eight hours.","Jane concentrates better after a short night's sleep.","Jane slept badly last night and felt tired today.","Jane needs a full night's sleep to concentrate."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["If does not say it always happens. It only says what follows each time it does.","The opposite: too little sleep, no concentration.","The sentence is about every time, not about last night. Check the When dial.","Yes: each time she sleeps too little, she can't concentrate, so sleep is what she needs."],"hint":"Does the sentence describe one night, or what follows each time?","why":"The negative zero conditional says what follows whenever Jane sleeps too little, so a full night's sleep is necessary for her concentration."},{"id":"w1l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"What happens if you leave your phone in the sun?"}],"stem":"What does Mook want to know?","options":["The usual result of doing this","What happened to a phone yesterday","What her friend plans to do tomorrow","Whether her friend owns a phone"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: happens is present simple, so she is asking what always follows.","Yesterday would need the past: what happened.","A plan would need a future form. Happens is present simple: a general question.","She is not asking about owning a phone. She is asking about a result."],"hint":"Look at the tense of the question verb. One time, or in general?","why":"What happens if is a present simple question, so it asks for the general result of an action."},{"id":"w1l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"If my sister ___ games after ten at night, she can't wake up for school.","options":["play","will play","plays","is play"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["My sister is one person, so the verb needs an s.","No will after if. If takes the photo: present simple.","Yes: present simple after if, with the s for my sister.","Is and a plain verb together are not an English form."],"hint":"Who is the subject of the if half? One person or more?","why":"The if half takes the present simple, and my sister is third person singular, so the verb is plays."},{"id":"w1l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"If our cat ___ her dinner on time, she wakes everyone up at five.","options":["don't get","not get","won't get","doesn't get"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Our cat is one animal, so the negative is doesn't, not don't.","Not cannot stand alone. A present simple negative needs doesn't or don't.","No will or won't after if. Use the present simple negative.","Yes: doesn't and the plain verb. The s lives in doesn't, so get has none."],"hint":"A present simple negative for one animal. Which helping word carries the s?","why":"For he, she or it, the present simple negative is doesn't and the plain verb, and won't cannot follow if."},{"id":"w1l3-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"If Pim stays up late, ___ sleepy in maths the next day.","options":["so she feels","she feel","she feels","so she feeling"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No so after an if half. The word if already joins the two halves.","Pim is she, so the verb needs its s.","Yes: no linking word after the comma, and the s for she.","Two problems: so is not needed, and an -ing word alone is not a verb."],"hint":"Does the result need a linking word? And check the verb ending.","why":"After an if half, the result starts straight after the comma with no so, and she takes the s form: she feels."},{"id":"w1l3-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"Our cat [[hides]] under [[the sofa, if]] she [[hears]] thunder [[outside]].","answer":1,"fix":"the sofa if","fb":["Fine: our cat is one animal, so hides has its s.","The if half comes second here, so there is no comma before if.","Fine: she takes the s form, hears.","Fine: outside just says where the thunder is."],"hint":"Where is the if half: first or second? What does that mean for the comma?","why":"The comma is used only when the if half comes first. Here the result comes first, so no comma is needed."},{"id":"w1l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"___ ice float if you put it in a glass of water? Yes, it does.","accept":["Does"],"model":"Does","hint":"A yes or no question about a fact. Look at the short answer too.","why":"A present simple question about one thing starts with Does, and the short answer 'it does' matches it."}],"world":"w1"},"w2l1":{"id":"w2l1","name":"A possible future: if + present, will (no will after if)","cefr":"B1","ctype":"1","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + present simple","result":"will / won't + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Predictions","ex":"If we take the MRT, we'll get there before the rain."},{"use":"Promises","ex":"If you come to my match, I'll save you a seat."},{"use":"Warnings","ex":"If you don't charge your phone, the battery will run out before lunch."},{"use":"Plans that depend on something","ex":"If Mum says yes, I'll come to the concert with you."},{"use":"Asking about a possible problem","ex":"What will we do if the shop is closed?"}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Use <b>if</b> with the present simple for a possible future, and <b>will</b> for the result.","body":["The first conditional, or type 1, talks about one possible future. The <b>if</b> half is the condition (the thing that may happen). The other half is the result (what happens next). The speaker thinks the condition is possible, but she is not sure. Maybe it will happen, maybe not. Nothing here is imagined. It is all really possible.","Look at the verbs. After <b>if</b> we use the present simple, but the time is the future. In the result we use <b>will</b> and the base verb (the verb with no ending). Remember this picture: <b>if</b> takes the photo, <b>will</b> makes the promise. The <b>if</b> half only shows a possible scene. The result half says what will come. So never put <b>will</b> after <b>if</b> for a plain future.","Check the small parts. With <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> or <em>it</em> the present verb takes <em>-s</em>: <em>if she wins</em>. For a negative condition, use <em>don't</em> or <em>doesn't</em>, never <em>won't</em>: <em>if he doesn't call</em>. For a negative result, use <em>won't</em> and the base verb. Two negatives together tell you what you need. <em>You won't improve if you don't practise</em> means you need to practise.","For a question, change the result half. Put <b>will</b> before the subject (the person or thing): <em>What will she say if we arrive late?</em> The <b>if</b> half stays the same. You can put either half first. When <b>if</b> comes first, write a comma after that half. When <b>if</b> comes second, you need no comma."],"examples":[{"s":"If the bus is late, I'll walk to school.","g":"A possible future. Present simple after if, will in the result."},{"s":"If Nan doesn't hurry, she won't catch the 7.15 train.","g":"Two negatives: doesn't after if, won't in the result. Nan needs to hurry."},{"s":"What will you wear if it's cold in the cinema?","g":"A question: will comes before you. The if half does not change."},{"s":"We'll have the picnic inside if it rains.","g":"The if half comes second, so there is no comma."},{"s":"<s>If it will rain tomorrow, we'll stay in.</s>","g":"Wrong. Say: If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay in. No will after if."}]},"B2":{"key":"The first conditional pairs <b>if</b> and a present form with <b>will</b> to talk about a real, possible future event and its result.","body":["The first conditional, or type 1, is about a future event that the speaker sees as a real possibility. She does not know whether it will happen, so she sets it up with <b>if</b> and then says what the result will be. Nothing in the sentence is denied or imagined; the event is simply possible. Typical jobs are predictions, promises, warnings and plans that depend on something.","The form surprises many learners. The <b>if</b> half uses the present simple even though the time is future, and only the result half takes <b>will</b> and the base verb. A useful picture is this: <b>if</b> takes the photo, <b>will</b> makes the promise. The <b>if</b> half just shows a possible scene, like a photo, and the result half does the predicting. So <b>will</b> after <b>if</b> is wrong when you only mean the future.","Build each half carefully. In the <b>if</b> half, remember the <em>-s</em> for <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> and <em>it</em>, and make negatives with <em>don't</em> or <em>doesn't</em>, not <em>won't</em>. In the result, <em>will</em> and <em>won't</em> are followed by the base verb with no <em>-s</em>, no <em>to</em> and no <em>-ing</em>. When both halves are negative, the sentence tells you what is necessary: <em>You won't improve if you don't practise</em> means that practice is needed.","Questions are formed in the result half only: <b>will</b> moves in front of the subject, as in <em>What will she say if we arrive late?</em> The <b>if</b> half keeps normal word order. Either half can come first without changing the meaning. By convention, write a comma after the <b>if</b> half when it comes first, and no comma when it comes second."],"examples":[{"s":"If the bus is late, I'll walk to school.","g":"A possible future event: present simple after if, will plus the base verb in the result."},{"s":"If Nan doesn't hurry, she won't catch the 7.15 train.","g":"Negative in both halves: doesn't after if, never won't; won't in the result. Together they show that hurrying is necessary."},{"s":"What will you wear if it's cold in the cinema?","g":"The question is made in the result half: will before the subject. The if half keeps normal order."},{"s":"We'll have the picnic inside if it rains.","g":"Result first, if half second: same meaning, and no comma."},{"s":"<s>If it will rain tomorrow, we'll stay in.</s>","g":"Wrong: will after if for a plain future. Correct: If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay in."}]},"C1":{"key":"In a first conditional the <b>if</b> clause presents an open future possibility in a present form, and <b>will</b> in the main clause predicts or commits to the consequence.","body":["The first conditional sits in the real row of the two dials: the time is future and the speaker treats the condition as open, neither true nor false yet. That is a matter of attitude, not of percentages; the same event could be framed as a daydream with other forms, which you meet in Dream Tycoon. Because nothing is denied, a first conditional hides no contrary fact. It is the natural form for predictions, promises, warnings and plans that depend on something.","Why a present form for future time? The <b>if</b> clause does not predict anything; it simply assumes a scene and asks you to hold it in mind, so English leaves it unmarked for the future. The prediction itself belongs to the main clause, where <b>will</b> appears. Hence the line: <b>if</b> takes the photo, <b>will</b> makes the promise. Adding <b>will</b> to the <b>if</b> clause just to show future time is a very common error for Thai learners, because Thai can mark both halves with the same word.","There is a genuine exception, which you are not tested on here. <b>Will</b> can follow <b>if</b> when it means being willing to do something, or when it makes a polite request, as in <em>If you'll wait a moment, I'll fetch the manager</em>. That use is taught in Formal Citadel. Whenever <b>will</b> would only mean <em>in the future</em>, leave it out.","The remaining details are mechanical but matter in writing. The present simple needs <em>-s</em> for <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> and <em>it</em>, negatives in the <b>if</b> clause take <em>don't</em> or <em>doesn't</em> rather than <em>won't</em>, and <em>will</em> or <em>won't</em> is followed by the base verb with nothing added. Two negatives together express necessity: <em>You won't improve if you don't practise</em> says that practice is required.","Questions invert only the main clause, giving <em>What will she say if we arrive late?</em>, while the <b>if</b> clause keeps statement order. The clauses can come in either order; an <b>if</b> clause that comes first is followed by a comma, and one that comes second normally is not. This is a punctuation convention rather than a grammar rule, but examiners expect it."],"examples":[{"s":"If the bus is late, I'll walk to school.","g":"Open future condition: the if clause sets the scene in the present simple and will predicts. Nothing is denied."},{"s":"If Nan doesn't hurry, she won't catch the 7.15 train.","g":"Present simple negative in the condition and won't in the main clause; the double negative expresses a necessary condition."},{"s":"What will you wear if it's cold in the cinema?","g":"Inversion happens only in the main clause; the if clause keeps statement order and its present form."},{"s":"We'll have the picnic inside if it rains.","g":"A final if clause adds the condition as new information and normally takes no comma."},{"s":"<s>If it will rain tomorrow, we'll stay in.</s>","g":"Error: will only marks future time here, so it cannot stand in the if clause. Correct: If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay in."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w2l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"If it rains this afternoon, we'll play badminton in the gym."}],"stem":"What does Fah think about rain this afternoon?","options":["It is certain to rain.","It is possible, not certain.","It is raining at the moment.","It rained and they moved inside."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["For something certain we say when, not if. Fah is not sure about the rain.","Yes: if shows a possible future. Fah does not know yet, so she plans for it.","The present form after if is about this afternoon, which is still the future.","Nothing has happened yet. Rains after if is a present form about the future."],"hint":"Does if show something sure, or something that may happen?","why":"If with a present verb sets up a possible future event. Fah does not know whether it will rain, so she plans for that case."},{"id":"w2l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"\"If Mint finishes her homework early tonight, she'll watch the concert stream.\" The verb <b>finishes</b> is in the present simple. What time is it about?","options":["Every night, as a habit","Right now, as we speak","Last night, in the past","Tonight, in the future"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["A habit would have a present verb in the result too. Here she'll and tonight point to one future evening.","She has not finished yet. The form is present, but the time is later tonight.","A past time needs a past form, and tonight has not happened yet.","Yes: after if, the present simple shows a possible future. Tonight tells you the time."],"hint":"Look at the time word and the result half, not only at the verb form.","why":"After if, English uses a present form for a possible future. The word tonight and she'll in the result show that the time is future."},{"id":"w2l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"We won't get seats if we don't leave now."}],"stem":"What does Bam mean?","options":["They need to leave now to get seats.","They have already lost their seats.","They will get seats at any time.","They never get seats at this place."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: two negatives together show what is needed. Leave now, and seats are possible.","Nothing is lost yet. This is a possible future, and there is still time to act.","No. Bam says the seats depend on leaving now.","That would be a habit, with two present verbs. Won't shows one future time."],"hint":"Which action makes the good result possible?","why":"Won't get seats is the result of not leaving now. So leaving now is what they need to do to get seats."},{"id":"w2l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"Praew's school bus is sometimes late. If it ___ late again tomorrow morning, she'll text the group.","options":["come","will come","comes","came"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["The bus is it, so the present verb needs -s: it comes.","No will after if. If takes the photo; will belongs in the result half.","Yes: present simple after if, with -s for it.","Tomorrow morning is the future. After if, a possible future takes the present simple, not a past form."],"hint":"Which half makes the prediction? What is the subject?","why":"After if, a possible future takes the present simple, and the bus is it, so the verb needs -s. Will stays in the result half."},{"id":"w2l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"Is Mook coming to the cinema tonight?"}],"stem":"Fah: She has a maths test on Monday. If she doesn't finish her revision today, she ___ with us.","options":["won't comes","won't come","not come","won't coming"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["After won't, use the base verb with no -s.","Yes: won't and the base verb make a negative result.","A negative result needs won't before the verb.","After won't, use the base verb, not the -ing form."],"hint":"What form of the verb follows will or won't?","why":"The result of a first conditional is will or won't with the base verb, and the base verb has no ending."},{"id":"w2l1-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"We have to be at the airport by six. What ___ if the taxi doesn't come?","options":["we will do","will we do","do we will","will we to do"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That is statement order. In a question, will comes before the subject.","Yes: question word, then will, then the subject, then the base verb.","Will makes its own questions. Do is not needed.","After will, use the base verb with no to."],"hint":"Where does will go in a question?","why":"Questions are made in the result half: will goes before the subject and the base verb follows."},{"id":"w2l1-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"If the rain [[will stop]] [[before four]], we [[will walk]] to the night market [[together]].","answer":0,"fix":"stops","fb":["This is the mistake. No will after if for a plain future: say stops.","Before four is a normal time phrase. It is fine.","Will walk is the result, so will is right here.","Together is fine at the end."],"hint":"Find the half that only shows a possible scene.","why":"Will after if is wrong for a plain future. The if half takes the present simple, so it should be stops."},{"id":"w2l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"If the parcel ___ (not / arrive) by Friday, I'll message the shop.","accept":["doesn't arrive","does not arrive","hasn't arrived","has not arrived"],"model":"doesn't arrive","hint":"Is this the if half or the result half? Check the subject too.","why":"A negative condition takes don't or doesn't, never won't. The parcel is it, so use doesn't and the base verb."}],"world":"w2"},"w2l2":{"id":"w2l2","name":"More results and what they do: might, may, can, should, going to, imperative","cefr":"B1","ctype":"1","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + present simple","result":"might / may / can / should / be going to + verb, or an imperative"},"uses":[{"use":"Deals and negotiating","ex":"If you teach me the dance, I'll help you with your maths."},{"use":"Offers","ex":"If you need a pen, you can use mine."},{"use":"Warnings","ex":"If you leave your bag there, someone might trip over it."},{"use":"Advice","ex":"If you can't sleep, you should put your phone away."},{"use":"Instructions and requests","ex":"If you get lost, call me."},{"use":"Plans already made","ex":"If it's sunny on Sunday, we're going to wash the dog."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"The result can use <b>might</b>, <b>can</b>, <b>should</b>, <b>going to</b> or an order, not only <b>will</b>.","body":["The <b>if</b> half does not change. It still takes the present simple. The result half is your choice, and each choice has its own meaning. <b>Will</b> means you are sure. <b>Might</b> or <b>may</b> means it is possible, but you are not sure. <b>Can</b> means it is allowed or possible for someone. <b>Should</b> gives advice (a good idea). <b>Going to</b> shows a plan you have already made.","You can also use an imperative (an order or instruction) as the result. It is the base verb with no subject: <em>If the bell rings, line up.</em> The negative is <em>don't</em> and the base verb: <em>If the floor is wet, don't run.</em> After <em>might</em>, <em>may</em>, <em>can</em> and <em>should</em>, use the base verb too. Do not add <em>to</em>, <em>-s</em> or <em>-ing</em>. <b>Going to</b> needs <em>am</em>, <em>is</em> or <em>are</em> before it.","Now think about the job of the sentence. A promise says what I will do for you. A deal has two sides: you do one thing, and I do another thing for you. An offer gives the listener something nice and asks for nothing: <em>If you're tired, you can take my seat.</em> Advice says what is a good idea for the listener.","A warning shows a bad result that the listener can still stop: <em>If you run on the wet floor, you'll fall.</em> A plan uses <b>going to</b>, because the speaker has already decided. A prediction just says what the speaker thinks will happen. To find the job, ask two questions. Is the result good or bad for the listener? Who does the action in each half?"],"examples":[{"s":"If you lend me your calculator, I'll share my snacks with you.","g":"A deal: you do one thing, I do another thing for you."},{"s":"If we leave now, we might catch the early ferry.","g":"Might: it is possible, but the speaker is not sure."},{"s":"If you feel dizzy, sit down and drink some water.","g":"An instruction: base verbs, no subject."},{"s":"If you don't understand the homework, I can explain it after class.","g":"An offer with can. The speaker asks for nothing back."},{"s":"If Pim gets the part, she's going to practise every evening.","g":"A plan Pim has already made: is going to and the base verb."}]},"B2":{"key":"Keep the present form after <b>if</b>, then choose the result that carries your meaning: <b>will</b>, <b>might</b> or <b>may</b>, <b>can</b>, <b>should</b>, <b>going to</b> or an imperative.","body":["<b>Will</b> is only the default result. Real conditionals about the future take any modal verb that fits, and each one changes the message. <b>Will</b> is a confident prediction or a promise. <b>Might</b> and <b>may</b> say the result is possible but not certain. <b>Can</b> gives permission, shows what is possible, or makes an offer. <b>Should</b> gives advice. <b>Going to</b> reports a plan that has already been decided.","The result can also be an imperative, which is how most instructions, requests and warnings on signs and in messages are written: <em>If the bell rings, line up.</em> There is no subject, and the negative is <em>don't</em> and the base verb. Every modal verb is followed by the base verb, with no <em>to</em>, no <em>-s</em> and no <em>-ing</em>, while <b>going to</b> needs <em>am</em>, <em>is</em> or <em>are</em> in front of it. The <b>if</b> half stays in the present whichever result you choose.","These forms let one pattern do many jobs. In a promise the speaker commits herself to an action. In a deal, or negotiation, each side does something for the other: you do this, and I will do that. An offer gives the listener something useful without asking for anything back, as in <em>If you're tired, you can take my seat</em>. Advice points to the sensible action for the listener.","A warning names an unpleasant result that the listener can still avoid, as in <em>If you run on the wet floor, you'll fall</em>. A plan with <b>going to</b> tells you the decision was made before speaking, and a prediction simply gives the speaker's view of what will follow. To name the job, check whether the result is good or bad for the listener and who acts in each half."],"examples":[{"s":"If you lend me your calculator, I'll share my snacks with you.","g":"A deal with will: each side does something for the other."},{"s":"If we leave now, we might catch the early ferry.","g":"Might plus the base verb: a possible result, not a certain one."},{"s":"If you feel dizzy, sit down and drink some water.","g":"Imperative result: an instruction, with base verbs and no subject."},{"s":"If you don't understand the homework, I can explain it after class.","g":"An offer: can plus the base verb, with nothing asked in return."},{"s":"If Pim gets the part, she's going to practise every evening.","g":"Going to shows a plan decided before speaking; note is before going to."}]},"C1":{"key":"<b>Will</b> is merely the default: a real future condition combines with whichever modal verb or imperative expresses the speaker's certainty, permission, advice, intention or instruction.","body":["In real use, the textbook pairing of a present tense with <b>will</b> accounts for under one conditional in ten; a great many real conditionals end in an imperative or another modal verb instead. The modal keeps its ordinary meaning. <b>Will</b> commits the speaker to a prediction or promise; <b>might</b> and <b>may</b> lower the certainty; <b>can</b> covers permission, possibility and offers; <b>should</b> advises; and <b>going to</b> reports an intention formed before the moment of speaking.","An imperative result turns the sentence into an instruction, request or warning: <em>If the bell rings, line up.</em> It has no subject and is made negative with <em>don't</em>. Formally, all the modal verbs take the base verb, so <em>to</em>, <em>-s</em> and <em>-ing</em> are all excluded, whereas <b>going to</b> requires <em>am</em>, <em>is</em> or <em>are</em>. None of this touches the <b>if</b> clause, which stays in a present form because it still only sets the scene.","What the sentence does depends on who benefits and who acts. A promise binds the speaker. A deal, the basic move in negotiating, trades one action for another. An offer benefits the listener at no cost, as in <em>If you're tired, you can take my seat</em>, and advice recommends a course of action to the listener. The same grammar therefore reads as generous, cautious or firm according to the result chosen.","A warning predicts an unwelcome but avoidable outcome, as in <em>If you run on the wet floor, you'll fall</em>. <b>Going to</b> marks a plan, and a bare <b>will</b> with no benefit or cost to the listener is simply a prediction. Note that <b>might</b>, <b>may</b> and <b>could</b> overlap so heavily in results that no sensible test asks you to choose between them; what matters is the contrast between certain and merely possible."],"examples":[{"s":"If you lend me your calculator, I'll share my snacks with you.","g":"Negotiation: one action is traded for another, and will commits the speaker to her side."},{"s":"If we leave now, we might catch the early ferry.","g":"Might lowers the certainty of the result; the condition itself stays in the present simple."},{"s":"If you feel dizzy, sit down and drink some water.","g":"Imperative main clause: the sentence instructs rather than predicts."},{"s":"If you don't understand the homework, I can explain it after class.","g":"Can frames the help as available, which makes the sentence an offer at no cost to the listener."},{"s":"If Pim gets the part, she's going to practise every evening.","g":"Be going to reports a prior intention that depends on the condition being met."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w2l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"If you help me carry these boxes, I'll buy you a bubble tea."}],"stem":"What is Nan doing?","options":["Giving a warning","Making a deal","Giving advice","Describing a habit"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A warning shows a bad result. A bubble tea is a good one.","Yes: you do one thing, and I do another thing for you. That is a deal.","Advice says what is good for the listener, often with should. Nan wants help for herself.","A habit has two present verbs. I'll shows one future time."],"hint":"Who does the action in each half?","why":"The listener carries the boxes and Nan buys the tea in return. Two sides each doing something is a deal."},{"id":"w2l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"If the queue is short, I might try the new mango smoothie."}],"stem":"What does Mint mean?","options":["She will definitely try it.","She is not allowed to try it.","She will possibly try it.","She has already tried it."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Definitely is the meaning of will. Might is weaker.","Might is about what is possible, not about rules.","Yes: might means possible but not sure, even with a short queue.","Might looks forward. She has not tried it yet."],"hint":"How sure is the speaker about the result?","why":"Might in the result means the result is possible, not certain. Mint has not decided, even if the queue is short."},{"id":"w2l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"Which sentence is a warning?","options":["If you're cold, you can borrow my hoodie.","If you finish early, you should check your spelling.","If you touch that pan, you'll burn your fingers.","If we win on Friday, we're going to order pizza."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["This gives the listener something nice. It is an offer.","Should gives advice: a good idea, not a danger.","Yes: a bad result that the listener can still stop.","Going to shows a plan the speakers have already made."],"hint":"Is the result good or bad for the listener?","why":"A warning shows a bad result the listener can still avoid. Burnt fingers are the only bad result here."},{"id":"w2l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"My cold is nearly gone. If my cough ___ better by Friday, I might come to your sleepover.","options":["will get","get","getting","gets"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["No will after if, even when the result has might.","My cough is it, so the present verb needs -s.","After if you need a full present verb, not an -ing form alone.","Yes: present simple after if, whatever the result is."],"hint":"Does the if half change when the result changes?","why":"The if half takes the present simple with every kind of result. My cough is like it, so the verb takes -s."},{"id":"w2l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"A notice by the lift says: If the fire alarm rings, ___ the lift.","options":["not use","don't use","doesn't use","you not use"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A negative instruction needs don't before the verb.","Yes: don't and the base verb, with no subject.","Doesn't is for he, she or it in a statement. An instruction uses don't.","An instruction has no subject, and the negative needs don't."],"hint":"How do you tell someone not to do something?","why":"The result is an instruction, so it is an imperative. The negative imperative is don't with the base verb."},{"id":"w2l2-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"We've already decided. If our team wins the quiz on Friday, we ___ the prize money on a class pizza party.","options":["going to spend","are going spend","are going to spend","are go to spend"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Going to needs am, is or are before it.","Don't drop to: the plan form is going to and the base verb.","Yes: are going to and the base verb, for a plan already made.","The form is going, not go."],"hint":"Which parts does this plan form need?","why":"A plan that is already decided takes be going to with the base verb. With we, the verb be is are."},{"id":"w2l2-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"If you [[are free]] [[on Saturday]], you [[should to come]] to our dance show [[at school]].","answer":2,"fix":"should come","fb":["Are free is a present form after if. It is fine.","On Saturday is the right time phrase.","This is the mistake. After should, use the base verb with no to.","At school is fine."],"hint":"Look at the verb after each helping word.","why":"Should is followed by the base verb with no to, so the correct form is should come."},{"id":"w2l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","given":"Perhaps I'll go to the night market if I finish early.","keyword":"MIGHT","stem":"If I finish early, I ___ to the night market.","accept":["might go"],"model":"might go","hint":"Which word in the first sentence shows she is not sure?","why":"Perhaps I'll go means it is possible but not sure. Might with the base verb says the same thing."}],"world":"w2"},"w2l3":{"id":"w2l3","name":"Zero or first? plus when, as soon as, until, before, after + present","cefr":"B1","ctype":"1","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If / when / as soon as / until / before / after + present simple","result":"present simple (always) or will + verb (this future time)"},"uses":[{"use":"What always happens (zero)","ex":"If you leave ice cream in the sun, it melts."},{"use":"This one future time (first)","ex":"If you leave that ice cream there, it'll melt."},{"use":"An event you expect (when)","ex":"When I get home, I'll feed the cat."},{"use":"Straight after (as soon as)","ex":"I'll text you as soon as the doors open."},{"use":"Up to a moment (until)","ex":"Wait here until I come back."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Present with present means always; present with <b>will</b> means this one future time, and time words like <b>when</b> follow the same rule as <b>if</b>.","body":["Zero and first conditionals look alike, so check the result half. In a zero conditional both verbs are present forms. <em>Can</em> and <em>can't</em> count as present forms too. A zero conditional tells you what always or usually happens: a fact, a habit or a rule.","In a first conditional the result has <b>will</b>, or a word like <em>might</em>. It is about one possible time in the future. Try adding <em>every time</em>. If it fits, the sentence is zero. If <em>this time</em> or <em>tonight</em> fits, it is first.","Time words work like <b>if</b>. They are <b>when</b>, <b>as soon as</b>, <b>until</b>, <b>before</b> and <b>after</b>. For the future, the verb after them is in the present simple. Do not put <b>will</b> there. <b>Will</b> goes in the other half: <em>I'll phone Gran after I eat.</em> Remember the <em>-s</em> for <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> and <em>it</em>.","Learn what each word means. <b>When</b> means at that time. <b>As soon as</b> means straight after, with no waiting. <b>Before</b> means earlier and <b>after</b> means later. <b>Until</b> means up to that moment, and then things change. <em>You can't leave until the bell rings</em> means you can leave when it rings, not earlier.","Choose <b>if</b> or <b>when</b> by how sure you are. Use <b>if</b> when the thing may or may not happen. Use <b>when</b> when you expect it to happen for sure. Night always comes, so we say <em>when it gets dark</em>. A prize is not certain, so we say <em>if I win</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"If you press this button, the screen turns off.","g":"Zero: present and present. It happens every time."},{"s":"If you press that button now, we'll lose the whole game.","g":"First: present and will. One possible time, now."},{"s":"When the lesson ends, I'll meet you at the gate.","g":"When, not if: the lesson is sure to end. Present simple after when."},{"s":"We'll keep practising until the music stops.","g":"Until: up to that moment. Present simple after until, with -s."},{"s":"<s>I'll call you as soon as I will get home.</s>","g":"Wrong. Say: as soon as I get home. No will after a time word."}]},"B2":{"key":"Two present forms describe what always happens, a present form with <b>will</b> describes one future occasion, and <b>when</b>, <b>as soon as</b>, <b>until</b>, <b>before</b> and <b>after</b> take a present form for the future just as <b>if</b> does.","body":["Zero and first conditionals are both real: nothing is imagined in either. They differ only in time. The zero conditional has present forms in both halves, including <em>can</em> or <em>can't</em>, and describes what generally or always happens, such as facts, habits and rules. The first conditional has <b>will</b> or another modal verb in the result and looks at one possible occasion in the future. A quick test is to add <em>every time</em> for zero, or <em>this time</em>, <em>tonight</em> or <em>tomorrow</em> for first.","The rule about <b>if</b> also covers future time clauses. After <b>when</b>, <b>as soon as</b>, <b>until</b>, <b>before</b> and <b>after</b>, use the present simple for a future event, with the usual <em>-s</em> for <em>he</em>, <em>she</em> and <em>it</em>, and keep <b>will</b> for the main half: <em>I'll phone Gran after I eat.</em> The present perfect is also possible after these words when you want to stress that the first action is complete.","Each word places the two events differently. <b>When</b> means at that time, and <b>as soon as</b> means immediately afterwards. <b>Before</b> and <b>after</b> put one event earlier or later than the other. <b>Until</b> means up to that point and no further, so <em>You can't leave until the bell rings</em> tells you that leaving becomes possible at the bell and not earlier.","<b>If</b> and <b>when</b> are not interchangeable for the future. <b>If</b> presents the event as uncertain, while <b>when</b> shows that the speaker expects it. Night always comes, so we say <em>when it gets dark</em>; a prize is uncertain, so we say <em>if I win</em>. In zero conditionals about general truths, however, <b>if</b> and <b>when</b> mean almost the same."],"examples":[{"s":"If you press this button, the screen turns off.","g":"Zero conditional: two present forms, true every time you press it."},{"s":"If you press that button now, we'll lose the whole game.","g":"First conditional: will in the result, about this one occasion."},{"s":"When the lesson ends, I'll meet you at the gate.","g":"When shows an expected event; the verb after it is in the present simple, and will is in the main half."},{"s":"We'll keep practising until the music stops.","g":"Until marks the end point of the practising; present simple with -s after it."},{"s":"<s>I'll call you as soon as I will get home.</s>","g":"Wrong: will after as soon as. Correct: I'll call you as soon as I get home."}]},"C1":{"key":"Zero and first conditionals share the real row and differ only in time reference, while future time clauses obey the same present-form rule as <b>if</b>, and <b>when</b> replaces <b>if</b> once the event is expected.","body":["Both patterns are open conditionals: neither denies anything or hides a contrary fact. The contrast is purely one of time. With present forms in both clauses, <em>can</em> and <em>can't</em> included, the sentence generalises over every occasion, which suits facts, habits and rules. With <b>will</b> or another modal verb in the main clause it predicts for a single future occasion. Time expressions usually settle the question: <em>always</em> and <em>every time</em> point to zero, whereas <em>tonight</em> or <em>this time</em> point to first.","Future time clauses behave exactly like <b>if</b> clauses, and for the same reason: the clause after <b>when</b>, <b>as soon as</b>, <b>until</b>, <b>before</b> or <b>after</b> fixes a scene rather than making a prediction, so it takes a present form and leaves <b>will</b> to the main clause, as in <em>I'll phone Gran after I eat</em>. A present perfect is equally correct after these words when completion matters.","The time words differ in how they order the events. <b>When</b> places the main event at that time and <b>as soon as</b> adds the idea of no delay. <b>Before</b> and <b>after</b> state the sequence. <b>Until</b> sets an end point for a continuing state, which is why <em>You can't leave until the bell rings</em> implies that you may leave once it rings. A main clause with <b>until</b> is therefore typically negative or describes something that continues.","The choice between <b>if</b> and <b>when</b> signals expectation. <b>If</b> leaves the event open; <b>when</b> takes for granted that it will occur. Using <b>when</b> for something merely hoped for sounds over-confident, and using <b>if</b> for something certain sounds oddly doubtful. Only in general truths does the difference fade, because there both words cover every occasion on which the condition is met."],"examples":[{"s":"If you press this button, the screen turns off.","g":"Open and general: present forms in both clauses cover every occasion."},{"s":"If you press that button now, we'll lose the whole game.","g":"Open and specific: will predicts the outcome of a single possible act. It also works as a warning."},{"s":"When the lesson ends, I'll meet you at the gate.","g":"When takes the ending for granted; the time clause has a present form and the main clause carries will."},{"s":"We'll keep practising until the music stops.","g":"Until bounds a continuing activity; the clause after it takes the present simple although the time is future."},{"s":"<s>I'll call you as soon as I will get home.</s>","g":"Error: a future time clause cannot carry will. Correct: I'll call you as soon as I get home."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w2l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"Which sentence is about one future time, not about what always happens?","options":["If I drink coffee at night, I can't sleep.","If you mix blue and yellow, you get green.","If it rains hard, our soi floods.","If Mum works late tonight, I'll cook the noodles."],"answer":3,"near":2,"fb":["Two present forms: this happens every time. It is a habit.","Two present forms: a fact that is always true.","Present and present: it happens every time it rains hard.","Yes: tonight and I'll show one possible future time."],"hint":"Try adding every time. Where does it not fit?","why":"Three sentences have present forms in both halves, so they are always true. Only the one with tonight and will is about one future time."},{"id":"w2l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"When I finish this level, I'll help you."},{"who":"Bam","text":"If I finish this level, I'll help you."}],"stem":"Who is sure she will finish the level?","options":["Jane","Bam","Both girls","Neither girl"],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: when shows that she expects it to happen.","If means maybe. Bam is not sure she will finish.","Only one of them is sure. If and when are different here.","One of them is sure. Look for the word that means it will happen."],"hint":"Which word shows that the speaker expects the event?","why":"When shows the speaker expects the event. If shows it may or may not happen. So one girl is sure and the other is not."},{"id":"w2l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mum","text":"You can't go on your phone until you finish your homework."}],"stem":"Fern finishes her homework at eight o'clock. What is true?","options":["She must stop using her phone at eight.","She can use her phone from eight.","She can use her phone before eight.","She can't use her phone at all tonight."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["It is the other way round. The phone is not allowed up to eight.","Yes: until marks the moment when things change. At eight the rule ends.","Until means not earlier than that moment.","Until gives an end point. After the homework, the phone is allowed."],"hint":"What changes at the moment after until?","why":"Can't until means not allowed up to that moment. When the homework is finished at eight, the phone is allowed."},{"id":"w2l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"The concert starts at seven. I'll message the group as soon as I ___ at the stadium.","options":["will arrive","arrive","arrived","arriving"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["No will after as soon as. It follows the same rule as if.","Yes: present simple after a time word for the future.","She is not there yet, so a past form is wrong.","After I you need a full verb, not an -ing form alone."],"hint":"Time words follow the same rule as which small word?","why":"After as soon as, a future event takes the present simple. Will stays in the other half."},{"id":"w2l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"Don't worry about the rain. If tonight's match is cancelled, I ___ you straight away.","options":["tell","told","will tell","telling"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Present with present means every time. This is about tonight only, so the result needs will.","Tonight is the future, so a past form is wrong.","Yes: one future time, so will goes in the result. It is a promise.","The result needs will and the base verb, not an -ing form alone."],"hint":"Is this about every time or about one future time?","why":"The sentence is about tonight only, one possible future time. That is a first conditional, so the result takes will."},{"id":"w2l3-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"The film ends at 9.15, and my phone has to stay off in the cinema. I'll call you ___ it ends.","options":["if","until","when","before"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["If is for something uncertain. The film is sure to end.","Until means up to that moment. She is making one call, at the end.","Yes: the end is expected, and the call comes at that time.","Her phone is off during the film, so she can't call earlier."],"hint":"How sure is the event? At what moment is she able to call?","why":"The film will certainly end, so if is wrong. The phone is off during the film, so the call comes at the end."},{"id":"w2l3-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"I [[will wait]] at the [[BTS exit]] until you [[will arrive]], so [[don't run]].","answer":2,"fix":"arrive","fb":["Will wait is the main half, so will is right.","BTS exit is fine.","This is the mistake. No will after until: say arrive.","Don't run is a correct negative instruction."],"hint":"Two verbs have will. Do both halves allow it?","why":"Until is a time word, so the verb after it is in the present simple for the future: until you arrive."},{"id":"w2l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","given":"First I'll finish my homework. Then I'll watch the series.","keyword":"AFTER","stem":"I'll watch the series ___ finish my homework.","accept":["after I"],"model":"after I","hint":"Which action comes first? Where does will belong?","why":"Homework comes first, so it follows after. After a time word the verb is in the present simple for the future, with no will."}],"world":"w2"},"w3l1":{"id":"w3l1","name":"The true meaning: real or imagined? One step back and the Truth receipt","cefr":"B1","ctype":"2","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + past form (one step back from reality, not past time)","result":"would / could + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Dreaming about a different life","ex":"If I had a puppy, I'd call him Mochi."},{"use":"Saying what is not true now","ex":"If I had more shelves, I'd keep all my comics here."},{"use":"Imagining a superpower","ex":"If I could fly, I'd never wait for the BTS again."},{"use":"Imagining you are someone or something else","ex":"If I were a cat, I would sleep all day."},{"use":"Imagining an unlikely tomorrow","ex":"If we had no school tomorrow, I'd sleep until ten."}],"thai":"ถอยหลังหนึ่งก้าว = ถอยห่างจากความจริง · Truth line: ความจริงคือ…","levels":{"B1":{"key":"In an imagined <em>if</em> sentence, a past form is not about past time: it means one step back from reality.","body":["Every <em>if</em> sentence asks two questions. When is it about? Is it real or imagined? A real sentence talks about something possible: <em>If it rains, we'll take a taxi.</em> An imagined sentence talks about something that is not true now. It is a dream or a game in your head: <em>If I had a car, I'd drive you home.</em> This imagined kind has a nickname: the second conditional, or type 2.","Look at <em>had</em>. It is a past form, but it is not about past time. The speaker is talking about now. In English, a past form can mean one step back from reality. So <em>If I had a car</em> does not mean I had one before. It means I don't have one now. It even works with tomorrow: <em>If we had no school tomorrow, I'd sleep until ten.</em> A real memory looks different. Here both halves are plain past, with no <em>would</em>: <em>If it rained, we stayed in.</em>","Think of a staircase. To go from real to imagined, each verb takes one step back. <em>Am</em> and <em>is</em> become <em>were</em>. <em>Have</em> becomes <em>had</em>. <em>Can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. <em>Will</em> becomes <em>would</em>. Other verbs take their past form too: <em>live</em> becomes <em>lived</em>, and <em>go</em> becomes <em>went</em>. The stepped-back verb goes in the <em>if</em> half. <em>Would</em> goes in the result half (what happens in the dream). Do not put <em>will</em> or <em>would</em> in the <em>if</em> half.","You may know this staircase from reported speech. <em>I am tired</em> becomes <em>She said she was tired.</em> The steps are the same, but the reason is different. Reported speech steps back in time. An imagined <em>if</em> sentence steps back from reality. Later, in Rewind Tower, you will take two steps for an imagined past.","Here is a habit that always works. After an imagined sentence, write its Truth receipt (a short line that says what is really true). Start with the word Truth and add a date. For <em>If I had a car</em>, write: Truth: I don't have a car, now. The Truth is the opposite of the <em>if</em> half. A real sentence has no receipt, because nothing in it is untrue. No receipt means no step back."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had a car, I'd drive you home.","g":"Truth: I don't have a car — now. \"Had\" is one step back from \"have\"."},{"s":"If I were a cat, I would sleep all day.","g":"Truth: I'm not a cat — now. \"Am\" steps back to \"were\", and \"will\" to \"would\"."},{"s":"If Ploy could cook, she'd make us noodles.","g":"Truth: Ploy can't cook — now. \"Can\" steps back to \"could\"."},{"s":"If it rains, we'll take a taxi.","g":"Real: rain is possible. No Truth line, so no step back."},{"s":"<s>If I have a time machine, I will visit the dinosaurs.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a time machine, I would visit the dinosaurs. Truth: I don't have a time machine — now."}]},"B2":{"key":"In an imagined <em>if</em> sentence the past form does not show past time; it shows one step back from reality.","body":["Every conditional sets two dials for each half: when is it about, and is it real or imagined? A real conditional keeps a possibility open: <em>If it rains, we'll take a taxi.</em> An imagined one describes a world that is different from the real one, now or in the future: <em>If I had a car, I'd drive you home.</em> Coursebooks call this the second conditional, or type 2. It is the language of dreams and what-ifs.","The surprise is the verb. <em>Had</em> is a past form, yet the sentence is about now. In English a past form has two jobs: it can show past time, or it can show distance from reality. Here it means one step back from what is true, so it works with <em>now</em> and even with <em>tomorrow</em>: <em>If we had no school tomorrow, I'd sleep until ten.</em> A real memory looks different: here both halves are plain past, and the result has no <em>would</em>: <em>If it rained, we stayed in.</em>","Picture a staircase. Moving from real to imagined, every verb goes down one step: <em>am</em> and <em>is</em> to <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> to <em>had</em>, <em>can</em> to <em>could</em>, <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>, and ordinary verbs to their past simple, so <em>live</em> gives <em>lived</em> and <em>go</em> gives <em>went</em>. The stepped-back verb sits in the <em>if</em> half, and <em>would</em> or <em>could</em> belongs in the result half. A plain <em>if</em> half takes neither <em>will</em> nor <em>would</em>.","You have climbed this staircase before, in reported speech, where <em>I am tired</em> becomes <em>She said she was tired.</em> The steps are identical but the reason is different: reported speech steps back in time, while an imagined conditional steps back from reality. For an imagined past you will need two steps, which is the work of Rewind Tower.","To check any sentence, write its Truth receipt: a short line beginning with the word Truth that states the real situation and dates it. For <em>If I had a car</em>, the receipt is: Truth: I don't have a car, now. The Truth is always the opposite of the <em>if</em> half. A real conditional has no receipt, because it denies nothing, and with no receipt there is no step back."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had a car, I'd drive you home.","g":"Truth: I don't have a car — now. \"Had\" is \"have\" one step back; \"I'd\" is \"I would\"."},{"s":"If I were a cat, I would sleep all day.","g":"Truth: I'm not a cat — now. \"Am\" steps back to \"were\", and \"will\" to \"would\"."},{"s":"If Ploy could cook, she'd make us noodles.","g":"Truth: Ploy can't cook — now. \"Can\" steps back to \"could\"."},{"s":"If it rains, we'll take a taxi.","g":"Real and open: it may rain. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line and no step back."},{"s":"<s>If I have a time machine, I will visit the dinosaurs.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a time machine, I would visit the dinosaurs. Truth: I don't have a time machine — now. Both halves must step back."}]},"C1":{"key":"The past form in an imagined conditional marks distance from reality, not past time: one step back on every verb.","body":["Each half of a conditional answers two questions: when is it about, and is it real or imagined? A real, or open, conditional leaves the matter undecided: <em>If it rains, we'll take a taxi.</em> An imagined, or remote, conditional sets up a world the speaker treats as different from the actual one, now or in the future: <em>If I had a car, I'd drive you home.</em> Its school nickname is the second conditional, or type 2. Real or imagined is the speaker's view, never a measured probability.","English past forms do three jobs. They place an event in past time, they shift tenses in reported speech, and they mark distance from the actual world. In <em>If I had a car</em>, the form shuts out reality, not the present moment, so it combines happily with <em>tomorrow</em>: <em>If we had no school tomorrow, I'd sleep until ten.</em> A past form after <em>if</em> can still be plain past time; the result decides. <em>If it rained, we stayed in</em> has no <em>would</em>, so it reports real past days.","The change is regular enough to draw as a staircase. From real to imagined, each verb moves one step back: <em>am</em> and <em>is</em> to <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> to <em>had</em>, <em>can</em> to <em>could</em>, <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>, and other verbs to the past simple, as in <em>lived</em>. The stepped-back verb belongs to the <em>if</em> half and <em>would</em> or <em>could</em> to the result. In a plain condition neither <em>will</em> nor <em>would</em> follows <em>if</em>. English has only one past inflection, so an imagined past needs a second step, with <em>had</em>.","The staircase is the one you know from reported speech, where <em>I am tired</em> becomes <em>She said she was tired.</em> The forms match but the motives differ: reporting steps back in time, away from the moment of speaking, whereas an imagined conditional steps back in reality. Keeping the two reasons apart helps with a common slip, the stray <em>would</em> in the <em>if</em> half, which may be carried over from reported speech.","A reliable test is the Truth receipt, the hidden line that says what is actually the case, with a date: for <em>If I had a car</em>, Truth: I don't have a car, now. The Truth is the opposite of the <em>if</em> half, and a real conditional has none because it denies nothing. Treat the receipt as a strong tendency. Sometimes a speaker steps back only to sound tentative or polite about something perfectly possible, and then the distance is social, not factual."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had a car, I'd drive you home.","g":"Truth: I don't have a car — now. The past form \"had\" marks distance from reality; \"I'd\" is \"I would\"."},{"s":"If I were a cat, I would sleep all day.","g":"Truth: I'm not a cat — now. \"Am\" becomes \"were\" and \"will\" becomes \"would\": one step each."},{"s":"If Ploy could cook, she'd make us noodles.","g":"Truth: Ploy can't cook — now. \"Could\" is \"can\" one step back, not past ability."},{"s":"If it rains, we'll take a taxi.","g":"An open conditional: rain is treated as a live possibility. It denies nothing, so there is no Truth line and no step back."},{"s":"<s>If I have a time machine, I will visit the dinosaurs.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a time machine, I would visit the dinosaurs. Truth: I don't have a time machine — now. The present forms would present the machine as a real possibility."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w3l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"Look at that kitten! If I had a cat, I'd share my fish with her."}],"stem":"What do we know about Fah?","options":["She has a cat at home now.","She had a cat when she was little.","She doesn't have a cat now.","She is getting a cat next week."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["\"If I had\" is a step back from reality, so the real situation is the opposite of this.","\"Had\" is a past form, but it is not past time here. Fah is talking about now.","Yes: \"If I had\" means it is not true now. Truth: Fah doesn't have a cat — now.","Nothing here talks about a plan. The sentence only imagines a different now."],"hint":"Write the Truth receipt. Is the if half true now?","why":"\"If I had a cat\" is one step back from reality, so the Truth is the opposite: Fah has no cat now. The past form does not show past time.","truth":"Fah doesn't have a cat — now."},{"id":"w3l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"This bus is so slow today. If I lived near school, I would walk there."}],"stem":"When is Mint's sentence about?","options":["The past: she lived near school before.","Now: she imagines a different present.","The past: she walked to school last year.","The future: she has a plan to move house."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["\"Lived\" looks past, but with \"would walk\" it is a step back from reality, not a step back in time.","Yes: the past form shows distance from reality. Mint is on the bus now, dreaming of a different now.","Mint is on the bus today, so \"would walk\" is a dream result here, not a memory of last year.","A real plan keeps the present after if, with will in the result. This is only imagined."],"hint":"Try a time word in the sentence: yesterday or now? Which one fits?","why":"Mint is on the bus today and does not live near school. \"Lived\" with \"would walk\" is one step back from reality, so the sentence imagines a different present.","truth":"Mint doesn't live near school — now."},{"id":"w3l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Which sentence tells us that Bam can't swim now?","options":["If Bam can swim, she can come to the pool with us.","If Bam is free on Saturday, she'll come to the pool with us.","If Bam was at the pool on Sunday, she swam with us.","If Bam could swim, she'd come to the pool with us."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"Can\" is the real form: the speaker doesn't know and keeps it open. There is no Truth line here.","This is a real possibility about Saturday. It says nothing about swimming.","Past plus past with \"on Sunday\" is real past time. Nothing is imagined.","Yes: \"could\" is \"can\" one step back, with \"she'd\" in the result. Truth: Bam can't swim — now."],"hint":"Which sentence has a hidden Truth line? Look for a verb one step back plus a dream result.","why":"Only the sentence with \"could\" and \"she'd\" steps back from reality, so its Truth is that Bam can't swim now. The other three are real possibilities or real past time.","truth":"Bam can't swim — now."},{"id":"w3l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Jane loves comics, but she can't draw well. She says: \"If I ___ draw well, I'd make my own comic.\"","options":["can","will","could","would"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["That is the real form. But the Truth is that Jane can't draw well, so take one step back.","Will is for a real future, and it does not go in the if half.","Yes: can steps back to could. Truth: Jane can't draw well — now.","Would belongs in the result half, and it is already there: \"I'd make\"."],"hint":"Find the real verb in the first sentence. Where does it go on the staircase?","why":"The Truth is \"Jane can't draw well\", so the if half is imagined. On the staircase, can goes one step back to could.","truth":"Jane can't draw well — now."},{"id":"w3l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Pim has three tests this week, so she has no free time today. She sighs: \"If I ___ free time today, I'd watch the whole series.\"","options":["have","had","would have","will have"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The present makes it sound real. But Pim has no free time today, so step back once.","Yes: have steps back to had. It is about today, not the past. Truth: Pim has no free time — today.","Would lives in the result half (\"I'd watch\"), not in the if half.","Will is for a real future, and it does not go in the if half."],"hint":"Is it true today? If not, how many steps back, and on which verb?","why":"Pim has no free time today, so the if half is imagined: have goes one step back to had. Will and would do not go in the if half.","truth":"Pim doesn't have free time — today."},{"id":"w3l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"I've been in this taxi on Sukhumvit Road for forty minutes!"}],"stem":"[[If I am]] a bird right now, [[I would fly]] over the traffic [[and land]] on the school roof [[before the bell]].","answer":0,"fix":"If I were","fb":["Yes: Mook is not a bird, so this is imagined. Am steps back to were: If I were a bird.","This part is fine: would plus the base verb is the dream result.","This part is fine: \"land\" follows \"would\" too (would fly and land).","This part is fine: it is only a time phrase."],"hint":"Is Mook really a bird? Which verb has not stepped back?","why":"Mook is not a bird, so the if half is imagined and needs one step back: am becomes were. The result \"would fly\" is already correct.","truth":"Mook is not a bird — now."},{"id":"w3l1-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"It never snows in Bangkok. But imagine: if it snowed here tomorrow, everyone ___ photos all day.","options":["will take","took","would take","is going to take"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Will is the real form. Snow in Bangkok is only imagined, so will takes one step back.","The result half needs a result word plus the verb. The past simple alone belongs in the if half.","Yes: will steps back to would. The sentence is about tomorrow, but it is a dream.","That is a real plan or prediction. This snow is imagined."],"hint":"Look at \"snowed\" with \"tomorrow\". Real or imagined? Then which result word matches?","why":"\"Snowed\" with \"tomorrow\" is not past time; it is one step back from reality. The result must step back too, so will becomes would.","truth":"It won't snow in Bangkok tomorrow."},{"id":"w3l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Fern doesn't speak Korean, so she needs subtitles. Complete her dream with the right form of the verb in brackets: \"If I ___ (speak) Korean, I'd understand every word without subtitles.\"","accept":["spoke","could speak","were able to speak","was able to speak"],"model":"spoke","hint":"Is it true now? Take one step back on the verb.","why":"Fern doesn't speak Korean now, so the if half is imagined. Speak goes one step back to spoke; the result already has would.","truth":"Fern doesn't speak Korean — now."}],"world":"w3"},"w3l2":{"id":"w3l2","name":"Building it: if + past, would / could / might + verb; were; If I were you","cefr":"B1","ctype":"2","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"If + past simple (were for every person)","result":"would / could / might + base verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Dreams about a different now","ex":"If we lived by the sea, we could swim every day."},{"use":"Giving advice","ex":"If I were you, I'd tell the teacher."},{"use":"Saying what is only possible in the dream","ex":"If Nan asked her mum, she might say yes."},{"use":"Explaining why you can't do something","ex":"If I had my phone with me, I could show you the photo."},{"use":"Asking what-if questions","ex":"What would you do if you had a superpower for a day?"}],"thai":"สมมติว่า… (suppose…) ก็คง… (would probably…) · ความจริงคือ… (the Truth line)","levels":{"B1":{"key":"Build an imagined sentence with the past simple after <em>if</em>, and <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> plus the base verb in the result.","body":["An imagined sentence has two halves. The <em>if</em> half takes the past simple: <em>had, lived, asked</em>. The result half (what happens in the dream) takes <em>would</em> plus the base verb (the verb with no ending). <em>If I had more time, I would learn Korean.</em> In speech, <em>would</em> often shrinks to <em>'d</em>: <em>I'd learn</em>. If the <em>if</em> half comes first, put a comma after it. If the result comes first, you need no comma.","You can choose from three result words. <em>Would</em> is the sure result in the dream. <em>Could</em> is <em>can</em> one step back. It means would be able to. <em>Might</em> means maybe: the speaker is not sure. So <em>I'd swim</em> is certain, <em>I could swim</em> is about being able, and <em>I might swim</em> is only perhaps. After all three, use the base verb. Say <em>would go</em>, never <em>would went</em> or <em>would to go</em>.","The verb <em>be</em> is special. In writing, use <em>were</em> for every person: <em>if I were, if she were, if it were</em>. You will hear <em>was</em> when people chat, and that is fine in speech. In tests and essays, write <em>were</em>. One phrase is worth learning by heart: <em>If I were you, I'd</em> and then a verb. It gives advice. The speaker imagines standing in your place and says what she would do.","Watch for three traps. First, the present in the <em>if</em> half. Check the Truth: if it is not true now, step back. Write <em>if I owned</em>, not <em>if I own</em>. Second, <em>will</em> or <em>can</em> in the result. The halves work as a pair: past in one, <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> in the other. Third, <em>would</em> in the <em>if</em> half. <em>Would</em> lives in the result half only."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had more time, I'd learn Korean.","g":"Truth: I don't have more time — now. \"I'd\" is \"I would\": the sure result."},{"s":"If we lived by the sea, we could swim every day.","g":"Truth: we don't live by the sea — now. \"Could\" means we would be able to."},{"s":"If Nan asked her mum, she might say yes.","g":"Truth: Nan hasn't asked her mum; asking is only an idea. \"Might\" means maybe yes, maybe no."},{"s":"If I were you, I'd tell the teacher.","g":"Truth: I'm not you. This is advice. Write \"were\", not \"am\"."},{"s":"<s>If I would have a dog, I will walk it every day.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a dog, I would walk it every day. Truth: I don't have a dog — now. No \"would\" after \"if\", no \"will\" in the result."}]},"B2":{"key":"The second conditional pairs the past simple after <em>if</em> with <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> plus the base verb in the result.","body":["The second conditional has two halves that work as a pair. The <em>if</em> half takes the past simple, and the result half takes <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> followed by the base verb: <em>If I had more time, I'd learn Korean.</em> The short form <em>'d</em> before a base verb is <em>would</em>. Either half can come first. When the <em>if</em> half leads, a comma follows it; when the result leads, no comma is needed.","The three result words are a choice of meaning. <em>Would</em> gives the result the speaker is sure of in her imagined world. <em>Could</em> is <em>can</em> one step back, so it means would be able to. <em>Might</em> means perhaps: the result is possible but not certain. All three are followed by the base verb, with no <em>to</em> and no ending, so <em>would went</em> and <em>could to go</em> are both wrong. The real forms <em>will</em> and <em>can</em> have no place in an imagined result.","With the verb <em>be</em>, write <em>were</em> for every person: <em>if I were, if she were, if the room were</em>. In conversation many speakers say <em>was</em>, and nobody minds, but <em>were</em> is the form for tests and essays. The fixed phrase <em>If I were you, I'd</em> followed by a verb is the standard way to give advice: the speaker imagines being in your position and says what she would do there.","Three errors are worth hunting for. The first is a present form in the <em>if</em> half when the Truth receipt shows the situation is not true now: write <em>if I owned</em>, not <em>if I own</em>. The second is <em>will</em> or <em>can</em> in the result after a stepped-back <em>if</em> half; the two halves must match. The third is <em>would</em> inside the <em>if</em> half. <em>Would</em> is the result word and stays there."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had more time, I'd learn Korean.","g":"Truth: I don't have more time — now. \"I'd learn\" is \"I would learn\": the result she is sure of."},{"s":"If we lived by the sea, we could swim every day.","g":"Truth: we don't live by the sea — now. \"Could\" means we would be able to."},{"s":"If Nan asked her mum, she might say yes.","g":"Truth: Nan hasn't asked her mum; asking is only an idea. \"Might\" means perhaps: the answer is not certain."},{"s":"If I were you, I'd tell the teacher.","g":"Truth: I'm not you. The fixed advice phrase, with \"were\" for every person."},{"s":"<s>If I would have a dog, I will walk it every day.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a dog, I would walk it every day. Truth: I don't have a dog — now. \"Would\" never sits in the if half here, and \"will\" cannot follow a stepped-back if half."}]},"C1":{"key":"A remote conditional pairs a past simple condition with a stepped-back modal, <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em>, plus the base verb.","body":["The second conditional is built as a matched pair. The condition takes the past simple, and the result takes a stepped-back modal, <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em>, with the base verb: <em>If I had more time, I'd learn Korean.</em> Before a base verb, <em>'d</em> is always <em>would</em>. The halves can come in either order. By convention a comma follows a condition that comes first, and none is used when the result leads.","The modal is chosen for its meaning, because each keeps its own sense one step back. <em>Would</em> presents the result as certain inside the imagined world, or as what the speaker would choose. <em>Could</em> is remote <em>can</em>: would be able to, or would be possible. <em>Might</em> is remote <em>may</em>: perhaps. Each takes the bare base verb. The present forms <em>will</em>, <em>can</em> and <em>may</em> do not appear in an imagined result, and for obligation English says <em>would have to</em>.","For <em>be</em>, careful writing uses <em>were</em> with every subject: <em>if I were, if she were</em>. <em>Was</em> is normal in everyday speech and is not a mistake there, but <em>were</em> is the safe choice in exams. When the past is real, <em>was</em> is the only choice: <em>If I was rude last night, I'm sorry.</em> <em>If I were you, I'd</em> plus a verb is the set formula for advice, and speakers often drop the condition and keep only <em>I'd</em>.","Three errors follow from how learners build the pair. A present form in the condition treats an untrue situation as open, so check the Truth receipt first: <em>if I owned</em>, not <em>if I own</em>. <em>Will</em> or <em>can</em> after a remote condition mixes two worlds, since both halves must step back. And <em>would</em> in the condition copies the result word into the wrong half. It is correct there only in a few special uses, such as a polite request for willingness, which belong to Formal Citadel."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had more time, I'd learn Korean.","g":"Truth: I don't have more time — now. \"Would\" presents learning Korean as the certain result in the imagined world."},{"s":"If we lived by the sea, we could swim every day.","g":"Truth: we don't live by the sea — now. \"Could\" is remote \"can\": we would be able to."},{"s":"If Nan asked her mum, she might say yes.","g":"Truth: Nan hasn't asked her mum; asking is only an idea. \"Might\" is remote \"may\": the answer is only possible."},{"s":"If I were you, I'd tell the teacher.","g":"Truth: I'm not you. The set advice formula; the condition is often dropped: \"I'd tell the teacher.\""},{"s":"<s>If I would have a dog, I will walk it every day.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a dog, I would walk it every day. Truth: I don't have a dog — now. \"Would\" has been copied into the condition, and \"will\" belongs to a real future."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w3l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"I always take the songthaew to school. If I had a bike, I might cycle instead."}],"stem":"How sure is Nan about cycling to school?","options":["Sure: she would cycle every single day.","Not sure: maybe she would, maybe not.","She cycles now, but only on some days.","She cycled in the past, then stopped."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That would need \"would\". Nan chose a weaker result word.","Yes: \"might\" means maybe. In her dream, cycling is possible, not certain.","\"If I had a bike\" is a step back from reality: she has no bike now, so she doesn't cycle.","The past form here is not past time. Nan is imagining a different now."],"hint":"Look at the result word. What does that one tell you about how certain she is?","why":"\"Might\" means maybe. Nan imagines having a bike and says cycling is possible, not certain. She has no bike now.","truth":"Nan doesn't have a bike — now."},{"id":"w3l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"I've got a bad cough, and badminton practice is tonight."},{"who":"Jane","text":"If I were you, I'd rest tonight."}],"stem":"What is Jane doing?","options":["Promising to rest tonight.","Saying she wants to be Pim.","Describing a time she was ill.","Giving Pim some advice."],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["A promise would use will. \"I'd rest\" is what Jane would do in Pim's place.","Nobody wants to swap places. She only imagines Pim's situation to say what is best.","\"Were\" is not past time here. It is one step back from reality: Jane isn't Pim.","Yes: \"If I were you, I'd\" plus a verb is the fixed phrase for advice."],"hint":"Jane isn't Pim. So why does she imagine being in Pim's place?","why":"\"If I were you, I'd\" plus a verb is the fixed way to give advice. Jane imagines being in Pim's place and says what she would do: rest.","truth":"Jane is not Pim."},{"id":"w3l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mook's phone has no storage left, so she can't download the new blocky game. Which sentence says this as a dream?","options":["If Mook's phone has more storage, she can download the game.","If Mook's phone had more storage, she could download the game.","If Mook's phone had more storage, she couldn't download the game.","If Mook downloaded the game, her phone could have more storage."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["This is the real form: it keeps the question open. But we know the Truth: there is no storage left.","Yes: had and could are both one step back. Could means she would be able to.","The forms have stepped back, but the meaning is upside down: more storage would make it possible.","The two halves have changed places, so the meaning is different."],"hint":"Write the Truth first. Then check both halves: has each verb stepped back, and is the meaning the same?","why":"The Truth is \"no storage, so she can't\". The dream turns both halves round and steps back: had more storage, could download. Could means would be able to.","truth":"Mook's phone doesn't have enough storage — now."},{"id":"w3l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Fern asks what time the school fair starts, but Bam has no idea. Bam: \"Sorry! If I ___ the time, I'd tell you.\"","options":["know","would know","knew","will know"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The present makes it sound real. But Bam has no idea, so the if half steps back.","Would lives in the result half (\"I'd tell\"), not in the if half.","Yes: the past simple after if. Truth: Bam doesn't know the time — now.","Will is for a real future, and it does not go in the if half."],"hint":"Is it true now? And which half are you filling: the if half or the result?","why":"Bam doesn't know the time now, so the if half is imagined and takes the past simple. Would stays in the result half.","truth":"Bam doesn't know the time — now."},{"id":"w3l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"I can't decide. The blue hoodie or the grey one?"}],"stem":"Mint: \"If I were you, I ___ the blue one. It goes with everything.\"","options":["will choose","would chose","am going to choose","would choose"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"If I were\" is the stepped-back half, so the result must step back too.","Right result word, wrong verb form. After it, use the base verb, with no past ending.","That is a real plan. Mint isn't Fern, so this is imagined advice.","Yes: \"If I were you\" pairs with would plus the base verb."],"hint":"The if half has stepped back. What must the result half do? And which verb form follows?","why":"\"If I were you\" is imagined, so the result needs would plus the base verb. Will is the real form, and \"chose\" is a past form.","truth":"Mint is not Fern."},{"id":"w3l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"My aunt [[lives]] in Chiang Mai. If she [[would live]] in Bangkok, I [[would visit]] her [[every weekend]].","answer":1,"fix":"lived","fb":["This part is fine: it is the real fact, in the present.","Yes: would does not go in the if half. The if half takes the past simple: lived.","This part is fine: would plus the base verb is the dream result.","This part is fine: it only says how often."],"hint":"Find the if half. Which word belongs only in the result half?","why":"The if half of an imagined sentence takes the past simple, so it should be \"lived\". Would belongs only in the result half.","truth":"My aunt doesn't live in Bangkok — now."},{"id":"w3l2-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Praew has hurt her wrist, so she can't play badminton this week. If her wrist were fine, she ___ in Friday's match.","options":["can play","could play","could played","could to play"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Can is the real form. \"Were\" shows this is imagined, so can takes one step back.","Yes: can steps back to could, then the base verb. She would be able to play.","After a result word, the verb has no ending: no past form here.","No \"to\" after would, could or might: just the base verb."],"hint":"The Truth says \"can't\". Real or imagined, then? And what form follows a result word?","why":"\"Were\" shows the if half is imagined, so can steps back to could. After could, use the base verb with no \"to\" and no ending.","truth":"Praew's wrist isn't fine — this week."},{"id":"w3l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","given":"My advice is to ask the teacher about it.","keyword":"WERE","stem":"If ___ ask the teacher about it.","accept":["I were you, I would","I were you I would","I were you, I'd","I were you I'd","I were you, I should","I were you I should"],"model":"I were you, I would","hint":"Which fixed phrase gives advice? Then add the result word.","why":"The fixed advice phrase is \"If I were you, I would\" plus the base verb: were for every person, then would in the result.","truth":"I am not you."}],"world":"w3"},"w3l3":{"id":"w3l3","name":"First or second? Dreams, advice, unlikely futures and polite distance","cefr":"B2","ctype":"2","gateway":"p.72","formula":{"if":"Planning: If + present  |  Dreaming: If + past","result":"Planning: will + verb  |  Dreaming: would + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Dreams","ex":"If I had a superpower, I'd stop the rain before PE."},{"use":"Advice","ex":"If I were you, I'd message her tonight."},{"use":"A future the speaker sees as unlikely","ex":"If our school moved to Chiang Mai, I'd miss the BTS."},{"use":"What-if questions","ex":"What would you do if you found a wallet outside 7-Eleven?"},{"use":"Polite requests","ex":"Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?"},{"use":"Gentle suggestions","ex":"If we met at five, would that work for you?"}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"First or second is the speaker's view: the first conditional is planning, the second is dreaming or keeping a polite distance.","body":["The first and second conditional can describe the same event. The difference is the speaker's view. In the first conditional she is planning: she sees a real chance. <em>If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.</em> In the second she is dreaming: she steps back from it. <em>If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.</em> Both sentences are about the future. It is not about numbers, and nobody counts a percentage. The speaker chooses how she sees it.","So read the situation. Has she entered? Is she packing, practising or making plans? Then she is planning. Use the present after <em>if</em>, and <em>will</em> in the result. Is it untrue now, or only a daydream? Then step back. Use the past after <em>if</em>, and <em>would</em> in the result. Keep each pair together. Do not mix a past <em>if</em> half with <em>will</em>. Do not put <em>will</em> or <em>would</em> in the <em>if</em> half.","People use the second conditional for four jobs. The first is dreams: a superpower, a pet you don't have, life in another city. The second is advice: <em>If I were you, I'd ask again.</em> The third is a future the speaker thinks is unlikely, or a what-if question: <em>What would you do if you found a wallet?</em>","The fourth job is polite distance. A step back from reality is also a step back from the listener. It sounds softer and less pushy. <em>Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?</em> Here <em>came</em> is not past time, and it does not mean impossible. It only makes the question gentle. Use it with teachers, neighbours and anyone you want to be careful with. You can suggest an idea gently too: <em>If we met at five, would that work?</em>"],"examples":[{"s":"If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.","g":"Planning: she thinks she has a real chance. No Truth line."},{"s":"If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.","g":"Dreaming: same prize, same future. Truth: she doesn't expect to win."},{"s":"What would you do if you found a wallet outside 7-Eleven?","g":"A what-if question. Truth: you haven't found a wallet — it is only imagined."},{"s":"Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?","g":"A polite request. \"Came\" is not past time: she means tomorrow. Truth: she hasn't come late; she is asking gently."},{"s":"<s>If I had a superpower, I will stop the rain.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a superpower, I would stop the rain. Truth: I don't have a superpower — now. A past if half goes with \"would\"."}]},"B2":{"key":"Choosing first or second shows how the speaker sees the event: as a real plan, or as a dream, an unlikely future or a polite, careful idea.","body":["The first and second conditional can describe exactly the same event; what changes is the speaker's view of it. With the first she is planning, because she sees a real chance: <em>If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.</em> With the second she steps back and dreams: <em>If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.</em> Both sentences are about the future. Neither is a percentage. A speaker can feel hopeful on Monday and dreamy on Tuesday about the same contest.","To choose, read the situation for the speaker's attitude. If she has entered, is preparing or is making arrangements, she is treating it as real: present after <em>if</em>, <em>will</em> in the result. If the situation is untrue now, or she is only daydreaming, she steps back: past after <em>if</em>, <em>would</em> in the result. The halves travel in pairs, so a past <em>if</em> half with <em>will</em> is a mismatch, and neither <em>will</em> nor <em>would</em> belongs in a plain <em>if</em> half.","The second conditional does four jobs in real life. It carries dreams, such as a superpower, a pet you don't have or life in another city. It gives advice: <em>If I were you, I'd ask again.</em> It talks about futures the speaker regards as unlikely, and asks what-if questions: <em>What would you do if you found a wallet?</em>","Its fourth job is polite distance. Stepping back from reality also steps back from the listener, so a request sounds softer and less demanding: <em>Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?</em> The past form here is neither past time nor a sign that the thing is impossible; it simply makes the request gentle. The same move makes a tentative suggestion: <em>If we met at five, would that work?</em> It suits teachers, neighbours and anyone you want to be careful with."],"examples":[{"s":"If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.","g":"Planning: she treats winning as a real chance. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.","g":"Dreaming: the same prize and the same future, seen from one step back. Truth: she doesn't expect to win."},{"s":"What would you do if you found a wallet outside 7-Eleven?","g":"A what-if question. Truth: you haven't found a wallet — the situation is only imagined."},{"s":"Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?","g":"A polite request about tomorrow. \"Came\" adds distance, not past time. Truth: she hasn't come late; she is asking gently."},{"s":"<s>If I had a superpower, I will stop the rain.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a superpower, I would stop the rain. Truth: I don't have a superpower — now. A stepped-back if half needs a stepped-back result."}]},"C1":{"key":"First versus second is a choice of stance, not of probability: the speaker either keeps the possibility open or steps back from it to dream, doubt or be polite.","body":["The first and second conditional do not divide events into likely and unlikely ones. They show the stance the speaker takes. The open form treats the condition as a live possibility to plan around: <em>If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.</em> The remote form steps back from it: <em>If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.</em> Both refer to the future. The remote form does not say the event is impossible, and no percentage separates the two.","The context usually reveals the stance. Someone who has entered, is preparing or is making arrangements is treating the event as real, so the condition takes a present form and the result takes <em>will</em>. Someone describing a situation that is untrue now, or merely daydreaming, steps back: past in the condition, <em>would</em> in the result. The two halves must agree. A remote condition followed by <em>will</em> mixes two worlds, and in a plain condition neither <em>will</em> nor <em>would</em> follows <em>if</em>.","In use, the remote form does four kinds of work. It expresses dreams, such as a superpower, a pet you don't have or life in another city. It gives advice through an imagined change of places: <em>If I were you, I'd ask again.</em> It discusses futures the speaker regards as unlikely and poses hypothetical questions: <em>What would you do if you found a wallet?</em>","The fourth is social. Distance from reality doubles as distance from the listener, so the remote form softens a request: <em>Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?</em> <em>Came</em> is not past time and nothing is impossible; the speaker simply avoids assuming the answer is yes. The same form floats a tentative suggestion: <em>If we met at five, would that work?</em> In casual speech a present form is also heard after <em>Would it be OK if</em>, but the past form is the careful choice."],"examples":[{"s":"If I win the art prize, I'll buy new paints.","g":"Open stance: winning is treated as a live possibility. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"If I won the art prize, I'd buy new paints.","g":"Remote stance: the same prize and the same future, held at one step's distance. Truth: she doesn't expect to win."},{"s":"What would you do if you found a wallet outside 7-Eleven?","g":"A hypothetical question. Truth: you haven't found a wallet — the situation is only imagined."},{"s":"Would it be OK if I came a little later tomorrow?","g":"Social distance, not past time: the request is about tomorrow. Truth: she hasn't come late; nothing is denied, the form is only gentle."},{"s":"<s>If I had a superpower, I will stop the rain.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had a superpower, I would stop the rain. Truth: I don't have a superpower — now. A remote condition followed by \"will\" mixes two worlds."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w3l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"If I win, I'll buy everyone bubble tea."},{"who":"Pim","text":"If I won, I'd buy everyone bubble tea."}],"stem":"Mook and Pim are both in Friday's singing contest. What is the difference between their sentences?","options":["Mook is talking about Friday; Pim is talking about a past contest.","Mook is 100% sure of winning; Pim thinks it is exactly 50%.","Mook sees a real chance of winning; Pim is only dreaming about it.","Mook will buy the bubble tea; Pim has already bought it."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["\"Won\" is a past form, but not past time. Both girls are talking about Friday.","The choice is not a number. It shows how each girl sees the same contest.","Yes: present plus will is planning; past plus would is one step back, a dream.","\"I'd buy\" is an imagined result, not something that has happened."],"hint":"Same event, same day. So what changes: the time, or how each girl sees it?","why":"Both sentences are about Friday. Present plus will shows Mook planning for a real chance; past plus would shows Pim stepping back to dream. It is a view, not a percentage.","truth":"Pim doesn't expect to win; for her it is only a dream."},{"id":"w3l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"Would it be OK if I handed in my project on Monday?"}],"stem":"Fah is talking to her teacher. Why does she say \"handed\", a past form?","options":["Because she handed it in last Monday.","To sound polite and not too direct.","Because handing it in is impossible.","Because she has decided not to do it."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The past form is not past time here. She is asking about next Monday.","Yes: one step back adds polite distance. It makes the request softer.","A step back does not mean impossible. She really hopes the teacher says yes.","She wants to hand it in on Monday. She is only asking in a careful way."],"hint":"Is Monday in the past here? And who is she talking to?","why":"The past form here is not past time. One step back makes the request softer and more polite, which suits a student asking a teacher.","truth":"Fah hasn't handed it in; she means next Monday and is asking gently."},{"id":"w3l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Nan's mum has said yes to a beach day on Sunday if the weather is good, and Nan is already packing her bag. Which sentence fits how Nan sees it?","options":["If the weather were good, we'd go to the beach.","If the weather was good, we went to the beach.","If the weather is good, we'll go to the beach.","If the weather had been good, we'd have gone to the beach."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["That is the dreaming form. But Nan is packing: for her this is a real plan.","Past plus past is about real past days, not this Sunday.","Yes: a real plan for Sunday. Present after if, will in the result, and no Truth line.","That looks back at a Sunday that is already over. This one hasn't come yet."],"hint":"Is Nan planning or dreaming? Look at what she is doing right now.","why":"Mum has agreed and Nan is packing, so she sees a real possibility: present after if, will in the result. Nothing is untrue, so there is no step back."},{"id":"w3l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Bam has never entered a contest and doesn't plan to. Still, she likes to dream: \"If I ___ a trip to Japan one day, I'd take my grandma with me.\"","options":["win","won","will win","would win"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The present is for planning. Bam is only dreaming, and the result \"I'd take\" has stepped back.","Yes: a dream takes the past after if, to match \"I'd take\". It is not past time.","Will does not go in the if half, and this is not a real plan.","Would is the result word. It is already there: \"I'd take\"."],"hint":"Planning or dreaming? Check the result half too: which pair does it belong to?","why":"Bam is only dreaming, and the result has would, so the if half takes the past simple. Will and would do not go in the if half.","truth":"Bam isn't in any contest; winning a trip is only a dream."},{"id":"w3l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"My scholarship interview is tomorrow at nine. I've practised all week."}],"stem":"Her teacher: \"Good. If you ___ calm tomorrow, you'll do well.\"","options":["will stay","stayed","would stay","stay"],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["Will makes the promise in the result half. After if, use the present, even for tomorrow.","A step back would make it a dream. But the interview is real, and the result says \"you'll\".","Would goes with dreams, and it does not go in a plain if half.","Yes: a real possibility about tomorrow takes the present after if."],"hint":"Is the interview real or imagined? Which pair does \"you'll\" belong to?","why":"The interview is a real plan and the result has will, so the if half takes the present simple, even though it is about tomorrow."},{"id":"w3l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"I [[don't have]] a ticket for Saturday's concert, but if I [[had]] one, I [[will take]] you [[with me]].","answer":2,"fix":"would take","fb":["This part is fine: it is the Truth, in the present.","This part is fine: have steps back to had in the if half.","Yes: a stepped-back if half needs a stepped-back result: would take, not will take.","This part is fine: it only completes the result."],"hint":"The speaker tells you the Truth first. Do both halves of the dream step back?","why":"The speaker has no ticket, so the sentence is imagined. \"Had\" has stepped back, and the result must match it: would take.","truth":"The speaker doesn't have a ticket — now."},{"id":"w3l3-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"Are you going to the school fair on Saturday?"}],"stem":"Nan: \"Definitely, I've already got my ticket. If I see the candyfloss stall, I ___ you some.\"","options":["'d get","'ll get","got","would got"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That is the dreaming result. But Nan has her ticket, and the if half is in the present: \"If I see\".","Yes: a real plan. Present after if, will in the result. It is a promise.","The result of a real plan needs will plus the verb, not a past form.","Would is the dream word, and the verb after it never has a past ending."],"hint":"Planning or dreaming? Look at the verb after \"if\" to see which pair this is.","why":"Nan is definitely going, and \"If I see\" is in the present, so this is a real plan. The matching result is will plus the base verb."},{"id":"w3l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Praew wants to sound as polite and careful as she can with her coach. Complete her request with the most careful form of the verb in brackets: \"Would it be all right if I ___ (bring) my little sister to practice on Sunday?\"","accept":["brought"],"model":"brought","hint":"This is a careful request. How does English add polite distance to the verb?","why":"After \"Would it be all right if\", the past form is the careful choice: it adds polite distance. It is not past time, because Praew means next Sunday.","truth":"Praew hasn't brought her sister; the past form only makes the request gentle."}],"world":"w3"},"w4l1":{"id":"w4l1","name":"The imagined past: if + had + V3, would have + V3 (two steps back)","cefr":"B2","ctype":"3","gateway":"p.76","formula":{"if":"If + had + past participle (V3)","result":"would have + past participle (V3)"},"uses":[{"use":"Looking back at a small mistake","ex":"If I had brought a jumper, I would have enjoyed the film more."},{"use":"Explaining what went wrong","ex":"If we had read the recipe properly, the brownies would have risen."},{"use":"Imagining a different ending","ex":"If our team had scored once more, we would have won the cup."},{"use":"Answering 'Why didn't you…?'","ex":"If I had known you were waiting, I would have hurried."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"To imagine a different past, step back twice: if with had and V3, then would have and V3.","body":["Every half of an <em>if</em> sentence has two dials. One dial asks: when? The other asks: real or imagined (not true)? In this world, the time is the past and the thing is imagined. It did not happen. We look back and picture a different past. This pattern is called the third conditional.","English shows 'imagined' by stepping back on the verb. For an imagined present, step back once: <em>am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> becomes <em>had</em>, <em>will</em> becomes <em>would</em>, <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. For an imagined past, step back twice: <em>went</em> becomes <em>had gone</em>, and <em>would</em> becomes <em>would have</em>. The form is past, but the job is distance from what is real. Reported speech uses the same staircase for a different reason: distance in time.","So build two halves. In the <em>if</em> half, use <em>had</em> and the past participle (V3): <em>if I had gone</em>. In the result half (what happens next), use <em>would have</em> and V3: <em>I would have seen her</em>. The little word <em>have</em> is the past ticket. Without it, <em>would go</em> is about now or later. The order is always <em>would</em>, then <em>have</em>, then V3. So say <em>would have gone</em>, never <s>would have went</s> or <s>would have go</s>.","Check the meaning with a Truth receipt. Write what really happened and put a date on it. Here both halves get a past date. <em>If I had gone, I would have seen her.</em> Truth: I didn't go, and I didn't see her, yesterday. Past time words are your clue: <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em>, <em>this morning</em>, <em>last week</em>. Other patterns say other things. Present forms with <em>will</em>, or present in both halves, are real: no receipt. One step back gives a receipt dated now.","Watch for three traps. The first is only one step back after <em>if</em>: <s>if I went yesterday</s>. The second is <s>if I have gone</s>. <em>Have gone</em> is about the time up to now, and it is not imagined. You need <em>had</em>. The third is a result with no ticket: <s>I would see her yesterday</s>. Each time, ask the same thing. Is this half about a finished past? Then take two steps."],"examples":[{"s":"If we had booked on Monday, we would have got front-row seats.","g":"Truth: we didn't book on Monday, and we didn't get those seats. Both dated in the past."},{"s":"If Fah had read the label, she would have ordered the right size.","g":"Truth: Fah didn't read the label, and she ordered the wrong size, last week."},{"s":"If it rains, we will cancel. If it rained, we would cancel. If it had rained, we would have cancelled.","g":"Real, then one step back, then two steps back. Truth of the last one: it didn't rain, and we didn't cancel, on that day."},{"s":"<s>If I have known, I would tell you.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had known, I would have told you. Truth: I didn't know, and I didn't tell you, yesterday."}]},"B2":{"key":"The third conditional imagines a different past: both verbs take two steps back, to had plus V3 and would have plus V3.","body":["The third conditional sets both dials in a new place. When? The past. Real or imagined? Imagined. The speaker looks back at something that is over and pictures it going differently. Nothing can be changed now, which is why this pattern so often carries a feeling.","Remember how English marks 'imagined': one step back on each verb. <em>Am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> becomes <em>had</em>, <em>will</em> becomes <em>would</em>, <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. That gave you the second conditional. When the imagined thing is also in the past, each verb takes a second step: <em>went</em> becomes <em>had gone</em>, and <em>would go</em> becomes <em>would have gone</em>. These are past forms, but their job is distance from reality. Reported speech climbs the same staircase for a different reason: distance in time.","So the <em>if</em> half takes <em>had</em> plus the past participle (V3), and the result half takes <em>would have</em> plus V3. Think of <em>have</em> as the past ticket. <em>I would go</em> has no ticket, so it is about now or the future; <em>I would have gone</em> is about then. The order never changes: <em>would</em>, then <em>have</em>, then V3. So it is <em>would have gone</em>, not <s>would have went</s> and not <s>would have go</s>.","To check the meaning, write the Truth receipt, the hidden 'but actually' line, and date it. <em>If I had gone, I would have seen her.</em> Truth: I didn't go, and I didn't see her. Both halves are denied, and both are dated in the past, so past time phrases such as <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em> or <em>this morning</em> are your signal. A sentence with present forms, with or without <em>will</em>, is real and has no receipt. One step back gives a receipt dated now.","Three slips are common. The first is stopping after one step in the <em>if</em> half: <s>if I went yesterday</s> for <em>if I had gone yesterday</em>. The second is the present perfect: <s>if I have gone</s>. <em>Have gone</em> means up to now and is not imagined, so it cannot do this job. The third is forgetting the ticket in the result: <s>I would see her yesterday</s> for <em>I would have seen her</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"If we had booked on Monday, we would have got front-row seats.","g":"Truth: we didn't book on Monday, so we didn't get front-row seats. Both halves are dated in the past."},{"s":"If Fah had read the label, she would have ordered the right size.","g":"Truth: Fah didn't read the label, so she ordered the wrong size. Notice had read and would have ordered."},{"s":"If it rains, we will cancel. If it rained, we would cancel. If it had rained, we would have cancelled.","g":"The staircase on one event: real future, imagined now or future, imagined past. Truth of the last one: it didn't rain and we didn't cancel, back then."},{"s":"<s>If I have known, I would tell you.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had known, I would have told you. Have known is not imagined, and would tell has no past ticket. Truth: I didn't know, so I didn't tell you."}]},"C1":{"key":"The third conditional is the imagined row of the grid at past time, so distance from reality is marked twice: had plus V3 in the condition, would have plus V3 in the result.","body":["Set the two dials for each clause: the time is past, and the speaker's stance is imagined. The third conditional is simply that cell of the grid. The speaker treats a finished situation as if it had gone otherwise, usually because it did not, and that implied opposite fact is what gives the pattern its emotional weight.","The forms follow from one principle: a past form that is not about past time signals distance from reality. Real to imagined is one step back: <em>am</em> to <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> to <em>had</em>, <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>, <em>can</em> to <em>could</em>. Here the time really is past, and a verb cannot take a second past ending, so the second step borrows the perfect: <em>went</em> becomes <em>had gone</em>, and <em>would go</em> becomes <em>would have gone</em>. Reported speech uses the same staircase to show distance in time rather than in reality.","Hence <em>had</em> plus the past participle (V3) in the condition, and <em>would have</em> plus V3 in the result. The <em>have</em> is the past ticket: it alone separates <em>would go</em>, an imagined now or future, from <em>would have gone</em>, an imagined then. Because it forms a perfect, the order is fixed, modal then <em>have</em> then V3, which rules out <s>would have went</s> and <s>would have go</s> alike.","Test the meaning with a Truth receipt: state what actually happened and date it. In a third conditional both clauses are denied and both receipts carry a past date, so past time phrases sit naturally in these sentences. A real conditional, with present forms and often <em>will</em>, denies nothing and has no receipt; one step back gives a receipt dated now. Strictly, 'it did not happen' is a strong tendency rather than a law, but it holds in nearly every sentence you will meet.","The typical slips are all failures to take the second step. A past simple condition, <s>if I went yesterday</s>, has stepped back only once. The present perfect, <s>if I have gone</s>, is a present form, about the time up to now, and carries no distance at all. A result without the ticket, <s>I would see her yesterday</s>, is still an imagined present. A real past conditional such as <em>If it rained, we stayed in</em> is a different thing: nothing is imagined, so nothing shifts."],"examples":[{"s":"If we had booked on Monday, we would have got front-row seats.","g":"Truth: no booking on Monday and no front-row seats; both receipts carry a past date."},{"s":"If Fah had read the label, she would have ordered the right size.","g":"Truth: she did not read the label and ordered the wrong size; had read and would have ordered both show the second step."},{"s":"If it rains, we will cancel. If it rained, we would cancel. If it had rained, we would have cancelled.","g":"The staircase on a single event. Only the third denies a past fact. Truth: it did not rain and the event was not cancelled, back then."},{"s":"<s>If I have known, I would tell you.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had known, I would have told you. The present perfect carries no distance, and the result lacks the ticket. Truth: I did not know, so I did not tell you."}]}},"thai":"ถ้า(ตอนนั้น)… ก็คงจะ…ไปแล้ว — แต่ความจริงคือ…ไม่ได้…","items":[{"id":"w4l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"I sent you the homework photo last night. Did you get it?"},{"who":"Mint","text":"Sorry! If I had charged my phone at school, I would have seen your message."}],"stem":"What really happened?","options":["She charged it but missed the message.","She didn't charge it and missed the message.","She didn't charge it but still saw the message.","She charged it and saw the message."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Half right. The if half is imagined too: 'had charged' means she did not charge it.","Yes: both halves are imagined, so the Truth is the opposite of each, dated in the past.","The result half is imagined as well: 'would have seen' means she did not see it.","That is the imagined world, not the real one. Flip both halves to find the Truth."],"hint":"Write the Truth receipt for each half. Did it happen?","why":"Had charged and would have seen are both two steps back, so neither thing happened: no charge at school, no message last night.","truth":"Mint didn't charge her phone at school, so she didn't see the message last night."},{"id":"w4l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence tells you that Praew did <b>not</b> bring an umbrella yesterday?","options":["If Praew brings an umbrella, she will stay dry.","If Praew brought an umbrella, she would stay dry.","If Praew had brought an umbrella, she would have stayed dry.","If Praew brings an umbrella, she always stays dry."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["This is a real, possible future. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth receipt.","One step back only: an imagined now or future. It says nothing about yesterday.","Yes: two steps back. Truth: she didn't bring one, and she didn't stay dry, yesterday.","A real habit, with the present in both halves. It does not tell you about yesterday."],"hint":"Count the steps back on each verb. Which sentence has a receipt dated in the past?","why":"Only had brought with would have stayed is two steps back, so only that sentence denies something in the past.","truth":"Praew didn't bring an umbrella yesterday, so she didn't stay dry."},{"id":"w4l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"We sat in a jam for two hours and got home late. Which time phrase fits? If we had left at six, we would have beaten the traffic ___.","options":["tomorrow evening","yesterday evening","every evening","later this evening"],"answer":1,"near":3,"fb":["Tomorrow is the future, but 'would have beaten' carries the past ticket.","Yes: had left and would have beaten are both dated in a finished past.","Every evening is a habit. A habit is real and uses present forms.","Later this evening has not happened yet, but the jam is already over. Two steps back points to a finished past."],"hint":"Look at the verbs in both halves. Which date goes on the receipt?","why":"Had left and would have beaten are two steps back, so the sentence imagines a finished past; only a past time phrase fits.","truth":"We didn't leave at six yesterday evening, and we didn't beat the traffic."},{"id":"w4l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"Did you get one of the limited-edition bubble tea cups on Saturday?"},{"who":"Fah","text":"No. The shop sold out before noon."}],"stem":"If we had gone in the morning, we ___ one each.","options":["would get","would have get","would have got","will get"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No past ticket: 'would get' is about now or later, but Saturday is over.","The ticket is there, but after 'have' you need the past participle: got.","Yes: would, then have, then V3 for an imagined past result.","Will is for a real future. Saturday is finished, and they did not go in the morning."],"hint":"When is the result? Is there a past ticket, and what form follows it?","why":"Saturday is past and they didn't go in the morning, so the result needs would have plus the past participle got.","truth":"They didn't go in the morning on Saturday, so they didn't get a cup."},{"id":"w4l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"I got soaked on the way home yesterday."}],"stem":"If I ___ the weather app before I left school, I would have taken my raincoat.","options":["checked","have checked","check","had checked"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["One step back only. 'Checked' after if imagines now, but this was yesterday.","'Have checked' is about the time up to now and is not imagined. The imagined past needs had.","The present is for a real, possible condition. Yesterday is over, and she did not check.","Yes: had with V3 in the if half for an imagined past."],"hint":"When is the if half about? How many steps back do you need?","why":"It was yesterday and Pim did not check, so the if half needs two steps back: had plus the past participle.","truth":"Pim didn't check the weather app yesterday, so she didn't take her raincoat."},{"id":"w4l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"[[If Nan had revised]] for the vocabulary quiz [[last Thursday]], she [[would get]] full marks [[instead of]] seven out of ten.","answer":2,"fix":"would have got","fb":["This part is fine: had with V3 in the if half for an imagined past.","This part is fine: a past time phrase matches two steps back.","Yes, here: the result is in the past too, so it needs the past ticket: would have got.","This part is fine: 'instead of' before a noun phrase is normal English."],"hint":"Both halves are about last Thursday. Does each verb show that?","why":"The quiz was last Thursday, so the result is past too: would get needs the past ticket, would have got.","truth":"Nan didn't revise last Thursday, and she got seven out of ten."},{"id":"w4l1-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Praew didn't hear her alarm this morning, so she missed the school van. If she ___ (hear) it, she would have been ready on time.","accept":["had heard","'d heard"],"model":"had heard","hint":"The if half is about this morning, and it didn't happen. How many steps back?","why":"She did not hear it this morning, so the if half is an imagined past: had plus the past participle heard.","truth":"Praew didn't hear her alarm this morning, so she wasn't ready on time."},{"id":"w4l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mint forgot her PE kit yesterday, so she sat and watched. Use <b>would</b> and <b>play</b>: If she had packed it the night before, she ___ in the match.","accept":["would have played","would've played","'d have played"],"model":"would have played","hint":"The match was yesterday. Does your verb carry the past ticket?","why":"The match was yesterday and Mint did not play, so the result needs would have plus the past participle played.","truth":"Mint didn't pack her PE kit, so she didn't play in the match yesterday."}],"world":"w4"},"w4l2":{"id":"w4l2","name":"could have / might have, negatives, 'd = had or would, and no would in the if half","cefr":"B2","ctype":"3","gateway":"p.76","formula":{"if":"If + had / hadn't + V3","result":"would / could / might (not) have + V3"},"uses":[{"use":"Saying what was possible","ex":"If we'd had ten more minutes, we could have finished the poster."},{"use":"Saying maybe, not surely","ex":"If you had asked nicely, she might have said yes."},{"use":"Being glad something happened","ex":"If Dad hadn't driven us, we would never have made it to the match."},{"use":"Speaking quickly with 'd","ex":"If she'd seen us, she'd have waved."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"The result can use would have, could have or might have, either half can be negative, and would never goes in the if half.","body":["The result half can use three modals (helper verbs like <em>would</em>). <em>Would have</em> means the speaker feels sure about the imagined result. <em>Could have</em> means it was possible, or someone was able to do it. <em>Might have</em> means maybe. All three need the past ticket <em>have</em>, and then the past participle (V3): <em>could have gone</em>, <em>might have gone</em>.","Both halves can be negative. In the <em>if</em> half, use <em>hadn't</em> and V3: <em>if I hadn't gone</em>. In the result half, use <em>wouldn't have</em>, <em>couldn't have</em> or <em>might not have</em>, and then V3. <em>Couldn't have</em> means it was not possible.","Be careful with the Truth receipt. Each half is imagined, so the Truth is the opposite. A negative half means it really happened. A positive half means it didn't. <em>If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time.</em> Truth: Mum called me, and I woke up in time. Sentences like this often tell a story of good luck.","In this pattern, <em>would</em> lives only in the result half. The <em>if</em> half takes <em>had</em>. So <s>if I would have gone</s> is wrong. Write <em>if I had gone</em>. You may hear some American speakers say it, but exams mark it wrong.","In fast speech, <em>had</em> and <em>would</em> both shrink to <em>'d</em>. Look at the next word. If a V3 comes next, <em>'d</em> is <em>had</em>: <em>I'd gone</em>. If a base verb (the plain form) comes next, <em>'d</em> is <em>would</em>: <em>I'd go</em>. If <em>have</em> and a V3 come next, it is <em>would have</em>: <em>I'd have gone</em>. So <em>'d have</em> cannot follow <em>if</em> either."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had brought my card, I could have bought the album.","g":"Could have: it was possible. Truth: I didn't bring my card, so I wasn't able to buy it, that day."},{"s":"If we had asked the teacher, she might have given us more time.","g":"Might have: maybe. Truth: we didn't ask, and we didn't get more time."},{"s":"If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time.","g":"Two negatives. Truth: Mum called me, and I woke up in time, this morning."},{"s":"If I'd seen your message, I'd have replied.","g":"The first 'd is had, before the V3 seen. The second is would, before have. Truth: I didn't see it, and I didn't reply."},{"s":"<s>If I would have seen it, I would have replied.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had seen it, I would have replied. Would stays in the result half. Truth: I didn't see it."}]},"B2":{"key":"Choose would, could or might have for the result you mean, make either half negative with hadn't or wouldn't have, and keep would out of the if half.","body":["The imagined past result is not always <em>would have</em>. The modal carries the meaning. <em>Would have</em> is the result the speaker is sure of. <em>Could have</em> says the result was possible, or that someone was able to do it. <em>Might have</em> says perhaps. Whichever you choose, the past ticket stays: the modal, then <em>have</em>, then the past participle (V3). So <em>she could have gone</em>, not <s>she could went</s>.","Either half can be negative. The condition becomes <em>hadn't</em> plus V3. The result becomes <em>wouldn't have</em>, <em>couldn't have</em> or <em>might not have</em> plus V3. <em>Couldn't have</em> means the result was impossible without the condition.","Negatives make the Truth receipt worth writing out, because each imagined half flips. A negative clause tells you the thing really happened; a positive clause tells you it didn't. Take <em>If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time</em>. Mum did call, and I did wake up in time. Notice that this kind of sentence usually reports good luck rather than a mistake.","One rule is firm in exam English for the imagined past: <em>would</em> belongs to the result half, and the <em>if</em> half takes <em>had</em>. <s>If I would have gone</s> must be <em>if I had gone</em>. You will hear <em>would have</em> after <em>if</em> from some American speakers, so recognise it, but examiners mark it wrong.","Finally, the contraction <em>'d</em> hides two different words, and the next word tells you which. Before a past participle it is <em>had</em>: <em>if I'd gone</em>. Before a base verb it is <em>would</em>: <em>I'd go</em>. Before <em>have</em> and a past participle it is <em>would have</em>: <em>I'd have gone</em>. That is why <em>if I'd have gone</em> is the same mistake as the one above, only harder to spot."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had brought my card, I could have bought the album.","g":"Could have shows ability or possibility. Truth: I didn't bring my card, so I wasn't able to buy the album that day."},{"s":"If we had asked the teacher, she might have given us more time.","g":"Might have shows a possible result, not a sure one. Truth: we didn't ask, so we got no extra time."},{"s":"If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time.","g":"Both halves are negative, so both flip. Truth: Mum called me, and I woke up in time."},{"s":"If I'd seen your message, I'd have replied.","g":"First 'd: had, because seen is a past participle. Second 'd: would, because have follows. Truth: I didn't see it, so I didn't reply."},{"s":"<s>If I would have seen it, I would have replied.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had seen it, I would have replied. Heard in some American speech, wrong in exams. Truth: I didn't see it."}]},"C1":{"key":"The result modal is a meaning choice between certainty, ability and possibility; negation flips the Truth of its clause; and would, even hidden inside a contraction, stays out of the condition.","body":["<em>Would have</em> is only the default result. The modal is chosen for its meaning. <em>Would have</em> presents the imagined outcome as certain in the speaker's mind; <em>could have</em> presents it as possible, or as something a person was able to do; <em>might have</em> presents it as no more than a chance. In every case the past ticket remains, modal then <em>have</em> then the past participle (V3), so <s>she could went</s> fails for the same reason as a result without <em>have</em>.","Negation is free in both clauses: <em>hadn't</em> plus V3 in the condition, and <em>wouldn't have</em>, <em>couldn't have</em> or <em>might not have</em> plus V3 in the result. <em>Couldn't have</em> makes the strong claim that the outcome was impossible without the condition.","Because each clause is imagined, each one implies its opposite, and negation therefore reverses the Truth receipt: a negative clause reports something that did happen, a positive clause something that did not. In <em>If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time</em>, she called and I woke. A negative condition of this kind is the usual shape for good fortune, since the fortunate event is real and only its absence is imagined.","<em>Would</em> is the result's modal, and <em>had</em> already carries all the distance the condition needs, so standard English does not repeat <em>would</em> after <em>if</em>: <s>if I would have gone</s> should be <em>if I had gone</em>. The doubled form is genuinely common in some American speech, which is worth recognising, but most reference grammars reject or ignore it and examiners penalise it.","The contraction <em>'d</em> stands for either word, and only what follows decides. Before a past participle it is <em>had</em>, as in <em>if I'd gone</em>; before a base verb it is <em>would</em>, as in <em>I'd go</em>; before <em>have</em> and a past participle it is <em>would have</em>, as in <em>I'd have gone</em>. It follows that <em>if I'd have gone</em> is the doubled form in disguise, and is wrong for the same reason."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had brought my card, I could have bought the album.","g":"Could have: the purchase was possible in the imagined past. Truth: no card, so no album, that day."},{"s":"If we had asked the teacher, she might have given us more time.","g":"Might have: the speaker commits only to a chance. Truth: nobody asked, and no extra time was given."},{"s":"If Mum hadn't called me, I wouldn't have woken up in time.","g":"Both clauses negative, so both receipts are positive. Truth: she called, and I woke in time."},{"s":"If I'd seen your message, I'd have replied.","g":"The first 'd precedes a past participle, so it is had; the second precedes have, so it is would. Truth: the message went unseen and unanswered."},{"s":"<s>If I would have seen it, I would have replied.</s>","g":"Fix: If I had seen it, I would have replied. The doubled would is a spoken American variant, not exam English. Truth: I did not see it."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w4l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"We needed one more player for doubles after school yesterday."},{"who":"Fern","text":"If I had brought my racket, I could have played with you."}],"stem":"What does Fern mean?","options":["She didn't have her racket, so she wasn't able to play.","She had her racket, so she was able to play.","She had her racket, but she wasn't allowed to play.","She doesn't have a racket, so she isn't able to play now."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: could have played means it was possible in the imagined past. Truth: no racket, no game.","That is the imagined world. Had brought and could have played are both two steps back, so flip them.","Could have here is about being able, not about permission, and the if half tells you she didn't have her racket.","Check the date. Could have with V3 carries the past ticket: this was yesterday."],"hint":"Flip both halves for the Truth. What does could add: sure, able or maybe?","why":"Had brought and could have played are two steps back: Fern didn't bring her racket yesterday, so she wasn't able to play.","truth":"Fern didn't bring her racket yesterday, so she wasn't able to play."},{"id":"w4l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"The girls missed the first bus to the concert last night. In which sentence is the speaker <b>not sure</b> about the imagined result?","options":["If we had caught the first bus, we would have got seats.","If we had caught the first bus, we might have got seats.","If we had caught the first bus, we would certainly have got seats.","If we had caught the first bus, we wouldn't have stood all night."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Would have is the result the speaker feels sure about.","Yes: might have means maybe. Seats were possible, not certain.","Would certainly have is even more sure, not less.","Wouldn't have is still sure: the speaker has no doubt about the result."],"hint":"Which modal means maybe, and which means a sure result?","why":"Might have with V3 shows a possible result in the imagined past; would have and wouldn't have show a result the speaker is sure about.","truth":"They didn't catch the first bus last night; seats were only a maybe."},{"id":"w4l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"It was Mum's birthday on Sunday. If Jane hadn't reminded me, I would have forgotten it."}],"stem":"What really happened?","options":["Jane reminded Pim, and Pim remembered the birthday.","Jane reminded Pim, but Pim forgot the birthday.","Jane didn't remind Pim, and Pim forgot the birthday.","Jane didn't remind Pim, but Pim remembered the birthday."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: a negative if half means it did happen, and a positive result means it didn't. Jane reminded her; Pim didn't forget.","The result half is imagined too: 'would have forgotten' means she did not forget.","That is the imagined world. Flip both halves: 'hadn't reminded' means Jane did remind her.","'Hadn't reminded' is imagined, so in real life Jane did remind her."],"hint":"Flip each half: what does a negative become, and what does a positive become?","why":"Hadn't reminded is imagined, so Jane did remind her; would have forgotten is imagined, so Pim did not forget.","truth":"Jane reminded Pim on Sunday, so Pim didn't forget the birthday."},{"id":"w4l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Ploy","text":"The canteen was packed at lunch yesterday. I stood for twenty minutes."}],"stem":"Fern replies: \"Sorry! If you ___ me you were coming, I wouldn't have given your seat away.\"","options":["would have told","had told","have told","told"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Would have belongs in the result half. Some American speakers say this, but exams mark it wrong.","Yes: had with V3 in the if half; the would have ticket stays in the result.","'Have told' is about the time up to now and is not imagined. The imagined past needs had.","One step back only. That imagines now, but lunch was yesterday."],"hint":"Which half are you filling? Where does would live?","why":"Lunch was yesterday and Ploy did not tell her, so the if half needs had plus V3; would have is only for the result.","truth":"Ploy didn't tell Fern she was coming, so Fern gave the seat away yesterday."},{"id":"w4l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Nan didn't know about yesterday's quiz. She says: \"If I'd known about it, I'd have revised.\" What do the two 'd stand for, in order?","options":["would, then had","had, then would","had, then had","would, then would"],"answer":1,"near":3,"fb":["Check what follows each 'd. Before the V3 'known' it is had; before 'have' it is would.","Yes: 'd before V3 is had; 'd before have and V3 is would.","The second 'd is followed by have. 'Had have' is not English here: it is would have.","Would cannot sit in the if half. Before a past participle, 'd is had."],"hint":"Look at the word straight after each 'd.","why":"'d before the past participle known is had; 'd before have revised is would. So: If I had known, I would have revised.","truth":"Nan didn't know about the quiz, so she didn't revise."},{"id":"w4l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"If Fern [[had asked]] me yesterday, I [[could lent]] her my charger [[for the whole]] afternoon [[without any problem]].","answer":1,"fix":"could have lent","fb":["This part is fine: had with V3 in the if half.","Yes, here: could needs the past ticket too: could have lent.","This part is fine: 'for the whole afternoon' is normal English.","This part is fine: 'without any problem' is normal English."],"hint":"Every modal in an imagined past result needs the same small word. Is it there?","why":"The result is about yesterday, so could needs the past ticket: could have plus the past participle lent.","truth":"Fern didn't ask yesterday, so the speaker didn't lend her the charger."},{"id":"w4l2-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Luckily, Nan woke me up on the BTS yesterday. If she ___ (not / wake) me up, I would have missed my stop.","accept":["hadn't woken","had not woken"],"model":"hadn't woken","hint":"She did wake you. To imagine the opposite past, which helper verb takes not?","why":"Nan did wake her yesterday, so the imagined opposite is negative and two steps back: hadn't plus the past participle woken.","truth":"Nan woke her up yesterday, so she didn't miss her stop."},{"id":"w4l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Luckily, it stayed dry on sports day last Friday. Use <b>would</b>, <b>not</b> and <b>finish</b>: If it had rained, we ___ the relay.","accept":["wouldn't have finished","would not have finished"],"model":"wouldn't have finished","hint":"They did finish. Is the imagined result positive or negative, and where does the past ticket go?","why":"In real life they finished, so the imagined past result is negative: wouldn't have plus the past participle finished.","truth":"It didn't rain last Friday, so they finished the relay."}],"world":"w4"},"w4l3":{"id":"w4l3","name":"Regret, relief and criticism; second or third?","cefr":"B2","ctype":"3","gateway":"p.76","formula":{"if":"Then: If + had + V3 · Now: If + past simple","result":"Then: would have + V3 · Now: would + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Regret (my own small mistake)","ex":"If I'd read the question properly, I wouldn't have lost those marks."},{"use":"Relief (luckily, it didn't happen)","ex":"Luckily I had my mask. If I hadn't worn it, I would have coughed all day."},{"use":"Criticism or blame","ex":"If you'd told me the time, I wouldn't have kept you waiting."},{"use":"Reasoning about the past","ex":"If the shop had been open, the lights would have been on."},{"use":"An excuse","ex":"If the bus had come on time, I wouldn't have been late, Miss."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"The third conditional shows regret, relief, blame or reasoning about the past; for an imagined now, step back only once.","body":["People use the imagined past for four main jobs. The Truth receipt tells you which job it is. Ask two questions. Was the real result good or bad? Whose action was it?","Regret means you feel sorry about your own action. The real result was bad, and it was your mistake. <em>If I had set my alarm, I wouldn't have missed the bus.</em> Relief is the opposite feeling. Something bad nearly happened, but it didn't, so you feel glad. These sentences often go with <em>luckily</em>.","Criticism, or blame, points at another person. The <em>if</em> half is about <em>you</em> or <em>she</em>, and the real result was bad. Keep it gentle. Reasoning has no strong feeling. You work out what happened. <em>If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us.</em> She isn't here yet, so she probably took a later one.","Now compare the second and third conditionals. Both are imagined. The difference is the date on the Truth receipt. If the Truth is about now, step back once: a past form, then <em>would</em> and the verb. If the Truth is about a finished past, step back twice: <em>had</em> and the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em> and V3.","So look for time clues before you build the verb. <em>Now</em>, <em>right now</em>, <em>today</em> and <em>at the moment</em> point to one step. <em>Yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em> and <em>last Saturday</em> point to two steps. Do not step back too far. <s>If I had had time now</s> is wrong, because the Truth is dated now. And if something is still possible, it is real: use the present and <em>will</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had set my alarm, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"Regret. Truth: I didn't set my alarm, and I missed the bus, this morning."},{"s":"Luckily, Pim had a spare charger. If she hadn't lent it to me, my phone would have died.","g":"Relief. Truth: Pim lent me her charger, and my phone didn't die, yesterday."},{"s":"If you had read the group chat, you would have known about the room change.","g":"Criticism. Truth: you didn't read the chat, and you didn't know, this morning."},{"s":"If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us.","g":"Reasoning. Truth: Nan didn't arrive before us, so she didn't take that train, this morning."},{"s":"If I knew the way, I would show you. If I had known the way yesterday, we wouldn't have got lost.","g":"Second, then third. Truth: I don't know the way now. I didn't know it yesterday, and we got lost."}]},"B2":{"key":"Use the third conditional for regret, relief, criticism and reasoning about the past, and let the date on the Truth decide between second and third.","body":["The third conditional is often taught as the grammar of regret, but regret is only one of its jobs. It is any imagined past. To see which job a sentence is doing, write its Truth receipt and ask two things: was the real result good or bad, and whose action was it?","If the real result was bad and the action was the speaker's own, the sentence is a regret: <em>If I had set my alarm, I wouldn't have missed the bus.</em> If something bad nearly happened but didn't, the sentence shows relief, and it often comes with <em>luckily</em> or <em>thank goodness</em>. The bad result stays safely in the imagined world.","If the action belongs to someone else and the real result was bad, the sentence is criticism or blame: <em>If you had read the group chat, you would have known.</em> Use it gently. Sometimes there is no feeling at all, only reasoning, when the speaker works out what happened. <em>If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us</em>, so she probably took a later one.","The second and third conditionals are both imagined, so the choice between them is only a question of time. Date the Truth. If it is true now, one step back is enough: a past form, then <em>would</em> plus the verb. If it was true at a finished past time, take two steps: <em>had</em> plus the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em> plus V3.","Time cues do the work. <em>Now</em>, <em>right now</em>, <em>today</em> and <em>at the moment</em> call for one step; <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em> and <em>last Saturday</em> call for two. Both mistakes are possible: too few steps for a past event, and too many for a present one, as in <s>if I had had time now</s>. And if the thing is still possible, it is not imagined at all: use the present and <em>will</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had set my alarm, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"Regret: my own action, a bad result. Truth: I didn't set my alarm, so I missed the bus this morning."},{"s":"Luckily, Pim had a spare charger. If she hadn't lent it to me, my phone would have died.","g":"Relief: the bad result stayed imagined. Truth: Pim lent me the charger, so my phone didn't die yesterday."},{"s":"If you had read the group chat, you would have known about the room change.","g":"Criticism: someone else's action, a bad result. Truth: you didn't read the chat, so you didn't know about the change."},{"s":"If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us.","g":"Reasoning about the past. Truth: she didn't arrive before us, so we work out that she didn't take the 7.15."},{"s":"If I knew the way, I would show you. If I had known the way yesterday, we wouldn't have got lost.","g":"One step for now, two steps for then. Truth: I don't know the way now; I didn't know it yesterday, so we got lost."}]},"C1":{"key":"The third conditional is any imagined past, so its force comes from the Truth behind it, and the date of that Truth is all that separates it from the second.","body":["Calling the third conditional 'the regret conditional' confuses a form with one of its uses. The form says only that a past situation is being imagined otherwise. What the speaker is doing with it depends on the implied facts: whether the actual outcome was good or bad, and who was responsible for it.","When the actual outcome was bad and the speaker was responsible, we hear regret. When a bad outcome was avoided, we hear relief, often flagged by <em>luckily</em> or <em>thank goodness</em>; here the condition is typically negative, because the fortunate thing did in fact happen and only its absence is imagined.","When someone else was responsible for a bad outcome, the same grammar becomes criticism or blame, which is why <em>If you had read the group chat…</em> can sound sharp and is best softened. Finally, the pattern serves plain reasoning about the past: <em>If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us</em> invites the conclusion that she took a later one. No feeling is involved, only inference from what we can see.","Second and third conditionals share the imagined setting and differ only on the time dial. The diagnostic is the date on the Truth receipt. A Truth that holds now takes one step back: a past form, with <em>would</em> plus the verb. A Truth that held at a finished past time takes two: <em>had</em> plus the past participle (V3), with <em>would have</em> plus V3.","Read the time cues before building either verb. <em>Now</em>, <em>today</em> and <em>at the moment</em> signal one step; <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em> and <em>last Saturday</em> signal two. Learners err in both directions: under-stepping a past event, and over-stepping a present one, as in <s>if I had had time now</s>. Where the outcome is still open, nothing is denied, so the real pattern with a present form and <em>will</em> is the right one."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had set my alarm, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"Regret: the speaker's own omission and a bad outcome. Truth: no alarm, and the bus was missed this morning."},{"s":"Luckily, Pim had a spare charger. If she hadn't lent it to me, my phone would have died.","g":"Relief, with the typical negative condition. Truth: the charger was lent and the phone survived, yesterday."},{"s":"If you had read the group chat, you would have known about the room change.","g":"Criticism: responsibility is placed on the listener. Truth: the chat went unread, so the change was missed."},{"s":"If Nan had taken the 7.15 train, she would have arrived before us.","g":"Inference rather than feeling. Truth: she did not arrive first, from which we conclude she did not take the 7.15."},{"s":"If I knew the way, I would show you. If I had known the way yesterday, we wouldn't have got lost.","g":"The same condition on two dates. Truth: I do not know the way now; I did not know it yesterday, and we got lost."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w4l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"My laptop died in the middle of my project last night. If I hadn't saved it five minutes before, I would have lost everything."}],"stem":"What is Fern doing with her second sentence?","options":["Showing regret about a mistake she made.","Showing relief that something bad didn't happen.","Criticising someone else for a mistake.","Making a promise about what she will do next time."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Regret needs something she is sorry about. Truth: she did save it, and that was the right thing to do.","Yes. Truth: she saved it, so she lost nothing. The bad result stayed imagined.","Nobody else is in the sentence, and nobody made a mistake.","Two steps back looks at a finished past, not at next time."],"hint":"Write the Truth. Was the real result good or bad, and whose action was it?","why":"The Truth is that Fern saved her project and lost nothing, so the imagined bad result shows relief.","truth":"Fern saved her project last night, so she didn't lose anything."},{"id":"w4l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"In which sentence is the speaker sorry about <b>her own</b> mistake?","options":["If you had told me earlier, I wouldn't have bought two tickets.","If Jane hadn't lent me her umbrella, I would have got soaked.","If I had revised for yesterday's quiz, I would have got more than five.","If Fah had been at home, she would have answered the door."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This points at 'you': the other person didn't tell her. That is blame, not regret.","Truth: Jane lent the umbrella and the speaker stayed dry. That is relief.","Yes. Truth: I didn't revise, and I got five. A small regret about my own action.","This is reasoning about the past: she didn't answer, so she was probably out."],"hint":"Find the Truth for each if half. Who did, or didn't do, the action?","why":"Only that sentence has the speaker's own action in the if half and a bad real result: she didn't revise, so she got five.","truth":"The speaker didn't revise for yesterday's quiz, so she got five."},{"id":"w4l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Pim is queuing at the bubble tea shop <b>right now</b>. She hasn't ordered yet, and she has only 40 baht. The large size costs 60. Which sentence fits?","options":["If I had 60 baht, I would get the large one.","If I had had 60 baht, I would have got the large one.","If I have 60 baht, I will get the large one.","If I had 60 baht, I would have got the large one."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the Truth is dated now (she has 40 baht now), so one step back is enough.","Two steps back puts everything in a finished past, but Pim is in the queue now.","Present with will is for a real, open possibility. Pim knows she has only 40 baht.","The result has a past ticket, but Pim has not ordered yet. Her choice is now, so the result needs would and the verb."],"hint":"Date the Truth receipt: now or then? Then count the steps.","why":"Pim's problem is true now, so the imagined sentence steps back once: a past form with would, and no past ticket.","truth":"Pim doesn't have 60 baht now, so she isn't getting the large one."},{"id":"w4l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Jane and Mook are at the bus stop now, in the rain. Jane says: \"Sorry, I don't have an umbrella. If I ___ one, I would share it with you.\"","options":["had","had had","have","would have"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the Truth is dated now, so one step back: a past form in the if half.","Two steps back is for a finished past. Jane is at the bus stop now.","The present is for a real possibility, but Jane has just said she doesn't have one.","Would lives in the result half, not in the if half."],"hint":"Is the Truth about now or about a finished past? One step or two?","why":"Jane has no umbrella now and the result is would share, so the if half steps back once: had.","truth":"Jane doesn't have an umbrella now, so she isn't sharing one."},{"id":"w4l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Jane got soaked on the way to school. She says: \"If I ___ my umbrella yesterday, I wouldn't have got so wet.\"","options":["brought","had brought","have brought","would bring"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["One step back imagines now. The Truth here is dated yesterday, and the result has a past ticket.","Yes: the Truth is dated yesterday, so two steps back: had with V3.","'Have brought' is about the time up to now and is not imagined.","Would lives in the result half. This half needs had with V3."],"hint":"Date the Truth receipt, then look at the result half. One step or two?","why":"Jane didn't bring it yesterday and the result is wouldn't have got, so the if half needs had plus the past participle.","truth":"Jane didn't bring her umbrella yesterday, so she got wet."},{"id":"w4l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Sorry, I [[can't lend]] you my charger. If I [[had had]] it with me right now, I [[would give]] it to you [[at once]].","answer":1,"fix":"had","fb":["This part is fine: it tells you the real situation now.","Yes, here: 'right now' means one step back, not two: If I had it with me.","This part is fine: would with a verb is the result for an imagined now.","This part is fine: 'at once' means immediately."],"hint":"The Truth is dated right now. Has one verb stepped back too far?","why":"The charger is missing now, not in a finished past, so the if half needs one step back, had, not had had.","truth":"The speaker doesn't have her charger with her now."},{"id":"w4l3-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Ploy missed Bam's party last Saturday because she was ill. Use <b>would</b> and <b>come</b>: \"I'm so sorry, Bam. If I hadn't been ill, I ___.\"","accept":["would have come","would've come","'d have come"],"model":"would have come","hint":"Last Saturday is over. Which form of the result carries the past ticket?","why":"The party was last Saturday and Ploy was ill, so the result is an imagined past: would have plus the past participle come.","truth":"Ploy was ill last Saturday, so she didn't come to the party."},{"id":"w4l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","given":"Nan didn't see the step, so she dropped her bubble tea.","keyword":"DROPPED","stem":"If Nan had seen the step, she definitely ___ her bubble tea.","accept":["wouldn't have dropped","would not have dropped"],"model":"wouldn't have dropped","hint":"She did drop it. In the imagined past, is the sure result positive or negative?","why":"In real life she dropped it, so the sure imagined result is the opposite and in the past: wouldn't have plus dropped.","truth":"Nan didn't see the step, so she dropped her bubble tea."}],"world":"w4"},"w5l1":{"id":"w5l1","name":"Mixed 3/2: imagined past condition, present result","cefr":"B2","ctype":"m32","formula":{"if":"If + had + V3 (imagined past)","result":"would / could / might + base verb (now)"},"uses":[{"use":"A past choice you still feel today","ex":"If I had gone to bed earlier, I wouldn't be so sleepy now."},{"use":"Relief: past luck behind a good present","ex":"If Fah hadn't lent me her charger, my phone would be dead by now."},{"use":"Gentle criticism: pointing to the cause","ex":"If you had saved the file, we wouldn't be typing it all again now."},{"use":"Explaining how life is today","ex":"If we hadn't moved to Bangkok, I'd still be living by the sea."},{"use":"An ability you would have now","ex":"If I had started piano at six, I could play this song today."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Mixed 3/2 joins an imagined past, with <b>had</b> plus V3, to an imagined result now, with <b>would</b> plus the base verb.","body":["Some imagined sentences have two times. The <b>if</b> half is about the past. The result (what happens) is about now. We call this mixed 3/2. The if half comes from type 3. The result half comes from type 2. You use it when something in the past still matters today.","Build each half on its own. First, the if half. It is an imagined past, so use <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3). Next, the result. It is imagined and it is now. So use <b>would</b> plus the base verb (the verb with no ending). You can also use <b>could</b> for ability or <b>might</b> for maybe. Do not put <b>have</b> in the result. <b>Have</b> is the past ticket, and this result is not past.","Remember the staircase. Real to imagined is one step back on each verb. <em>Am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>will</em> becomes <em>would</em>, <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. An imagined past is two steps back. <em>Went</em> becomes <em>had gone</em>, and <em>would</em> becomes <em>would have</em>. The past form does not mean past time here. It means not real. Reported speech uses the same staircase, but for a different reason.","Now check with Truth lines. A mixed sentence has two Truth lines with two dates. Take <em>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now</em>. Truth one: I didn't sleep well, last night. Truth two: I am tired, now. One past date and one now date means mixed 3/2. Two past dates means type 3. A real conditional has no Truth line at all.","Time words help you. The if half often has a past word like <em>last night</em> or <em>this morning</em>. The result has <em>now</em>, <em>still</em>, <em>today</em> or <em>by now</em>. With those words, <em>would have been</em> is wrong, because it is about the past. Keep <b>would</b> out of the if half too. And do not use <b>will</b>, <b>can</b> or a present verb in an imagined result."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now.","g":"Truth: I didn't sleep well — last night. I am tired — now. Two dates, so mixed 3/2."},{"s":"If Mint hadn't missed the 7.15 train, she would be in class by now.","g":"Truth: Mint missed the train — this morning. She isn't in class — now. \"By now\" shows the result is present."},{"s":"If we hadn't adopted Mochi three years ago, she might still be living on the street.","g":"Truth: we adopted Mochi — three years ago. She lives with us — now. \"Might\" means maybe."},{"s":"If I hadn't left my glasses at home this morning, I could read the board now.","g":"Truth: I left my glasses at home — this morning. I can't read the board — now. \"Could\" is imagined ability."},{"s":"<s>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't have been tired now.</s>","g":"Wrong: \"now\" is not past, so no past ticket. Say: I wouldn't be tired now."}]},"B2":{"key":"In mixed 3/2 the <b>if</b> half is an imagined past (<b>had</b> plus V3) and the result is an imagined present (<b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb).","body":["A mixed conditional is an imagined sentence whose two halves live at different times. In mixed 3/2, the <b>if</b> half is about the past and the result half is about now. Something did or did not happen earlier, and you can still feel the result today. The name is the recipe: the if half comes from the third conditional, and the result half comes from the second.","Build each half separately, from its own time. Remember the staircase: real to imagined is one step back on each verb, so <em>am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>will</em> becomes <em>would</em> and <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. An imagined past is two steps: <em>went</em> becomes <em>had gone</em>, and <em>would</em> becomes <em>would have</em>. So the if half takes <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3). The result is imagined but present, so it takes only one step: <b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb.","There is no <b>have</b> in the result, because <b>have</b> is the past ticket and this result is not in the past. The past forms here do not show past time; they show distance from reality. Reported speech uses the same staircase for a different reason. <b>Would</b> gives the imagined result, <b>could</b> an imagined ability and <b>might</b> an imagined possibility.","Check the meaning with the Truth receipt. A mixed sentence has two Truth lines with two different dates. For <em>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now</em>, the lines are: I didn't sleep well, last night; I am tired, now. One line dated in the past and one dated now means mixed 3/2. If both lines are dated in the past, it is a plain third conditional. A real conditional has no Truth line, because nothing is denied.","Look for the time words. The if half often carries a past cue such as <em>last night</em>, <em>this morning</em> or <em>in M1</em>. The result carries a present cue: <em>now</em>, <em>still</em>, <em>today</em> or <em>by now</em>. With one of those in the result, <em>would have been</em> is a time mismatch. Keep <b>would</b> out of the if half, as always, and keep <b>will</b>, <b>can</b> and present forms out of an imagined result."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now.","g":"Truth: I didn't sleep well — last night. I am tired — now. Two receipts, two dates: mixed 3/2."},{"s":"If Mint hadn't missed the 7.15 train, she would be in class by now.","g":"Truth: Mint missed the train — this morning. She isn't in class — now. \"By now\" dates the result in the present."},{"s":"If we hadn't adopted Mochi three years ago, she might still be living on the street.","g":"Truth: we adopted Mochi — three years ago. She lives with us — now. \"Might\" gives an imagined possibility; \"still\" is the present cue."},{"s":"If I hadn't left my glasses at home this morning, I could read the board now.","g":"Truth: I left my glasses at home — this morning. I can't read the board — now. \"Could\" gives imagined ability."},{"s":"<s>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't have been tired now.</s>","g":"Time mismatch: \"would have been\" carries the past ticket, but the result is dated now. Fix: I wouldn't be tired now."}]},"C1":{"key":"Mixed 3/2 dates its halves differently: an imagined past condition, two steps back, and an imagined present result, one step back.","body":["A mixed conditional is not a sixth rule; it is what happens when the two halves of an imagined sentence carry different dates. In mixed 3/2 the condition is an imagined past and the result is an imagined present: a past event, or non-event, whose consequence is still with you. The label is a recipe. The <b>if</b> half is borrowed from the third conditional and the result half from the second.","Each half is built from its own time on the staircase. Real to imagined is one step back on each verb: <em>am</em> to <em>were</em>, <em>have</em> to <em>had</em>, <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>, <em>can</em> to <em>could</em>. An imagined past needs a second step, and because English has no past of the past, that step is the perfect: <em>went</em> to <em>had gone</em>, <em>would</em> to <em>would have</em>. So the condition takes <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3), and the present result takes <b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb.","The forms mean what they mean because of distance. A past form that is not about past time marks a step away from reality, just as backshift in reported speech marks a step away in time: the same staircase, a different reason. That is why the result has no <b>have</b>. <b>Have</b> is the past ticket; it dates a result in the past. A result you can feel now needs distance from reality only, which is one step.","The Truth receipt confirms the type. Mixed 3/2 gives two Truth lines with two dates: for <em>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now</em>, I didn't sleep well, last night, and I am tired, now. Two past dates would make it a third conditional, and the same if half can take either result: <em>I would have caught the bus</em> is over, while <em>I would be at school by now</em> reaches the present. A real conditional denies nothing and has no Truth line.","The time words carry the decision, so read them before the verbs. Past cues such as <em>last night</em> or <em>in M1</em> belong to the condition; <em>now</em>, <em>still</em>, <em>today</em> and <em>by now</em> belong to the result, and with them <em>would have been</em> is a time mismatch. <b>Would</b> stays out of the if half, and <b>will</b>, <b>can</b> and present forms stay out of an imagined result. One extension: the result can also be an imagined future, as in <em>we would be flying tomorrow</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't be tired now.","g":"Truth: I didn't sleep well — last night. I am tired — now. The condition is two steps back, the result one."},{"s":"If Mint hadn't missed the 7.15 train, she would be in class by now.","g":"Truth: Mint missed the train — this morning. She isn't in class — now. \"By now\" anchors the result in the present."},{"s":"If we hadn't adopted Mochi three years ago, she might still be living on the street.","g":"Truth: we adopted Mochi — three years ago. She lives with us — now. \"Might\" keeps the imagined result only possible; \"still\" is the present cue."},{"s":"If I hadn't left my glasses at home this morning, I could read the board now.","g":"Truth: I left my glasses at home — this morning. I can't read the board — now. \"Could\" here means \"would be able to\"."},{"s":"<s>If I had slept well last night, I wouldn't have been tired now.</s>","g":"Time mismatch: the past ticket \"have\" dates the result in the past, but \"now\" dates it in the present. Fix: I wouldn't be tired now."}]}},"thai":"ถ้าตอนนั้น… ป่านนี้ก็คง…แล้ว (ตอนนั้น = back then, ป่านนี้ = by now)","items":[{"id":"w5l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Pim says: <em>If I hadn't stayed up watching videos last night, I wouldn't be so sleepy in class now.</em> What is true?","options":["Pim stayed up last night, and she is sleepy now.","Pim went to bed early last night, but she is sleepy now.","Pim stayed up last night, but she is not sleepy now.","Pim may stay up tonight, and then she will be sleepy."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: each Truth line is the opposite of its half. She stayed up, last night; she is sleepy, now.","Check the if half. \"Hadn't stayed up\" is imagined, so the truth is the opposite: she did stay up.","Half right. The result is imagined too: \"wouldn't be sleepy now\" means that she is sleepy now.","This reads it as a real future. Had plus V3 with \"last night\" is an imagined past, not a plan for tonight."],"hint":"Write the two Truth lines. Each one is the opposite of its half.","why":"Both halves are imagined, so each Truth line reverses its half: she did stay up last night, and she is sleepy now.","truth":"Pim stayed up — last night. She is sleepy — now."},{"id":"w5l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"<em>If Fern had charged her phone this morning, she could call her mum now.</em> When is each half about?","options":["Both halves are about this morning.","The if half is about now; the result is about this morning.","Both halves are about now.","The if half is about this morning; the result is about now."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Look at the result again. \"Could call\" has no past ticket, and \"now\" dates it in the present.","The other way round. \"Had charged\" with \"this morning\" is the past half; \"now\" belongs to the result.","The if half has had plus V3 and \"this morning\". That is two steps back: an imagined past.","Yes: a past condition and a present result. Two dates, so this is mixed 3/2."],"hint":"Find the time word in each half, then look at each verb.","why":"\"Had charged\" with \"this morning\" is an imagined past; \"could call\" with \"now\" is an imagined present. Each half has its own date.","truth":"Fern didn't charge her phone — this morning. She can't call her mum — now."},{"id":"w5l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"Mook forgot her umbrella this morning. Now it is pouring, and she is stuck at the BTS station."}],"stem":"Which sentence matches the story?","options":["If Mook had brought her umbrella, she wouldn't have been stuck at the station yesterday.","If Mook hadn't brought her umbrella, she would be stuck at the station now.","If Mook had brought her umbrella, she wouldn't be stuck at the station now.","If Mook brings her umbrella, she won't get stuck at the station."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The if half is right, but this result is dated yesterday. In the story she is stuck now.","Write the Truth lines: this one says she did bring it and is not stuck. The story says the opposite.","Yes: Truth one, she didn't bring it, this morning; Truth two, she is stuck, now.","This is a real future with no Truth line. The story is about this morning and now, and both things are already decided."],"hint":"Date the two facts in the story. Which sentence gives those two Truth lines?","why":"She forgot the umbrella this morning and is stuck now, so the if half is an imagined past and the result an imagined present.","truth":"Mook didn't bring her umbrella — this morning. She is stuck at the station — now."},{"id":"w5l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"You understood that whole Korean interview without subtitles!"},{"who":"Mint","text":"I know! I started learning from fan subtitles two years ago."}],"stem":"If Mint hadn't started two years ago, she ___ Korean interviews without subtitles now.","options":["can't follow","couldn't have followed","didn't follow","couldn't follow"],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["\"Can't\" is a real present form. This result is imagined, so the verb needs one step back.","The past ticket \"have\" puts the result in the past, but the time word is \"now\".","The past simple states a real past fact. An imagined result needs would, could or might.","Yes: one step back for an imagined ability now, and no past ticket because of \"now\"."],"hint":"When is the result? Does it need a past ticket?","why":"The result is about now, so it takes could plus the base verb with no have; the starting was two years ago.","truth":"Mint started learning — two years ago. She can follow Korean interviews — now."},{"id":"w5l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Praew is still queuing outside the cinema. If she ___ the tickets online yesterday, she would be inside by now.","options":["has booked","had booked","would have booked","books"],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["\"Has booked\" is the present perfect, which is a real form. An imagined past needs had, not has.","Yes: \"yesterday\" and imagined, so two steps back in the if half.","\"Would\" belongs in the result half. The if half of an imagined past takes had plus V3.","A present form cannot go with \"yesterday\", and this booking is imagined, not real."],"hint":"When is the if half? How many steps back does an imagined past take?","why":"The booking is imagined and dated yesterday, so the if half takes had plus the past participle; would stays in the result.","truth":"Praew didn't book online — yesterday. She isn't inside — now."},{"id":"w5l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"This afternoon Bam is at the concert stall. The T-shirt costs 450 baht and she has only 300."}],"stem":"If Bam [[had saved]] her pocket money [[last month]], she [[would have been]] able to buy the T-shirt [[right now]].","answer":2,"fix":"would be","fb":["This part is fine: last month is past and imagined, so had plus V3 is right.","This part is fine: it dates the if half in the past, which matches the story.","This is the slip. The result is dated right now, so it cannot carry the past ticket. Say \"would be able to buy\".","This part is fine: the story is about this afternoon, so the result really is about now."],"hint":"Date each half from the story. Which verb does not match its time word?","why":"Bam is at the stall now, so the result is present: would plus the base verb. \"Would have been\" belongs to a past result.","truth":"Bam didn't save her pocket money — last month. She can't buy the T-shirt — now."},{"id":"w5l1-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"It is 11 p.m. and Ploy is still doing her homework. If she ___ (not / watch) three episodes after dinner, she would be asleep by now.","accept":["hadn't watched","had not watched"],"model":"hadn't watched","hint":"When was the watching? How many steps back for an imagined past?","why":"The watching was after dinner, in the past, and it really happened, so the if half needs had not plus the past participle.","truth":"Ploy watched three episodes — after dinner. She isn't asleep — now."},{"id":"w5l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","given":"Fern missed the try-outs in May, so she isn't in the school badminton team today.","stem":"If Fern hadn't missed the try-outs in May, she ___ the school badminton team today.","accept":["would be in","'d be in","could be in","might be in","would be on","'d be on","could be on","might be on","would be part of","'d be part of","would be a member of"],"model":"would be in","hint":"Look at the time word at the end. Does this result need a past ticket?","why":"The result is about today, so it takes would plus the base verb with no have, even though the try-outs were in May.","truth":"Fern missed the try-outs — in May. She isn't in the team — today."}],"world":"w5"},"w5l2":{"id":"w5l2","name":"Mixed 2/3: a permanent or present condition, past result","cefr":"C1","ctype":"m23","formula":{"if":"If + past simple / were (still true now)","result":"would / could / might have + V3 (past)"},"uses":[{"use":"An excuse: a lasting fact explains why you didn't","ex":"If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday."},{"use":"Praise: character behind a past success","ex":"If Jane weren't so calm, she wouldn't have won that tie-break last week."},{"use":"Thanks to a friend","ex":"If you weren't such a good friend, you wouldn't have waited in the rain yesterday."},{"use":"Gentle criticism of a habit","ex":"If you checked the group chat more often, you'd have seen the room change."},{"use":"A skill you don't have, and a past chance","ex":"If I spoke Japanese, I could have helped those tourists yesterday."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Mixed 2/3 takes a fact that is still true now, imagines it away, and joins it to an imagined result in the past.","body":["Mixed 2/3 is the other mixed type. The <b>if</b> half is about something true now or always true. It can be a fear, a skill, a habit or where you live. The result (what happened) is about one time in the past. The if half comes from type 2. The result half comes from type 3.","Build each half on its own. First, the if half. The fact is true now, and you imagine it away. So take one step back: use the past simple, <b>were</b> or <b>could</b>. <em>Am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>like</em> becomes <em>liked</em>, <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. This past form is not past time. It means not real. Reported speech uses the same staircase, but for a different reason.","Next, the result. It is imagined and it is past, so take two steps back. Use <b>would have</b> plus the past participle (V3). <b>Have</b> is the past ticket. You can also say <b>could have</b> for ability or <b>might have</b> for maybe. Without <b>have</b>, the result is about now or the future. So <em>would go</em> cannot sit with <em>yesterday</em>. And <b>had</b> plus V3 alone is for an if half, not for a result.","Check with two Truth lines. Take <em>If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday</em>. Truth one: I am afraid of heights, always and still now. Truth two: I didn't go up, yesterday. One line says now or always. The other has a past date. That means mixed 2/3.","Ask one question about the if half: is it still true today? If yes, use the past simple. If it was true only at that past time, use <b>had</b> plus V3. Then the sentence is type 3. Watch two mistakes. Do not leave the if half in the present, even for a true fact. And do not put <b>will</b> or <b>would</b> in the if half."],"examples":[{"s":"If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday.","g":"Truth: I am afraid of heights — always, still now. I didn't go up — yesterday. Two dates, so mixed 2/3."},{"s":"If Jane weren't so calm under pressure, she wouldn't have won the tie-break last week.","g":"Truth: Jane is calm — always. She won the tie-break — last week."},{"s":"If I spoke Japanese, I could have helped the tourists at Siam station yesterday.","g":"Truth: I don't speak Japanese — now. I didn't help them — yesterday. \"Could have\" is imagined ability."},{"s":"If we lived nearer the school, I might not have been late this morning.","g":"Truth: we don't live near the school — now. I was late — this morning. \"Might\" means maybe."},{"s":"<s>If I weren't afraid of heights, I would go up the tower yesterday.</s>","g":"Wrong: \"yesterday\" is past, so the result needs the past ticket. Say: I would have gone up."}]},"B2":{"key":"In mixed 2/3 the <b>if</b> half imagines away something still true now (past simple or <b>were</b>) and the result is an imagined past (<b>would have</b> plus V3).","body":["Mixed 2/3 is the mirror of mixed 3/2. The <b>if</b> half imagines away something that is true now or always true: a fear, a skill, a habit, where you live. The result half is about one moment in the past. The recipe is in the name: the if half comes from the second conditional and the result half from the third.","Build each half from its own time. The if half is an imagined present, so it takes one step back: past simple, <b>were</b> or <b>could</b>. On the staircase, <em>am</em> becomes <em>were</em>, <em>like</em> becomes <em>liked</em> and <em>can</em> becomes <em>could</em>. This past form is not past time; it shows distance from reality, on the same staircase that reported speech uses for a different reason. The result is an imagined past, so it takes two steps: <b>would have</b>, <b>could have</b> or <b>might have</b> plus the past participle (V3).","Write two Truth lines. For <em>If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday</em>: I am afraid of heights, always and still now; I didn't go up, yesterday. One line is dated now or always, the other is dated in the past. That pair of dates is the signature of mixed 2/3.","The test for the if half is one question: is this still true today? If the condition belonged only to that past moment and is over, such as being ill last Friday, use <b>had</b> plus V3 and you have a plain third conditional. If it is still true, such as not speaking Japanese, the past simple says so.","Two things go wrong. First, the result loses its past ticket: <em>would go</em> is about now or the future, so with <em>yesterday</em> or <em>last week</em> you need <b>would have</b> plus V3, and <b>had</b> plus V3 alone belongs in an if half. Second, the if half stays in the present or takes <b>will</b> or <b>would</b>. Even a true, permanent fact needs one step back when you imagine it away."],"examples":[{"s":"If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday.","g":"Truth: I am afraid of heights — always, still now. I didn't go up — yesterday. Two receipts, two dates: mixed 2/3."},{"s":"If Jane weren't so calm under pressure, she wouldn't have won the tie-break last week.","g":"Truth: Jane is calm — always. She won the tie-break — last week. A lasting quality explains a past event."},{"s":"If I spoke Japanese, I could have helped the tourists at Siam station yesterday.","g":"Truth: I don't speak Japanese — now. I didn't help them — yesterday. \"Could have\" gives imagined past ability."},{"s":"If we lived nearer the school, I might not have been late this morning.","g":"Truth: we don't live near the school — now. I was late — this morning. \"Might not have\" keeps the result only possible."},{"s":"<s>If I weren't afraid of heights, I would go up the tower yesterday.</s>","g":"No past ticket: \"would go\" is about now or the future, but the result is dated yesterday. Fix: I would have gone up."}]},"C1":{"key":"Mixed 2/3 sets a condition that still holds, one step back, against a single past result, two steps back.","body":["Mixed 2/3 runs the other way from mixed 3/2. The condition imagines away something that holds now or permanently, such as a fear, a skill, a habit or an address, and the result is a single event in the past. Because the state was already true when the event happened, and is still true, English keeps the condition in the second-conditional form and gives only the result the third-conditional form.","Build each half from its own date. The condition is an imagined present, one step back on the staircase: <em>am</em> to <em>were</em>, <em>like</em> to <em>liked</em>, <em>can</em> to <em>could</em>. The past form marks distance from reality, not past time; reported speech climbs the same staircase to mark distance in time. The result is an imagined past, two steps back: <b>would have</b>, <b>could have</b> or <b>might have</b> plus the past participle (V3), where <b>have</b> is the past ticket.","The Truth receipt has two lines with two dates. For <em>If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday</em>: I am afraid of heights, always and still now; I didn't go up, yesterday. A line dated now or always beside a line dated in the past is the signature of mixed 2/3. Speakers use it for excuses, for praise and thanks, and to explain a past event by someone's character.","The deciding question for the condition is whether it is still true today. A state that belonged only to that moment and is over, such as being ill last Friday, takes <b>had</b> plus V3 and gives an ordinary third conditional. A lasting state takes the past simple, which tells the listener that nothing has changed. You will hear <b>had been</b> for lasting states too; the past simple is simply the more exact choice.","Errors come in two kinds. The result loses its ticket: <em>would go</em> points at now or the future, so it clashes with <em>yesterday</em> or <em>last week</em>, while <b>had</b> plus V3 alone is a condition form, not a result. Or the condition fails to step back: it stays in the present because the fact is true, or it takes <b>will</b> or <b>would</b>, which belong in the result. Mixed forms are rare in real English, so use one only when the time logic is truly mixed."],"examples":[{"s":"If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone up the tower yesterday.","g":"Truth: I am afraid of heights — always, still now. I didn't go up — yesterday. The condition is one step back, the result two."},{"s":"If Jane weren't so calm under pressure, she wouldn't have won the tie-break last week.","g":"Truth: Jane is calm — always. She won the tie-break — last week. Character explains a past event; here the meaning is praise."},{"s":"If I spoke Japanese, I could have helped the tourists at Siam station yesterday.","g":"Truth: I don't speak Japanese — now. I didn't help them — yesterday. \"Could have\" means \"would have been able to\"."},{"s":"If we lived nearer the school, I might not have been late this morning.","g":"Truth: we don't live near the school — now. I was late — this morning. \"Might not have\" keeps the imagined result only possible."},{"s":"<s>If I weren't afraid of heights, I would go up the tower yesterday.</s>","g":"No past ticket: \"would go\" points at now or the future, but \"yesterday\" dates the result in the past. Fix: I would have gone up."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w5l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"Nan says: <em>If I liked spicy food, I'd have tried the som tam at the market yesterday.</em> What is true?","options":["She didn't like spicy food yesterday, but she does like it now.","She doesn't like spicy food, and she didn't try the som tam.","She likes spicy food, so she tried the som tam yesterday.","She might try the som tam if she starts to like spicy food."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Close, but \"liked\" here is not past time. It is one step back from a fact that is still true: she doesn't like spicy food.","Yes: Truth one, she doesn't like spicy food, always; Truth two, she didn't try it, yesterday.","Both halves are imagined, so both truths are the opposite of what the sentence imagines.","This reads it as a real future. \"I'd have tried\" with \"yesterday\" is an imagined past; nothing is planned."],"hint":"Is \"liked\" past time here, or one step back from a present fact?","why":"The past simple imagines away a fact that is still true, and would have plus V3 imagines yesterday differently, so both truths are negative.","truth":"Nan doesn't like spicy food — always. She didn't try the som tam — yesterday."},{"id":"w5l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"<em>If Mook could swim, she would have joined the boat trip last Saturday.</em> Which description fits the two halves?","options":["The if half is about last Saturday only; the result is about how things are now.","Both halves are about a possible trip in the future.","The if half imagines away a present fact; the result is about last Saturday.","Both halves are about last Saturday only, and nothing is said about now."],"answer":2,"near":3,"fb":["The other way round. \"Could swim\" has no had plus V3, and the past ticket is in the result.","Nothing here is open or possible. Both halves are imagined, and \"last Saturday\" is over.","Yes: she can't swim, now and in general; she didn't join, last Saturday. Two dates: mixed 2/3.","The result is past, yes. But \"could swim\" is one step back from \"can swim\", so it tells us about now too."],"hint":"How many steps back is each verb? One step and two steps mean different dates.","why":"\"Could swim\" is one step back from a present fact, she can't swim; \"would have joined\" is two steps back, an imagined last Saturday.","truth":"Mook can't swim — now, in general. She didn't join the boat trip — last Saturday."},{"id":"w5l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"Fern can't ride a bike. Last Sunday her friends cycled round the park, and Fern stayed at home."}],"stem":"Which sentence explains why Fern stayed at home last Sunday?","options":["If Fern could ride a bike, she would have gone with them last Sunday.","If Fern had ridden a bike, she would be with her friends now.","If Fern couldn't ride a bike, she would have stayed at home last Sunday.","If Fern learns to ride a bike, she will go with them next time."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: Truth one, she can't ride a bike, still true; Truth two, she didn't go, last Sunday.","This is mixed 3/2: one past ride and a result now. The story is about a lasting fact and a past Sunday.","Write the Truth line for the if half: it would mean that she can ride a bike. The story says she can't.","A real future with no Truth line. It may happen, but it does not explain last Sunday."],"hint":"Write the story as two Truth lines with dates. Which sentence gives exactly those?","why":"Not being able to ride is still true and the ride was last Sunday, so the if half is one step back and the result two.","truth":"Fern can't ride a bike — still true. She didn't go with her friends — last Sunday."},{"id":"w5l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Ploy","text":"Did you try the durian ice cream at the night market last night?"},{"who":"Praew","text":"No. I can't stand durian. I never could."}],"stem":"If I liked durian, I ___ it last night, I promise.","options":["would try","will try","would have tried","had tried"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No past ticket: \"would try\" is about now or the future, but the result is dated last night.","\"Will\" is for a real future. This result is imagined, and last night is over.","Yes: an imagined past result, two steps back, with the \"have\" ticket.","Had plus V3 is the form for an imagined past if half. A result needs would, could or might."],"hint":"When is the result? Is there a past ticket?","why":"The result is dated last night and did not happen, so it needs would have plus the past participle; the dislike is still true.","truth":"Praew doesn't like durian — always. She didn't try the ice cream — last night."},{"id":"w5l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"Mint has never been a morning person, and she still isn't. If she ___ a morning person, she wouldn't have missed the 6.30 school bus on Monday.","options":["is","would be","were","will be"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The fact is real, but the sentence imagines it away. An imagined present needs one step back from \"is\".","\"Would\" belongs in the result half, not after \"if\".","Yes: one step back for a present fact that the speaker imagines away.","No \"will\" after \"if\", and this is not a real future anyway."],"hint":"Is the if half real or imagined? How many steps back from the present?","why":"Mint still isn't a morning person, so the if half imagines a present fact away: one step back, to were.","truth":"Mint isn't a morning person — always. She missed the bus — on Monday."},{"id":"w5l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"Jane loves cats. Last night she stopped outside 7-Eleven to feed a stray."}],"stem":"If Jane [[didn't love]] cats so much, she [[wouldn't stop]] to feed the stray [[outside 7-Eleven]] [[last night]].","answer":1,"fix":"wouldn't have stopped","fb":["This part is fine: she still loves cats, so the if half is one step back, past simple.","This is the slip. The result is dated last night, so it needs the past ticket: \"wouldn't have stopped\".","This part is fine: it only says where the cat was.","This part is fine: the story says she stopped last night, so the result really is past."],"hint":"Date each half from the story. Which verb does not match its date?","why":"The stopping was last night, so the result needs would not have plus the past participle. Her love of cats is still true.","truth":"Jane loves cats — always. She stopped to feed the stray — last night."},{"id":"w5l2-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"Bam doesn't play any instrument. If she played the guitar, she ___ (join) the school band last term.","accept":["would have joined","'d have joined","would've joined","could have joined","could've joined","might have joined","might've joined"],"model":"would have joined","hint":"When is the result? Count the steps back and check for the past ticket.","why":"The result is dated last term and did not happen, so it needs would have plus the past participle.","truth":"Bam doesn't play an instrument — now. She didn't join the band — last term."},{"id":"w5l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","given":"Pim is so forgetful that she left her PE kit at home yesterday.","stem":"If Pim weren't so forgetful, she ___ her PE kit at home yesterday.","accept":["wouldn't have left","would not have left"],"model":"wouldn't have left","hint":"The if half is still true today. What date does the result carry?","why":"Leaving the kit happened yesterday, so the imagined opposite is would not have plus the past participle.","truth":"Pim is forgetful — always. She left her PE kit at home — yesterday."}],"world":"w5"},"w5l3":{"id":"w5l3","name":"All six types: name the type from the time of each half","cefr":"B2","ctype":"mix","formula":{"if":"Each half: real or imagined? Past, now or future?","result":"Six names: 0, 1, 2, 3, mixed 3/2, mixed 2/3"},"uses":[{"use":"Zero: facts and habits","ex":"If I drink bubble tea after six, I can't sleep."},{"use":"First: plans, promises and warnings","ex":"If you miss the last BTS, call me."},{"use":"Second: dreams and advice","ex":"If I were you, I'd tell the teacher today."},{"use":"Third: regret and relief about the past","ex":"If Fah hadn't texted me, I'd have waited in the wrong room."},{"use":"Mixed 3/2: a past cause you still feel","ex":"If I had charged my phone, it wouldn't be dead now."},{"use":"Mixed 2/3: a lasting fact behind a past event","ex":"If I liked horror films, I'd have come with you last night."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"To name the type, date each half: is it real or imagined, and is it past, now or future?","body":["There are six types, and one method finds them all. Look at each half alone. Ask two questions. Is it real or imagined? When is it? Real halves use normal tenses and have no Truth line. Imagined halves step back, and each one has a Truth line with a date.","Two types are real. The zero conditional is always true. It uses the present simple in both halves, as in <em>If you heat ice, it melts</em>. The first conditional is a possible future. It uses the present simple after <b>if</b>. The result uses <b>will</b>, <b>might</b>, <b>can</b> or an instruction (an order like <em>wait</em>). Never put <b>will</b> after <b>if</b>.","Two types are imagined, and both halves have the same time. The second conditional imagines now or the future. It is one step back: past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb. The third conditional imagines the past. It is two steps back: <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3), then <b>would have</b> plus V3. <b>Have</b> is the past ticket. A past form here means not real, not past time.","Two types are mixed. They are imagined, but the halves have different times. Mixed 3/2 has a past if half and a result now. Use <b>had</b> plus V3, then <b>would</b> plus the base verb. Look for <em>now</em>, <em>still</em> or <em>by now</em>. Mixed 2/3 has an if half that is still true and a past result. Use the past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would have</b> plus V3. The first number names the if half.","Here is a quick check. Look for <b>had</b> plus V3 in the if half. Look for <b>have</b> after <b>would</b> in the result. Both are there: third. Only the first: mixed 3/2. Only the second: mixed 2/3. Neither, but a past form and <b>would</b>: second. Then read the time words and write the Truth lines. The time decides the type."],"examples":[{"s":"If it rains this afternoon, we'll play badminton indoors.","g":"First conditional: real, future. No Truth line. Present after \"if\", then \"will\"."},{"s":"If it were sunny now, we'd be on the court.","g":"Second conditional. Truth: it isn't sunny — now. We aren't on the court — now."},{"s":"If it had rained yesterday, the match would have been cancelled.","g":"Third conditional. Truth: it didn't rain — yesterday. The match wasn't cancelled — yesterday."},{"s":"If it hadn't rained last night, the court would be dry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2. Truth: it rained — last night. The court is wet — now."},{"s":"If our court had a roof, we wouldn't have missed practice yesterday.","g":"Mixed 2/3. Truth: the court has no roof — still true. We missed practice — yesterday."}]},"B2":{"key":"Name any conditional by dating each half: real or imagined, and past, present or future.","body":["To name any conditional, date each half. Ask two questions about the <b>if</b> half and then about the result: is it real or imagined, and when is it? Real halves keep their normal tenses and have no Truth line, because nothing is denied. Imagined halves step back on the staircase, and each one has a Truth line with a date. Six combinations have names.","Two types are real. The zero conditional is always true: present simple in both halves, as in <em>If you heat ice, it melts</em>. The first conditional is a possible future: present simple after <b>if</b>, then <b>will</b>, or <b>might</b>, <b>can</b> or an instruction. Remember that there is no <b>will</b> after <b>if</b>. Real or imagined is the speaker's view, not a percentage.","Two types are imagined, with both halves at the same time. The second conditional imagines now or the future: past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb. That is one step back, and the past form shows distance from reality, not past time. The third conditional imagines the past: <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3), then <b>would have</b> plus V3. That is two steps back, and <b>have</b> is the past ticket.","Two types are mixed: imagined, with the halves at different times. Mixed 3/2 has a past if half and a present result: <b>had</b> plus V3, then <b>would</b> plus the base verb, often with <em>now</em>, <em>still</em> or <em>by now</em>. Mixed 2/3 has an if half that is still true now and a past result: past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would have</b> plus V3. In each name, the first number is the if half.","A quick way to read the verbs: look for <b>had</b> plus V3 in the if half and for <b>have</b> after <b>would</b> in the result. Both there: third. Only the first: mixed 3/2. Only the second: mixed 2/3. Neither, but a past form and <b>would</b>: second. Present forms in both halves: zero or first. Then check the time words and the Truth lines, because the time decides, not the label."],"examples":[{"s":"If it rains this afternoon, we'll play badminton indoors.","g":"First conditional: real and future, so no Truth line. Present after \"if\"; \"will\" only in the result."},{"s":"If it were sunny now, we'd be on the court.","g":"Second conditional, one step back. Truth: it isn't sunny — now. We aren't on the court — now."},{"s":"If it had rained yesterday, the match would have been cancelled.","g":"Third conditional, two steps back. Truth: it didn't rain — yesterday. The match wasn't cancelled — yesterday."},{"s":"If it hadn't rained last night, the court would be dry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2. Truth: it rained — last night. The court is wet — now. Two dates."},{"s":"If our court had a roof, we wouldn't have missed practice yesterday.","g":"Mixed 2/3. Truth: the court has no roof — still true. We missed practice — yesterday. Two dates."}]},"C1":{"key":"The six type names are labels for six combinations of two decisions per half: real or imagined, and when.","body":["Every conditional can be named by dating its two halves. For each half ask whether it is real or imagined, and whether it is past, present or future. Real halves keep whatever tense fits their time and deny nothing, so they have no Truth line. Imagined halves step back on the staircase, and each carries a Truth line with a date. The six names are labels for six combinations.","Two combinations are real. The zero conditional states what is always true, with the present simple in both halves: <em>If you heat ice, it melts</em>. The first conditional is a possible future: a present form after <b>if</b>, because the if half sets the scene and only the result predicts, and then <b>will</b>, <b>might</b>, <b>can</b> or an imperative. Real and imagined are the speaker's view of the situation, not a percentage of likelihood.","Two are imagined with both halves at one time. The second conditional imagines the present or future, one step back: past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> plus the base verb. The third imagines the past, two steps back: <b>had</b> plus the past participle (V3), then <b>would have</b> plus V3, with <b>have</b> as the past ticket. The past form shows distance from reality, as reported speech uses it to show distance in time.","Two are mixed: imagined, with the halves at different times. Mixed 3/2 sets a past condition against a present result: <b>had</b> plus V3, then <b>would</b> plus the base verb, usually with <em>now</em>, <em>still</em>, <em>today</em> or <em>by now</em>. Mixed 2/3 sets a condition that is still true against a past result: past simple or <b>were</b>, then <b>would have</b> plus V3. In both labels the first number is the if half, and each gives two Truth lines with two dates.","The verbs give a fast first reading. <b>Had</b> plus V3 in the if half and <b>have</b> after <b>would</b> in the result: third. Only the first: mixed 3/2. Only the second: mixed 2/3. Neither, with a past form and <b>would</b>: second. Present forms throughout: zero or first, and <b>will</b> or a single future event separates first from zero. Confirm with the time words, because the time decides and the label only follows."],"examples":[{"s":"If it rains this afternoon, we'll play badminton indoors.","g":"First conditional: real and future, nothing denied, so no Truth line. The if half sets the scene; only the result predicts."},{"s":"If it were sunny now, we'd be on the court.","g":"Second conditional, one step back in both halves. Truth: it isn't sunny — now. We aren't on the court — now."},{"s":"If it had rained yesterday, the match would have been cancelled.","g":"Third conditional, two steps back in both halves. Truth: it didn't rain — yesterday. The match wasn't cancelled — yesterday."},{"s":"If it hadn't rained last night, the court would be dry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2: condition two steps back, result one. Truth: it rained — last night. The court is wet — now."},{"s":"If our court had a roof, we wouldn't have missed practice yesterday.","g":"Mixed 2/3: condition one step back, result two. Truth: the court has no roof — still true. We missed practice — yesterday."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w5l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which type is this? <em>If Ploy had set her alarm last night, she wouldn't be running for the BTS now.</em>","options":["Third conditional","Mixed 3/2","Mixed 2/3","Second conditional"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The if half fits, but a third conditional result needs \"would have\" plus V3 and a past date. This result is dated now.","Yes: the if half is an imagined past, last night, and the result is an imagined present, now.","In mixed 2/3 the if half is still true now and the result is past. Here it is the other way round.","A second conditional has a past simple if half. \"Had set\" with \"last night\" is two steps back."],"hint":"Date each half. Which half is past, and which is now?","why":"Had plus V3 with \"last night\" is an imagined past; would plus the base verb with \"now\" is an imagined present. Past condition, present result.","truth":"Ploy didn't set her alarm — last night. She is running for the BTS — now."},{"id":"w5l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which type is this? <em>If Mum says yes, I might go to the concert on Saturday.</em>","options":["Zero conditional","Second conditional","Mixed 2/3","First conditional"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Zero is for things that are always true. This is about one Saturday that has not come yet.","A second conditional steps back: \"said\" and \"might\" for a dream. \"Says\" is a present form, and nothing is denied.","Mixed 2/3 is imagined and has a past result. Nothing here is imagined or past.","Yes: real and future. Present after \"if\", and \"might\" for a possible result. No Truth line."],"hint":"Is anything denied here? Is it always true, or about one future time?","why":"Mum may say yes, so the sentence is real, and Saturday is one future time: present after if, with might in the result."},{"id":"w5l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"Which type is this? <em>If Fah weren't so shy, she would have asked the singer for a photo yesterday.</em>","options":["Mixed 2/3","Third conditional","Second conditional","Mixed 3/2"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the if half imagines away something still true, and the result is an imagined past, yesterday.","The result fits, but a third conditional if half needs had plus V3. \"Weren't\" is one step back from a present fact.","The if half fits, but a second conditional result has no past ticket. \"Would have asked\" is dated yesterday.","In mixed 3/2 the if half is past and the result is now. Here it is the other way round."],"hint":"Date each half. Which one is still true today, and which one is over?","why":"\"Weren't\" is one step back from a fact that is still true; \"would have asked\" with \"yesterday\" is an imagined past result.","truth":"Fah is shy — always. She didn't ask the singer for a photo — yesterday."},{"id":"w5l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Nan doesn't have a cat, but she has already chosen a name. If she ___ one, she would call it Tofu.","options":["has","had","will have","would have"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["She has no cat, so this is imagined. An imagined present needs one step back from \"has\".","Yes: one step back for an imagined present. The past form shows it is not real.","No \"will\" after \"if\", and the cat is imagined, not a real future.","\"Would\" belongs in the result half. It is already there, in \"would call\"."],"hint":"Is the cat real or imagined? How many steps back?","why":"Nan has no cat now, so the if half is an imagined present: one step back, to the past simple.","truth":"Nan doesn't have a cat — now."},{"id":"w5l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B2","stem":"Every rainy season it is the same: if it ___ hard for an hour, our soi floods.","options":["will rain","rained","would rain","rains"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["No \"will\" after \"if\". The if half sets the scene with a present form.","A past form would mean a real past or an imagined world. This is true every rainy season, and \"floods\" is present.","\"Would\" never goes in a plain if half, and nothing here is imagined.","Yes: always true, so present simple in both halves."],"hint":"Real or imagined? One time, or every time?","why":"It happens every rainy season, so it is real and always true: present simple in both halves."},{"id":"w5l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"It is lunchtime. Praew has no PE kit today, because she didn't see the teacher's message last night."}],"stem":"If Praew [[had seen]] the message [[last night]], she [[would bring]] her PE kit [[this morning]].","answer":2,"fix":"would have brought","fb":["This part is fine: last night is past and imagined, so had plus V3 is right.","This part is fine: it dates the if half in the past, as in the story.","This is the slip. This morning is over, so the result needs the past ticket: \"would have brought\".","This part is fine: at lunchtime, this morning is already in the past."],"hint":"It is lunchtime now. Is each half past or present?","why":"At lunchtime both last night and this morning are past, so the result needs would have plus the past participle.","truth":"Praew didn't see the message — last night. She didn't bring her PE kit — this morning."},{"id":"w5l3-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mum's message to Fern: <em>If the BTS is too crowded at Siam, ___ the next train. There is another one in three minutes.</em>","options":["waited for","wait for","would wait for","had waited for"],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["A past form is a step back into an imagined world. The if half is present and real.","Yes: a real, possible situation, and the result is an instruction.","\"Would\" is for an imagined result. \"Is\" shows that this is a real, possible situation.","Had plus V3 is two steps back, for an imagined past. This is about Fern's journey today."],"hint":"Is the crowded train real and possible, or imagined? What is Mum doing with her sentence?","why":"The if half is a real present possibility, so the result keeps a real form; here Mum gives an instruction."},{"id":"w5l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fern forgot to water her basil all last week. If she had watered it, it ___ (still / be) alive today.","accept":["would still be","'d still be","might still be","could still be"],"model":"would still be","hint":"Find the time word at the end. Does this result need a past ticket?","why":"The watering was last week, but the result is about today, so it takes would plus the base verb with no have.","truth":"Fern didn't water her basil — last week. It isn't alive — today."}],"world":"w5"},"w6l1":{"id":"w6l1","name":"unless","cefr":"B2","ctype":"conn","gateway":"pp.72–73","formula":{"if":"unless + present (positive verb, even for the future)","result":"will / can / might + verb, or an imperative"},"uses":[{"use":"Giving a warning","ex":"You'll miss the BTS unless you leave right now."},{"use":"Stating a rule","ex":"You can't enter the library unless you show your student card."},{"use":"A plan with one exception","ex":"We'll walk to school unless Dad offers us a lift."},{"use":"Setting a limit","ex":"I won't lend you my charger unless you promise to bring it back."},{"use":"Describing a habit","ex":"Mum never phones me at school unless something is wrong."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Unless means except if: it names the one thing that stops the result.","body":["<b>Unless</b> means <b>except if</b>. It names the one thing that can stop the result (what happens). <em>We'll eat outside unless it rains.</em> The plan is to eat outside. Only rain can stop it. Try the except test: put in \"except if\". Does the sentence still make sense? Then <b>unless</b> is right.","Unless already has \"not\" inside it. So the verb after it is usually positive. <em>If you don't hurry</em> and <em>unless you hurry</em> mean the same. Never write <s>unless you don't hurry</s>. That is two negatives, and it says the opposite.","For the future, use the present after unless. Do not use will there. Say <em>unless it rains tomorrow</em>, not <s>unless it will rain tomorrow</s>. Will, can or might go in the other half. You can also give an order: <em>Don't call me unless it's important.</em>","Sometimes if … not cannot change to unless. First, when the thing that does not happen is the reason for the result: <em>I'll be sad if you don't come.</em> You not coming makes me sad. It is not an exception. Second, in sentences about an imagined past: <em>If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten.</em> Keep if there.","In rewriting tasks, watch the verbs. <em>I'll only go if you go</em> becomes <em>I won't go unless you go</em>. The first half turns negative. The half after unless stays positive. With if … not, just take out the not."],"examples":[{"s":"We'll eat outside unless it rains.","g":"Except if it rains. Rain is the one thing that stops the plan."},{"s":"You can't come in unless you take off your shoes.","g":"A rule. There is one way in: shoes off."},{"s":"<s>Unless you don't hurry, you'll be late.</s>","g":"Wrong: two negatives. Say: Unless you hurry, you'll be late."},{"s":"I'll be sad if you don't come to my party.","g":"No unless here. You not coming is the reason I'm sad. It is not an exception."},{"s":"If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten my PE kit.","g":"An imagined past, so keep if. Truth: Fern reminded me, and I didn't forget."}]},"B2":{"key":"Unless means except if, takes a positive present verb for the future, and cannot replace every if … not.","body":["<b>Unless</b> means <b>except if</b>: it names the one situation that stops the result. In <em>We'll eat outside unless it rains</em>, eating outside is the plan and rain is the only exception. The except test checks it: if you can say \"except if\" in the same place and the sentence still makes sense, unless is correct.","Because the negative idea is already inside unless, the verb that follows is normally positive. <em>If you don't hurry</em> becomes <em>unless you hurry</em>. Writing <s>unless you don't hurry</s> doubles the negative and reverses the meaning, which is one of the most common mistakes with this word.","Like if, unless takes a present form when it talks about the future: <em>unless it rains tomorrow</em>, never <s>unless it will rain</s>. The other half carries will, can, might, must or an imperative: <em>Don't call me unless it's important.</em> The unless half can come first, followed by a comma, or second with no comma.","Unless does not replace every if … not. It fails when the thing that does not happen is the cause of the result: in <em>I'll be sad if you don't come</em>, your absence causes the sadness; it is not an exception to it. It also fails in most sentences about an imagined past, such as regrets: <em>If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten.</em>","Exam rewrites often move between only if and unless. <em>I'll go only if you go</em> has the same meaning as <em>I won't go unless you go</em>: the main half becomes negative and the unless half stays positive. With if … not, simply drop the not: <em>if it doesn't stop</em> becomes <em>unless it stops</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"We'll eat outside unless it rains.","g":"Passes the except test: we'll eat outside, except if it rains. Present form for the future."},{"s":"You can't come in unless you take off your shoes.","g":"A rule with one way out of the no. The verb after unless is positive."},{"s":"<s>Unless you don't hurry, you'll be late.</s>","g":"Double negative. Correct: Unless you hurry, you'll be late. Or: If you don't hurry, you'll be late."},{"s":"I'll be sad if you don't come to my party.","g":"Fails the except test: your absence causes the sadness, so if … not must stay."},{"s":"If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten my PE kit.","g":"Imagined past: unless does not work here. Truth: Fern did remind me, so I didn't forget my kit."}]},"C1":{"key":"Unless presents a single exception to what otherwise holds, which explains both its built-in negative and the cases where it cannot replace if … not.","body":["<b>Unless</b> means <b>except if</b>. It presents the main clause as what holds by default and then names the single circumstance that cancels it. That is why the except test works: wherever \"except if\" fits without changing the sense, unless is right, as in <em>We'll eat outside unless it rains</em>.","The word began as an older phrase, \"on less than\", meaning on a lesser condition than. The exception, and with it the negative, is part of the word itself, so the clause after it is normally affirmative. <em>Unless you hurry</em> equals <em>if you don't hurry</em>; <s>unless you don't hurry</s> is a double negative that states the reverse. A negative verb after unless is possible only when the exception itself is negative, which is rare and best avoided in exams.","As in other conditional and time clauses, future time after unless is shown with a present form, because the clause sets up a scene and does not predict: <em>unless it rains tomorrow</em>, not <s>unless it will rain</s>. The main clause is free: will, can, might, must, an imperative, or a plain present for habits.","Unless is not a mechanical substitute for if … not. It fails when the non-event is the cause of the result and not an exception to it: <em>I'll be sad if you don't come</em>. It also fails in most counterfactual sentences, especially regret and relief about the past: <em>If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten</em>. There the if-clause describes what did not happen; it is not an exception to a default.","For key-word rewrites, remember the switch between positive and negative. <em>I'll go only if you go</em> and <em>I won't go unless you go</em> are equivalent: only if states the necessary condition positively, while unless states it as the sole exception to a negative. From if … not, remove the negative and keep everything else, including the present tense."],"examples":[{"s":"We'll eat outside unless it rains.","g":"The default is eating outside; rain is the single exception. Present form, no will."},{"s":"You can't come in unless you take off your shoes.","g":"A negative main clause with its sole exception. Same meaning: you can come in only if you take off your shoes."},{"s":"<s>Unless you don't hurry, you'll be late.</s>","g":"The negation is already inside unless, so don't reverses the sense. Correct: Unless you hurry, you'll be late."},{"s":"I'll be sad if you don't come to my party.","g":"The non-event is the cause of the result, not an exception to a default, so unless is unavailable."},{"s":"If Fern hadn't reminded me, I'd have forgotten my PE kit.","g":"Counterfactual relief about the past; unless is not used. Truth: Fern reminded me, and I remembered the kit."}]}},"thai":"เว้นแต่ = unless (except if). After it, no second negative.","items":[{"id":"w6l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Coach","text":"Training is on the outdoor court at four unless it rains."}],"stem":"What does the coach mean?","options":["We train outside, except if it rains.","We train outside only if it rains.","We train outside whatever the weather is.","We train outside because it won't rain."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: unless means except if. Rain is the one thing that moves training.","That turns it round. Rain is the exception here, not the condition for training.","That means rain changes nothing. The coach says rain does change the plan.","The coach doesn't know the weather. Unless leaves rain as a possibility."],"hint":"Try the except test: what is the one thing that stops the plan?","why":"Unless means except if, so outdoor training is the plan and rain is the only thing that cancels it."},{"id":"w6l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Try the except test. In which sentence can you change <b>if … not</b> to <b>unless</b>?","options":["I'll be upset if my parcel doesn't arrive today.","I'll come to your show if I don't have tutoring.","If Ploy hadn't lent me her notes, I would have failed.","I'd be so happy if I didn't have maths today."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Not here. The parcel not arriving is the reason for the feeling, not an exception to it.","Yes: I'll come, except if I have tutoring. Tutoring is the one thing that stops me.","Not here. This is an imagined past, and unless does not work in sentences like that.","Not here. Having no maths is the cause of the happiness, not an exception to it."],"hint":"Where is the missing event an exception, and where is it the cause?","why":"Only the tutoring sentence passes the except test. In the others, the missing event causes the result, or the sentence imagines a different past."},{"id":"w6l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence means the same as this? <em>Mum will let Mint go to the concert only if she finishes her project.</em>","options":["Mum will let Mint go unless she finishes her project.","Mum won't let Mint go unless she doesn't finish her project.","Mum won't let Mint go unless she finishes her project.","Mum will let Mint go if she doesn't finish her project."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["This says finishing the project stops her going. That is the opposite of Mum's rule.","Two negatives after unless turn the meaning round: now not finishing is her way to go.","Yes: only if becomes won't … unless, and the verb after unless stays positive.","This makes not finishing her way to go. Mum wants the project finished first."],"hint":"Which half turns negative, and which half stays positive?","why":"Only if gives the one way to go. Unless says the same from the other side: she can't go, except if she finishes."},{"id":"w6l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Kru Ann","text":"The homework portal closes at midnight tonight."}],"stem":"I can't accept your essay unless you ___ it before then.","options":["will upload","don't upload","uploaded","upload"],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["No will after unless. The present already shows the future here.","Unless already carries the negative. Adding don't reverses the meaning.","Midnight tonight is still in the future, so the past form doesn't fit.","Yes: unless plus a positive present verb, even for tonight."],"hint":"Unless works like if: which tense shows the future after it? And how many negatives do you need?","why":"After unless, a positive present form shows the future: no will, and no second negative."},{"id":"w6l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Aunt","text":"I'm away for two weeks. Please remember the orchid on the balcony."}],"stem":"___ you don't water it every few days, it will die in this heat.","options":["Unless","If","Until","Whether"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The verb is already negative. Unless you don't water it would mean that watering harms the orchid.","Yes: the negative is in don't, so plain if is the word.","Until is about time up to a point. This sentence is a condition.","Whether needs two possibilities. Here there is one condition and one result."],"hint":"Look at the verb after the gap. Is the negative already there?","why":"The clause already has don't, so if is needed. Unless would add a second negative."},{"id":"w6l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Praew says she [[won't come]] to the night market [[unless]] the rain [[will stop]] [[before]] six.","answer":2,"fix":"stops","fb":["This part is fine: the result can be negative.","This part is fine: the rain stopping is the one thing that changes her no, so unless fits.","Yes, this is the mistake: no will after unless. Use the present: unless the rain stops.","This part is fine: before six gives the time limit."],"hint":"Check the verb after unless. Which tense shows the future there?","why":"Unless works like if: the present shows the future, so it is unless the rain stops, not unless the rain will stop."},{"id":"w6l1-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"I'll meet you at the bubble tea shop at five ___ late.","given":"I'll meet you at the bubble tea shop at five if my tutoring doesn't finish late.","keyword":"UNLESS","accept":["unless my tutoring finishes"],"model":"unless my tutoring finishes","hint":"The negative moves inside the key word. What happens to the verb?","why":"If it doesn't finish late equals unless it finishes late: unless carries the negative, so the verb turns positive and stays present."},{"id":"w6l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Bam doesn't answer LINE messages ___ from her mum.","given":"Bam only answers LINE messages if they are from her mum.","keyword":"UNLESS","accept":["unless they are","unless they're","unless they come"],"model":"unless they are","hint":"The first half is negative now. Keep the half after the key word positive.","why":"Only … if becomes doesn't … unless. The verb after unless stays positive and in the present."}],"world":"w6"},"w6l2":{"id":"w6l2","name":"as long as, provided / providing (that), on condition that, only if","cefr":"B2","ctype":"conn","gateway":"pp.72–73","formula":{"if":"as long as / provided (that) / providing (that) / on condition that / only if + present","result":"will / can / may + verb: the deal"},"uses":[{"use":"Giving permission with a rule","ex":"You can stay out till nine as long as you text me."},{"use":"Making a deal with a friend","ex":"I'll help with your maths provided you explain the science homework."},{"use":"Writing rules","ex":"Students may use the lab provided that a teacher is present."},{"use":"A formal agreement","ex":"The school lent us the hall on condition that we cleaned it afterwards."},{"use":"The one necessary condition","ex":"You can sit the retest only if you missed the first test."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"These words make a deal: yes, but only if one thing happens.","body":["These words all make a deal: <b>as long as</b>, <b>provided (that)</b>, <b>providing (that)</b>, <b>on condition that</b> and <b>only if</b>. They mean \"if, and only in this case\". The speaker says yes, but one thing must happen. <em>You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.</em> No lock, no bike.","They are stronger than a simple if. Parents, teachers and friends use them to say yes with a rule. They are the opposite of unless. <em>You can go as long as you finish</em> means the same as <em>You can't go unless you finish</em>.","The grammar is the same as for if. For the future, use the present after these words. Do not use will. Say <em>provided we keep the receipt</em>, not <s>provided we will keep</s>. Will, can or may go in the other half.","Check the small words. After provided and providing, <b>that</b> is optional (you can leave it out). Then comes a subject and a verb. After as long as, do not add that. You can also say so long as. On condition that is formal. It starts with on and ends with that.","<b>Only if</b> gives the one necessary condition (the thing you cannot skip). <em>You can enter only if you are a student here.</em> Other people cannot enter. Do not mix it up with <em>if only</em>, which is a wish. In rewriting tasks, these words often replace <em>but you must</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.","g":"A deal. Lock it and the answer is yes. No lock, no bike."},{"s":"We'll get a refund provided (that) we keep the receipt.","g":"That is optional. Keep is present, but it is about the future."},{"s":"The teacher agreed to move the test on condition that everyone came on Friday.","g":"Formal. The deal was in the past, so came is a real past."},{"s":"You can enter only if you are a student here.","g":"The one condition you cannot skip. Other people cannot enter."},{"s":"<s>I'll help you as long as you will help me.</s>","g":"Wrong: no will after as long as. Say: as long as you help me."}]},"B2":{"key":"As long as, provided or providing (that), on condition that and only if all make a bargain: the result happens only in this one case.","body":["<b>As long as</b>, <b>provided (that)</b>, <b>providing (that)</b>, <b>on condition that</b> and <b>only if</b> all mean \"if, and only in this case\". They turn a sentence into a bargain: the speaker agrees, but one thing has to happen. <em>You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.</em> If you don't lock it, the answer becomes no.","They are stronger than plain if, and people use them to give permission with a rule, to make deals and to write rules. They point the opposite way from unless: <em>You can go as long as you finish</em> says the same as <em>You can't go unless you finish</em>. One names the condition for a yes; the other names the only escape from a no.","The tense rule is the rule for if. A present form shows the future after all of them, and will stays in the other half: <em>We'll get a refund provided we keep the receipt</em>, not <s>provided we will keep</s>. If the deal was made in the past, both halves are past: <em>She agreed on condition that everyone came on Friday.</em>","Watch the small words. Provided and providing can take <b>that</b> or leave it out; both are followed by a full clause with a subject and a verb. As long as never takes that, and so long as is a variant. On condition that is a fixed, formal phrase: on at the start, that at the end.","<b>Only if</b> names the one necessary condition: <em>You can enter only if you are a student here</em> shuts out everyone else. Do not confuse it with <em>if only</em>, which expresses a wish. In rewriting tasks, these connectors usually replace <em>but you must</em> or <em>but only if</em>, and the verb after them stays in the present."],"examples":[{"s":"You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.","g":"A bargain: locking it is the one condition. Present form after as long as."},{"s":"We'll get a refund provided (that) we keep the receipt.","g":"Provided with or without that; present keep for the future, will only in the result."},{"s":"The teacher agreed to move the test on condition that everyone came on Friday.","g":"The most formal of the group. Both halves are past because the deal was made in the past."},{"s":"You can enter only if you are a student here.","g":"Only if names the necessary condition and shuts out everyone else. Not the same as if only."},{"s":"<s>I'll help you as long as you will help me.</s>","g":"No will in the condition half. Correct: I'll help you as long as you help me."}]},"C1":{"key":"These connectors present one condition as the price of the result, which is why they sound like a bargain.","body":["<b>As long as</b>, <b>provided (that)</b>, <b>providing (that)</b>, <b>on condition that</b> and <b>only if</b> present a single condition as the price of the result. Plain if merely says the result follows from the condition; these add that nothing else will do. Hence the feel of a bargain: <em>You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.</em>","They differ in register. As long as and so long as are everyday; provided and providing (that) are neutral to formal and common in rules and essays; on condition that is the most formal and suits agreements. All of them mirror unless: <em>You can go as long as you finish</em> and <em>You can't go unless you finish</em> describe one deal from opposite sides.","Because the clause sets up a condition and does not predict, future time is shown with a present form: <em>provided we keep the receipt</em>, never <s>provided we will keep</s>. A bargain reported from the past is simply past in both halves, with no step back in reality: <em>She agreed on condition that everyone came on Friday</em> is a real past deal.","The frames are fixed. Provided and providing take an optional <b>that</b> and then a full clause; other forms of the verb are not connectors. As long as takes no that. On condition that normally keeps both on and that. As long as also has a time sense, \"for the whole time that\", which the context usually separates from the bargain sense.","<b>Only if</b> marks a necessary condition and is not <em>if only</em>, the wish. When only if opens the sentence, formal English inverts the main clause, as after other restrictive openers: <em>Only if you are a student here can you enter.</em> Mid-sentence there is no inversion. In key-word rewrites these connectors replace <em>but you must</em> or <em>but only if</em>."],"examples":[{"s":"You can borrow my bike as long as you lock it.","g":"Everyday bargain; the condition is presented as the only thing that matters. No that after as long as."},{"s":"We'll get a refund provided (that) we keep the receipt.","g":"Neutral to formal; optional that; the present form marks a condition, not a prediction."},{"s":"The teacher agreed to move the test on condition that everyone came on Friday.","g":"Formal fixed phrase. Came is real past time here, not a step back from reality."},{"s":"You can enter only if you are a student here.","g":"Necessary condition. Fronted and formal, it inverts: Only if you are a student here can you enter."},{"s":"<s>I'll help you as long as you will help me.</s>","g":"Will does not mark plain future in the condition clause. Correct: as long as you help me."}]}},"thai":"ตราบใดที่ = as long as: a deal with one condition.","items":[{"id":"w6l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mum","text":"Fern, you can go to the concert as long as you're home by ten."}],"stem":"What is Mum doing?","options":["Refusing, because ten is too late.","Giving permission with no conditions.","Telling Fern how long the concert lasts.","Giving permission with one condition."],"answer":3,"near":2,"fb":["She says you can go. The answer is yes, with a rule attached.","As long as adds a condition: home by ten. Without that, the yes disappears.","Here as long as is not about time. It sets the deal: yes, but only if you're home by ten.","Yes: as long as makes a bargain. Fern may go, but only if she is home by ten."],"hint":"Is this a yes, a no, or a yes with a rule?","why":"As long as means only if: Mum says yes, and being home by ten is the one condition."},{"id":"w6l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence means the same as this? <em>You can borrow my camera, but you must give it back on Monday.</em>","options":["You can borrow my camera unless you give it back on Monday.","You can borrow my camera provided you give it back on Monday.","You can borrow my camera because you give it back on Monday.","You can borrow my camera so that you give it back on Monday."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Unless makes giving it back the thing that stops the loan. That is the reverse of the deal.","Yes: provided makes a bargain. Giving it back on Monday is the condition for borrowing.","Because gives a reason that is already true. Here giving it back is a condition for the future.","So that gives a purpose. Giving it back is the condition for the loan, not its purpose."],"hint":"Is giving it back the condition for the loan, or the thing that stops it?","why":"But you must states a condition. Provided means only if, so it keeps the bargain."},{"id":"w6l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Poster","text":"Students can enter the art contest only if they are under 16."}],"stem":"Jane is 16 and Fern is 14. Who can enter?","options":["Both girls.","Jane, but not Fern.","Fern, but not Jane.","Neither girl."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Only if gives a condition nobody can skip. Jane is 16, which is not under 16.","Check the ages again: under 16 means 15 or younger.","Yes: only if names the one necessary condition, and only Fern meets it.","One of them is 14, so she meets the condition."],"hint":"Only if names the one condition you cannot skip. Who meets it?","why":"Only if gives the necessary condition. Fern is under 16, so she can enter; Jane, at 16, cannot."},{"id":"w6l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fah can join the school trip ___ her parents sign the form by Friday.","options":["provide that","provided to","provided that","providing for"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Close, but the connector is provided or providing, not the plain verb provide.","After provided comes that or nothing, then a subject and a verb. Not to.","Yes: provided that, then a clause with a present verb.","Providing can take that, not for. Then it needs a subject and a verb."],"hint":"Which forms of this word work as connectors, and what small word may follow?","why":"The connector is provided (that) or providing (that), followed by a clause: her parents sign."},{"id":"w6l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"How did you get to stay in the library after five yesterday?"}],"stem":"The librarian let us stay ___ we kept quiet.","options":["on condition if","in condition that","with condition of","on condition that"],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["The phrase ends in that, not if. On condition that already means if.","Nearly: the phrase starts with on, not in.","This phrase doesn't exist. The fixed frame starts with on and ends with that.","Yes: on condition that is the fixed, formal phrase for a deal."],"hint":"This formal phrase is fixed. Which word starts it and which word ends it?","why":"On condition that is a fixed phrase. It is followed by a clause: we kept quiet."},{"id":"w6l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Dad says we [[can have]] the party in the garden [[providing]] the weather [[will stay]] dry [[until]] the evening.","answer":2,"fix":"stays","fb":["This part is fine: can gives permission in the result half.","This part is fine: providing means only if, with or without that.","Yes, this is the mistake: no will after providing. The present shows the future: the weather stays dry.","This part is fine: until the evening says how long."],"hint":"These words follow the same tense rule as if. Check the verb after the connector.","why":"After providing, the present shows the future: providing the weather stays dry, not will stay."},{"id":"w6l2-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"You can stay up late ___ get up on time tomorrow.","given":"You can stay up late, but you must get up on time tomorrow.","keyword":"LONG","accept":["as long as you","so long as you"],"model":"as long as you","hint":"Build the bargain phrase around the key word, then add the subject. No must is needed.","why":"As long as means only if. It replaces but you must, and the present get up shows the future."},{"id":"w6l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"I'll lend you my umbrella ___ it back tomorrow.","given":"I'll lend you my umbrella, but only if you give it back tomorrow.","keyword":"PROVIDED","accept":["provided you give","provided that you give"],"model":"provided you give","hint":"After the key word: that or nothing, then subject and verb. Which tense for tomorrow?","why":"Provided (that) means only if. The verb after it stays in the present for the future: you give."}],"world":"w6"},"w6l3":{"id":"w6l3","name":"in case (and why it is not if)","cefr":"B2","ctype":"conn","gateway":"pp.72–73","formula":{"if":"action now + in case + present (after a past action: in case + past)","result":"the precaution comes first; the possible event comes later"},"uses":[{"use":"Taking a precaution","ex":"I'll take a book in case the queue is long."},{"use":"Giving advice","ex":"Write your name on it in case you lose it."},{"use":"Explaining why you got ready","ex":"I saved the file twice in case my laptop crashed."},{"use":"Keeping it short","ex":"I'll bring some extra cash, just in case."},{"use":"On notices, with of and a noun","ex":"In case of fire, use the stairs."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"In case means do it now to be ready, because something might happen later.","body":["<b>In case</b> is for a precaution (something you do early to be safe). You act now, before anything happens, because maybe it will. <em>Take an umbrella in case it rains.</em> You take the umbrella first. Later it rains, or it doesn't. Either way, you are ready.","This is the umbrella test. Ask: do I do the action before the possible event? If yes, use in case. In case does not mean if. <em>Take an umbrella if it rains</em> means wait: take it only when the rain comes. With if, the action follows the event. With in case, the action comes first.","After in case, use the present for the future. Do not use will. Say <em>in case we get hungry</em>, not <s>in case we will get hungry</s>. When the action is in the past, use the past after in case too: <em>I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.</em> Maybe I forgot it, maybe not.","A sentence with <em>because … might</em> can often change to in case. <em>Charge your phone because the trip might be long</em> becomes <em>Charge your phone in case the trip is long.</em> The word might goes away. People also say <em>just in case</em> at the end of a sentence, with nothing after it.","On signs you will see <b>in case of</b> plus a noun (a thing word): <em>In case of fire, use the stairs.</em> This one does mean \"if there is a fire\". It is formal and is for notices. Do not put a subject and a verb after in case of."],"examples":[{"s":"Take an umbrella in case it rains.","g":"Take it now, before any rain. Maybe it rains, maybe not."},{"s":"Take an umbrella if it rains.","g":"A different meaning: wait, and take it only when it is raining."},{"s":"I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.","g":"A past action, so the past after in case. Forgetting was only a maybe."},{"s":"<s>I'll bring snacks in case we will get hungry.</s>","g":"Wrong: no will after in case. Say: in case we get hungry."},{"s":"In case of fire, use the stairs.","g":"A sign. In case of plus a noun means: if there is a fire."}]},"B2":{"key":"In case means you do something now as a precaution, because something might happen later; it does not mean if.","body":["<b>In case</b> introduces a precaution: you do something in advance because an event is possible. <em>Take an umbrella in case it rains.</em> The umbrella goes into the bag before anyone knows about the rain. Whether it rains or not, the action has already happened.","That is the umbrella test: if the action comes before the possible event, in case is right. It is not a synonym of if. <em>Take an umbrella if it rains</em> tells you to wait and take it only once the rain has started. With if, the action depends on the event; with in case, it depends only on the possibility.","After in case, a present form shows the future, and will is not used: <em>in case we get hungry</em>, not <s>in case we will get hungry</s>. When the precaution was taken in the past, the verb after in case is past as well: <em>I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.</em> The sentence does not say whether I forgot it.","A reason with <em>because … might</em> can usually be rewritten with in case: <em>Charge your phone because the trip might be long</em> becomes <em>Charge your phone in case the trip is long.</em> Notice that might disappears. In speech, <em>just in case</em> often ends a sentence with nothing after it.","Do not confuse it with <b>in case of</b>, which is followed by a noun and belongs on notices: <em>In case of fire, use the stairs.</em> That phrase does mean \"if there is a fire\". It cannot be followed by a clause, and in case cannot be followed by a noun alone."],"examples":[{"s":"Take an umbrella in case it rains.","g":"A precaution: the action comes first; rain is only possible. Present form for the future."},{"s":"Take an umbrella if it rains.","g":"Not a precaution: the action waits for the event."},{"s":"I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.","g":"Past precaution, past verb after in case. We don't know whether I forgot it."},{"s":"<s>I'll bring snacks in case we will get hungry.</s>","g":"No will after in case. Correct: I'll bring snacks in case we get hungry."},{"s":"In case of fire, use the stairs.","g":"Notice language: in case of plus a noun means if there is. Different from in case plus a clause."}]},"C1":{"key":"In case links a precaution to a merely possible later event, so the action never depends on the event actually happening.","body":["<b>In case</b> gives the reason for a precaution: the action is taken in advance because an event is possible. In <em>Take an umbrella in case it rains</em>, the taking is unconditional; only the rain is uncertain. The clause answers \"why do it now?\", not \"under what condition?\".","That is the difference from if, and the umbrella test captures it. <em>Take an umbrella if it rains</em> makes the action wait for the event. In British English and in exams, the two are not interchangeable. In American English, in case is also heard with the meaning of if; recognise it, but do not write it.","The clause refers to a possible future seen from the time of the precaution, and it follows the usual rule: a present form, not will, as in <em>in case we get hungry</em>. After a past precaution the verb is past, and the outcome stays open: <em>I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.</em> Formal writing sometimes adds should: <em>in case it should rain</em>.","Because the idea is \"because this might happen\", sentences with <em>because … might</em> convert neatly, and might drops out: <em>Charge your phone in case the trip is long.</em> The in case clause normally follows the main clause. <em>Just in case</em> can stand alone at the end, leaving the possible event unsaid.","<b>In case of</b> plus a noun is a different item. On notices it means \"if there is\": <em>In case of fire, use the stairs.</em> So the noun form is conditional while the clause form is a precaution, which is exactly why learners mix them up. Neither frame accepts what follows the other."],"examples":[{"s":"Take an umbrella in case it rains.","g":"The taking is unconditional; only the rain is uncertain. Passes the umbrella test."},{"s":"Take an umbrella if it rains.","g":"Conditional, not a precaution: the action depends on the rain actually falling."},{"s":"I wrote the address down in case I forgot it.","g":"The possibility is seen from the past moment of the precaution, so the verb is past. Whether it happened is left open."},{"s":"<s>I'll bring snacks in case we will get hungry.</s>","g":"Will does not appear in the in case clause. Correct: in case we get hungry."},{"s":"In case of fire, use the stairs.","g":"The noun form is conditional and formal; it is not the precaution clause."}]}},"thai":"เผื่อ = in case. Get ready now, because maybe.","items":[{"id":"w6l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"I'm taking my power bank to the concert in case my phone dies."}],"stem":"When does Pim put the power bank in her bag?","options":["Later, only when her phone dies.","Now, before anything goes wrong.","After the concert, back at home.","Never, as her phone will be fine."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That would be if. With in case, she doesn't wait for the problem.","Yes: in case is a precaution. She acts first, because the phone might die.","A precaution after the event is too late. In case means before.","She doesn't know that. In case means maybe, so she gets ready."],"hint":"Run the umbrella test: which comes first, the action or the possible problem?","why":"In case marks a precaution: the action comes first, because the problem might happen later."},{"id":"w6l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which instruction means: put a mask in your bag before you leave, because the haze may get worse?","options":["Carry a mask if the haze gets worse.","Carry a mask when the haze gets worse.","Carry a mask unless the haze gets worse.","Carry a mask in case the haze gets worse."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["With if, you wait: the mask comes out only once the haze is worse.","When says the haze will certainly get worse, and the mask waits until then.","Unless would mean: carry it, except if the haze gets worse. That makes no sense.","Yes: in case means do it now, to be ready for a maybe."],"hint":"Does the action come before the possible event or after it?","why":"Before you leave, because it may happen: that is a precaution, and in case is the precaution word."},{"id":"w6l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"I took a jacket in case the cinema was cold."}],"stem":"What do we know?","options":["Nan took a jacket. Was it cold? We can't tell.","Nan took a jacket only after she felt cold.","Nan didn't take a jacket, and she was cold.","The cinema was freezing, so Nan bought a jacket there."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: taking the jacket is a fact; the cold was only a possibility.","That would be if or when. With in case, she took it first, as a precaution.","She says I took a jacket. That part is a fact.","In case doesn't tell us it was cold, and took is not bought."],"hint":"Which part really happened, and which part was only a maybe?","why":"The precaution, taking the jacket, happened. In case only says cold was possible, not that it happened."},{"id":"w6l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"The bus leaves in ten minutes, and the app shows a small chance of storms on the walk."}],"stem":"I'm packing a second pair of socks now ___ my shoes get wet.","options":["if","in case","unless","as soon as"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["If would make the packing wait for wet shoes. She is packing now, before the walk.","Yes: she acts now because wet shoes are possible later. That is the umbrella test.","Unless means except if. Wet shoes are a reason to pack the socks, not to stop.","As soon as would make her wait until the shoes are wet. She is packing now."],"hint":"She acts now. Is the event certain, or only possible later?","why":"Fern packs before the walk because wet shoes are possible: a precaution, so in case."},{"id":"w6l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Notice on the school gate: ___ heavy rain, sports day will move to the hall.","options":["In case","In case of","In case that","In the case"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["In case needs a subject and a verb after it. Heavy rain is a noun phrase.","Yes: on a notice, in case of plus a noun means if there is.","In case that is not used. Before a noun, the notice form has of.","In the case would need of, and it talks about one example. It doesn't make a condition."],"hint":"What follows the gap: a subject and a verb, or a noun phrase?","why":"Heavy rain is a noun phrase, so the notice form in case of is needed. It means if there is."},{"id":"w6l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"[[Take]] a jumper to the exam tomorrow [[in case]] the hall [[will be]] cold [[again]].","answer":2,"fix":"is","fb":["This part is fine: an imperative gives the advice.","This part is fine: the jumper is a precaution, so in case fits.","Yes, this is the mistake: no will after in case. Use the present: in case the hall is cold.","This part is fine: again tells us it was cold before."],"hint":"Check the verb after in case. Which tense shows the future there?","why":"After in case, the present shows the future: in case the hall is cold, not will be."},{"id":"w6l3-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Save your essay now ___ off.","given":"Save your essay now because the power might go off.","keyword":"CASE","accept":["in case the power goes","in case the power should go"],"model":"in case the power goes","hint":"Because … might becomes the precaution phrase. Which tense follows it?","why":"Because it might happen is a precaution, so in case. After it, the present shows the future: goes."},{"id":"w6l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"I bought two sandwiches ___ hungry.","given":"I bought two sandwiches because I thought Ploy might be hungry.","keyword":"CASE","accept":["in case Ploy was","in case she was","in case Ploy got","in case she got"],"model":"in case Ploy was","hint":"The buying is in the past. Which tense follows the precaution phrase then?","why":"The precaution is in the past, so the verb after in case is past too: in case Ploy was hungry."}],"world":"w6"},"w6l4":{"id":"w6l4","name":"even if, even though, whether or not, otherwise, or (else)","cefr":"B2","ctype":"conn","formula":{"if":"even if + maybe / even though + fact / whether or not + either way","result":"the result does not change; otherwise / or (else) + what happens if not"},"uses":[{"use":"Showing determination","ex":"I'm going to the match even if I have to stand."},{"use":"Admitting a fact that changed nothing","ex":"Even though the queue was long, we waited for bubble tea."},{"use":"Saying it makes no difference","ex":"The trip is on whether or not it's sunny."},{"use":"A warning with otherwise","ex":"Leave now; otherwise you'll be stuck in traffic."},{"use":"A warning with or","ex":"Wear your helmet, or Dad won't let you ride."},{"use":"A promise with and","ex":"Help me carry these and I'll buy you an ice cream."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Even though is for a fact, even if is for a maybe, and otherwise and or mean if not.","body":["<b>Even if</b> and <b>even though</b> both say: this will not change the result. The difference is truth. Use the truth test. Is the thing a fact (really true)? Use even though. Is it only a maybe? Use even if. <em>Even though it rained yesterday</em>: it did rain. <em>Even if it rains tomorrow</em>: nobody knows yet.","After even if, use the present for the future, with no will: <em>even if it rains</em>. Even if can also be imagined, one step back: <em>Even if I had wings, I'd still be late.</em> Truth: I don't have wings. Never use even alone as a joining word. <s>Even it rains</s> is wrong.","<b>Whether or not</b> means: both answers give the same result. <em>I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join.</em> They join: I join. They don't join: I still join. You can also split it: <em>whether my friends join or not</em>. Use the present for the future here too.","<b>Otherwise</b> means \"if not\". It starts a new idea, so put a full stop or a semicolon before it. <em>Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.</em> That means: if you don't finish it now, you'll be up late. Otherwise can also start a new sentence, with a comma after it.","<b>Or</b> and <b>or else</b> do the same job after an order (an imperative): <em>Hurry up, or we'll miss the train.</em> That means: if you don't hurry, we'll miss it. Here a comma is enough. And is the opposite: <em>Hurry up and we'll catch it</em> means if you do."],"examples":[{"s":"Even if it rains tomorrow, we'll go to the market.","g":"Rain tomorrow is a maybe. It will not change the plan."},{"s":"Even though it rained yesterday, we went to the market.","g":"The rain was a fact. We went anyway."},{"s":"I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join.","g":"They join or they don't. Either way, I join."},{"s":"Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.","g":"Otherwise means: if you don't finish it now."},{"s":"Hurry up, or we'll miss the train.","g":"An order plus or means: if you don't hurry."}]},"B2":{"key":"Even though goes with a fact and even if with a maybe; whether or not means either way; otherwise and or mean if not.","body":["<b>Even if</b> and <b>even though</b> both say that something will not change the result. The truth test separates them. If the thing is a fact, use even though: <em>Even though it rained yesterday, we went to the market.</em> If it is only possible, or imagined, use even if: <em>Even if it rains tomorrow, we'll go.</em>","Even if follows the grammar of if: a present form for the future, with no will, and a past form for an imagined situation, as in <em>Even if I had wings, I'd still be late.</em> Truth: I have no wings. Even though can take any tense, because it reports a fact. Even on its own cannot join two clauses: <s>Even it rains</s> is wrong.","<b>Whether or not</b> means that both possibilities lead to the same result: <em>I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join.</em> The words or not can also go at the end: <em>whether my friends join or not</em>. It takes a present form for the future.","<b>Otherwise</b> means \"if not\", pointing back to what was just said. It begins a new clause, so it follows a full stop or a semicolon, not a comma alone: <em>Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.</em> In other words, if you don't finish it now, you'll be up late. At the start of a new sentence it is followed by a comma.","After an imperative, <b>or</b> and <b>or else</b> do the same job with just a comma: <em>Hurry up, or we'll miss the train</em> means \"if you don't hurry\". An imperative plus and is the positive twin: <em>Hurry up and we'll catch it</em> means \"if you do\". These are conditionals without if, and they are common in warnings and advice."],"examples":[{"s":"Even if it rains tomorrow, we'll go to the market.","g":"Truth test: tomorrow's rain is only possible, so even if. Present form, no will."},{"s":"Even though it rained yesterday, we went to the market.","g":"Truth test: it did rain, so even though. The fact didn't stop us."},{"s":"I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join.","g":"Both possibilities give the same result. Also: whether my friends join or not."},{"s":"Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.","g":"Otherwise means if not, after a semicolon or full stop."},{"s":"Hurry up, or we'll miss the train.","g":"Imperative plus or: a conditional without if, meaning if you don't hurry."}]},"C1":{"key":"Even if and whether or not say a condition makes no difference to the result; even though concedes a fact; otherwise and or state the negative condition without if.","body":["<b>Even if</b> and <b>even though</b> both say the result holds in spite of something. They differ in what they assume. Even though takes a fact for granted: <em>Even though it rained yesterday, we went to the market.</em> Even if leaves the matter open or imagined: <em>Even if it rains tomorrow, we'll go.</em> The truth test, fact or maybe, decides.","Even if is conditional, so it obeys the conditional rules: a present form for the future, no will, and one step back for an imagined case, as in <em>Even if I had wings, I'd still be late</em>; truth: no wings. Even though is not conditional and takes whatever tense the fact needs. Even by itself is an adverb, never a conjunction, so <s>Even it rains</s> fails.","<b>Whether or not</b> names both alternatives and declares that neither matters: <em>I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join</em>, or <em>whether my friends join or not</em>. It behaves like even if applied to both branches at once. Because the result is asserted in every case, <em>then</em> is not used after it, or after even if.","<b>Otherwise</b> is an adverb meaning \"if not\", so it cannot join clauses by itself. It needs a full stop or semicolon before it: <em>Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.</em> A comma alone is common in casual writing but is best avoided in exams. The result after it can be real, with will, or imagined, with would.","<b>Or</b> and <b>or else</b> after an imperative make a compact negative conditional: <em>Hurry up, or we'll miss the train.</em> With and, the same frame is a positive conditional used for promises and threats: <em>Hurry up and we'll catch it.</em> Much real conditional meaning in speech travels in these forms, with no if at all."],"examples":[{"s":"Even if it rains tomorrow, we'll go to the market.","g":"An open condition that makes no difference to the result. Present for the future."},{"s":"Even though it rained yesterday, we went to the market.","g":"Concession of a fact; not a conditional, so any tense the fact needs."},{"s":"I'm joining the club whether or not my friends join.","g":"Both branches are named and set aside; the result holds in each."},{"s":"Finish your homework now; otherwise you'll be up late.","g":"An adverb standing for the negative condition; hence the semicolon, not a comma alone."},{"s":"Hurry up, or we'll miss the train.","g":"Imperative plus or: a negative conditional with no if, typical of spoken warnings."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w6l4-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"Even though I revised all weekend, I'm nervous about the test."}],"stem":"Did Bam revise at the weekend?","options":["Maybe: nobody knows yet.","No: she only imagines it.","Yes, she did: it is a fact.","Not yet: it is her plan for later."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Maybe would need even if. Even though tells us it is true.","An imagined weekend would need even if and a step back. This is real.","Yes: even though introduces a fact. She revised, and she is still nervous.","Revised is past and real here. The weekend is over."],"hint":"Run the truth test on the first half.","why":"Even though introduces a fact, so Bam really revised. The fact just doesn't change her feeling."},{"id":"w6l4-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Coach","text":"The match starts at nine whether or not it's hot."}],"stem":"What happens if it is very hot?","options":["The match is cancelled.","The match still starts at nine.","The match moves to a cooler time.","The coach decides on the day."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["Whether or not means the heat changes nothing.","Yes: hot or not hot, the result is the same.","That would be a change. Whether or not says both cases give the same result.","The coach has already decided: nine, either way."],"hint":"Whether or not covers two cases. What happens in each?","why":"Whether or not means either way: hot or not, the match starts at nine."},{"id":"w6l4-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence means the same as this? <em>Set an alarm; otherwise you'll sleep through the first lesson.</em>","options":["If you set an alarm, you'll sleep through the first lesson.","Even though you set an alarm, you'll sleep through the first lesson.","Unless you sleep through the first lesson, set an alarm.","If you don't set an alarm, you'll sleep through the first lesson."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Otherwise means if not. This sentence blames the alarm for the sleeping.","Even though means the alarm makes no difference. The speaker thinks it does.","This mixes up the two halves: the alarm is the condition, sleeping is the result.","Yes: otherwise stands for if you don't do that."],"hint":"Otherwise points back to the first half. Does it mean if you do, or if you don't?","why":"Otherwise means if not: if you don't set an alarm, you'll sleep through the lesson."},{"id":"w6l4-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"The ticket prices come out on Friday. Nobody knows yet how much they'll be."}],"stem":"___ they cost a lot, I'm going to that concert.","options":["Even though","Even","Even if","Whether"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Even though needs a fact. The prices aren't known yet, so it is still a maybe.","Even alone can't join two clauses. It needs if or though.","Yes: the price is a maybe, and it won't change her plan.","Whether needs both possibilities named: whether or not they cost a lot."],"hint":"Truth test: is the price a fact yet, or still a maybe?","why":"The prices are unknown, so the condition is only possible: even if, not even though."},{"id":"w6l4-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"Praew was ill yesterday. She coughed all through the listening test."}],"stem":"She still got full marks ___ she had a terrible cold.","options":["even if","whether or not","otherwise","even though"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Even if is for a maybe. Fah knows Praew was ill: the cold was real.","Whether or not covers two possibilities. Here there is one known fact.","Otherwise means if not, and it starts a new clause about what happens then.","Yes: the cold was a fact, and it didn't stop her."],"hint":"Truth test: do we know she had a cold?","why":"The cold really happened, so the fact word is needed: even though."},{"id":"w6l4-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"I [[am going]] to finish this drawing tonight [[even if]] it [[will take]] me [[until]] midnight.","answer":2,"fix":"takes","fb":["This part is fine: am going to shows her plan.","This part is fine: how long it takes is a maybe, so even if fits.","Yes, this is the mistake: no will after even if. The present shows the future: it takes.","This part is fine: until midnight gives the time limit."],"hint":"Even if follows the same tense rule as if.","why":"After even if, the present shows the future: even if it takes me until midnight."},{"id":"w6l4-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Put your phone on silent, ___ the teacher will take it away.","options":["and","so","or","unless"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["And after an order means if you do. Silence doesn't make the teacher take the phone.","So gives a result of doing it. The teacher takes the phone if you don't.","Yes: an order plus or means if you don't do it, this will happen.","Unless would need a present verb after it, not will, and the meaning gets tangled."],"hint":"Does the second half happen if you do it, or if you don't?","why":"An imperative plus or means if you don't: if you don't silence it, the teacher will take it."},{"id":"w6l4-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"The school fair is on this Saturday ___ rains.","given":"Rain or no rain, the school fair is on this Saturday.","keyword":"WHETHER","accept":["whether or not it"],"model":"whether or not it","hint":"Either way, same result. Build the phrase around the key word, then add a subject.","why":"Whether or not means either way. It is followed by a clause in the present: it rains."}],"world":"w6"},"w6l5":{"id":"w6l5","name":"suppose / supposing, what if, imagine, assuming (that); when vs if","cefr":"B2","ctype":"conn","formula":{"if":"Suppose / Supposing / What if / Imagine + present (real possibility), past (imagined), had + V3 (imagined past)","result":"What will you do? / What would you do? / What would you have done?"},"uses":[{"use":"Worrying about a real possibility","ex":"What if the shop is closed when we get there?"},{"use":"Making a gentle suggestion","ex":"What if we met at the BTS station instead?"},{"use":"Daydreaming","ex":"Imagine you had your own café. What would you sell?"},{"use":"Looking back at a past that didn't happen","ex":"Suppose you had lost your ticket. What would you have done?"},{"use":"Planning on something you expect","ex":"Assuming the rain stops, we'll walk to the market."},{"use":"Sure or maybe","ex":"When I get home, I'll call you. If I'm late, start without me."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"After suppose, what if and imagine, the present is real and the past is imagined; when is sure, if is maybe.","body":["<b>Suppose</b>, <b>supposing</b>, <b>what if</b> and <b>imagine</b> invite someone to picture a situation. The verb after them shows how real it is. Use the present for a real possibility: <em>What if the bus doesn't come?</em> It could really happen. Do not use will or would after these words.","Use the past for an imagined situation. This is one step back from reality: past form, not past time. <em>Imagine you could fly.</em> Truth: you can't. With what if, the past also makes a gentle suggestion: <em>What if we went by boat instead?</em> It is softer than \"Let's go by boat\".","Use had plus the past participle (V3) for an imagined past. This is two steps back. <em>Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?</em> Truth: you didn't miss it. The question after it matches: will for real, would for imagined, would have for an imagined past.","<b>Assuming (that)</b> means \"if this is true, and I think it is\". Use it to plan. <em>Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.</em> Use the present after it for the future. Supposing and assuming can take <b>that</b>, or not.","<b>When</b> or <b>if</b>? Use when for something you are sure will happen. Use if for a maybe. <em>When I get home, I'll call you</em>: I will get home. <em>If I get home early, I'll call you</em>: perhaps I won't be early. Both take the present for the future."],"examples":[{"s":"What if the bus doesn't come?","g":"Present: a real worry. It could really happen."},{"s":"What if we went by boat instead?","g":"Past form, not past time. A gentle idea. Truth: we haven't chosen the boat."},{"s":"Imagine you could fly. Where would you go?","g":"One step back: can becomes could. Truth: you can't fly."},{"s":"Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?","g":"Two steps back for an imagined past. Truth: you didn't miss the flight."},{"s":"Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.","g":"I think the tickets will arrive, so I plan on it. Present after assuming."}]},"B2":{"key":"Suppose, supposing, what if and imagine take the present for a real possibility, the past for an imagined one and had plus V3 for an imagined past; when is for the expected, if for the possible.","body":["<b>Suppose</b>, <b>supposing</b>, <b>what if</b> and <b>imagine</b> invite the listener to picture a situation. The verb form shows how real the speaker thinks it is. A present form means a real possibility, often a worry: <em>What if the bus doesn't come?</em> Will and would are not used after these words.","A past form means the situation is imagined: one step back from reality, past form but not past time. <em>Imagine you could fly.</em> Truth: you can't. With what if, the past form also makes a tentative suggestion: <em>What if we went by boat instead?</em> is softer and more polite than a direct proposal.","For an imagined past, step back twice: had plus the past participle (V3). <em>Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?</em> Truth: you didn't miss it. The question that follows matches the scene: will for a real possibility, would for an imagined one, would have for an imagined past.","<b>Assuming (that)</b> is different in attitude: the speaker treats the condition as probably true and plans on it. <em>Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.</em> It takes a present form for the future. Supposing and assuming can both be followed by that.","<b>When</b> and <b>if</b> differ in certainty, not in grammar. When is for an event the speaker expects; if is for one that may or may not happen. <em>When I get home, I'll call you</em> takes getting home for granted. <em>If I get home early, I'll call you</em> does not. Both use a present form for the future."],"examples":[{"s":"What if the bus doesn't come?","g":"Present form: a real possibility. No will after what if."},{"s":"What if we went by boat instead?","g":"Past form for a tentative suggestion. Truth: we haven't decided to go by boat; it is only an idea."},{"s":"Imagine you could fly. Where would you go?","g":"Imagined now: can steps back to could, and the question uses would. Truth: you can't fly."},{"s":"Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?","g":"Imagined past: had plus V3, then would have. Truth: you didn't miss the flight."},{"s":"Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.","g":"The speaker expects the condition to be true and plans on it. Present form for the future."}]},"C1":{"key":"These openers set up a scenario, and the tense after them shows the speaker's stance: open possibility, imagined, or imagined past.","body":["<b>Suppose</b>, <b>supposing</b>, <b>what if</b> and <b>imagine</b> set up a scenario and leave the result to a following question or to the listener. The tense carries the speaker's stance. A present form treats the scenario as an open possibility: <em>What if the bus doesn't come?</em> As after if, will and would for a plain future are kept out of the scenario clause.","A past form marks distance from reality, not past time: <em>Imagine you could fly</em>; truth: you can't. The same distance works socially. <em>What if we went by boat instead?</em> is a suggestion made tentative by the step back, which leaves the listener free to refuse. The choice is a matter of stance, not of probability. After imagine and suppose, speakers sometimes keep the present even for an unreal scene; the past form marks the distance clearly, and it is the form exams expect.","An imagined past needs both steps, had plus the past participle (V3): <em>Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?</em> Truth: you didn't miss it. The follow-up clause agrees with the scenario, so will, would and would have line up with the open, the imagined and the imagined past.","<b>Assuming (that)</b> signals that the speaker accepts the condition as a working premise: <em>Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.</em> In confidence it sits between when and if. It takes a present form for the future, and like supposing it may keep or drop that.","<b>When</b> and <b>if</b> share their grammar and differ in commitment. When presents the event as expected; if presents it as open. Compare <em>When I get home, I'll call you</em> with <em>If I get home early, I'll call you</em>. In general truths the two nearly merge, but for a single future event the choice tells the listener how sure you are."],"examples":[{"s":"What if the bus doesn't come?","g":"Open possibility; the present form carries the future meaning."},{"s":"What if we went by boat instead?","g":"The step back adds social distance: a suggestion that is easy to refuse. Truth: nothing is decided; the boat is only an idea."},{"s":"Imagine you could fly. Where would you go?","g":"Imagined scenario with a matching would question. Truth: you can't fly."},{"s":"Suppose you had missed the flight. What would you have done?","g":"Imagined past scenario with a would have question. Truth: you caught the flight."},{"s":"Assuming the tickets arrive tomorrow, we'll meet at six.","g":"A working premise the speaker accepts; present form, no will."}]}},"thai":"สมมติว่า = suppose, imagine, what if.","items":[{"id":"w6l5-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"I'll message you when I finish my homework."},{"who":"Fern","text":"I'll message you if I finish my homework."}],"stem":"Who is sure that she will finish her homework?","options":["Fern","Jane","Both of them","Neither of them"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["If shows doubt: Fern may not finish.","Yes: she uses the word for something the speaker expects to happen.","Only one of them uses the word for a sure event. Compare the two connectors.","One of them is sure. Which connector shows that the event is expected?"],"hint":"One connector is for a sure event and one is for a maybe.","why":"When shows the speaker expects the event; if shows it is only possible. So Jane is the sure one."},{"id":"w6l5-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"We'll never finish the science project by Friday."},{"who":"Mook","text":"What if we asked Kru Ann for one more day?"}],"stem":"What is Mook doing?","options":["Describing what they did last week.","Suggesting an idea in a gentle way.","Worrying about a real danger.","Saying that Kru Ann has agreed."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The past form asked is not about past time here. It is one step back from reality.","Yes: what if plus a past form makes a soft, careful suggestion.","A worry would use the present: What if she says no?","Nobody has asked Kru Ann yet. It is only an idea."],"hint":"The verb is past, but is the time past?","why":"What if plus a past form is a tentative suggestion: past form, not past time.","truth":"They haven't asked Kru Ann. It is only an idea."},{"id":"w6l5-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Aunt","text":"Nan, supposing you had left your phone on the BTS last night, what would you have done?"}],"stem":"What is true about Nan's phone?","options":["She left it on the BTS last night.","She may well leave it on the BTS later tonight.","She didn't leave it on the BTS last night.","She has never taken it on the BTS."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Had left after supposing is two steps back: an imagined past, not a real one.","The time is last night, and the form is for an imagined past, not a future worry.","Yes: supposing plus had left imagines a past that didn't happen.","The sentence says nothing about that. It only imagines last night."],"hint":"Two steps back after supposing: real past or imagined past?","why":"Supposing plus had and the past participle imagines a different past, so in reality it didn't happen.","truth":"Nan didn't leave her phone on the BTS last night."},{"id":"w6l5-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"Look at those dark clouds, and the school fair starts in an hour."}],"stem":"What if it ___ during the fair?","options":["will rain","rains","would rain","had rained"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["No will after what if. The present shows the future.","Yes: a real worry about a real possibility takes the present.","Would doesn't belong after what if here. For an imagined idea, use a past form.","Had rained imagines a past. The fair hasn't started yet."],"hint":"Real possibility or imagined? Then check the tense rule after if.","why":"Dark clouds make rain a real possibility in the future, so what if takes the present: rains."},{"id":"w6l5-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"Sadly, our school has no swimming pool."}],"stem":"What if we ___ one? We could swim every lunchtime!","options":["have","will have","would have","had"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The school has no pool, so this is imagined. Imagined needs one step back.","No will after what if, and this is not a real plan.","Would goes in the result, not after what if.","Yes: one step back for something that is not true now. Past form, not past time."],"hint":"Is it true now? If not, how many steps back?","why":"The school has no pool, so the situation is imagined now: one step back, have becomes had.","truth":"The school has no swimming pool now."},{"id":"w6l5-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Imagine you [[have lost]] your voice [[before]] the speech contest last month. [[What]] [[would you have done]]?","answer":0,"fix":"had lost","fb":["Yes, this is the mistake: an imagined past needs two steps back: had lost.","This part is fine: before the contest gives the time.","This part is fine: what starts the question about the result.","This part is fine: would have done is the right result for an imagined past."],"hint":"Last month, and it didn't happen. How many steps back?","why":"An imagined past after imagine needs had plus the past participle: had lost, not have lost."},{"id":"w6l5-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Assuming that everyone ___ (be) on time tomorrow, the bus will leave at seven.","accept":["is"],"model":"is","hint":"Which tense shows the future after a connector such as assuming?","why":"After assuming (that), the present shows the future: everyone is on time, with no will."},{"id":"w6l5-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Cats can't talk, sadly. But suppose you ___ (can) talk to your cat. What would you ask her?","accept":["could","were able to","was able to"],"model":"could","hint":"Not true now, so one step back on the verb in brackets.","why":"Talking to a cat is imagined, so can steps back once to could. The question uses would for the same reason.","truth":"You can't talk to your cat."}],"world":"w6"},"w7l1":{"id":"w7l1","name":"wish / if only + past: wishes about now","cefr":"B2","ctype":"wish","gateway":"p.77","formula":{"if":"I wish / If only + subject","result":"past simple (had, knew, didn't …) · were · could + verb"},"uses":[{"use":"Wanting something you don't have","ex":"I wish I had a bigger desk."},{"use":"Wanting an ability","ex":"I wish I could draw like you."},{"use":"Grumbling about how things are now","ex":"If only this bus came more often!"},{"use":"Missing someone","ex":"I wish you were here with us."},{"use":"Saying no kindly","ex":"I wish I could help, but I have tutoring tonight."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Use <b>wish</b> or <b>if only</b> with a past form to talk about something now that you want to be different.","body":["Sometimes you are not happy with something now. You want it to be different. In English you say <b>I wish</b>, and then you move the verb one step back. Have becomes had. Know becomes knew. Can becomes could. Am, is and are become were. So a girl with no cat says: I wish I had a cat.","The past form here is not about past time. It is about now. It means: this is not real. Every wish has a Truth (the real fact), and the Truth is the opposite. I wish I had a cat. Truth: I don't have a cat, now. I wish I didn't have a cough. Truth: I have a cough, now. A positive wish hides a negative Truth. A negative wish hides a positive Truth.","Be careful with three mistakes. First, never use a present form after wish: <s>I wish I have</s> is wrong. Second, never use will or can after wish. Say could, not can. Third, do not use would for your own situation: <s>I wish I would know her name</s> is wrong. For what you have, know, are or can do, use the simple past form: I wish I knew her name.","For something happening right now, step back from are doing to were doing. They are shouting, so you say: I wish they weren't shouting. After I, he, she and it, careful writers use were. Many people say was when they speak. Both are fine.","<b>If only</b> means the same as I wish, but the feeling is stronger. It uses the same past forms. It can stand alone, often with an exclamation mark: If only I could remember! Do not mix it up with only if, which gives a condition (something that must happen first). This is the same step back as the second conditional: If I had a cat, I would be happy."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had a cat.","g":"Truth: I don't have a cat — now. Have steps back to had."},{"s":"If only I didn't have so much homework!","g":"Truth: I have a lot of homework — now. A negative wish, a positive Truth. If only is stronger than I wish."},{"s":"I wish I could play the guitar.","g":"Truth: I can't play the guitar — now. Can steps back to could."},{"s":"I wish you were here.","g":"Truth: you aren't here — now. Are steps back to were."},{"s":"<s>I wish I have a cat.</s>","g":"Wrong: a present form after wish. Say: I wish I had a cat."}]},"B2":{"key":"<b>Wish</b> and <b>if only</b> take a past form to show that a present situation is not the way you want it: one step back from reality.","body":["When you are unhappy with the way things are now, English uses <b>I wish</b> followed by a verb that has moved one step back: have becomes had, know becomes knew, can becomes could, and am, is or are become were. A student who cannot remember a new classmate's name says: I wish I knew her name. The past form does not point to past time. It is the distance past of the second conditional, and it signals that the idea is imagined, not real.","Because the wish is imagined, the Truth is its opposite, and the Truth is dated now. I wish I had a cat: Truth, I don't have one. I wish I didn't have a cough: Truth, I have one. Notice that the polarity flips, so a positive wish hides a negative fact and a negative wish hides a positive fact. For an ability, use could: I wish I could play the guitar means that you can't.","Three errors are common. A present form after wish, as in <s>I wish I have</s>, is the classic one for Thai learners, because Thai does not change the verb. Will and can are also impossible after wish. Finally, would is not used for your own situation or for a state: say I wish I knew her name, not <s>I wish I would know her name</s>. Wish with would has a special job, which you will meet two lessons from now.","For an action in progress, step back from the present continuous to the past continuous: they are shouting, so I wish they weren't shouting. With I, he, she and it, were is the careful choice in writing and was is normal in speech, so neither is treated as a mistake here.","<b>If only</b> expresses the same wish with more feeling, and takes exactly the same forms. It needs no result clause: If only I could remember! is a complete sentence. Keep it apart from only if, which states a necessary condition and has nothing to do with wishing."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had a cat.","g":"Truth: I don't have a cat — now. One step back: have becomes had."},{"s":"If only I didn't have so much homework!","g":"Truth: I have a lot of homework — now. The polarity flips, and if only adds feeling."},{"s":"I wish I could play the guitar.","g":"Truth: I can't play it — now. Can becomes could; never can after wish."},{"s":"I wish you were here.","g":"Truth: you aren't here — now. Are becomes were."},{"s":"<s>I wish I have a cat.</s>","g":"Wrong: no step back. A present form after wish says it is real. Correct: I wish I had a cat."}]},"C1":{"key":"After <b>wish</b> and <b>if only</b>, a past form marks distance from reality, not from the present moment, so the situation described is contrary to present fact.","body":["English past forms do more than report past time. After <b>wish</b> they express remoteness from reality: the speaker presents a situation as not being the case now. The verb therefore moves one step back, on the staircase of the second conditional and, for a different reason, of reported speech: have to had, know to knew, can to could, will to would, am or is to were. I wish I knew her name is entirely about the present; nothing in it refers to an earlier time.","Each wish carries an implied opposite fact, the Truth, dated now. I wish I had a cat presupposes that I have none; I wish I didn't have a cough presupposes that I have one. The polarity always reverses. Could expresses a missing ability or opportunity: I wish I could play the guitar. This is why wish belongs with the imagined conditional: If I had a cat, I'd be happier and I wish I had a cat rest on the same Truth.","The typical learner error is to leave the verb in the present, <s>I wish I have</s>, or to use will or can, since many languages, Thai included, mark a wish with a separate word and leave the verb alone. A second error is would for the speaker's own circumstances or for a state: <s>I wish I would know her name</s>. Would after wish is reserved for another person's willingness to behave differently, which is treated later on this island.","Continuous meaning steps back in the same way, from are shouting to weren't shouting. With a first or third person singular subject, were is the traditional form and the safe one in formal writing, while was is normal in conversation; the two differ in register, not in correctness. One exception to notice: wish followed by a to-infinitive, as in I wish to make a complaint, is simply a formal way of saying want, and involves no step back.","<b>If only</b> is the emphatic counterpart of I wish. It takes identical forms, usually stands alone as an exclamation and needs no result clause, though one can be added: If only I could remember her name, I could introduce you. It must not be confused with only if, which introduces a necessary condition."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had a cat.","g":"Truth: I have no cat — now. The past form marks distance from reality, not past time."},{"s":"If only I didn't have so much homework!","g":"Truth: I have a great deal of homework — now. Reversed polarity; if only is the emphatic form."},{"s":"I wish I could play the guitar.","g":"Truth: I can't play it — now. Could is the remote form of can, for a missing ability."},{"s":"I wish you were here.","g":"Truth: you aren't here — now. Were is the remote form of are."},{"s":"<s>I wish I have a cat.</s>","g":"Wrong: the present form fails to mark the wish as unreal. Correct: I wish I had a cat."}]}},"thai":"ถอยหลังหนึ่งก้าว = ถอยห่างจากความจริง: I wish I had a cat. ความจริงคือ ตอนนี้ไม่มีแมว","items":[{"id":"w7l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"I wish I lived nearer the school."}],"stem":"What is true about Fah?","options":["She lives further from school than she wants.","She lived near the school in the past.","She lives near the school at the moment.","She is going to move nearer the school soon."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the wish is the opposite of the Truth, and the Truth is dated now.","The past form \"lived\" is not past time here. After wish it means not real now.","That is what she wants, not what is true. Flip the wish to find the Truth.","Nothing in the sentence is a plan. A wish with a past form is imagined, not expected."],"hint":"The past form after wish: is it about past time, or about distance from what is real?","why":"After wish, a past form means not real now, so the Truth is the opposite: Fah doesn't live as near the school as she wants.","truth":"Fah lives further from the school than she wants — now."},{"id":"w7l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"It's pouring, we're outside the BTS station, and neither of us has an umbrella."}],"stem":"Which wish fits Nan's situation?","options":["I wish we had an umbrella.","I wish we didn't have an umbrella.","I wish it rained more often.","I wish we weren't indoors."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the Truth is negative (no umbrella), so the wish is positive, one step back.","The Truth is that they have no umbrella. A negative Truth needs a positive wish.","It is already raining, and that is the problem. A wish asks for the opposite of the Truth.","They are outside, not indoors. Check the Truth before you flip it."],"hint":"Write the Truth first, then flip it.","why":"The Truth is negative (they have no umbrella now), so the wish is positive, with have one step back: had.","truth":"They don't have an umbrella — now."},{"id":"w7l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Praew","text":"If only I knew the answer to question five!"}],"stem":"How is Praew's sentence different from \"I wish I knew the answer to question five\"?","options":["Same wish, with a stronger feeling","Same wish, but about the past","It gives a condition, like \"only if\"","It means Praew knows the answer"],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: if only is I wish with more feeling, and it takes the same past form.","Both sentences are about now. Knew is one step back from reality, not past time.","Only if gives a condition. If only, in that order, is a wish.","The Truth is the opposite: she doesn't know the answer now."],"hint":"The verb form is the same in both. So what changes: the time, the Truth, or the feeling?","why":"If only and I wish take the same forms and have the same Truth; if only simply sounds stronger.","truth":"Praew doesn't know the answer — now."},{"id":"w7l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"This game keeps freezing. My phone is five years old!"}],"stem":"I wish I ___ a newer one.","options":["have","had","will have","would have"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A present form after wish says it is real. Mook's phone is old, so step back once.","Yes: have steps back to had. Truth: she doesn't have a newer phone now.","Will never follows wish. This is not a real future; it is an imagined now.","Would after wish is not for your own situation, or for a state like having something. Use the simple past form."],"hint":"Is this real now? If not, how many steps back?","why":"Mook doesn't have a newer phone now, so have takes one step back to had; wish never takes a present form or will.","truth":"Mook doesn't have a newer phone — now."},{"id":"w7l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"We've lost every doubles match this term."}],"stem":"I wish we ___ better at doubles.","options":["are","were","would be","will be"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A present form says it is true now. Sadly it is not, so step back once.","Yes: are steps back to were. Truth: they aren't good at doubles now.","Would is not used after wish for your own situation, or for a state such as being good at something.","Will never follows wish. Step back from the real form."],"hint":"What is the Truth now? Then move the verb one step back.","why":"The Truth is that they are not good at doubles now, so are takes one step back to were.","truth":"They aren't good at doubles — now."},{"id":"w7l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"[[If only]] our classroom [[has]] air conditioning! [[It is]] so hot in here [[today]].","answer":1,"fix":"had","fb":["If only is fine: it starts a strong wish.","Yes, this is the error: a present form after if only. The classroom has no air conditioning now, so step back to had.","It is so hot is the real situation, so the present form is right.","Today is the time cue: the wish is about now."],"hint":"Which verb describes something that is not real now?","why":"After if only, the verb for the imagined situation steps back: has becomes had. The second sentence is real, so it stays present.","truth":"The classroom doesn't have air conditioning — now."},{"id":"w7l1-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Ploy lives next to a building site. The noise starts at seven every morning."}],"stem":"Ploy: If only we ___ (not live) next to a building site!","accept":["didn't live","did not live","weren't living","were not living"],"model":"didn't live","hint":"The Truth is positive and dated now. Flip it and step back once.","why":"The Truth is that they live there now, so the wish is negative and one step back: didn't live.","truth":"Ploy's family lives next to a building site — now."},{"id":"w7l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Mint can't speak Korean, so she needs subtitles for every video of her favourite K-pop group."}],"stem":"Mint: I wish I ___ (can / speak) Korean.","accept":["could speak"],"model":"could speak","hint":"Which of the two verbs steps back, and what does it become?","why":"Mint can't speak Korean now, so can takes one step back to could, and speak stays in the base form.","truth":"Mint can't speak Korean — now."}],"world":"w7"},"w7l2":{"id":"w7l2","name":"wish / if only + past perfect: regrets","cefr":"B2","ctype":"wish","gateway":"p.77","formula":{"if":"I wish / If only + subject","result":"had / hadn't + past participle (V3)"},"uses":[{"use":"Regretting something you did","ex":"I wish I hadn't spent all my pocket money on stickers."},{"use":"Regretting something you didn't do","ex":"If only I'd set an alarm!"},{"use":"Gentle criticism of someone else","ex":"I wish you'd told me earlier."},{"use":"Disappointment about what happened","ex":"I wish you had been there. The concert was amazing."},{"use":"Learning from a mistake","ex":"I wish I'd read the question properly. Next time I will."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Use <b>wish</b> or <b>if only</b> with <b>had</b> and the past participle (V3) for a regret: something in the past that you want to change but can't.","body":["A regret is a sad feeling about the past. Something happened, or did not happen, and you cannot change it now. For a wish about now, you step back once. For a wish about the past, you step back twice. Went becomes had gone. Forgot becomes had forgotten. So the form is <b>had</b> and the past participle (V3): I wish I had brought an umbrella.","The Truth (the real fact) is the opposite again, but now it has a past date. I wish I had brought an umbrella. Truth: I didn't bring one, this morning. If only I hadn't left my homework at home! Truth: I left it at home. So ask two questions. Did it happen? When? A past time word, like yesterday or last night, tells you to use had and V3.","In speech, had is often short: I wish I'd known. After wish, the short form before a V3 is always had. It is not would. You can also wish about another person's past action: I wish you had reminded me about the test. Truth: you didn't remind me.","Watch three mistakes. The simple past is for wishes about now, so it is wrong for the past: <s>I wish I studied harder last term</s>. Have and V3 is wrong too. You need had. And do not put would have after wish. Keep would have for the result half of a third conditional.","A regret with wish means almost the same as <b>should have</b>. I should have gone to bed earlier means I wish I had gone to bed earlier. I shouldn't have said that means I wish I hadn't said that. Should have sounds like advice to yourself. Wish shows the feeling. If only shows a stronger feeling."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had brought an umbrella.","g":"Truth: I didn't bring one — this morning. Two steps back: had and V3."},{"s":"If only I hadn't left my homework at home!","g":"Truth: I left it at home — this morning. A negative wish, a positive Truth."},{"s":"I wish you had reminded me about the test.","g":"Truth: you didn't remind me — last week. A wish about another person's past action."},{"s":"I wish I'd gone to bed earlier.","g":"Truth: I went to bed late — last night. The short form is had. Same idea: I should have gone to bed earlier."},{"s":"<s>I wish I studied harder last term.</s>","g":"Wrong: only one step back, but last term is past. Say: I wish I had studied harder last term."}]},"B2":{"key":"<b>Wish</b> and <b>if only</b> with the past perfect express regret: the Truth is a past fact that can no longer be changed.","body":["A wish about now steps back once, to the past simple. A wish about the past has to step back twice, because the first step is already used up by past time: went becomes had gone, brought becomes had brought. The form is therefore <b>had</b> and the past participle (V3), the same form as the if half of a third conditional: I wish I had brought an umbrella.","The Truth is still the opposite of the wish, but it now carries a past date. I wish I had brought an umbrella: Truth, I didn't bring one this morning. If only I hadn't left my homework at home: Truth, I left it there. To choose between the two kinds of wish, ask when the Truth is dated. A past time marker such as yesterday, last night or this morning calls for had and V3.","In speech had usually contracts: I wish I'd known. After wish, the contraction before a past participle is always had, never would. The regret can also be about what someone else did or failed to do: I wish you had reminded me about the test means that you didn't remind me, and can sound like a gentle criticism.","Three wrong forms appear often. The past simple belongs to wishes about now, so <s>I wish I studied harder last term</s> does not work in careful English. Have and V3 is not far enough back; you need had. And would have does not follow wish in standard British English or in exams: it belongs in the result half of a third conditional.","A regret with wish is very close to <b>should have</b>. I should have gone to bed earlier and I wish I had gone to bed earlier describe the same Truth, and I shouldn't have said that matches I wish I hadn't said that. Should have judges the action; wish expresses the feeling; if only makes that feeling stronger. Keep regrets small and useful: say what you will do next time."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had brought an umbrella.","g":"Truth: I didn't bring one — this morning. Past perfect for a past Truth."},{"s":"If only I hadn't left my homework at home!","g":"Truth: I left it at home — this morning. The polarity flips; if only is stronger."},{"s":"I wish you had reminded me about the test.","g":"Truth: you didn't remind me — last week. With another subject it can sound like gentle criticism."},{"s":"I wish I'd gone to bed earlier.","g":"Truth: I went to bed late — last night. The contraction is had; compare I should have gone to bed earlier."},{"s":"<s>I wish I studied harder last term.</s>","g":"Wrong: the past simple is for wishes about now. Correct: I wish I had studied harder last term."}]},"C1":{"key":"With the past perfect, <b>wish</b> and <b>if only</b> present a past situation as contrary to fact: remoteness in reality added to remoteness in time.","body":["The past perfect after <b>wish</b> combines two kinds of distance. One step back marks past time; the second marks unreality. English has no separate inflection for a past of the past, so the perfect does the work: brought becomes had brought, didn't leave becomes hadn't left. The clause is built exactly like the condition of a third conditional, and the two share one Truth: I wish I had brought an umbrella; if I had brought one, I wouldn't have got wet.","The implied fact is the reverse of the wish and is dated in the past. I wish I had brought an umbrella presupposes that I didn't; if only I hadn't left my homework at home presupposes that I did. The decision between past simple and past perfect therefore depends on the date of the Truth, not on the date of the feeling: the regret is felt now, but the fact it reverses is past.","Had contracts freely, as in I wish I'd known, and after wish a contraction before a past participle can only be had. With a different subject the structure reports disappointment or mild reproach: I wish you had reminded me about the test. A missed ability or opportunity uses could have: I wish I could have stayed longer.","Three forms are to be avoided. The past simple with a past time reference, <s>I wish I studied harder last term</s>, is heard in casual speech but is not accepted in exams. The present perfect, have and V3, takes no step back at all. Would have after wish is common in some American speech, yet it is non-standard and marked wrong in formal writing: would have belongs to the result clause.","Regret with wish runs parallel to <b>should have</b>. I should have gone to bed earlier evaluates the action; I wish I had gone to bed earlier reports the feeling about the same fact; if only intensifies it. Polarity follows the same pattern, so shouldn't have said pairs with wish I hadn't said. Since a wished-for past cannot be changed, the natural continuation is a plan: next time I will."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish I had brought an umbrella.","g":"Truth: I didn't bring one — this morning. The perfect adds unreality to past time."},{"s":"If only I hadn't left my homework at home!","g":"Truth: I left it at home — this morning. Reversed polarity, emphatic form."},{"s":"I wish you had reminded me about the test.","g":"Truth: you didn't remind me — last week. With a different subject: mild reproach."},{"s":"I wish I'd gone to bed earlier.","g":"Truth: I went to bed late — last night. Contracted had; parallel to I should have gone to bed earlier."},{"s":"<s>I wish I studied harder last term.</s>","g":"Not accepted in exams: a past Truth needs the past perfect. Correct: I wish I had studied harder last term."}]}},"thai":"ความจริงคือ ตอนนั้นไม่ได้เอาร่มมา: I wish I had brought an umbrella.","items":[{"id":"w7l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"I wish I'd taken the earlier train."}],"stem":"What is true?","options":["Nan didn't take the earlier train.","Nan took the earlier train.","Nan would like to take the earlier train tomorrow.","Nan takes the earlier train every day."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: had taken is two steps back, so the Truth is the opposite and it is dated in the past.","That is the wish, not the Truth. Flip it.","The short form comes before a V3 (taken), so it is had, not would. The sentence is a regret about the past.","A habit would be a real present. Had taken points to one past moment that did not happen."],"hint":"What is the full form of the contraction before a V3? Then flip the wish.","why":"I'd taken is I had taken: a wish about the past, so the Truth is that Nan did not take that train.","truth":"Nan didn't take the earlier train — earlier today."},{"id":"w7l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence is a regret about the past, not a wish about now?","options":["If only I hadn't stayed up so late.","If only I didn't feel so sleepy.","If only I could go back to bed.","If only this lesson finished earlier."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: hadn't and V3 is two steps back. Truth: I stayed up late, last night.","Didn't feel is a past form, but only one step back. Its Truth is dated now: I feel sleepy.","Could is one step back from can. Truth: I can't go back to bed, now.","Finished is one step back. Truth: the lesson finishes late, as things are now."],"hint":"Count the steps back in each verb. Which Truth has a past date?","why":"Only hadn't stayed is two steps back, so only that Truth is dated in the past; the others are wishes about now.","truth":"I stayed up late — last night."},{"id":"w7l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"I shouldn't have bought these shoes online. They're too small."}],"stem":"Which wish means the same?","options":["I wish I hadn't bought them online.","I wish I had bought them online.","I wish I could buy them online.","I wish I bought shoes online more often."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: shouldn't have bought and wish I hadn't bought share one Truth: she bought them online.","Wrong way round. She did buy them online; that is what she regrets.","Could buy is a wish about now. Mook's problem is something she already did.","That is a wish about a habit now, and the opposite of her feeling."],"hint":"What did Mook do? Is she happy about it?","why":"She bought them online and regrets it, so the wish is negative and about the past: hadn't bought.","truth":"Mook bought the shoes online — in the past."},{"id":"w7l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"The concert tickets went on sale at nine yesterday morning and sold out in ten minutes. I was in the shower!"}],"stem":"If only I ___ the website at nine!","options":["checked","had checked","have checked","would check"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Checked is only one step back, for a wish about now. Nine o'clock yesterday is past, so step back twice.","Yes: had and V3 for a regret. Truth: she didn't check the website at nine yesterday.","Have and V3 takes no step back at all. A regret needs had.","Would after if only is for someone else's behaviour, and it is not about the past."],"hint":"When is the Truth dated: now or yesterday? How many steps back is that?","why":"The moment is over (nine yesterday) and Pim did not check, so the verb goes two steps back: had checked.","truth":"Pim didn't check the website at nine — yesterday."},{"id":"w7l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"You told Mum about the surprise party last night! Now it isn't a surprise."}],"stem":"I wish you ___ her.","options":["didn't tell","hadn't told","haven't told","wouldn't have told"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["One step back is for a wish about now. Last night is past, so you need had.","Yes: hadn't and V3. Truth: you told her, last night.","Haven't told is a present perfect: no step back at all. A regret about last night needs had.","Would have belongs in the result half of a third conditional, not after wish."],"hint":"The telling is finished. Which form takes a past fact and makes it unreal?","why":"She told Mum last night, so the wish reverses a past fact: two steps back and negative, hadn't told.","truth":"She told Mum about the party — last night."},{"id":"w7l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"My serve [[is still]] terrible. I wish I [[have listened]] to the coach [[when]] she explained it [[last week]].","answer":1,"fix":"had listened","fb":["Is still describes the real present, so the present form is right.","Yes, this is the error: have and V3 takes no step back. A regret about last week needs had listened.","When is fine: it joins the listening to the coach's real past action, explained.","Last week is the past time cue that calls for had and V3."],"hint":"Find the verb for the thing that did not happen. Is it far enough back?","why":"The regret is about last week, so wish needs had and V3: had listened. Have listened is not a step back.","truth":"She didn't listen to the coach — last week."},{"id":"w7l2-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Fah didn't charge her phone last night. Now it's dead and she can't pay for her bubble tea."}],"stem":"Fah: I wish I ___ (charge) my phone last night.","accept":["had charged","'d charged"],"model":"had charged","hint":"Last night is finished. How many steps back for a past Truth?","why":"Fah didn't charge it last night, so the wish reverses a past fact: had and the past participle, had charged.","truth":"Fah didn't charge her phone — last night."},{"id":"w7l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","given":"I should have looked at the timetable before I left home.","keyword":"WISH","stem":"I ___ at the timetable before I left home.","accept":["wish I had looked","wish I'd looked","wish that I had looked","wish that I'd looked"],"model":"wish I had looked","hint":"Should have and a regret share one Truth. Which form follows the key word for a past fact?","why":"Should have looked means she didn't look, so the regret is wish, then had and V3: I wish I had looked.","truth":"She didn't look at the timetable — before she left home."}],"world":"w7"},"w7l3":{"id":"w7l3","name":"wish / if only + would: annoying habits; wish vs hope","cefr":"B2","ctype":"wish","gateway":"p.77","formula":{"if":"I wish / If only + another person or thing","result":"would / wouldn't + verb (but: I hope + present / will for a real possibility)"},"uses":[{"use":"Complaining about a habit","ex":"I wish you wouldn't sing in the library."},{"use":"Wanting someone to act now","ex":"I wish you'd hurry up! The film starts at six."},{"use":"Things that won't cooperate","ex":"I wish this printer would work."},{"use":"Waiting for a change in the weather","ex":"If only the rain would stop!"},{"use":"A real hope: use hope, not wish","ex":"I hope you get the scholarship."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Use <b>wish</b> with <b>would</b> or <b>wouldn't</b> when you want another person or thing to act differently; use <b>hope</b> for things that can really happen.","body":["Sometimes another person does something again and again, and it annoys you. You want them to change. For this, use <b>I wish</b>, then the person, then <b>would</b> or <b>wouldn't</b>, then the verb. I wish you'd stop tapping your pen. I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking. The Truth (the real fact): she borrows it, and I don't like it.","You can also use it for one action that you want right now. I wish you would hurry up! And you can use it for things, as if they had a mind of their own: I wish it would stop raining. I wish this printer would work. Would shows that somebody or something is not willing to do what you want. The short form is the same as for had, but a base verb follows it: I wish you'd listen.","There are two limits. First, do not use would about yourself. You control your own actions, so <s>I wish I would</s> is wrong. Say I wish I could, or use the simple past form: I wish I didn't lose things so often. Second, do not use would for a state (how things are, not an action), with verbs like be, have, know or live. Say I wish I had more time, not <s>I wish I would have more time</s>.","Never use will or a present form after wish. Will is one step too near. Step back to would: <s>I wish he will call</s> becomes I wish he would call.","<b>Hope</b> is different. Use hope for something that can really happen. After hope there is no step back. Use a present form or will: I hope it stops raining soon. I hope you'll like the present. Wish with a past form or would means you do not expect it. So for a friend's exam or match tomorrow, the right verb is hope."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish you'd stop tapping your pen.","g":"Truth: you keep tapping — and it annoys me. The short form here is would."},{"s":"I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking.","g":"Truth: she borrows it without asking — again and again."},{"s":"I wish it would stop raining.","g":"Truth: it is still raining — now. Would works for things too."},{"s":"I hope it stops raining soon.","g":"A real possibility, so no Truth and no step back: hope and a present form."},{"s":"<s>I wish I would have more time.</s>","g":"Wrong: would is not for yourself or for a state. Say: I wish I had more time. Truth: I don't have much time — now."}]},"B2":{"key":"<b>Wish</b> with <b>would</b> complains about what someone or something does and asks for a change; <b>hope</b>, with a present form or will, is for real possibilities.","body":["<b>I wish</b> followed by another subject and <b>would</b> or <b>wouldn't</b> is the form for behaviour you want to change. It is typical for habits that annoy you: I wish you'd stop tapping your pen; I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking. The Truth is that the behaviour goes on, and the speaker is criticising it. Would is will taken one step back, and here it keeps its meaning of willingness: the other person is not willing to do what you want.","The same form asks for a single action that you are waiting for right now, as in I wish you would hurry up, and it works for things that seem to refuse to cooperate: I wish it would stop raining; I wish this printer would work. If only can replace I wish for a stronger feeling: If only they'd turn the music down!","Two limits follow from the idea of willingness. You cannot use would about yourself, because you control your own actions: instead of <s>I wish I would</s>, say I wish I could, or use the past simple, as in I wish I didn't lose things so often. And you cannot use would for a state, with verbs such as be, have, know or live: say I wish I had more time, not <s>I wish I would have more time</s>. States take the past simple.","Will and present forms never follow wish: <s>I wish he will call</s> must become I wish he would call.","<b>Hope</b> works differently, because a hope is a real possibility and needs no step back. It is followed by a present form or will, with future meaning: I hope it stops raining soon; I hope you'll like the present. Compare I wish it would stop raining, which suggests the speaker does not expect it to. For good wishes about a real future event, such as a friend's exam or match, the verb is hope."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish you'd stop tapping your pen.","g":"Truth: you keep tapping — and it annoys me. The contraction is would, because a base verb follows."},{"s":"I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking.","g":"Truth: she borrows it without asking — a habit I am criticising."},{"s":"I wish it would stop raining.","g":"Truth: it is still raining — now, and I don't expect it to stop soon."},{"s":"I hope it stops raining soon.","g":"No Truth: the speaker thinks it can really happen, so hope takes a present form."},{"s":"<s>I wish I would have more time.</s>","g":"Wrong: not would for your own situation or for a state. Correct: I wish I had more time. Truth: I don't have much time — now."}]},"C1":{"key":"<b>Wish</b> with <b>would</b> expresses dissatisfaction with someone's or something's unwillingness to act; <b>hope</b> treats the event as an open possibility and takes no remote form.","body":["Would after <b>wish</b> is the remote form of volitional will: it presents the desired change as something the other party is unwilling to make. Hence its typical use for criticism of habits, as in I wish you'd stop tapping your pen and I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking, and for impatience about one awaited action: I wish you would hurry up. The implied Truth is that the behaviour continues, or that the action has not yet come.","The construction extends to things and events treated as if they had a will: I wish it would stop raining; I wish this printer would work. Unlike wish with the past simple, it looks forward, towards a change the speaker wants but does not really expect. If only substitutes for I wish with greater force.","Two restrictions follow. The subject of would cannot normally be the speaker, who controls her own will: <s>I wish I would</s> gives way to I wish I could, or to the past simple, as in I wish I didn't lose things so often. Nor can would describe a state, since states are not acts of will: I wish I had more time, never <s>I wish I would have more time</s>. For someone else's habit the past simple is also possible, but it merely describes; would complains.","Will and present forms cannot follow wish in this sense: <s>I wish he will call</s> is corrected to would call. The one present pattern, wish with two objects, as in I wish you a happy birthday, is a fixed way of giving good wishes and is not a wish clause at all.","<b>Hope</b> presents the event as open, so nothing steps back: it takes a present form with future meaning, or will, as in I hope it stops raining soon and I hope you'll like the present. The contrast is one of stance, not of time: I hope she comes treats her coming as a live possibility, whereas I wish she would come implies doubt that she will. Encouragement about a real future event therefore uses hope."],"examples":[{"s":"I wish you'd stop tapping your pen.","g":"Truth: the tapping continues — now. Contracted would: unwillingness to change."},{"s":"I wish my sister wouldn't borrow my charger without asking.","g":"Truth: she borrows it without asking — habitual behaviour, criticised."},{"s":"I wish it would stop raining.","g":"Truth: it is still raining — now. The rain is treated as if it had a will."},{"s":"I hope it stops raining soon.","g":"No Truth: an open possibility, so no remote form; present with future meaning."},{"s":"<s>I wish I would have more time.</s>","g":"Wrong: would is volitional, so it cannot take the speaker as subject or describe a state. Correct: I wish I had more time. Truth: I have little time — now."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w7l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"I wish you wouldn't leave your wet umbrella on my bag."}],"stem":"What is Jane doing?","options":["Complaining about what her friend keeps doing","Regretting something she did herself last week","Promising to move the umbrella a bit later on","Saying that her friend never does this to her"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: wish and wouldn't complain about another person's habit and ask for a change.","A regret about your own past uses had and V3. Here the subject is you, and the verb is wouldn't.","There is no promise here. Wouldn't after wish shows what Jane wants her friend to stop.","The Truth is the opposite: her friend does this, and it annoys Jane."],"hint":"Who is the subject after wish? What does the helper verb add?","why":"Wish, then another person, then wouldn't is a complaint about that person's repeated behaviour: the friend keeps leaving the umbrella there.","truth":"Her friend keeps leaving the wet umbrella on Jane's bag."},{"id":"w7l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which speaker thinks the thing she talks about can really happen?","options":["I hope the coach picks me for Saturday.","I wish the coach picked me more often.","If only the coach had picked me last week.","I wish I could play in Saturday's match."],"answer":0,"near":3,"fb":["Yes: hope and a present form, with no step back, is for a real possibility.","Picked is one step back: imagined. Truth: the coach doesn't pick her often.","Had picked is two steps back: a regret. Truth: the coach didn't pick her last week.","Saturday is in the future, but could is a step back from can. Truth: she can't play."],"hint":"Look for the verb that has not stepped back.","why":"Hope takes a present form because the speaker sees a real possibility; the three wishes all step back, so they are imagined."},{"id":"w7l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"I wish my little sister would knock before she comes into my room."}],"stem":"What is true?","options":["Her sister usually walks in without knocking.","Her sister always knocks before she comes in.","Her sister walked in without knocking once, last year.","Pim is going to knock on the door from now on."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: wish and would ask for a change, so the Truth is that the sister doesn't knock.","That is what Pim wants. The Truth is the opposite.","One past event would be a regret, with had and V3. Would points to behaviour that still goes on.","Would is about the sister's behaviour, not Pim's. You can't use it for yourself."],"hint":"Flip the wish. Is it about one past moment or about behaviour that goes on?","why":"Wish and would are about behaviour that continues, so the Truth is that the sister comes in without knocking.","truth":"Pim's sister comes in without knocking — again and again."},{"id":"w7l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"I've been late for school three times this week."}],"stem":"I wish I ___ up earlier in the mornings.","options":["got","would get","will get","get"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: for your own habit, use the simple past form. Truth: Mint gets up late.","Would after wish is for another person or thing. You control your own actions, so it is not for yourself.","Will never follows wish. Step back.","A present form after wish says it is real. The Truth is that she gets up late."],"hint":"Who is the subject after wish: the speaker or someone else?","why":"The subject is I, so would is not possible; her present habit takes one step back: got.","truth":"Mint gets up late — these days."},{"id":"w7l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"My best friend moved to Chiang Mai last year. We only talk on LINE now."}],"stem":"I wish she still ___ in Bangkok.","options":["lived","would live","lives","will live"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: live is a state here, so use the simple past form. Truth: she lives in Chiang Mai now.","Would after wish is for an action someone could choose to do, not for a state like living somewhere.","A present form after wish says it is real. She doesn't live in Bangkok now.","Will never follows wish."],"hint":"Is this an action that someone refuses to do, or a state (how things are)?","why":"Living somewhere is a state, not a behaviour, so wish takes the past simple, lived, not would.","truth":"Fern's friend lives in Chiang Mai — now."},{"id":"w7l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"We [[have been waiting]] for [[forty minutes]]. I wish the bus [[will come]] [[soon]]!","answer":2,"fix":"would come","fb":["Have been waiting is real: it started in the past and continues, so the form is right.","Forty minutes is a length of time, so it is right after for.","Yes, this is the error: will never follows wish. Step back to would come.","Soon is fine: it says when the speaker wants the bus to arrive."],"hint":"One part is real and one part is only wished for. Has the wished-for verb stepped back?","why":"Will cannot follow wish. For something you want a person or thing to do, step back to would: would come.","truth":"The bus isn't coming — now."},{"id":"w7l3-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Praew is doing her homework. Her little sister is playing a game with the sound on, and Praew wants her to turn it off right now."}],"stem":"Praew: I wish you ___ (turn) the sound off!","accept":["would turn","'d turn"],"model":"would turn","hint":"Praew wants another person to act now. Which helper verb shows that?","why":"Praew wants another person to do one thing now, so wish takes would and the base verb: would turn.","truth":"Her sister isn't turning the sound off — now."},{"id":"w7l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Fern's friend Bam is in the badminton final tomorrow, and Bam has a real chance."}],"stem":"Fern: I hope you ___ (win) tomorrow!","accept":["win","will win","'ll win"],"model":"win","hint":"Imagined, or a real possibility? Does the verb need a step back?","why":"Hope is for a real possibility, so there is no step back: a present form or will follows it."}],"world":"w7"},"w7l4":{"id":"w7l4","name":"it's (high) time, would rather, as if / as though","cefr":"C1","ctype":"wish","formula":{"if":"It's (high) time + subject · I'd rather + another subject · as if / as though + subject","result":"past simple (but: I'd rather + base verb for yourself)"},"uses":[{"use":"Saying something is overdue","ex":"It's time we went home. It's nearly dark."},{"use":"Criticising a delay","ex":"It's about time you answered my message!"},{"use":"Your own preference","ex":"I'd rather walk than wait for the bus."},{"use":"A polite request not to do something","ex":"I'd rather you didn't sit there, sorry."},{"use":"Comparing with something untrue","ex":"She plans her weekends as if she had no homework at all."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"After <b>it's time</b>, <b>I'd rather</b> with another person, and <b>as if</b>, a past form means not real now: the same step back as wish.","body":["Three more phrases use the step back. The first is <b>it's time</b>, then a person, then a past form: It's time we left. This is about now, not the past. The Truth (the real fact): we haven't left yet, and we are a little late. Add high or about to make it stronger: It's high time I cleaned out my school bag. It often sounds like a complaint.","There is also a simple form with to: It's time to go. This only says that the moment has come. Nobody is late. When a person comes after it's time, do not use will, and do not use to. <s>It's time we go</s> is heard in speech, but exams want It's time we went.","The second phrase is <b>would rather</b>. The short form is I'd rather. It means prefer. When you talk about your own action, use the base verb (the verb with no to and no ending): I'd rather stay at home tonight. I'd rather not go. I'd rather walk than wait.","When you want another person to do something, add that person and step back to a past form: I'd rather you didn't tell Mum. I'd rather you stayed a bit longer. This is about now or the future, not the past. It is a soft, polite way to ask. Never use to or will here, and exams do not accept a present form.","The third phrase is <b>as if</b>, or as though. They mean the same. With a past form, they compare something to a thing that is not true: She talks as if she owned the place. Truth: she doesn't own it. With a present form, the speaker thinks it may be true: It looks as if it's going to rain."],"examples":[{"s":"It's time we left.","g":"Truth: we haven't left yet — now. We are a little late."},{"s":"It's high time I cleaned out my school bag.","g":"Truth: I haven't cleaned it out — and I should. High makes it stronger."},{"s":"I'd rather stay at home tonight.","g":"My own action: base verb, no to, no step back. Nothing is imagined here."},{"s":"I'd rather you didn't tell Mum.","g":"Truth: you want to tell her, and I don't want that — now. A polite request."},{"s":"She talks as if she owned the place.","g":"Truth: she doesn't own it — now."}]},"B2":{"key":"<b>It's time</b>, <b>would rather</b> with a new subject, and <b>as if</b> all take the distance past: a past form that means not real now.","body":["The step back you know from wish appears after three more expressions. <b>It's time</b> followed by a subject and the past simple says that something should already be happening: It's time we left means that we haven't left yet and are slightly late. High or about makes it stronger and more critical: It's high time I cleaned out my school bag. The meaning is present, in spite of the past form.","Compare the neutral pattern with a to-infinitive: It's time to go, or It's time for us to go. That simply announces the moment, with no idea of lateness. When a subject follows it's time directly, will is impossible and so is a to-infinitive, and the present form in <s>It's time we go</s>, although you may hear it, is not accepted in exams.","<b>Would rather</b>, usually shortened to I'd rather, expresses a preference. If the preference is about your own action, the base verb follows, without to: I'd rather stay at home tonight; I'd rather not go; I'd rather walk than wait for the bus.","If you want someone else to do something, a new subject comes in and the verb steps back: I'd rather you didn't tell Mum; I'd rather you stayed a bit longer. The time is now or the future, and the effect is a polite, indirect request. A to-infinitive is wrong here, will is impossible, and a present form such as <s>I'd rather you don't</s> is not accepted in exams. For a regret about something already done, step back twice: I'd rather you hadn't told her.","<b>As if</b> and <b>as though</b> are interchangeable. Followed by a past form, they make a comparison with something untrue: She talks as if she owned the place, and the Truth is that she doesn't. Followed by a present form, they describe something the speaker thinks may well be true: It looks as if it's going to rain."],"examples":[{"s":"It's time we left.","g":"Truth: we haven't left yet — now. Past form, present meaning."},{"s":"It's high time I cleaned out my school bag.","g":"Truth: I haven't cleaned it out — it is overdue. High adds criticism."},{"s":"I'd rather stay at home tonight.","g":"Same subject, so the base verb follows; no step back and no Truth."},{"s":"I'd rather you didn't tell Mum.","g":"Truth: you may tell her, and that is not what I prefer — now or later. A new subject, so step back."},{"s":"She talks as if she owned the place.","g":"Truth: she doesn't own it — now. A past form after as if means untrue."}]},"C1":{"key":"<b>It's time</b>, <b>would rather</b> with its own subject, and <b>as if</b> belong to the distance family: their past forms encode a gap between what is said and what is real.","body":["The modal remoteness seen in conditionals and wishes extends to a small family of expressions. After <b>it's time</b> with a subject, the past simple signals that the action is not yet happening although it ought to be: It's time we left. The Truth is that we are still here. High time and about time intensify the reproach, as in It's high time I cleaned out my school bag. The reference is wholly present; the form marks the gap between obligation and fact.","The infinitive patterns, It's time to go and It's time for us to go, are neutral announcements that carry no such gap. In the finite pattern, will is ungrammatical, as is a to-infinitive straight after the subject, and the present, <s>It's time we go</s>, though widespread in speech, counts as non-standard in examinations, which key the past form.","<b>Would rather</b> states a preference. With no change of subject it takes a bare infinitive: I'd rather stay at home tonight; I'd rather not go; I'd rather walk than wait for the bus. To never appears, and the contraction stands for would, since a base verb follows.","When the preference concerns someone else's action, a clause with its own subject follows and its verb steps back: I'd rather you didn't tell Mum; I'd rather you stayed a bit longer. The past form keeps the request tentative, and so more polite than please don't. The present, <s>I'd rather you don't</s>, is common in speech but not accepted in exams; will and a to-infinitive are impossible. Where the action is already done, the past perfect expresses the regret: I'd rather you hadn't told her.","<b>As if</b> and <b>as though</b> introduce a comparison, and the tense shows the speaker's stance. A past form after a present verb marks the comparison as untrue: She talks as if she owned the place. A past perfect makes it an unreal past: She describes the concert as if she had been there. A present form leaves the matter open, or likely: It looks as if it's going to rain. In informal speech like often replaces as if; write as if in exams."],"examples":[{"s":"It's time we left.","g":"Truth: we are still here — now. The past form marks the gap between what should be and what is."},{"s":"It's high time I cleaned out my school bag.","g":"Truth: it hasn't been cleaned out — now. High time intensifies the reproach."},{"s":"I'd rather stay at home tonight.","g":"No change of subject: bare infinitive, no remote form, nothing counterfactual."},{"s":"I'd rather you didn't tell Mum.","g":"Truth: the telling is possible and unwanted — now or later. Own subject, remote form, tentative request."},{"s":"She talks as if she owned the place.","g":"Truth: she doesn't own it — now. The past form marks the comparison as unreal."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w7l4-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Coach","text":"It's high time we left for the stadium."}],"stem":"What does the coach mean?","options":["We should already be on our way.","We left for the stadium a while ago.","We still have plenty of time to get there.","The plan says we leave at this exact minute."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: it's high time and a past form mean it is late, and the action hasn't started yet.","Left is not past time here. It is the distance past: the Truth is that they haven't left.","High time means the opposite: the right moment has already passed.","That neutral meaning needs it's time to leave. The past form adds the idea of being late."],"hint":"Past form, present meaning. Has the action happened yet?","why":"After it's high time, the past form means the action should already be happening: they haven't left, and they are late.","truth":"They haven't left yet — now."},{"id":"w7l4-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"My cat sits on my homework as if it belonged to her."}],"stem":"What does Mook think?","options":["The homework isn't really the cat's.","The homework used to be the cat's.","The homework probably is the cat's.","The cat sat on the homework yesterday."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: as if and a past form compare with something untrue.","Belonged is not past time here. It is one step back from reality, about now.","For something that may be true, as if takes a present form. The past form says it is not true.","Sits is present, so this is what the cat does now; belonged is not about yesterday."],"hint":"What does a past form after as if tell you about the comparison?","why":"As if followed by a past form marks the comparison as unreal: the homework does not belong to the cat.","truth":"The homework doesn't belong to the cat — now."},{"id":"w7l4-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"I'd rather you didn't call me after ten."}],"stem":"What is Jane saying?","options":["Please don't call me after ten.","You called me after ten, and I was upset.","I prefer not to call you after ten.","Please call me after ten."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: I'd rather, another person and a past form make a polite request about now or the future.","A complaint about a past call would need hadn't called. Didn't here is one step back, not past time.","The person after rather is you, so the calling is done by the listener, not by Jane.","The verb is negative: didn't call. Jane wants no calls after ten."],"hint":"Who does the calling? Is didn't about past time here?","why":"Would rather, then you, then a past form states what Jane prefers the other person to do from now on: not to call after ten.","truth":"Her friend may call late; Jane prefers that she doesn't — from now on."},{"id":"w7l4-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Mum","text":"That library book was due back last week, Fern."}],"stem":"It's high time you ___ it back.","options":["took","take","will take","to take"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: it's high time and a subject take the past form. Truth: Fern hasn't taken it back yet.","You may hear this in speech, but exams want the step back after it's high time and a subject.","Will is impossible after it's time. The form is a step back, not a future.","To take follows it's time with no subject. Here there is a subject, you, so a verb with a tense is needed."],"hint":"There is a subject after time. What form shows that the action is overdue?","why":"After it's high time and a subject, the verb takes the past form, took, to show the action is overdue.","truth":"Fern hasn't taken the book back — yet."},{"id":"w7l4-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Ploy","text":"Shall we get a taxi to Siam?"},{"who":"Nan","text":"It's only two stops, and the traffic is terrible."}],"stem":"I'd rather ___ the BTS.","options":["take","to take","took","taking"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: would rather and the base verb, because the preference is about Nan's own action.","No to after would rather. It behaves like would: the base verb comes next.","The past form is only for a different subject, as in I'd rather you took it. Here Nan speaks about herself.","An ing form cannot follow would rather."],"hint":"Whose action is it: the speaker's own or another person's?","why":"Nan is talking about her own action, so would rather is followed by the base verb with no to."},{"id":"w7l4-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","stem":"It's [[about time]] the school [[will fix]] the air conditioner; we [[have been]] melting [[since]] May.","answer":1,"fix":"fixed","fb":["About time is fine: it makes it's time stronger.","Yes, this is the error: will cannot follow it's time. Step back to the past form, fixed.","Have been melting is real, from May until now, so the form is right.","Since and a starting point is correct with have been."],"hint":"Which verb describes the overdue action? Has it stepped back?","why":"After it's about time and a subject, the verb takes the past form, fixed; will is never possible there.","truth":"The air conditioner hasn't been fixed — yet."},{"id":"w7l4-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","given":"Please don't use my laptop while I'm out.","keyword":"RATHER","stem":"I ___ my laptop while I'm out.","accept":["would rather you didn't use","'d rather you didn't use","would rather you did not use","'d rather you did not use"],"model":"would rather you didn't use","hint":"The preference is about another person's action. What form follows the new subject?","why":"Would rather with another subject takes a past form for a present or future preference: I would rather you didn't use it.","truth":"The speaker doesn't want her laptop used — while she is out."},{"id":"w7l4-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Jane has never been to Japan, but she has watched hundreds of travel videos. Use the form that shows the comparison is untrue."}],"stem":"Jane talks about Tokyo as if she ___ (know) every street.","accept":["knew"],"model":"knew","hint":"Is the comparison true? If not, how many steps back?","why":"Jane doesn't really know every street, so careful English marks the comparison as untrue with a past form: knew. In speech you may also hear knows.","truth":"Jane doesn't know every street in Tokyo — now."}],"world":"w7"},"w8l1":{"id":"w8l1","name":"Inversion: Should you…, Were I… / Were it to…, Had I known…","cefr":"C1","ctype":"formal","formula":{"if":"Should you… / Were I… / Were it to… / Had I + V3… (no if)","result":"after Should: will / can / imperative; after Were: would / could / might + verb; after Had: would / could / might have + V3"},"uses":[{"use":"A polite offer in a formal email","ex":"Should you have any questions, please contact the school office."},{"use":"An instruction on a notice","ex":"Should the lift be out of order, please use the stairs by the library."},{"use":"A proposal in an essay","ex":"Were lessons to start an hour later, students would arrive more awake."},{"use":"Formal advice","ex":"Were I in your position, I would ask for a few more days."},{"use":"Explaining politely what went wrong","ex":"Had I known you were ill, I would have brought your homework round."},{"use":"Thanking someone","ex":"Had you not reminded me, I would have missed the deadline."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"In formal writing, should, were or had can move to the front and do the job of if.","body":["In formal (serious, official) writing, English can make a condition without the word if. A helping verb moves to the front, before the subject (the person or thing doing the action). This move is called inversion. It means the same as an if-sentence. It only sounds more formal.","Only three verbs can do this: should, were and had. <b>Should you need</b> help means if you need help. It is real and possible, and very polite. Here should is not advice. <b>Were I</b> you, or <b>were it to</b> rain, means if I were you, or if it rained. That is imagined, about now or the future. <b>Had I known</b> means if I had known. That is an imagined past. No other verb can start a condition like this.","The result (what happens) does not change. After should, use will, can or an order, such as please call me. After were, use would, could or might. After had, use would have, could have or might have, plus the past participle (V3). After should, the verb stays in its base form. Say should it rain, never <s>should it rains</s>.","Three rules keep it correct. One: never put if in the same clause. Say had I known, not <s>if had I known</s>. Two: use were, never was. Say were I to choose. Three: in a negative, not comes after the subject and stays a full word. Write had she not left, never <s>hadn't she left</s>.","Always put a comma after the inverted part. Use this form in letters, emails to teachers, notices and essays. Use it once in a paragraph at most. With friends, plain if sounds more natural."],"examples":[{"s":"Should you need a new library card, please ask at the front desk.","g":"Means: if you need a new card. Real and possible, so there is no Truth line. Polite and formal."},{"s":"Were the school to add a Saturday class, few students would be pleased.","g":"Means: if the school added a Saturday class. Truth: there is no Saturday class and no plan for one, now."},{"s":"Had I known about the road works, I would have taken the MRT.","g":"Means: if I had known. Truth: I didn't know about the road works back then, so I didn't take the MRT."},{"s":"Had she not set two alarms, she would have missed her flight.","g":"Not comes after the subject as a full word. Truth: she set two alarms and caught her flight."},{"s":"<s>If had I known, I would have called.</s>","g":"Wrong: if and inversion together. Say: Had I known, I would have called. Or: If I had known, I would have called. Truth: I didn't know, back then."}]},"B2":{"key":"Formal English can drop if and put should, were or had before the subject, with the same meaning as the if-sentence.","body":["Formal English has a second way to build a condition. Instead of using if, the auxiliary verb moves in front of the subject, as it does in a question: <b>had I known</b>, not if I had known. This is called inversion. The meaning and the time stay exactly the same as in the if-sentence; only the tone becomes more formal.","Only three verbs can do this, and each has its own place on the two dials. <b>Should you need</b> help means if you need help: a real, possible future, and very polite. Here should is not advice. <b>Were I</b> in your place, or <b>were it to</b> rain, means if I were, or if it rained: imagined, now or future. <b>Had I known</b> means if I had known: an imagined past. Do, would and could cannot start a condition in this way.","The result half keeps its normal form. After should, the result is real: will, can, or an imperative such as please contact us. After were, it is would, could or might plus a verb. After had, it is would have, could have or might have plus the past participle (V3). Should itself is followed by a base verb: should it rain, never <s>should it rains</s>.","Three form rules matter. First, if and inversion never share a clause: <s>if had I known</s> is wrong. Second, the verb is always were, never was: were I to choose. Third, in a negative, not stays a full word after the subject: had she not left, should you not receive a reply. A contracted form at the front, such as <s>hadn't she left</s>, is wrong.","Put a comma after the inverted clause. Use inversion where the reader is official: letters, emails to a teacher or a university, notices and essays. One in a paragraph is enough; more sounds forced. In everyday talk, plain if is the natural choice."],"examples":[{"s":"Should you need a new library card, please ask at the front desk.","g":"Same as: If you need a new library card. A real, possible future with a polite instruction. No Truth line, because nothing is denied."},{"s":"Were the school to add a Saturday class, few students would be pleased.","g":"Same as: If the school were to add a Saturday class. Imagined future, so the result has would. Truth: there is no Saturday class and no plan for one, now."},{"s":"Had I known about the road works, I would have taken the MRT.","g":"Same as: If I had known. Imagined past, so the result has would have plus V3. Truth: I didn't know about the road works back then, so I didn't take the MRT."},{"s":"Had she not set two alarms, she would have missed her flight.","g":"Negative inversion: had, subject, not, V3. Never a contraction at the front. Truth: she set two alarms and caught her flight."},{"s":"<s>If had I known, I would have called.</s>","g":"Wrong: if cannot sit inside an inverted clause. Choose one: Had I known… or If I had known… Truth: I didn't know, back then."}]},"C1":{"key":"Conditional inversion replaces if with a fronted should, were or had; it changes the register, not the meaning or the time.","body":["English can mark a condition by word order alone. The auxiliary moves in front of the subject, exactly as in a question, and if disappears: <b>had I known</b>, <b>were it to rain</b>, <b>should you need</b> help. Grammars call this conditional inversion. Because the fronted verb is already doing the work of if, the two can never appear together. The meaning and the time reference are identical to the if-version; what changes is the register, which rises to formal.","Modern English allows only three verbs here. Should opens a real future condition and presents it as slightly less likely, which is why it suits courteous offers; it has nothing to do with advice. Were, often with to, opens an imagined present or future. Had opens an imagined past. Do, would, could and the rest cannot invert in this way, and was is impossible: only were survives in the inverted form, whatever the subject.","The result clause is built as usual, because inversion touches only the condition. After should, expect will, can or an imperative. After were, expect would, could or might plus a base verb. After had, expect would have, could have or might have plus the past participle (V3). Should takes a bare base verb whatever the subject: should the weather change, not <s>should the weather changes</s>.","Negatives show the formality most clearly. Not cannot be contracted onto the fronted verb, so it stays as a full word after the subject: had she not left, were it not for the rain, should you not receive a reply. A form like <s>hadn't she left</s> is not a condition at all; it reads as a question.","A comma always follows the inverted clause. Treat inversion as a tool for official readers: letters, formal emails, notices and essays. One per paragraph shows range; several sound forced. Should you have any questions is a fixed email formula worth learning whole. The one inverted form that is also common in conversation is had I known."],"examples":[{"s":"Should you need a new library card, please ask at the front desk.","g":"A real future condition, presented as slightly unlikely, with an imperative result. No Truth line: nothing is denied."},{"s":"Were the school to add a Saturday class, few students would be pleased.","g":"Were plus subject plus to: an imagined future, matched by would in the result. Truth: there is no Saturday class and no plan for one, now."},{"s":"Had I known about the road works, I would have taken the MRT.","g":"Had plus subject plus V3: an imagined past, with the have ticket in the result. Truth: I didn't know about the road works back then, so I didn't take the MRT."},{"s":"Had she not set two alarms, she would have missed her flight.","g":"Uncontracted not after the subject; a fronted hadn't would read as a question. Truth: she set two alarms and caught her flight."},{"s":"<s>If had I known, I would have called.</s>","g":"Wrong: the fronted had already does the work of if, so if must go. Had I known… or If I had known… Truth: I didn't know, back then."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w8l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"School notice","text":"Should you lose your student card, please report it to the office at once."}],"stem":"Which sentence means the same as the notice?","options":["You should lose your student card and then report it to the office.","If you lose your student card, please report it to the office at once.","If you had lost your student card, you would have reported it at once.","As you have lost your student card, please report it to the office now."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Here \"should\" is not advice. At the front of the clause it does the job of \"if\".","Yes: \"Should you lose\" is a formal way to say \"If you lose\", a real and possible future.","That is an imagined past, which would start \"Had you lost\". The notice is about a possible future.","The notice does not say the card is lost. It only says what to do if that happens."],"hint":"Is the notice giving advice, or naming a condition? Which time is it about?","why":"Should at the front replaces if. The condition is real and possible, so it matches if plus a present form, with a polite instruction as the result."},{"id":"w8l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Fah's travel blog","text":"Had the ferry left Koh Samet on time, we would have caught the last coach home."}],"stem":"What really happened?","options":["The ferry left on time, and they caught the last coach home.","The ferry may leave late, so they may miss the last coach home.","The ferry always leaves late, so they never catch the last coach.","The ferry left late, and they missed the last coach home."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Flip both halves. \"Had the ferry left on time\" is imagined, so in reality it left late.","This is not about the future. \"Had\" plus a past participle looks back at a finished day.","A habit would use present forms. This sentence is about one past journey.","Yes: the opposite of each half is what really happened."],"hint":"Had plus a past participle at the front: real or imagined? Past, now or future?","why":"Had at the front means if it had: an imagined past. The Truth is the opposite on both sides: the ferry left late and they missed the coach.","truth":"The ferry left late, so they missed the last coach home."},{"id":"w8l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Student council essay","text":"Were the school to move the final exams to March, students would have three more weeks to revise."}],"stem":"How does the writer see the move to March?","options":["As an imagined idea: it is not the plan now.","As a past event: the exams were moved last year.","As a fixed plan: the exams are moving to March.","As a regular habit: the exams move every year."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: \"were … to\" with \"would\" is one step back from reality. The writer is only imagining the move.","\"Were\" here is not past time. It shows distance from reality, about now or the future.","A fixed plan would be real: \"When the exams move to March, students will have…\"","A habit needs present forms in both halves, with no \"were to\" and no \"would\"."],"hint":"Were … to plus would: is that past time, or distance from reality?","why":"Were plus subject plus to is the formal form of if it were to: an imagined future. The result has would, so the writer is not reporting a plan.","truth":"The exams are not being moved to March; the writer only imagines it."},{"id":"w8l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Letter to parents","text":"The M2 trip to Ayutthaya leaves at 6.30 a.m. on Friday."}],"stem":"___ further information about the trip, please email Ms Carter before Wednesday.","options":["If should you require","Would you require","Should you required","Should you require"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"If\" and inversion cannot share a clause. Choose one: \"If you require\" or the inverted form.","\"Would\" cannot open a condition. Only should, were and had can move to the front.","After \"should\" the verb stays in its base form: require, not required.","Yes: \"should\" moves to the front, takes a base verb, and no \"if\" is needed."],"hint":"Which three verbs can open a condition without if? What verb form follows this one?","why":"A real, possible condition in a formal letter: should plus subject plus base verb, with no if in the clause."},{"id":"w8l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"So you caught the right train in Chiang Mai last night?"},{"who":"Mint","text":"Only just. Praew told me the platform had changed."}],"stem":"___ me about the change, I would have got on the wrong train.","options":["Hadn't Praew told","Had Praew not told","Has Praew not told","If had Praew not told"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["In an inverted negative, \"not\" stays a full word after the subject. No contraction at the front.","Yes: had, then the subject, then \"not\", then the past participle.","\"Has\" is present perfect. An imagined past needs \"had\".","No \"if\" inside an inverted clause. The front \"had\" already does that job."],"hint":"Where does \"not\" go when had moves to the front? Is it contracted?","why":"Last night is an imagined past, so had opens the clause; in the negative, not follows the subject uncontracted, and if is left out.","truth":"Praew told Mint about the change, so she got on the right train."},{"id":"w8l1-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"The minibus was forty minutes late yesterday, so we missed our first match."}],"stem":"___ on time, we would have played all three matches.","options":["Were it to arrive","Should it arrive","Had it arrived","Had it arrive"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["\"Were it to arrive\" imagines the future. Yesterday is past, and the result has \"would have\".","\"Should it arrive\" is a real, possible future. This match day is over.","Yes: an imagined past takes had at the front plus the past participle.","\"Had\" needs the past participle: arrived, not arrive."],"hint":"When was the minibus late? Which front verb goes with that time?","why":"Yesterday plus would have played shows an imagined past, so the condition needs had plus the past participle at the front.","truth":"The minibus didn't arrive on time yesterday, so they missed the first match."},{"id":"w8l1-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Fern's essay","text":"At present the library is closed at weekends."}],"stem":"[[Were]] the library [[to open]] on Sundays, far more students [[will use]] it [[for revision]].","answer":2,"fix":"would use","fb":["\"Were\" is right at the front. \"Was\" can never open an inverted condition.","\"To open\" is right: were, then the subject, then to plus a base verb.","The condition is imagined, so the result must step back too: would use, not will use.","\"For revision\" is fine. The problem is the verb in the result."],"hint":"Is the condition real or imagined? Check that the form of each half agrees.","why":"Were the library to open imagines the future, one step back from reality, so the result needs would plus a verb, not will.","truth":"The library does not open on Sundays."},{"id":"w8l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","given":"If I had checked the timetable, I wouldn't have waited an hour at the pier.","keyword":"HAD","stem":"___ the timetable, I wouldn't have waited an hour at the pier. (formal: do not use if)","accept":["Had I checked"],"model":"Had I checked","hint":"Drop if and move one verb in front of the subject.","why":"If I had checked becomes Had I checked: had moves in front of the subject and if disappears. The meaning, an imagined past, stays the same.","truth":"I didn't check the timetable, so I waited an hour."}],"world":"w8"},"w8l2":{"id":"w8l2","name":"were to, happen to, should; but for, without, if it weren't for / hadn't been for","cefr":"C1","ctype":"formal","formula":{"if":"If + subject + were to / should / happen to + verb; But for / Without / If it weren't for / If it hadn't been for + noun","result":"were to: would / could / might + verb; should, happen to: will / can / imperative; noun conditions: would + verb (now), would have + V3 (past)"},"uses":[{"use":"A remote future in an essay or debate","ex":"If the city were to ban cars near schools, the air would be cleaner."},{"use":"A favour that depends on chance","ex":"If you happen to find my blue pencil case, please message me."},{"use":"A polite instruction for a small chance","ex":"If anyone should ring while I am out, please take a message."},{"use":"Thanking someone","ex":"Without your notes, I would have failed the quiz."},{"use":"Giving the one reason, formally","ex":"But for the storm, the sports day would have gone ahead."},{"use":"Saying what you depend on now","ex":"If it weren't for my aunt, I wouldn't be learning the violin."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Were to, should and happen to change how far away a condition feels; but for and without make a condition from a noun.","body":["Sometimes a future event feels far from real life to the speaker. Then she can say if, plus <b>were to</b>, plus a verb. If the school were to close means the same as if the school closed. It is only more formal and more distant. The condition is imagined. So the result (what happens) takes would, could or might, never will or can.","Other times the event is possible, but only by chance. Then use <b>happen to</b>, or <b>should</b>, after if. If you happen to see her means if you see her by chance. If anyone should call means the same and is a little formal. These are real conditions. So the result takes will, can or an order. After should, the verb keeps its base form. Never put will or would in this if half.","Think of a line from sure to far away. When means I expect it. Plain if means maybe. If with should or happen to means maybe, by chance. If with were to means I am only imagining it. This is the speaker's view. It is not a percentage.","Some conditions have no verb at all. <b>But for</b> and <b>without</b> take a noun (a thing or a person). They never take a clause (words with a subject and a verb). Say but for your help, not <s>but for you helped</s>. Without your help means if you had not helped me. But for means the same and is formal. The noun is real. Only the result is imagined.","The longer form is <b>if it weren't for</b> plus a noun. That is about now. For the past, say <b>if it hadn't been for</b> plus a noun. The verb steps back because the condition is imagined. Do not drop the word for, and do not add would. In the result, would plus a verb is about now. Would have plus the past participle (V3) is about the past. Formal writing also has were it not for, and had it not been for. They mean the same."],"examples":[{"s":"If the school were to move to a four-day week, how would parents react?","g":"Were to: only imagined, about the future. Truth: the school has a five-day week and no plan to change it."},{"s":"If you happen to see Fern, tell her the rehearsal starts at four.","g":"Happen to: by chance. This is real and possible, so there is no Truth line. The result is an order."},{"s":"If anyone should ask for me, I'll be in the art room.","g":"Should after if: by chance, a little formal. Base verb after should, and will in the result. No Truth line."},{"s":"But for the tour guide, we would have got lost in the old town.","g":"Means: if the guide had not been there. Truth: the guide was there, back then, and we did not get lost."},{"s":"If it weren't for the rain, we would be on the beach now.","g":"About now. Truth: it is raining now, so we are not on the beach."}]},"B2":{"key":"Were to pushes a future condition away from reality, should and happen to add by chance, and but for, without or if it weren't for build an imagined condition around a real noun.","body":["A speaker can show how far away an event feels. If plus <b>were to</b> plus a verb presents a future event as remote and sounds formal: if the school were to close means if the school closed, only more distant. Because the condition is imagined, the result takes would, could or might, never will or can.","<b>Happen to</b> and <b>should</b> do something different: they keep the condition real but add the idea of by chance. If you happen to see her and if you should see her both mean if you see her, which I am not counting on. The result stays real, so will, can and imperatives are all fine. Should is followed by a base verb, and plain will or would cannot take its place in the if half.","So there is a scale of stance. When means the speaker expects the event. Plain if leaves it open. If with should or happen to leaves it open and up to chance. If with were to steps back into imagination. This is the speaker's point of view, not a percentage: two people can describe the same event with different forms.","A condition can also be packed into a noun. <b>Without</b> and the more formal <b>but for</b> are followed by a noun phrase, never by a clause: but for your help, not <s>but for you helped</s>. They mean if that thing did not exist, or had not happened. The noun is real; the result is the imagined part. Its form gives the time: would plus a verb for now, would have plus the past participle (V3) for the past.","The full version is <b>if it weren't for</b> plus a noun for a present situation, and <b>if it hadn't been for</b> plus a noun for a past one. The verb is stepped back because the condition is imagined. Keep the word for, and keep would out of the if half. In formal writing these can be inverted: were it not for the rain, had it not been for the rain. Learn to recognise them; they mean exactly the same."],"examples":[{"s":"If the school were to move to a four-day week, how would parents react?","g":"A remote, imagined future, so the result has would. Truth: the school has a five-day week and no plan to change it."},{"s":"If you happen to see Fern, tell her the rehearsal starts at four.","g":"A real, open condition with by chance added; imperative result. No Truth line, because nothing is denied."},{"s":"If anyone should ask for me, I'll be in the art room.","g":"If plus should plus base verb: an open condition, by chance. The result keeps will. No Truth line."},{"s":"But for the tour guide, we would have got lost in the old town.","g":"But for plus a noun, formal. Same as: Without the tour guide, or If it hadn't been for the tour guide. Truth: the guide was there, back then, and we did not get lost."},{"s":"If it weren't for the rain, we would be on the beach now.","g":"If it weren't for plus a noun, with would be for a present result. Truth: it is raining now, so we are not on the beach."}]},"C1":{"key":"Were to marks a future condition as remote, should and happen to mark an open one as chance, and but for, without and if it weren't for imagine the absence of something real.","body":["Were to is the verb be to, the form used for arranged or expected events, moved one step back from reality. That is why if the school were to close sounds more remote and more formal than if the school closed: the speaker holds the event at a distance. It needs an event, so it is awkward with state verbs such as know. The result must be imagined as well: would, could or might, not will or can.","Should and <b>happen to</b> work on the other dial. They leave the condition real and open but mark it as a matter of chance: if anyone should call, if you happen to see her, or both together, if you should happen to see her. Since nothing is imagined, the result keeps will, can or an imperative. Should takes a base verb, and a plain will or would cannot replace it in the if half.","Together these give a scale of stance: when for what the speaker expects, plain if for an open question, if with should or happen to for an open question left to chance, and if with were to for a scene that is only imagined. None of this measures probability. It records how the speaker chooses to present the event, and the same event can be presented in more than one way.","A condition can be reduced to a noun phrase. Without is neutral; <b>but for</b> is formal and rarer. Both must be followed by a noun phrase, never a clause, and both present that thing as real while the result imagines its absence. Because there is no verb in the condition, only the result shows the time: would plus a verb for the present, would have plus the past participle (V3) for the past.","The clause versions are <b>if it weren't for</b>, for a present situation, and <b>if it hadn't been for</b>, for a past one. The verb is stepped back, as in any imagined condition; for is part of the chunk, and would never enters the if half. Formal prose inverts them as were it not for and had it not been for, with not kept whole. All of these single out one real cause and credit it, or blame it, for the way things turned out."],"examples":[{"s":"If the school were to move to a four-day week, how would parents react?","g":"Be to, one step back: the change is held at a distance. Truth: the school has a five-day week and no plan to change it."},{"s":"If you happen to see Fern, tell her the rehearsal starts at four.","g":"Open condition marked as chance; imperative result. No Truth line: nothing is denied."},{"s":"If anyone should ask for me, I'll be in the art room.","g":"If plus should: open but left to chance, slightly formal; real result with will. No Truth line."},{"s":"But for the tour guide, we would have got lost in the old town.","g":"Noun-phrase condition; would have got shows the past. Truth: the guide was there, back then, and we did not get lost."},{"s":"If it weren't for the rain, we would be on the beach now.","g":"Present situation, present result; formal version: Were it not for the rain. Truth: it is raining now, so we are not on the beach."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w8l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"Four students talk about the canteen closing next term. Who treats it as the most distant, imagined possibility?","options":["Ploy: When the canteen closes, we'll bring a packed lunch.","Jane: If the canteen closes, we'll bring a packed lunch.","Fern: If the canteen should close, we'll bring a packed lunch.","Pim: If the canteen were to close, we'd bring a packed lunch."],"answer":3,"near":2,"fb":["\"When\" means Ploy expects it to happen. That is the surest view, not the most distant.","Plain \"if\" with a present form is an open, real possibility.","\"Should\" adds by chance, but the condition is still real, with \"we'll\" in the result.","Yes: \"were to\" with \"we'd\" steps back from reality. Pim is only imagining it."],"hint":"Look at each result: will or would? Which condition has stepped back from reality?","why":"Were to plus would marks an imagined, remote future. When is expected, plain if is open, and if with should is open but by chance.","truth":"Pim does not expect the canteen to close; she only imagines it."},{"id":"w8l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"School newsletter","text":"But for Coach Dao's advice, Bam would have lost the badminton final last Saturday."}],"stem":"What really happened?","options":["The coach gave no advice, and Bam lost the final.","The coach gave advice, but Bam still lost the final.","The coach gave advice, and Bam won the final.","The coach gave no advice, but Bam won the final anyway."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The noun after \"but for\" is real: the advice was given. And losing is only imagined.","\"Would have lost\" is imagined, so the real result is the opposite: she won.","Yes: the advice is real, and losing is only the imagined result.","\"But for\" means if it had not been for. The advice was there, and it mattered."],"hint":"Is the noun after \"but for\" real or imagined? And the result?","why":"But for plus a noun means if it had not been for it. The advice was real; would have lost is imagined, so Bam won.","truth":"Coach Dao gave advice, and Bam won the final."},{"id":"w8l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mum","text":"If you happen to pass a 7-Eleven on your way home, could you get some milk?"}],"stem":"What does \"happen to\" tell us?","options":["There is certainly a 7-Eleven on the way home.","Passing one is possible, but only by chance.","Passing one is imagined and cannot be true.","You passed a 7-Eleven earlier this morning."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["If Mum were sure, she would say \"when you pass\". \"Happen to\" shows she is not sure.","Yes: \"happen to\" adds by chance to a real, open condition.","Nothing is imagined here. The verb is present and the request is real.","\"On your way home\" is still ahead. The present form after \"if\" points to the future."],"hint":"Is Mum sure, unsure or only imagining?","why":"Happen to after if means by chance. The condition stays real and open, so the result can be a request."},{"id":"w8l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Tour guide","text":"The sea looks calm, so tomorrow's boat trip to the island is going ahead."}],"stem":"If the weather ___ worse during the trip, we will come straight back to the pier.","options":["should gets","will get","should get","would get"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["After \"should\" the verb stays in its base form, with no -s.","\"If\" takes the photo; \"will\" makes the promise. A plain future condition has no \"will\".","Yes: \"if\" plus \"should\" plus a base verb means if by chance, and the result keeps \"will\".","\"Would\" does not go in the if half, and the result here is a real \"will\"."],"hint":"Is this condition real or imagined? Look at the result, then check the verb form.","why":"The result has will, so the condition is real. If plus should plus a base verb means if by chance; will and would cannot fill the if half."},{"id":"w8l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"Our flight to Chiang Mai next month is confirmed, so I'm not worried."}],"stem":"Still, if the airline were to cancel it, we ___ the night train instead.","options":["can take","will take","could have taken","could take"],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["\"Can\" is a real result. After an imagined \"were to\", step back: could.","\"Will\" is a real promise. An imagined condition needs would, could or might.","\"Have\" is the past ticket, but the flight is next month.","Yes: an imagined future result, and \"could\" shows it would be possible."],"hint":"Real or imagined condition? And when is the result?","why":"Were to cancel imagines a future event, so the result steps back to could plus a base verb; next month rules out could have.","truth":"The flight is confirmed; Jane only imagines a cancellation."},{"id":"w8l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"[[But for]] [[Mook reminded me]], I [[would have left]] my passport [[in the hotel safe]].","answer":1,"fix":"Mook's reminder","fb":["\"But for\" is fine here: it is formal and means if it had not been for.","\"But for\" takes a noun, never a clause with a subject and a verb: Mook's reminder.","\"Would have left\" is right: an imagined past result with the \"have\" ticket.","\"In the hotel safe\" is fine. Look at what follows \"but for\"."],"hint":"This condition has no if. What must follow its opening words, and is the result's time right?","why":"But for and without are followed by a noun phrase, not a clause, so Mook reminded me must become Mook's reminder.","truth":"Mook reminded her, so she did not leave her passport behind."},{"id":"w8l2-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Praew","text":"The PM2.5 is bad again today, so PE is in the hall."}],"stem":"If it ___ the haze, we would be playing volleyball outside right now.","options":["isn't for","weren't for","wouldn't be for","weren't"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Present for an imagined present. The haze is real, so step back: weren't.","Yes: \"if it weren't for\" plus a noun, one step back, about now.","No \"would\" in the if half. \"Would\" belongs in the result.","The chunk needs \"for\" before the noun: if it weren't for the haze."],"hint":"Is the haze real today? How many steps back, and is the chunk complete?","why":"The haze is real now, so the condition is imagined: if it weren't for plus a noun, with would be playing as the present result.","truth":"There is haze today, so they are not playing outside."},{"id":"w8l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","given":"Fern only caught the last BTS train because the taxi driver knew a shortcut.","keyword":"HADN'T","stem":"If it ___ the taxi driver's shortcut, Fern would have missed the last BTS train.","accept":["hadn't been for"],"model":"hadn't been for","hint":"A fixed chunk before a noun, about the past. It has three words.","why":"The shortcut is real and past, so the imagined version is if it hadn't been for plus the noun, with would have missed as the result.","truth":"The driver knew a shortcut, so Fern caught the train."}],"world":"w8"},"w8l3":{"id":"w8l3","name":"When the rules bend: polite will / would after if, real past conditionals, evidence if, and if so / if not / if necessary / if any","cefr":"C1","ctype":"formal","formula":{"if":"If + any tense that fits the facts (will / would only for willingness, insistence or politeness)","result":"any tense, modal or imperative that fits; chunks: if so, if not, if necessary, if possible, if any, if in doubt"},"uses":[{"use":"A polite request in a formal email","ex":"I would be grateful if you would confirm my place on the course."},{"use":"Asking someone to be willing","ex":"If you'll just sign the register, you can go straight in."},{"use":"A real past habit","ex":"In primary school, if we finished early, we played outside."},{"use":"Reasoning from evidence","ex":"If the gate is locked, the caretaker has already left."},{"use":"Making an offer","ex":"There are spare pens on my desk if you need one."},{"use":"Short chunks that point back","ex":"Is the pool open? If so, bring your swimsuit. If not, we'll play badminton."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Not every if-sentence is a prediction; when it is not, use the tenses that fit the facts and do not fix it.","body":["The type patterns are for predictions (saying what will follow) and for imagined worlds. Many good if-sentences do something else. So first ask one question. Is this a prediction, or is it imagined? If not, any tenses that fit the facts are fine. Leave the sentence alone. Do not fix it.","Will after if is right when it means be willing to. If you'll wait here means if you agree to wait here. Only a person can be willing. A strong will can also mean you keep doing it: if you will stay up so late, of course you feel tired. Would after if is right in a polite request: I'd be grateful if you would reply. If will only means in the future, take it out. Use a present form.","Some if-sentences are about a real past. If it rained, we stayed in tells a past habit. Here if means whenever. Both verbs are past simple because both things really happened. Another kind is a real past chance: if I was rude, I apologise. Maybe I was rude; I am not sure. Use was here, not were. Nothing is imagined, so there is no Truth line.","Some if-sentences use evidence (a clue). If her bag is here, she's still at school. The speaker sees a clue and works out a fact. Others give the reason for speaking. There's water in the fridge if you're thirsty. The water is there anyway. The if-part only says why I am telling you. Both kinds take any tense that is true. They do not need will.","Last, learn six short chunks. If so means if that is true. If not means if that is not true. Both point back to an idea just before them. If necessary means if it is needed. If possible means if you can. If in doubt means if you are not sure. If any follows few or little, between two commas: few, if any, seats are left."],"examples":[{"s":"If you'll wait here, I'll fetch the key to the music room.","g":"Will after if means are willing to: if you agree to wait. A polite request. Nothing is imagined, so no Truth line."},{"s":"I'd be grateful if you would check my essay plan.","g":"Would after if makes a request very polite. Good for emails to teachers. No Truth line."},{"s":"If it rained, we stayed in and played cards.","g":"A real past habit: if means whenever. Past simple twice. Both things really happened, so no Truth line."},{"s":"If her bag is here, she's still at school.","g":"Evidence: the bag is the clue; she is still at school is the conclusion. No Truth line."},{"s":"There's water in the fridge if you're thirsty.","g":"An offer: the water is there anyway. The if-part says why I am telling you. No Truth line."}]},"B2":{"key":"The type patterns apply only to predictions and imagined worlds; polite will and would, real past, evidence and offer conditionals, and fixed chunks follow the facts instead.","body":["The patterns called zero, first, second and third describe predictions and imagined worlds. A great deal of natural English uses if for other jobs, and there the patterns do not apply. So before you correct an if-sentence, ask one question: is the speaker predicting or imagining? If the answer is no, any tenses that fit the facts are correct, and the sentence should be left alone.","<b>Will and would after if.</b> Will is right after if when it means be willing to: if you'll wait here means if you agree to wait. Only a person can be willing. Said with stress, will can also mean that someone insists: if you will stay up so late, of course you feel tired. Would is right in polite requests: I'd be grateful if you would reply. If will only says in the future, take it out and use a present form.","<b>Real past.</b> If it rained, we stayed in describes a past habit; if means whenever, and both verbs are past simple because both things happened. A real past possibility works the same way: if I was rude, I apologise. The speaker thinks it may really have happened, so she uses was, not were, and no past perfect. Nothing is imagined, so there is no Truth line to write.","<b>Evidence and offers.</b> In if her bag is here, she's still at school, the speaker reasons from a clue to a conclusion. The tenses simply match the clue and the conclusion, present or past. In there's water in the fridge if you're thirsty, the water is there in any case; the if-part only explains why the speaker mentions it. Neither sentence predicts anything, so neither needs will.","<b>Chunks.</b> Some if-phrases are fixed and have no verb. If so and if not point back to the idea just before them: if that is true, if that is not true. If necessary and if possible mean if it is necessary and if it is possible. If in doubt means if you are not sure. If any follows few or little, with a comma on each side: few, if any, seats are left."],"examples":[{"s":"If you'll wait here, I'll fetch the key to the music room.","g":"Willingness will: swap in agree to and it still works. A real request, so no Truth line."},{"s":"I'd be grateful if you would check my essay plan.","g":"Polite would in a formal request. It is not an imagined condition, so no Truth line."},{"s":"If it rained, we stayed in and played cards.","g":"Real past, past plus past, with if close to whenever. Do not change it to a third conditional. No Truth line."},{"s":"If her bag is here, she's still at school.","g":"Evidence conditional: clue, then conclusion, both in the present. Not a prediction, so no will. No Truth line."},{"s":"There's water in the fridge if you're thirsty.","g":"Offer conditional: the main clause is true in any case; if explains why it is mentioned. No Truth line."}]},"C1":{"key":"Only predictive conditionals obey the type patterns; modal will and would, real past, evidence and speech-act conditionals, and reduced chunks are correct with whatever forms are true.","body":["The type patterns describe one use of if: predicting a result from a condition, real or imagined. Much everyday English uses if differently, to reason, to offer, to be polite or to recall a habit. In those uses the patterns are suspended, correctly. The first question for any if-sentence is therefore whether it is predictive at all. If it is not, the right tenses are simply the true ones, and correcting them damages good English.","Will after if is acceptable whenever it carries modal meaning, not plain future time. It can mean willingness, which only a person has: if you'll wait here. Stressed and uncontracted, it means insistence, usually as criticism: if you will stay up so late, of course you feel tired. Would makes a formal request more courteous: I'd be grateful if you would reply. Only a will that means nothing more than in the future is an error: the if-clause sets a scene and does not predict.","Real past conditionals report facts. If it rained, we stayed in pairs two past simples because both events occurred, repeatedly; if is close to whenever. A real past possibility, as in if I was rude, I apologise, is open, not imagined: the speaker accepts that it may have happened. That is why was is required and were would be an overcorrection, and why the result can be in any tense, including the present. No Truth line exists, because nothing is denied.","Evidence conditionals move from a clue to a conclusion: if her bag is here, she's still at school; if she said that, she was joking. The if-clause conditions the conclusion, not an event, so the tenses match whatever the clue and the conclusion are. Speech-act conditionals, such as there's water in the fridge if you're thirsty, condition the relevance of speaking. The main clause is true regardless, which is why no will is needed and why adding then would spoil it.","Finally, reduced chunks behave like vocabulary. If so and if not stand for a whole clause and need an earlier idea to point back to. If necessary, if possible and if in doubt are shortened clauses, with it is or you are left out. If any attaches to few or little and sits between commas: few, if any, seats are left. Learn each with its position and its commas."],"examples":[{"s":"If you'll wait here, I'll fetch the key to the music room.","g":"Modal will after if: willingness, not future time. Real, so no Truth line."},{"s":"I'd be grateful if you would check my essay plan.","g":"Courteous would in the if-clause of a request; a fixed formal pattern. No Truth line."},{"s":"If it rained, we stayed in and played cards.","g":"Real past conditional: two past simples reporting repeated facts. Nothing is denied, so no Truth line."},{"s":"If her bag is here, she's still at school.","g":"Epistemic: the if-clause supports a conclusion, not an event. Tenses follow the facts. No Truth line."},{"s":"There's water in the fridge if you're thirsty.","g":"Speech-act conditional: if conditions the relevance of the offer, not the water. No Truth line."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w8l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"Is Ms Carter still in school? I need to hand in my project."},{"who":"Jane","text":"If the lights in the music room are off, she has already gone home."}],"stem":"Is Jane's sentence fine, or does it need fixing?","options":["Fix it: the result needs will, so write she will go home.","Fix it: write If the lights were off, she would go home.","Leave it: Jane reasons from a clue to a conclusion.","Leave it, but only in casual American English."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This is not a prediction about the future. Jane is working out what has already happened.","Nothing is imagined here, so there is no step back. The lights are a real clue.","Yes: it is an evidence conditional, so any tenses that fit the facts are right.","Evidence conditionals are standard English everywhere, in speech and in writing."],"hint":"Is Jane predicting, imagining, or working something out?","why":"Jane uses the lights as evidence for a conclusion about now. It is not a prediction, so the type patterns do not apply and nothing needs fixing."},{"id":"w8l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Aunt Noi's note","text":"There are clean towels on the top shelf if you want a shower after practice."}],"stem":"What does the if-part do here?","options":["It says why she mentions the towels; they are there anyway.","It says the towels will be there only when you want a shower.","It shows the towels are imagined and not really on the shelf.","It shows a mistake: the note needs there will be clean towels."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: the if-part makes the offer relevant. The main clause is simply true.","The towels do not depend on your shower. They are on the shelf either way.","Both verbs are present and real. There is no step back and no Truth line.","Nothing needs fixing. This kind of \"if\" does not predict, so no \"will\" is needed."],"hint":"Would the towels still be on the shelf if you did not want a shower?","why":"It is an offer: the towels are on the shelf whatever happens, and the if-part only explains why Aunt Noi is telling you."},{"id":"w8l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Mook, at badminton practice","text":"If you'll hold my racket for a second, I'll tie my shoelace."}],"stem":"Which statement about Mook's sentence is true?","options":["It is wrong: will can never follow if.","It is right: will after if suits any future event.","It is wrong: it should be If you would have held.","It is right: will here means are willing to."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Too strict. \"Will\" after \"if\" is fine when it carries its own meaning, such as willingness.","Too loose. For a plain future event, \"if\" takes a present form.","That mixes an imagined past into a simple request about now.","Yes: \"if you'll hold\" means if you agree to hold. It is a polite request."],"hint":"What job is will doing here: time, or something else?","why":"Here will means be willing to, so the sentence is a polite request. Will is only wrong after if when it just means in the future."},{"id":"w8l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"I loved our primary school trips."}],"stem":"If we ___ time on the way back, the driver always stopped for ice cream.","options":["had had","had","have","would have"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["\"Had had\" is for an imagined past. On these trips they really did have time, again and again.","Yes: a real past habit takes the past simple in both halves. \"If\" means whenever here.","The trips are over and the result is past: stopped. The condition must be past too.","\"Would\" does not go in the if half, and nothing here is imagined."],"hint":"Did these trips really happen? What tense is the result?","why":"This is a real past habit, not an imagined past: if means whenever, and both verbs take the past simple."},{"id":"w8l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"Which sentence uses will after if correctly?","options":["If it'll rain during the match, we'll all get very wet.","If the bell will ring, we'll go back to class.","If you'll follow me, I'll show you to the exam room.","If I'll pass the test, Mum will be pleased."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Rain cannot be willing or unwilling. It is a plain future event, so say \"if it rains\".","A bell cannot agree to ring. Plain future: \"if the bell rings\".","Yes: \"if you'll follow me\" means if you are willing to follow me, a polite request.","Passing a test is not a matter of being willing. Plain future: \"if I pass\"."],"hint":"In which sentence does will mean more than future time?","why":"Only a person can be willing. If you'll follow me means if you agree to; the other three are plain future conditions and need a present form."},{"id":"w8l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"[[If you would]] take a seat, Ms Carter [[will see]] you shortly; [[if it will be]] urgent, [[please knock]] on her door.","answer":2,"fix":"if it is","fb":["\"If you would\" is a polite request, so \"would\" is right here.","\"Will see\" is a real promise in the result. That is where \"will\" belongs.","This \"will\" carries no willingness or politeness, and \"it\" cannot be willing. Use a present form: if it is.","An imperative is a fine result for a real condition."],"hint":"Two verbs follow if. Which one is polite, and which is only about time?","why":"Would after if is fine as a polite request, but the second if-clause is a plain condition, so it takes the present: if it is urgent."},{"id":"w8l3-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Teacher's comment","text":"A careful final draft, Nan. Well done."}],"stem":"There are few, ___, spelling mistakes in this essay.","options":["if so","if not","if any","if necessary"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["\"If so\" points back to a whole earlier idea. It cannot sit after \"few\".","\"If not\" points back to an earlier idea too. It makes no sense between \"few\" and its noun.","Yes: \"few, if any\" means very few and perhaps none. Note the commas on both sides.","\"If necessary\" means if it is needed. Nobody needs spelling mistakes."],"hint":"What is the teacher saying about the number of mistakes?","why":"Few, if any, means very few and perhaps none at all. The chunk follows few or little and sits between two commas."},{"id":"w8l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","given":"Is the museum open on Mondays? If it is open on Mondays, let's go then.","keyword":"SO","stem":"Is the museum open on Mondays? ___, let's go then.","accept":["If so"],"model":"If so","hint":"Two words that point back to the question before them.","why":"If so means if that is true, here if the museum is open on Mondays. It stands for the whole clause."}],"world":"w8"},"w9l1":{"id":"w9l1","name":"Same meaning, new words: rewriting with a key word","cefr":"B2","ctype":"mix","formula":{"if":"Sentence one, plus a key word you must not change","result":"Sentence two: fill the gap with 2–5 words, same meaning"},"uses":[{"use":"Swapping if … not for unless","ex":"I won't go to the party unless Fah goes too."},{"use":"Turning but only if into a deal","ex":"You can have the window seat provided you wake me at our stop."},{"use":"Turning because it might into a precaution","ex":"I'll save your number in case I get lost."},{"use":"Turning a past fact into an imagined past","ex":"If we had left earlier, we wouldn't have missed the first song."},{"use":"Turning I'm sorry into a wish","ex":"If only I hadn't left my umbrella on the BTS."},{"use":"Turning without into if it weren't for","ex":"If it weren't for my alarm, I would sleep until noon."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Say the same idea with the key word: never change that word, and write two to five words.","body":["In this exam task you get a full sentence and a key word. You also get a second sentence with a gap. Fill the gap so that both sentences mean the same. There are two rules. Never change the key word, not even one letter. Write between two and five words. In Cambridge exams, a short form like <em>didn't</em> counts as two words. First ask: what is true in sentence one, and when?","Some swaps only change the linking word. <em>If … not</em> becomes <b>unless</b>, and the <em>not</em> goes away: <em>We'll be late unless we run</em>. <em>But only if</em> becomes <b>as long as</b> or <b>provided (that)</b>. It is a deal. <em>Because it might happen</em> becomes <b>in case</b>: you do something first, to be safe. Plain <em>if</em> would mean you wait until it happens. After all these words use a present verb, never <em>will</em>.","Some swaps turn a fact into an imagined sentence. A fact about now goes one step back. Take <em>She can't swim, so she isn't in the team</em>. It becomes <em>If she could swim, she would be in the team</em>. A fact about the past goes two steps back. Use <em>if</em> with <em>had</em> and the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em> and V3. Flip each half too. A <em>didn't</em> half becomes <em>had</em>, and a <em>did</em> half becomes <em>hadn't</em>.","<em>I'm sorry</em> and <em>I regret</em> become <b>I wish</b> or <b>If only</b>. For a past regret, use <em>had</em> and V3, and flip again. <em>I'm sorry I shouted</em> becomes <em>I wish I hadn't shouted</em>. A past simple after <em>wish</em> is different. It is about now: <em>I wish I had a pet</em>. So it cannot rewrite something that happened yesterday.","Two more swaps finish the set. The first one is formal. <em>If I had known</em> becomes <b>Had I known</b>: <em>had</em> comes first and <em>if</em> disappears. The result half stays the same, so it still needs <em>would have</em> and V3. <b>Without</b> and a noun becomes <b>if it weren't for</b> and the same noun. An example is <em>If it weren't for the fan, this room would be too hot</em>. Then count your words again."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had checked the timetable, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"It starts as: I didn't check the timetable, so I missed the bus. Truth: I didn't check, and I missed it — past."},{"s":"You can use my charger as long as you bring it back.","g":"It starts as: You can use it, but only if you bring it back. A deal, so nothing is imagined."},{"s":"Bring a jacket in case the cinema is cold.","g":"It starts as: Bring a jacket because the cinema might be cold. You pack it first, to be safe."},{"s":"I wish I had listened to the weather forecast.","g":"It starts as: I'm sorry I didn't listen to the forecast. Truth: I didn't listen — past."},{"s":"Had I seen your message, I would have waited for you.","g":"It starts as: If I had seen your message. Truth: I didn't see it, so I didn't wait — past."}]},"B2":{"key":"A key-word transformation keeps the meaning and changes the grammar: keep the key word exactly and use two to five words.","body":["A key-word transformation gives you a complete sentence, a key word and a second sentence with a gap. Your answer must make the second sentence mean the same as the first. Two rules decide the marks: use the key word exactly as it is given, and write between two and five words. Cambridge exams count a short form such as <em>didn't</em> as two words. Before you write, find the facts in sentence one: what is true, and when is it true?","Three swaps change only the connector. <em>If … not</em> becomes <b>unless</b>, which already contains the negative, so the <em>not</em> disappears: <em>We'll be late unless we run</em>. <em>But only if</em> becomes <b>as long as</b> or <b>provided (that)</b>, a deal between two people. <em>Because something might happen</em> becomes <b>in case</b>, a precaution you take beforehand; plain <em>if</em> would mean you wait until it happens. All of them take a present verb for the future, never <em>will</em>.","Other swaps turn a fact into an imagined sentence, so the verbs step back. A present fact takes one step: <em>She can't swim, so she isn't in the team</em> becomes <em>If she could swim, she would be in the team</em>. A past fact takes two: <em>if</em> with <em>had</em> and the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em> and V3. Each half also flips, because you are imagining the opposite: <em>didn't</em> becomes <em>had</em>, and <em>did</em> becomes <em>hadn't</em>.","<em>I'm sorry</em>, <em>I regret</em> and <em>it's a pity</em> become <b>I wish</b> or <b>If only</b>, with the same steps and the same flip. A past regret takes <em>had</em> and V3: <em>I'm sorry I shouted</em> becomes <em>I wish I hadn't shouted</em>. A past simple after <em>wish</em> is different: it is about now, as in <em>I wish I had a pet</em>, so it cannot rewrite something that happened yesterday.","Two more swaps complete the set, and the first is formal. <em>If I had known</em> becomes <b>Had I known</b>: <em>had</em> moves to the front and <em>if</em> disappears, but the result half does not change, so it still needs <em>would have</em> and V3. <b>Without</b> and a noun becomes <b>if it weren't for</b> and the same noun: <em>If it weren't for the fan, this room would be too hot</em>. Finally, read both sentences again and count your words."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had checked the timetable, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"From: I didn't check the timetable, so I missed the bus. Two steps back, both halves flipped. Truth: I didn't check, and I missed it — past."},{"s":"You can use my charger as long as you bring it back.","g":"From: You can use it, but only if you bring it back. As long as states the deal; nothing is imagined."},{"s":"Bring a jacket in case the cinema is cold.","g":"From: Bring a jacket because the cinema might be cold. In case is a precaution taken beforehand."},{"s":"I wish I had listened to the weather forecast.","g":"From: I'm sorry I didn't listen to the forecast. Wish with had and V3, flipped. Truth: I didn't listen — past."},{"s":"Had I seen your message, I would have waited for you.","g":"From: If I had seen your message. Had moves to the front and if goes. Truth: I didn't see it, so I didn't wait — past."}]},"C1":{"key":"A transformation tests whether you can hold the meaning still while the grammar moves: same facts, same time, the key word untouched, two to five words.","body":["In a key-word transformation the meaning is fixed and the grammar must move around one given word. That word may not be changed in any way, so <em>provided</em> cannot become <em>providing</em>. The gap takes two to five words, and Cambridge exams count a contraction as two. The safest method is to read sentence one for its facts before you look at the gap: what actually happened or is true, and at what time? Those facts are the Truth that your new sentence has to keep.","Connector swaps keep the verbs and change the logic word. <b>Unless</b> means except if, so it absorbs the negative of <em>if … not</em>; a second <em>not</em> reverses the sense. <b>As long as</b> and <b>provided (that)</b> restate <em>but only if</em> as a bargain. <b>In case</b> restates <em>because it might happen</em>: the action comes first, as a precaution, whereas <em>if</em> would make the action wait for the event. Since these clauses only set the scene, they take a present form for future time, never <em>will</em>.","When sentence one states a fact with <em>so</em> or <em>because</em>, the rewrite imagines the opposite, and the verbs step back to show it. A present fact needs one step: <em>She can't swim, so she isn't in the team</em> becomes <em>If she could swim, she would be in the team</em>. A past fact needs two: <em>had</em> with the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em> with V3. Because the new sentence denies the facts, each half changes polarity: <em>didn't</em> gives <em>had</em>, <em>did</em> gives <em>hadn't</em>.","Regret verbs go to <b>I wish</b> or <b>If only</b>, which belong to the same family and follow the same steps. A regret about a finished action takes <em>had</em> and V3 with reversed polarity: <em>I'm sorry I shouted</em> becomes <em>I wish I hadn't shouted</em>. A past simple after <em>wish</em> refers to the present or to habits, as in <em>I wish I had a pet</em>, so it cannot restate a single past event, however natural it may sound in fast speech.","Two further equivalents are worth knowing, and the first is formal. <b>Had I known</b> is <em>If I had known</em> with the auxiliary moved to the front and <em>if</em> removed; only the condition changes, so the result keeps <em>would have</em> and V3, and a negative stays uncontracted, as in <em>Had I not known</em>. <b>If it weren't for</b> and a noun restates <em>without</em> and that noun for the present; for a past situation the form is <em>if it hadn't been for</em>. Check the word count last."],"examples":[{"s":"If I had checked the timetable, I wouldn't have missed the bus.","g":"From: I didn't check the timetable, so I missed the bus. Past facts, so two steps back with reversed polarity. Truth: I didn't check, and I missed it — past."},{"s":"You can use my charger as long as you bring it back.","g":"From: You can use it, but only if you bring it back. A bargain restated; an open condition, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"Bring a jacket in case the cinema is cold.","g":"From: Bring a jacket because the cinema might be cold. The action precedes the possible event, which is what in case means."},{"s":"I wish I had listened to the weather forecast.","g":"From: I'm sorry I didn't listen to the forecast. Wish with the past perfect, polarity reversed. Truth: I didn't listen — past."},{"s":"Had I seen your message, I would have waited for you.","g":"From: If I had seen your message. Inversion replaces if; the result is unchanged. Truth: I didn't see it, so I didn't wait — past."}]}},"thai":"เว้นแต่ = unless · ตราบใดที่ = as long as · เผื่อ = in case","items":[{"id":"w9l1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mint didn't set an alarm last night, so she missed the school van this morning. Which sentence means the same?","options":["If Mint set an alarm, she wouldn't miss the school van.","If Mint had set an alarm, she wouldn't have missed the school van.","If Mint hadn't set an alarm, she would have missed the school van.","If Mint sets an alarm, she won't miss the school van."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["One step back is about now or the future. Last night and this morning are past, so the verbs need two steps.","Yes: both facts are past and both are flipped. She didn't set it, so had set; she missed the van, so wouldn't have missed.","Right time, wrong flip. This says she did set the alarm and did catch the van.","This is a real plan for the future. Sentence one reports what has already happened."],"hint":"When did it happen? Then check each half: did it happen or not?","why":"Both facts are past, so the rewrite takes two steps back, and each half flips: didn't set becomes had set, missed becomes wouldn't have missed.","truth":"Mint didn't set an alarm, and she missed the van."},{"id":"w9l1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Take a power bank to the concert because your phone might die. Which sentence means the same?","options":["Take a power bank to the concert in case your phone dies.","Take a power bank to the concert if your phone dies.","Take a power bank to the concert unless your phone dies.","Take a power bank to the concert provided your phone dies."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: in case means pack it now, before you know, because the phone might die later.","With if, you only take it once the phone has died. By then it is too late to pack anything.","Unless means except if, so this says: leave the power bank at home when the phone dies.","Provided makes a deal: take it only when the phone dies. Nobody plans it that way."],"hint":"Do you pack the power bank before the problem or after it?","why":"Because it might happen shows a precaution taken beforehand, and that is exactly the meaning of in case."},{"id":"w9l1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"I'm sorry I told Bam about the surprise party yesterday. Which sentence means the same?","options":["I wish I had told Bam about the surprise party.","I wish I didn't tell Bam about the surprise party.","I wish I hadn't told Bam about the surprise party.","I wish I could tell Bam about the surprise party."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["Right tense, wrong flip. This says she kept the secret and now regrets keeping it.","A past simple after wish is about now or about habits. This regret is about yesterday, so it needs had and V3.","Yes: she did tell Bam yesterday, so the wish goes two steps back and turns negative.","This is a wish about now: she wants to tell Bam but can't. In sentence one she has already told her."],"hint":"Did she tell Bam or not? And when?","why":"She told Bam yesterday and regrets it, so wish takes had and V3, flipped to the negative: hadn't told.","truth":"She told Bam yesterday."},{"id":"w9l1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Key word: PROVIDED. Sentence one: You can borrow my calculator, but only if you give it back before maths. Sentence two: You can borrow my calculator ___ back before maths. Which answer gets the marks?","options":["providing you give it","provided you give it","provided that you promise to give it","as long as you give it"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The meaning is right, but the key word has been changed. It must stay exactly as it is given.","Yes: the key word is unchanged, the gap has four words, and the deal means the same.","Seven words is over the limit of five, and promise is a new idea that sentence one never had.","The meaning is the same, but the key word is missing, so the answer scores nothing."],"hint":"Check the two exam rules: the key word, and the number of words.","why":"This answer keeps the key word unchanged, stays within two to five words and keeps the meaning of but only if."},{"id":"w9l1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Key word: UNLESS. Sentence one: If you don't show your student card, you won't get the discount. Sentence two: You won't get the discount ___ your student card.","options":["unless you don't show","unless you will show","unless you show","unless you showed"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Unless already means if not. With a second not, the meaning turns upside down.","Like if, unless takes a present verb for the future. Will stays in the result half.","Yes: unless replaces if and not together, and the verb stays in the present.","Sentence one has a present verb, don't show, because the rule is real. A past form changes the time, so the meaning is not the same."],"hint":"What happens to not when if becomes unless?","why":"Unless means if not, so the not disappears, and the verb stays in the present because the rule is real."},{"id":"w9l1-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"I didn't know the café was closed on Mondays, so I walked all the way there yesterday."}],"stem":"[[Had I known]] the café [[was closed]] on Mondays, I [[wouldn't walk]] all the way there [[yesterday]].","answer":2,"fix":"wouldn't have walked","fb":["This part is fine: had comes first and if disappears. It means If I had known.","This part is fine: the café really is closed on Mondays, so a plain past form after known works.","Yes, this is the slip: yesterday is past, so the result needs the have ticket: wouldn't have walked.","This part is fine: yesterday is the time cue. It tells you the walk is in the past."],"hint":"When was the walk? Check that every verb agrees with that time.","why":"Had I known is only a formal If I had known, so the result about yesterday still needs would have and V3.","truth":"Fern didn't know, and she walked there yesterday."},{"id":"w9l1-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","given":"Without my glasses, I couldn't read the board from the back row.","keyword":"WEREN'T","stem":"If it ___ my glasses, I couldn't read the board from the back row.","accept":["weren't for"],"model":"weren't for","hint":"Without is followed by a noun. What links your verb to that noun?","why":"Without and a noun means if it weren't for and the same noun: she has her glasses, so the idea is imagined now.","truth":"She has her glasses, so she can read the board — now."},{"id":"w9l1-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","given":"We didn't book early last week, so we didn't get seats together.","keyword":"HAD","stem":"If we ___ early last week, we would have got seats together.","accept":["had booked"],"model":"had booked","hint":"Sentence one is a past fact. Flip it, then step back twice.","why":"Didn't book is a past fact, so the imagined condition goes two steps back and turns positive: had and the past participle, booked.","truth":"They didn't book early, and they didn't get seats together."}],"world":"w9"},"w9l2":{"id":"w9l2","name":"Conditionals in essays and speaking (IELTS)","cefr":"C1","ctype":"mix","formula":{"if":"If + present (essay: full forms, comma after the if half)","result":"may / might / could + verb to hedge · would + verb to propose"},"uses":[{"use":"A careful prediction (a hedge)","ex":"If this trend continues, prices may rise."},{"use":"A proposal with would","ex":"Free bus passes would help students who live far from school."},{"use":"Setting a condition in an argument","ex":"Online lessons work well provided that every student has a quiet place."},{"use":"Naming the one exception","ex":"The air will not improve unless more people leave their cars at home."},{"use":"Conceding a point","ex":"Even if tickets were free, some people would still drive."},{"use":"Speaking: natural short forms","ex":"If I had more free time, I'd learn to play the guitar."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"In essays, be correct before you are clever: hedge with may or might, propose with would, and write full forms.","body":["In an essay you give opinions about what could happen. So do not sound too sure. <em>Will</em> sounds completely sure. A present result and <em>going to</em> sound sure too. <em>May</em>, <em>might</em> and <em>could</em> say that something is possible. This careful style is called hedging. After <em>if</em>, keep a present verb, as always: <em>If rents go up, some shops may close</em>.","Use <em>would</em> for a proposal (an idea that is not real yet). You do not always need <em>if</em>: <em>A new park would give children a safe place to play</em>. With <em>if</em>, step back in the first half and write <em>if the city built a park</em>. Never put <em>would</em> in the <em>if</em> half. Stay in one world too. A real sentence has a present verb with <em>will</em> or <em>may</em>. An imagined one has a past form with <em>would</em>.","Three linking words make an argument stronger. <b>Provided that</b> sets a condition and means only if. <b>Unless</b> means if not: it names the one thing that can change the result. <b>Even if</b> says the result stays the same anyway. Compare: <em>The plan will fail unless people support it. Even if people support it, it will be expensive.</em> Always read the sentence before, then choose by meaning.","A formal opening can replace <em>if</em>: <em>Were the city to build more parks, families might go out more</em>. The order is <em>were</em>, then the subject, then <em>to</em> and the verb. There is no <em>if</em>. Use this once in an essay, not more. Do not push in a third conditional to look clever. It is only for an imagined past. Examiners want correct sentences first. A correct simple sentence beats a wrong difficult one.","Writing and speaking are different. In an essay, write full forms: <em>do not</em>, <em>would not</em>, <em>they would</em>. In the speaking test, short forms are natural: <em>I'd</em>, <em>I'll</em>, <em>don't</em>. Last, check the comma. When the <em>if</em> half comes first, put a comma after it. When the <em>if</em> half comes second, you need no comma. The two halves are one sentence, so never put a full stop between them."],"examples":[{"s":"If online shopping keeps growing, small shops may struggle.","g":"Real and careful: may, not will. No Truth line, because nothing is imagined."},{"s":"A later start would give students more sleep.","g":"A proposal with no if. Truth: lessons do not start later — now."},{"s":"Homework is useful provided that students understand its purpose.","g":"A condition: only if they understand. Real, so no Truth line."},{"s":"Were schools to close their libraries, many students would have nowhere quiet to study.","g":"Formal: were, subject, to and verb, no if. Truth: schools are not closing their libraries — now."},{"s":"<s>If the city will lower fares, more people would use the trains.</s>","g":"Two slips: will after if, and will mixed with would. Write: If the city lowered fares, more people would use the trains. Truth: fares are not lower — now."}]},"B2":{"key":"In an essay, accuracy earns more than rare grammar: hedge real conditionals, use would for proposals, and keep to full forms.","body":["An essay argues about what is likely, so a good writer avoids sounding certain. <em>Will</em>, <em>going to</em> and a present simple result all present the outcome as sure. <em>May</em>, <em>might</em> and <em>could</em> present it as possible, and this is called hedging. The condition itself does not change: it keeps a present verb, never <em>will</em>, as in <em>If rents go up, some shops may close</em>. A hedged real conditional is the most useful sentence you can put in an essay.","<em>Would</em> introduces a proposal, an idea that is not real yet. It often needs no <em>if</em> at all: in <em>A new park would give children a safe place to play</em>, the condition, if the city built one, is understood. When you do write the condition, step it back to the past simple and keep <em>would</em> out of it: <em>if the city built a park</em>. Stay in one world in each sentence: present with <em>will</em> or <em>may</em> for real, past form with <em>would</em> for imagined.","Three connectors sharpen an argument. <b>Provided that</b> sets the condition on which something works, meaning only if. <b>Unless</b> means if not, and names the single thing that could change the result. <b>Even if</b> concedes a point and says the result stays the same anyway. Compare: <em>The plan will fail unless people support it. Even if people support it, it will be expensive.</em> The sentence before the gap usually tells you which meaning the writer needs.","For a formal touch, <em>if the city built</em> can become <em>Were the city to build more parks, families might go out more</em>: <em>were</em>, the subject, <em>to</em> and the verb, with no <em>if</em>. One inversion in an essay is enough; several sound forced. In the same way, do not force a third conditional into an essay to show range, since it belongs only to an imagined past. Examiners reward accuracy first, so a correct common form earns more than a rare form with a mistake.","Register matters as well. Essays use full forms, such as <em>do not</em>, <em>would not</em> and <em>they would</em>, while in the speaking test contractions like <em>I'd</em>, <em>I'll</em> and <em>don't</em> are natural and sound fluent. Finally, punctuate the sentence: when the <em>if</em> half comes first, a comma follows it; when it comes second, there is normally no comma. Either way the two halves form one sentence, so a full stop never separates them."],"examples":[{"s":"If online shopping keeps growing, small shops may struggle.","g":"A hedged real conditional: present after if, may in the result. Nothing is imagined, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"A later start would give students more sleep.","g":"A proposal; the condition is understood. Truth: lessons do not start later — now."},{"s":"Homework is useful provided that students understand its purpose.","g":"Provided that sets the condition: only if they understand. Real, so no Truth line."},{"s":"Were schools to close their libraries, many students would have nowhere quiet to study.","g":"One formal inversion: were, subject, to and verb, with no if. Truth: schools are not closing their libraries — now."},{"s":"<s>If the city will lower fares, more people would use the trains.</s>","g":"Will after if, then will mixed with would. Choose one world: If the city lowered fares, more people would use the trains. Truth: fares are not lower — now."}]},"C1":{"key":"In academic writing a conditional is a tool for controlled claims: hedge what is real, mark proposals with would, and never trade accuracy for display.","body":["Academic argument deals in likelihood, not certainty, so the result clause should carry the writer's degree of confidence. <em>Will</em>, <em>going to</em> and the present simple all commit you to the outcome; <em>may</em>, <em>might</em> and <em>could</em> hedge it. The condition clause is untouched by this choice: it only sets the scene, so it keeps a present form, as in <em>If rents go up, some shops may close</em>. The hedged real conditional is the workhorse of a good essay because it is easy to get right.","<em>Would</em> marks a proposal as one step away from present reality, and the condition is frequently left unstated: <em>A new park would give children a safe place to play</em> implies if one were built. When the condition is stated, it takes the past simple, never <em>would</em>: <em>if the city built a park</em>. Choose one stance per sentence: a present form with <em>will</em> or <em>may</em> for real, a past form with <em>would</em> for imagined. Mixing them leaves the reader unsure which world you mean.","Connectors let you state the logic precisely. <b>Provided that</b> gives the condition under which a claim holds, meaning only if. <b>Unless</b> means except if, and isolates the one circumstance that would alter the result. <b>Even if</b> concedes a possibility while insisting that the result is unaffected. Compare: <em>The plan will fail unless people support it. Even if people support it, it will be expensive.</em> The choice depends on the argument around the sentence, not on the grammar inside it.","Inversion gives a formal alternative to <em>if</em> with a past form: <em>Were the city to build more parks, families might go out more</em>. The order is <em>were</em>, subject, <em>to</em> and the verb, and <em>if</em> is dropped; only <em>were</em>, <em>had</em> and <em>should</em> can open a conditional this way. Use it once at most, because repeated inversion reads as display. Likewise, the third conditional describes an imagined past, so forcing one into an argument about the future produces errors. Accuracy is rewarded before range.","Register separates the two halves of the exam. In essays, write every auxiliary in full, such as <em>do not</em>, <em>would not</em> and <em>they would</em>; in the speaking test, contractions like <em>I'd</em>, <em>I'll</em> and <em>don't</em> are what a fluent speaker uses, and avoiding them sounds stiff. The comma is a convention, not grammar, but examiners expect it: after a condition that comes first, and normally not before one that comes second. A full stop between the clauses is an error, since a condition cannot stand alone."],"examples":[{"s":"If online shopping keeps growing, small shops may struggle.","g":"An open condition with a hedged result: the writer commits to a possibility only. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"A later start would give students more sleep.","g":"A proposal with an unstated condition; would marks the distance from the present. Truth: lessons do not start later — now."},{"s":"Homework is useful provided that students understand its purpose.","g":"Provided that limits the claim to one condition. An open condition, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"Were schools to close their libraries, many students would have nowhere quiet to study.","g":"Inversion with were and to, replacing if with a past form; once per essay. Truth: schools are not closing their libraries — now."},{"s":"<s>If the city will lower fares, more people would use the trains.</s>","g":"A scene-setting clause cannot take will, and the stance changes mid-sentence. Imagined throughout: If the city lowered fares, more people would use the trains. Truth: fares are not lower — now."}]}},"items":[{"id":"w9l2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"In an essay, a hedged sentence is careful: the writer is not completely sure. Which sentence is hedged?","options":["If more people cycle to work, the air will become cleaner.","If more people cycle to work, the air becomes cleaner.","If more people cycle to work, the air may become cleaner.","If more people cycle to work, the air is going to become cleaner."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Will is a confident prediction. It leaves no room for doubt.","A present result states a fact that is always true. That is the opposite of careful.","Yes: may says the cleaner air is possible, not certain.","Going to sounds just as sure as will. There is no hedge here."],"hint":"Look only at the result half. Which verb leaves room for doubt?","why":"May shows a possible result, while will, going to and the present simple all present the result as certain."},{"id":"w9l2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"mix","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence is too informal for an essay?","options":["If schools started later, students would not be so tired in class.","Unless fares fall, most families will continue to travel by car.","If students had more free time, they'd probably read more.","Provided that parks are safe, children may play outside more."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This one suits an essay: would not is written in full.","This one suits an essay: unless with a present verb, and no short forms.","Yes: they'd is a short form. In an essay, write they would.","This one suits an essay: provided that is formal, and may is a careful hedge."],"hint":"Formal writing spells every word out. Look closely at the verbs.","why":"They'd is a contraction, and essays use full forms; the other three sentences have no short forms."},{"id":"w9l2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"From an essay: Teaching is the missing piece. Recycling bins will not solve the problem ___ people learn how to use them.","options":["even if","unless","provided that","if"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Even if would mean that learning changes nothing. But the writer calls teaching the missing piece.","Yes: unless names the one thing that can change the result. With no teaching, the bins fail.","Provided that would make learning the condition for failure. That turns the argument upside down.","If would say the bins fail when people do learn. The writer means the opposite."],"hint":"Read the sentence before the gap. Does learning change the result or not?","why":"The writer says teaching is what is missing, so learning is the one thing that changes the result: if not, which is unless."},{"id":"w9l2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"If the number of cars in Bangkok ___ to grow, journeys to school may take even longer.","options":["will continue","continued","would continue","continues"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["No will after if. The if half takes a present verb; the careful may is already in the result.","A past form makes the sentence imagined, but may belongs to a real one. Stay in one world.","Would never goes in the if half, and the result here is real, with may.","Yes: a present verb after if, even for the future, and may to hedge the result."],"hint":"Is this sentence real or imagined? Look at the result half, then choose the verb after if.","why":"The result uses may, so the sentence is real, and a real if half about the future takes the present simple."},{"id":"w9l2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"Few schools give breakfast at the moment. ___ free breakfast, fewer students might arrive hungry.","options":["Were schools to offer","If were schools to offer","Were schools offer","Would schools to offer"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: were, then the subject, then to and the verb, with no if.","The inverted form replaces if. Keep one or the other, never both.","The pattern needs to before the verb: were, subject, to, verb.","This formal opening is built on were, followed by the subject and to. Would cannot start it."],"hint":"This opening replaces if. Check how it is built, one word at a time.","why":"The formal pattern is were, the subject, to and the verb, with no if; it means if schools offered.","truth":"Few schools offer free breakfast now."},{"id":"w9l2-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Our city has very few bike lanes. [[If there would be]] more of them, [[far more people]] [[would cycle]] [[to work or school]].","answer":0,"fix":"If there were","fb":["Yes, this is the slip: would never goes in the if half. One step back there is a past form: were.","This part is fine: it is simply the subject of the result half.","This part is fine: would and a verb is the right result for an idea that is not real yet.","This part is fine: cycle to work or school is correct."],"hint":"The idea is imagined. Does each half show that in the right way?","why":"There are few lanes now, so the if half steps back to a past form, were; would belongs only in the result.","truth":"The city has very few bike lanes now."},{"id":"w9l2-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fares are high at the moment. Which sentence is written correctly for an essay?","options":["If public transport were cheaper. Fewer people would drive.","If public transport were cheaper, fewer people would drive.","If public transport were cheaper, fewer people'd drive.","If public transport would be cheaper, fewer people would drive."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The verbs are right, but a full stop cuts the if half off from its result. Join the two halves with a comma.","Yes: a past form after if, would in the result, a comma after the if half and no short forms.","People'd is a short form from fast speech. Essays use the full form.","Would never goes in the if half. One step back there gives were."],"hint":"Check three things: the verb after if, the punctuation and short forms.","why":"It has were in the if half, a comma after that half and would written in full, which is what an essay needs.","truth":"Public transport is not cheaper now."},{"id":"w9l2-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","given":"If lessons started at nine, we'd get more sleep.","stem":"Change this spoken sentence into an essay sentence with no short forms: If lessons started at nine, students ___ more sleep.","accept":["would get"],"model":"would get","hint":"The grammar stays the same. What changes when you move from speaking to an essay?","why":"In speech we'd is natural, but an essay uses the full form: would get.","truth":"Lessons do not start at nine now."}],"world":"w9"},"w9l3":{"id":"w9l3","name":"Mixed mastery: find and fix across all worlds","cefr":"B2","ctype":"mix","formula":{"if":"Check 1: real or imagined? Check 2: when is each half?","result":"Then build each half: no will or would after if; have + V3 for an imagined past"},"uses":[{"use":"A fact, habit or rule (type 0)","ex":"If I drink bubble tea at night, I can't sleep."},{"use":"A real plan or warning (type 1)","ex":"If the rain stops, we'll walk to the BTS."},{"use":"A dream or advice (type 2)","ex":"If I had a cat, I'd call her Mochi."},{"use":"Regret or relief about the past (type 3)","ex":"If Pim had checked the map, we wouldn't have got lost."},{"use":"A past cause with a result now (mixed 3/2)","ex":"If we had taken the BTS, we would be there by now."},{"use":"A wish instead of an if","ex":"I wish I had brought an umbrella."}],"levels":{"B1":{"key":"Before you fix any sentence, ask two questions: is it real or imagined, and when is each half?","body":["Every conditional answers two questions. Is it real or imagined? When is each half: past, now or future? Look for cue words such as <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em>, <em>now</em>, <em>still</em>, <em>tomorrow</em> and <em>always</em>. An imagined sentence has a hidden Truth line. Write it and date it. A real sentence has no Truth line, because nothing is denied.","Real sentences use present verbs after <em>if</em>. For a fact, habit or rule, the result is present too (type 0, the zero conditional). Remember the <em>-s</em> for she, he or one person. For a possible future, the result has <em>will</em>, <em>might</em> or <em>can</em> (type 1, the first conditional). <em>Will</em> never goes after <em>if</em>. The same is true after <em>when</em>, <em>as soon as</em> and <em>unless</em>.","Imagined sentences step back. For an imagined now, use a past form after <em>if</em>, with <em>were</em> for <em>am</em> and <em>is</em>. The result is <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> and a verb. This is type 2, the second conditional. For an imagined past, use <em>had</em> and the past participle (V3). The result is <em>would have</em> or <em>could have</em> and V3. This is type 3, the third conditional. The word <em>have</em> is the past ticket. <em>Would</em> never goes in the <em>if</em> half.","Sometimes the two halves have different times. Then build each half alone. A past condition with a result now is mixed 3/2. Use <em>had</em> and V3, then <em>would</em> or <em>could</em> and a verb. There is no <em>have</em>. A condition that is always true with a past result is mixed 2/3. Use a past form such as <em>were</em>, then <em>would have</em> and V3. Name the type from the time of each half.","Three more checks. <em>Unless</em> means if not, so never add a second <em>not</em>. After <em>wish</em>, step back too. Use a past form for now, and <em>had</em> and V3 for a past regret. Use <em>would</em> for a change you want. A formal sentence can start with <em>Had</em>, <em>Were</em> or <em>Should</em> and no <em>if</em>. Put <em>if</em> back in your head, and the checks are the same."],"examples":[{"s":"If Nan misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"Type 0: a habit, present in both halves. No Truth line, because nothing is denied."},{"s":"If I had my phone with me, I'd call Mum now.","g":"Type 2: one step back. Truth: I don't have my phone — now."},{"s":"If we had left at six, we would have caught the ferry.","g":"Type 3: two steps back, with the have ticket. Truth: we didn't leave at six, and we missed the ferry — past."},{"s":"If I had eaten breakfast, I wouldn't be hungry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2: two times. Truth: I didn't eat breakfast — past; I am hungry — now."},{"s":"<s>If I would have known, I would have told you.</s>","g":"No would in the if half. Write: If I had known, I would have told you. Truth: I didn't know — past."}]},"B2":{"key":"Every find-and-fix question is solved the same way: decide real or imagined, date each half, then check each verb against that.","body":["Whatever the sentence looks like, start with two questions: is the speaker describing something real or something imagined, and when is each half, past, now or future? Cue words such as <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em>, <em>now</em>, <em>still</em> and <em>tomorrow</em> give you the time. An imagined sentence hides a Truth line, the fact it denies; write that line and date it. A real sentence has no Truth line, because nothing is denied.","Real conditionals keep a present verb after <em>if</em>. For facts, habits and rules the result is present as well, which is the zero conditional (type 0); do not forget the <em>-s</em> for a single person or thing. For a possible future the result takes <em>will</em>, <em>might</em>, <em>can</em> or an imperative, the first conditional (type 1). <em>Will</em> does not follow <em>if</em>, and the same rule holds after <em>when</em>, <em>as soon as</em> and <em>unless</em>.","Imagined conditionals step back. An imagined present or future takes a past form after <em>if</em>, with <em>were</em> for <em>am</em> and <em>is</em>, and <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> with a verb: the second conditional (type 2). An imagined past takes <em>had</em> with the past participle (V3), and <em>would have</em>, <em>could have</em> or <em>might have</em> with V3: the third conditional (type 3). That <em>have</em> is the past ticket. <em>Would</em> never belongs in the <em>if</em> half.","When the halves have different times, build each one separately. An imagined past condition with a present result is mixed 3/2: <em>had</em> and V3, then <em>would</em> or <em>could</em> and a verb with no <em>have</em>, usually with <em>now</em> or <em>still</em>. A condition that is always true with a past result is mixed 2/3: a past form such as <em>were</em>, then <em>would have</em> and V3. You name the type from the time of each half, not from the first verb you notice.","Finally, three families from the later worlds. <em>Unless</em> means if not, so a second <em>not</em> reverses the meaning. <em>Wish</em> follows the same steps as <em>if</em>: a past form for a wish about now, <em>had</em> and V3 for a past regret, and <em>would</em> for a change you want from someone or something. Formal sentences may begin with <em>Had</em>, <em>Were</em> or <em>Should</em> instead of <em>if</em>; put the <em>if</em> back mentally and run the same checks."],"examples":[{"s":"If Nan misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"Type 0: a real habit, present in both halves, with -s on both verbs. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"If I had my phone with me, I'd call Mum now.","g":"Type 2: a past form, then would and a verb. Truth: I don't have my phone — now."},{"s":"If we had left at six, we would have caught the ferry.","g":"Type 3: had and V3, then would have and V3. Truth: we didn't leave at six, and we missed the ferry — past."},{"s":"If I had eaten breakfast, I wouldn't be hungry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2: a past condition and a present result with no have. Truth: I didn't eat breakfast — past; I am hungry — now."},{"s":"<s>If I would have known, I would have told you.</s>","g":"Would has no place in the if half. Write: If I had known, I would have told you. Truth: I didn't know — past."}]},"C1":{"key":"Two settings generate every conditional, so one routine corrects them all: fix the stance, date each clause, then test every verb against both.","body":["Every conditional, however unfamiliar it looks, is set by two dials: whether the speaker treats the situation as real or imagined, and the time of each clause. Find the time from cue words such as <em>yesterday</em>, <em>last night</em>, <em>now</em>, <em>still</em> and <em>tomorrow</em>. An imagined clause denies a fact, so it carries a hidden Truth line that you can write and date; a real clause denies nothing and has none. Most errors are a verb that disagrees with one of those two settings.","In a real conditional the <em>if</em> clause only sets the scene, so it takes a present form even for future time, and the same holds after <em>when</em>, <em>as soon as</em> and <em>unless</em>. If the result is a fact, habit or rule, it is present too, the zero conditional (type 0), with third-person <em>-s</em> where needed. If the result predicts, it takes <em>will</em>, <em>might</em>, <em>can</em> or an imperative: the first conditional (type 1). The prediction word belongs to the result alone.","Imagined clauses show their distance from reality by stepping back. One step, a past form with <em>were</em> for <em>am</em> and <em>is</em>, then <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> with a verb, gives an imagined present or future: the second conditional (type 2). Two steps, <em>had</em> with the past participle (V3), then <em>would have</em>, <em>could have</em> or <em>might have</em> with V3, give an imagined past: the third conditional (type 3). The <em>have</em> is the past ticket, and <em>would</em> stays out of the <em>if</em> clause.","Because each clause sets its own time, the two can differ. An imagined past condition with a present result is mixed 3/2: <em>had</em> with V3, then <em>would</em> or <em>could</em> with a bare verb, typically beside <em>now</em> or <em>still</em>. A permanent or present condition with a past result is mixed 2/3: a past form such as <em>were</em>, then <em>would have</em> with V3. Name the type from the time of each clause; a past ticket on a result about now is the classic mismatch.","The remaining families obey the same logic. <em>Unless</em> means except if and already contains the negative, so adding <em>not</em> reverses the sense. <em>Wish</em> steps back exactly as <em>if</em> does: a past form for the present, <em>had</em> with V3 for a past regret, and <em>would</em> for a change you want in someone else's behaviour. Inverted conditions open with <em>Had</em>, <em>Were</em> or <em>Should</em> and drop <em>if</em>; restore the <em>if</em> mentally and the same two dials apply."],"examples":[{"s":"If Nan misses the bus, she walks to school.","g":"Type 0: an open, habitual condition, present in both clauses with third-person -s. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"s":"If I had my phone with me, I'd call Mum now.","g":"Type 2: one step back for an imagined present. Truth: I don't have my phone — now."},{"s":"If we had left at six, we would have caught the ferry.","g":"Type 3: two steps back for an imagined past, with the have ticket in the result. Truth: we didn't leave at six, and we missed the ferry — past."},{"s":"If I had eaten breakfast, I wouldn't be hungry now.","g":"Mixed 3/2: each clause keeps its own time, so the result has no have. Truth: I didn't eat breakfast — past; I am hungry — now."},{"s":"<s>If I would have known, I would have told you.</s>","g":"Heard in some speech, but wrong in exams: would stays out of the if clause. Write: If I had known, I would have told you. Truth: I didn't know — past."}]}},"thai":"ถอยหลังหนึ่งก้าว = ถอยห่างจากความจริง","items":[{"id":"w9l3-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"I can hardly keep my eyes open in class today."},{"who":"Nan","text":"If you hadn't watched dramas until 2 a.m., you wouldn't be so sleepy now."}],"stem":"Which type is Nan's sentence?","options":["Third conditional: imagined past in both halves","Mixed 3/2: imagined past condition, present result","Second conditional: imagined present in both halves","First conditional: a real possibility in the future"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The condition is past, but look at now in the result. Wouldn't be has no have ticket, so the result is present.","Yes: hadn't watched is an imagined past, and wouldn't be so sleepy now is an imagined present.","The result is present, but hadn't watched is two steps back: it is about last night.","Nothing here is a real plan. Fah did watch dramas, and she is sleepy."],"hint":"Give each half its own time word. Are the two times the same?","why":"The condition is about last night (hadn't watched) and the result is about now (wouldn't be), so the halves have different times: mixed 3/2.","truth":"Fah watched dramas until 2 a.m. (past); she is sleepy (now)."},{"id":"w9l3-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","stem":"Had the rain stopped last Saturday, sports day would have gone ahead. What do we know?","options":["The rain stopped, so sports day went ahead.","The rain may stop, and then sports day will go ahead.","The rain didn't stop, and sports day didn't go ahead.","The rain stopped, but sports day still didn't go ahead."],"answer":2,"near":3,"fb":["That reads an imagined sentence as real. Had the rain stopped means that it did not stop.","Last Saturday is over. Had and V3 look back at the past, not forward.","Yes: Had the rain stopped means If the rain had stopped, so both halves are the opposite of what happened.","Half right: sports day didn't go ahead. But the first half is imagined too, so the rain did not stop."],"hint":"Put if back into the sentence. Then write the Truth line for each half.","why":"Had the rain stopped is a formal If the rain had stopped: an imagined past, so in fact the rain continued and sports day did not go ahead.","truth":"The rain didn't stop, and sports day didn't go ahead."},{"id":"w9l3-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Coach","text":"You can't play in Saturday's match unless you come to training on Thursday."}],"stem":"What does the coach mean?","options":["If you come to training on Thursday, you can't play on Saturday.","If you don't come to training on Thursday, you can play on Saturday.","If you don't come to training on Thursday, you can't play on Saturday.","If you come to training on Thursday, you don't have to play on Saturday."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This turns the rule around. Training is what lets you play, not what stops you.","The first half is right, but the result is flipped. Missing training is what keeps you out of the match.","Yes: unless means if not. No training on Thursday, no match on Saturday.","Nobody is being excused from the match. The coach is saying what you must do to be in it."],"hint":"Change unless into if. What else has to change?","why":"Unless means if not, so the coach's rule is: if you don't come to training, you can't play."},{"id":"w9l3-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"It is a rule at our school: if a student ___ her ID card, she has to sign in at the office.","options":["forget","forgets","will forget","would forget"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The time is right, but a student is one person, so the present verb needs -s.","Yes: a rule is always true, so present simple in both halves, with -s for a student.","No will after if. And this is a rule that is always true, not a prediction.","Would never goes in the if half. And a school rule is real, so nothing steps back."],"hint":"Is this about one day or every day? And who is the subject?","why":"A school rule is always true, so the if half takes the present simple, and a student needs the -s form."},{"id":"w9l3-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"Why didn't you say hello to the exchange students yesterday?"},{"who":"Jane","text":"You know me. I'm shy with new people."}],"stem":"If I ___ so shy, I would have talked to them yesterday.","options":["am not","wouldn't be","weren't","won't be"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Jane is shy, so not shy is imagined. An imagined condition needs one step back from am.","Would never goes in the if half. Step the verb back instead.","Yes: being shy is always true of Jane, so one step back from am gives weren't, even with a past result.","Will never follows if here, and the idea is imagined, not a plan."],"hint":"Is Jane shy only yesterday, or always? Step back from that time.","why":"Jane is always shy, so the condition is an imagined present, one step back: weren't. The result about yesterday keeps would have and V3.","truth":"Jane is shy (always); she didn't talk to them (yesterday)."},{"id":"w9l3-6","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"[[Unless you don't hurry]], [[we'll miss]] the last BTS train, [[and a taxi]] home [[costs]] a lot at night.","answer":0,"fix":"Unless you hurry","fb":["Yes, this is the slip: unless already means if not, so the extra not turns the meaning upside down.","This part is fine: will belongs in the result half of a real future sentence.","This part is fine: and simply adds a second idea.","This part is fine: a general fact about taxis takes the present simple with -s."],"hint":"Read it for meaning first: does hurrying save the train or lose it?","why":"Unless means if not, so Unless you hurry already says If you don't hurry; a second not reverses it."},{"id":"w9l3-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"The concert tickets sold out in two minutes last night!"},{"who":"Fern","text":"I know. My wifi was so slow that I didn't get one."}],"stem":"If my wifi had been faster, I ___ (buy) one for sure.","accept":["would have bought","would've bought","'d have bought","could have bought","could've bought"],"model":"would have bought","hint":"When is the result? Does your verb carry a past ticket?","why":"Last night is past and Fern didn't get a ticket, so the result needs would have and the past participle, bought.","truth":"Fern's wifi was slow last night, and she didn't buy a ticket."},{"id":"w9l3-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mint is stuck on question 5 right now and has no idea. She says: I wish I ___ (know) the answer!","accept":["knew"],"model":"knew","hint":"Is the wish about now or about the past? Step back from that time.","why":"Mint doesn't know the answer now, so wish takes one step back from know: knew.","truth":"Mint doesn't know the answer now."}],"world":"w9"}},"gates":{"w1":{"id":"w1g","items":[{"id":"w1g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"In which sentence can <b>when</b> replace <b>if</b> with the same meaning?","options":["If I were a cat, I'd sleep all day.","If Mint passes tomorrow's test, her mum will buy pizza.","If you freeze water, it becomes ice.","If I had a robot, it would do my homework."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["This is imagined. Truth: I'm not a cat. When would make it sound real.","This is one future day, and nobody knows the result yet. When would say she is sure to pass.","Yes: a fact that is true every time, so if and when mean the same.","This is imagined. Truth: I don't have a robot. When does not fit a dream."],"hint":"Which sentence is true every time?","why":"Only in facts and habits does if mean 'every time', so only the freezing sentence keeps its meaning with when."},{"id":"w1g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Pool notice","text":"If you have long hair, you must wear a swimming cap."}],"stem":"What does the notice mean for swimmers with long hair?","options":["A cap is a good idea, but they can choose.","A cap is necessary.","A cap is allowed, but nobody needs one.","A cap is possible on some days."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A good idea would be should. Must leaves no choice.","Yes: must makes it a rule with no choice.","Allowed would be can. Must is much stronger.","Possible would be may or might. Must is a rule for every day."],"hint":"Is the swimmer free to choose?","why":"Must in the result makes a rule: for swimmers with long hair, the cap is necessary, not just allowed or advised."},{"id":"w1g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"The projector doesn't work if you don't plug in the long cable. What is true?","options":["The projector works without the long cable.","The projector never works.","Somebody unplugged the cable this morning.","The projector needs the long cable."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The opposite: no cable, no picture.","It fails in one situation only: when the cable is not plugged in.","This is a general fact, not a story about this morning. Check the When dial.","Yes: every time the cable is missing, it fails, so the cable is necessary."],"hint":"Two negatives. What follows each time the cable is missing?","why":"The sentence says the projector fails whenever the cable is not plugged in, so it needs the cable to work."},{"id":"w1g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"What ___ if you shake a fizzy drink and open it straight away?","options":["happen","is happen","happens","happening"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["What is the subject here and counts as one thing, so the verb needs an s.","Is and a plain verb together are not an English form.","Yes: present simple with s, because what is the subject.","An -ing word alone cannot be the verb of a question."],"hint":"What is the subject of this question? Is it singular?","why":"In What happens if, what is the subject and is singular, so the present simple verb takes s."},{"id":"w1g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If you have a student card, you ___ in free on Sundays. The museum allows it.","options":["can to get","can get","can gets","can getting"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["No to after can. Modal verbs go straight to the plain verb.","Yes: can and the plain verb, for permission.","No s after a modal verb, for any subject.","No -ing after can. Use the plain verb."],"hint":"What form of the verb follows a modal?","why":"Can is a modal verb, so the plain verb follows it with no to, no s and no -ing."},{"id":"w1g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","stem":"Which sentence follows the comma rule for if sentences?","options":["If the lift is full we all walk up.","We use the stairs, if the lift is full.","If the lift is full, we take the stairs.","If, the lift is full we climb the stairs."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["The if half comes first, so it needs a comma after it.","The if half comes second here, so no comma is needed.","Yes: if half first, then a comma, then the result.","The comma goes at the end of the whole if half, not straight after if."],"hint":"Where is the if half? What does that mean for the comma?","why":"A comma follows the if half only when it comes first; when the result comes first, there is no comma."},{"id":"w1g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"If you [[will leave]] milk out of the fridge, it [[goes]] bad [[in]] a few [[hours]].","answer":0,"fix":"leave","fb":["No will after if. If takes the photo: use the present simple, leave.","Fine: milk is one thing, so goes has its s.","Fine: in a few hours is the normal phrase for time.","Fine: a few is followed by a plural noun."],"hint":"Check the verb in the half that takes the photo.","why":"The if half of a general fact uses the present simple, so will leave must be leave."},{"id":"w1g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","stem":"___ (not touch) the plate if it is hot. Use the cloth.","accept":["Don't touch","Do not touch"],"model":"Don't touch","hint":"A negative instruction. Which helping word goes before the plain verb?","why":"A negative instruction is don't and the plain verb. The result comes first here, so there is no comma."}]},"w2":{"id":"w2g","items":[{"id":"w2g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"If I see Ploy at lunch, I'll give her your notebook."}],"stem":"Is Jane sure she will see Ploy at lunch?","options":["Yes, she always sees her then.","No, it is only possible.","Yes, she has already seen her.","No, Ploy is absent today."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["If shows she is not sure. For a sure meeting she would say when.","Yes: if with a present verb shows a possible future, not a certain one.","Lunch is still in the future. The present form after if looks forward.","We don't know that. If leaves it open: maybe yes, maybe no."],"hint":"Is the condition sure or only possible?","why":"If with a present verb shows a possible future. Jane does not know whether she will see Ploy."},{"id":"w2g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"The film starts in ten minutes. If the bus ___ in the next two minutes, we'll miss the beginning.","options":["won't come","don't come","doesn't come","isn't come"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No will or won't after if for a plain future.","The bus is it, so the negative is doesn't.","Yes: doesn't and the base verb after if.","The present simple negative uses doesn't, not isn't."],"hint":"Which half is this? Check the subject too.","why":"A negative condition about the future takes the present simple. The bus is it, so the negative uses doesn't."},{"id":"w2g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"If you're hungry, you can have half of my sandwich."}],"stem":"What is Pim doing?","options":["Asking for permission","Giving a warning","Describing a habit","Making an offer"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Pim is giving, not asking. Her can lets the listener take something.","A warning shows a bad result. Half a sandwich is a good one.","A habit happens every time. This is for one hungry friend now.","Yes: she gives the listener something nice and asks for nothing back."],"hint":"Who gets something, and does the speaker ask for anything?","why":"Pim gives the listener something useful and wants nothing back. That is an offer, made with can."},{"id":"w2g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"The haze is bad this week. If your throat hurts outside, you ___ a mask.","options":["should to wear","should wear","should wearing","should wears"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["After should, use the base verb with no to.","Yes: should and the base verb give advice.","After should, use the base verb, not the -ing form.","After should, the verb takes no -s."],"hint":"What form of the verb follows a word like can or might?","why":"Should gives advice and is followed by the base verb, with no to, -s or -ing."},{"id":"w2g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"If I eat mango sticky rice late at night, I can't sleep."}],"stem":"What is Mook talking about?","options":["Her plan for tonight","Something generally true for her","A promise to a friend","A thing that happened last night"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A plan for one night would have will or going to in the result.","Yes: present forms in both halves show what happens every time.","A promise says what the speaker will do for someone. Nothing is promised here.","Both verbs are present forms, so this is not about the past."],"hint":"Try adding every time. Does it fit?","why":"Both halves have present forms and there is no will, so this is a zero conditional: it happens every time."},{"id":"w2g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"Sports day is outside this year. Where ___ lunch if it rains?","options":["will we eat","we will eat","do we will eat","will we to eat"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: will before the subject, then the base verb.","That is statement order. In a question, will moves before the subject.","Will makes the question without do.","After will, use the base verb with no to."],"hint":"What is the word order in a question about the future?","why":"A first conditional question puts will before the subject in the result half, followed by the base verb."},{"id":"w2g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"[[If you feel]] sick on the day of the trip, [[tell]] the teacher [[before]] the bus [[will leave]].","answer":3,"fix":"leaves","fb":["Present form after if: correct.","Tell is an instruction, which is a correct result.","Before is the right time word here.","This is the mistake. No will after before: say leaves."],"hint":"Check the verb after if and the verb after the time word.","why":"Before is a time word, so the verb after it takes the present simple for the future: before the bus leaves."},{"id":"w2g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","stem":"As soon as Mum ___ (get) home, we'll order the pizza.","accept":["gets","has got"],"model":"gets","hint":"Time word and future: which form? Check the subject too.","why":"After as soon as, a future event takes the present simple. Mum is she, so the verb needs -s."}]},"w3":{"id":"w3g","items":[{"id":"w3g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"I share a room with my two sisters. If I had my own room, I'd paint it green."}],"stem":"Which Truth receipt is right?","options":["Truth: Jane had her own room — last year.","Truth: Jane will get her own room — soon.","Truth: Jane painted her room green — yesterday.","Truth: Jane doesn't have her own room — now."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"Had\" is a past form, but not past time. She shares a room now.","Nothing is planned here. The sentence only imagines a different now.","\"I'd paint\" is a dream result, not something she did.","Yes: the Truth is the opposite of the if half, and its date is now."],"hint":"The Truth is the opposite of the if half. What date goes on it?","why":"\"If I had my own room\" is one step back from reality, not past time. So the Truth is the opposite, dated now: Jane doesn't have her own room.","truth":"Jane doesn't have her own room — now."},{"id":"w3g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fern has sent in her form for a summer art scholarship. She thinks she has a real chance and is already making plans. Which sentence fits how she sees it?","options":["If I got the scholarship, I'd study in Chiang Mai.","If I get the scholarship, I'll study in Chiang Mai.","When I got the scholarship, I studied in Chiang Mai.","If I had the scholarship, I'd be in Chiang Mai now."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That is the dreaming form: one step back. But Fern sees a real chance and is planning.","Yes: planning. Present after if, will in the result, and no Truth line.","That is real past time. Fern is still waiting for the result.","That imagines a different now. Fern is looking ahead to a real possibility."],"hint":"Is Fern planning or dreaming? What does the situation tell you about her view?","why":"Fern sees a real chance and is planning, so she keeps it real: present after if, will in the result. The past form would turn it into a dream."},{"id":"w3g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"Our kitchen is tiny. If we had a bigger kitchen, we could bake together."}],"stem":"What does \"could bake\" mean here?","options":["We would be able to bake.","We were able to bake before.","We are allowed to bake now.","We would have to bake."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: could is can one step back. In the dream kitchen, baking together is possible.","Could is not past time here. The kitchen is tiny now, and this is a dream.","That would be the real form, can. And the tiny kitchen stops them now.","Could is about being able, not about a duty."],"hint":"Is this result word about past time, or one step back from reality? Step back from which verb?","why":"In an imagined result, could is can one step back: it means would be able to. It is not about the past, because the kitchen is small now.","truth":"Mint's kitchen isn't big — now."},{"id":"w3g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"Our classroom is on the fifth floor and, sadly, the lift is always full. If our classroom ___ on the ground floor, I'd never be late.","options":["is","were","will be","would be"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The present makes it sound real. But the classroom is on the fifth floor, so step back.","Yes: is steps back to were. Truth: the classroom isn't on the ground floor — now.","Will is for a real future, and it does not go in the if half.","Would lives in the result half (\"I'd never be\"), not in the if half."],"hint":"Is it true now? And which half is the gap in?","why":"The classroom is really on the fifth floor, so the if half is imagined: is steps back to were. Will and would do not go in the if half.","truth":"The classroom isn't on the ground floor — now."},{"id":"w3g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"I'm a beginner at chess and I hardly ever practise. But if I practised every day, I ___ you one day. Who knows?","options":["will beat","can beat","might beats","might beat"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Will is the real form. \"Practised\" has stepped back, so the result must step back too.","Can is a real form. In an imagined result it takes one step back.","The result word fits, but the verb after it has no ending.","Yes: might plus the base verb. It means maybe, which fits \"Who knows?\""],"hint":"Has the if half stepped back? Then check the result word and the verb form after it.","why":"\"Practised\" is one step back, so the result needs a stepped-back word plus the base verb. Might means maybe, which fits \"Who knows?\"","truth":"The speaker doesn't practise every day — now."},{"id":"w3g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Bam asks her teacher politely: \"I have a dentist's appointment tomorrow. Would it be all right if I ___ the test on Friday instead?\"","options":["will take","took","would take","taking"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Will does not go in the if half, even though Friday is in the future.","Yes: the past form adds polite distance. It is not past time: she means Friday.","Would is already at the front of the question. It does not go in the if half as well.","The if half needs a full verb form, not an -ing form alone."],"hint":"This is a careful request about the future. Which form adds polite distance after \"if\"?","why":"After \"Would it be all right if\", the past simple adds polite distance. Friday is in the future; will and would do not go in the if half.","truth":"Bam hasn't taken the test; the past form only makes the request gentle."},{"id":"w3g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","stem":"[[Sadly]], I can't speak Japanese. If I [[can]] speak it, I [[would watch]] anime [[without subtitles]].","answer":1,"fix":"could","fb":["This part is fine: it only shows the speaker's feeling about the Truth.","Yes: the Truth is \"I can't\", so the if half is imagined. Can steps back to could.","This part is fine: would plus the base verb is the dream result.","This part is fine: it only completes the result."],"hint":"The first sentence is the Truth. Which verb in the dream has not stepped back?","why":"The speaker can't speak Japanese, so the if half is imagined: can steps back to could. The result \"would watch\" is already right.","truth":"The speaker can't speak Japanese — now."},{"id":"w3g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","given":"We don't have a garden, so we can't grow strawberries.","stem":"If we ___ a garden, we could grow strawberries.","accept":["had"],"model":"had","hint":"Find the real verb in the first sentence. Take it one step back.","why":"The Truth is \"we don't have a garden\", so the dream steps back once: have becomes had. The result already has could.","truth":"We don't have a garden — now."}]},"w4":{"id":"w4g","items":[{"id":"w4g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"After last Saturday's tournament, the coach says: \"If the rain had stopped by three, we would have played the final.\" What really happened?","options":["The rain stopped by three, and they played the final.","The rain stopped by three, but they didn't play the final.","The rain didn't stop by three, and they didn't play the final.","The rain didn't stop by three, but they still played the final."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["That is the imagined world. Both verbs are two steps back, so flip both halves.","Half right. The if half is imagined too: 'had stopped' means it did not stop.","Yes: both halves are imagined and dated last Saturday. No break in the rain, no final.","The result half is imagined as well: 'would have played' means they did not play."],"hint":"Write the Truth receipt for each half and date it.","why":"Had stopped and would have played are both two steps back, so neither happened last Saturday.","truth":"The rain didn't stop by three last Saturday, so they didn't play the final."},{"id":"w4g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Mook says about last night's homework: \"If Pim hadn't shared her notes, I couldn't have finished the worksheet.\" What really happened?","options":["Pim shared her notes, and Mook was able to finish.","Pim shared her notes, but Mook wasn't able to finish.","Pim didn't share her notes, so Mook wasn't able to finish.","Pim didn't share her notes, but Mook finished anyway."],"answer":0,"near":2,"fb":["Yes: both halves are negative and imagined, so both really happened. Notes shared, worksheet finished.","'Couldn't have finished' is imagined, so in real life she was able to finish.","That is the imagined world. Flip both halves to find the Truth.","'Hadn't shared' is imagined, so in real life Pim did share her notes."],"hint":"Flip each half. What does a negative imagined half tell you about real life?","why":"Hadn't shared is imagined, so Pim did share; couldn't have finished is imagined, so Mook was able to finish.","truth":"Pim shared her notes last night, so Mook was able to finish the worksheet."},{"id":"w4g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"We sat in silence at the picnic on Sunday. If you had charged the speaker, we would have had music."}],"stem":"What is Mook doing with her second sentence?","options":["Feeling relieved that the picnic went well.","Regretting a mistake that she made herself.","Blaming her friend for not doing something.","Asking her friend to charge the speaker next time."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["Relief needs a bad result that didn't happen. Here the bad result was real: no music.","The if half is about 'you', not about Mook herself.","Yes. Truth: the friend didn't charge the speaker, so there was no music. That is criticism.","Two steps back looks at a finished past, not at next time."],"hint":"Write the Truth. Was the real result good or bad, and whose action was it?","why":"The if half is about the friend's action and the real result was bad, so the sentence is criticism or blame.","truth":"Mook's friend didn't charge the speaker, so there was no music at the picnic on Sunday."},{"id":"w4g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Jane missed the BTS this morning because she left home at half past seven. Which sentence about this morning is correct?","options":["If Jane had left at seven, she would catch the train.","If Jane has left at seven, she would have caught the train.","If Jane left at seven, she would catch the train.","If Jane had left at seven, she would have caught the train."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The if half is right, but the result has no past ticket: 'would catch' is about now or later.","'Has left' is about the time up to now and is not imagined. The if half needs had.","Fine for an imagined now or future, but this morning is over: step back twice.","Yes: had with V3, then would have with V3. Truth: she left late and missed it."],"hint":"Both halves are about this morning. Has each verb taken two steps back?","why":"This morning is over and Jane left late, so both halves are imagined past: had left, would have caught.","truth":"Jane didn't leave at seven this morning, so she didn't catch the train."},{"id":"w4g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"There was a haze warning yesterday, but Fah didn't see it. She says: \"If I ___ about the warning, I'd have worn a mask.\"","options":["'d know","'d have known","'d known","have known"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["'d before a base verb is would, and would cannot go in the if half.","This is 'would have known'. That ticket belongs in the result half; after if, exams mark it wrong.","Yes: 'd before V3 is had. If I had known, I would have worn a mask.","'Have known' is about the time up to now and is not imagined. Use had."],"hint":"What does 'd stand for before a past participle, and before a base verb?","why":"The if half of an imagined past needs had plus V3, and 'd before the past participle known is had.","truth":"Fah didn't know about the haze warning yesterday, so she didn't wear a mask."},{"id":"w4g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fah is stuck indoors because it is raining. She says: \"If it ___ raining right now, I would be at the park with my dog.\"","options":["isn't","hadn't been","weren't","wouldn't be"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["The present is for a real possibility, but it is raining: this is imagined.","Two steps back is for a finished past. 'Right now' needs only one step.","Yes: the Truth is dated now, so one step back: weren't.","Would lives in the result half, not in the if half."],"hint":"Date the Truth receipt. Is this about now or then?","why":"It is raining now and the result is would be, so the if half steps back once: weren't.","truth":"It is raining now, so Fah isn't at the park."},{"id":"w4g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"[[If you would have]] sent me the address [[yesterday]], I [[wouldn't have got]] lost [[on the way]] to your house.","answer":0,"fix":"If you had","fb":["Yes, here: would have cannot go in the if half. Write: If you had sent.","This part is fine: a past time word matches two steps back.","This part is fine: wouldn't have with V3 is the negative result for an imagined past.","This part is fine: 'on the way to' is normal English."],"hint":"Which half is allowed to hold would?","why":"The if half of an imagined past takes had plus V3. Would have after if is heard in some American speech, but exams mark it wrong.","truth":"The friend didn't send the address yesterday, so the speaker got lost."},{"id":"w4g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Bam left her student card at home last Friday, so she paid the full price at the cinema. Use <b>could</b> and <b>save</b>: If she had taken her card, she ___ forty baht.","accept":["could have saved","could've saved"],"model":"could have saved","hint":"It was possible, but it was last Friday. What goes between the modal and the verb?","why":"Saving was possible in the imagined past, so could takes the past ticket: could have plus the past participle saved.","truth":"Bam didn't take her student card last Friday, so she didn't save forty baht."}]},"w5":{"id":"w5g","items":[{"id":"w5g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"The coach says: <em>If you had done the warm-up properly this morning, your legs wouldn't hurt now.</em> What do we know?","options":["The player did the warm-up properly, and her legs are fine.","The player didn't do the warm-up properly, but her legs are fine now.","The player didn't do the warm-up properly, and her legs hurt now.","The player's legs hurt this morning, but they don't hurt now."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["Both halves are imagined, so both truths are the opposite of what the coach imagines.","Half right. \"Wouldn't hurt now\" is imagined too, so the truth is that they do hurt now.","Yes: two Truth lines, two dates. No proper warm-up, this morning; sore legs, now.","Check the dates. \"This morning\" belongs to the warm-up, and \"now\" to the sore legs."],"hint":"Write the two Truth lines and date each one.","why":"Each imagined half reverses to its Truth line: she didn't warm up properly this morning, and her legs hurt now.","truth":"The player didn't do the warm-up properly — this morning. Her legs hurt — now."},{"id":"w5g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Story","text":"Bam is scared of dogs. Yesterday there was a big dog on her street, so she took the long way home."}],"stem":"Which sentence says the same as the story?","options":["If Bam hadn't taken the long way home, she wouldn't be scared of dogs now.","If Bam weren't scared of dogs, she wouldn't have taken the long way home yesterday.","If Bam were scared of dogs, she would have taken the long way home yesterday.","If Bam isn't scared of dogs tomorrow, she will take the short way home."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["This turns the story round: it makes the long walk the cause of the fear.","Yes: Truth one, she is scared of dogs, always; Truth two, she took the long way, yesterday.","Write the Truth line for the if half: it would mean that she is not scared. The story says she is.","A real future with no Truth line. It says nothing about yesterday."],"hint":"Which fact is still true, and which one is dated yesterday?","why":"Her fear is still true and the long walk was yesterday, so the if half is one step back and the result two.","truth":"Bam is scared of dogs — always. She took the long way home — yesterday."},{"id":"w5g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which type is this? <em>If the rain had stopped earlier, we would have finished the match yesterday.</em>","options":["Mixed 3/2","Second conditional","Mixed 2/3","Third conditional"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The if half fits, but in mixed 3/2 the result is about now, with no \"have\". This result is dated yesterday.","A second conditional is one step back and about now or the future. Both halves here are two steps back.","In mixed 2/3 the if half is still true now, with a past simple. \"Had stopped\" is an imagined past.","Yes: both halves are imagined and both are past, so both take two steps back."],"hint":"Date each half. Do the two halves have the same date or different dates?","why":"Had plus V3 and would have plus V3 are both two steps back, and both halves are dated yesterday: imagined past in both.","truth":"The rain didn't stop earlier — yesterday. We didn't finish the match — yesterday."},{"id":"w5g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Our dance team didn't book the hall last week, so we are practising in the car park today. If we had booked it, we ___ on a proper stage right now.","options":["would have danced","will be dancing","are dancing","would be dancing"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The past ticket \"have\" dates the result in the past, but the time words are \"right now\".","\"Will\" is for a real future. This result is imagined: the team is in the car park.","A present form states a real fact. The stage is imagined, so the verb needs one step back.","Yes: an imagined result now, one step back, with no past ticket."],"hint":"When is the result? Does it need a past ticket?","why":"The result is dated right now and is not real, so it takes would be dancing: one step back, with no have.","truth":"The team didn't book the hall — last week. They aren't on a proper stage — now."},{"id":"w5g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fah's phone is at 2% and she is nowhere near a plug. If she ___ her power bank this morning, she could charge it now.","options":["packs","would have packed","had packed","has packed"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["A present form cannot go with \"this morning\" here, and the packing is imagined, not real.","\"Would\" belongs in the result half. An imagined past if half takes had plus V3.","Yes: imagined and dated this morning, so two steps back in the if half.","\"Has packed\" is the present perfect, a real form. An imagined past needs had, not has."],"hint":"When is the if half? How many steps back does it take?","why":"The packing is imagined and dated this morning, so the if half takes had plus the past participle.","truth":"Fah didn't pack her power bank — this morning. She can't charge her phone — now."},{"id":"w5g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","stem":"Mook hates crowds, and she always has. If she didn't hate crowds, she ___ to the countdown party with us last New Year's Eve.","options":["would come","would have come","will come","would have came"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["No past ticket: \"would come\" is about now or the future, but the party was last New Year's Eve.","Yes: an imagined past result, two steps back, with \"have\" plus the past participle.","\"Will\" is for a real future. The party is over, and Mook did not come.","The ticket is there, but \"have\" needs the past participle: come, not came."],"hint":"When is the result? Check the ticket and the verb after it.","why":"The party was last New Year's Eve and Mook did not come, so the result needs would have plus the past participle.","truth":"Mook hates crowds — always. She didn't come to the party — last New Year's Eve."},{"id":"w5g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"If I [[will see]] Nan [[at lunch]] today, I [[will give]] her [[your notebook]].","answer":0,"fix":"see","fb":["This is the slip. No \"will\" after \"if\": the if half takes a present form, \"see\".","This part is fine: it only says when the two girls may meet.","This part is fine: \"will\" belongs in the result, where the promise is.","This part is fine: it is simply the thing she will hand over."],"hint":"Is this real or imagined? Which half makes the promise?","why":"Lunch is still to come and nothing is denied, so this is a real future: present after if, and will only in the result."},{"id":"w5g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","given":"Praew doesn't have a library card, so she couldn't borrow the novel last Friday.","stem":"If Praew had a library card, she ___ the novel last Friday.","accept":["could have borrowed","would have borrowed","'d have borrowed","could've borrowed","would've borrowed","might have borrowed","might've borrowed","would have been able to borrow","'d have been able to borrow","would've been able to borrow"],"model":"could have borrowed","hint":"The if half is still true today. What date does the result carry?","why":"The borrowing is imagined and dated last Friday, so the result needs could have or would have plus the past participle.","truth":"Praew doesn't have a library card — now. She didn't borrow the novel — last Friday."}]},"w6":{"id":"w6g","items":[{"id":"w6g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Sign","text":"No swimming unless a lifeguard is on duty."}],"stem":"When can you swim?","options":["At any time, with or without a lifeguard.","Only when there is no lifeguard on duty.","Only when there is a lifeguard on duty.","Never: the pool is closed to swimmers."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["Unless adds an exception to the no. The lifeguard matters.","That adds a second negative. The lifeguard is what makes swimming possible.","Yes: no swimming, except if a lifeguard is there.","Unless gives one exception to the no: a lifeguard on duty."],"hint":"Except test: what is the one exception to the rule?","why":"Unless means except if: swimming is not allowed, except when a lifeguard is on duty."},{"id":"w6g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"I'll bring a mat in case the grass is wet."},{"who":"Jane","text":"I'll bring a mat if the grass is wet."}],"stem":"Who brings a mat for certain?","options":["Jane","Mint","Both of them","Neither of them"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["With if, Jane waits to see. Dry grass, no mat.","Yes: her mat is a precaution, so it goes in the bag before anyone knows about the grass.","Only one of them brings it whatever happens. Compare the two connectors.","One of them acts before she knows about the grass."],"hint":"Umbrella test: who acts before she knows?","why":"In case means she brings it anyway, as a precaution. If means she brings it only when the grass is wet."},{"id":"w6g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Ploy","text":"Even if Nan says sorry, I'm not lending her my charger again."}],"stem":"What do we know?","options":["Nan has already said sorry.","Nan may or may not say sorry.","Nan will never say sorry.","Ploy will lend it if Nan says sorry."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A fact would need even though. Even if leaves it open.","Yes: even if is for a maybe, and it won't change Ploy's answer.","Even if doesn't say never. It says maybe, and that it makes no difference.","Even if means the result stays the same: no charger either way."],"hint":"Truth test: fact or maybe?","why":"Even if marks a possibility, not a fact, and says the result stays the same either way."},{"id":"w6g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Dad","text":"I'm not cleaning up after it. That's your job."}],"stem":"You can keep the kitten ___ you clean its litter tray yourself.","options":["unless","as long as","even though","otherwise"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Unless would make cleaning the tray the thing that stops her keeping the kitten.","Yes: a bargain. Cleaning the tray is the condition for keeping the kitten.","Even though needs a fact that changes nothing. Here cleaning is the condition.","Otherwise means if not, and it starts a new clause after a full stop or semicolon."],"hint":"Is cleaning the tray the condition for a yes, or the thing that stops it?","why":"Dad is making a deal: yes, but only if she cleans the tray. As long as states that bargain."},{"id":"w6g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"Mum's flight has just landed, so she'll be home soon."}],"stem":"I'll ask her about the trip ___ she gets home.","options":["if","unless","when","supposing"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["If shows doubt, but the flight has landed: she is certainly coming home.","Unless means except if. Her coming home doesn't stop the asking.","Yes: the event is expected, so it is a matter of time, not of maybe.","Supposing invites someone to picture a situation. This one is already certain."],"hint":"Is her coming home sure, or only possible?","why":"Mum is certainly coming home, so the sure-event word is needed: when, not if."},{"id":"w6g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Charge your phone tonight. ___, it will die during the trip tomorrow.","options":["Unless","Provided","Whether","Otherwise"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Unless needs its own clause after it. It can't stand alone before a comma.","Provided needs a clause and makes a bargain. This is a warning.","Whether needs two possibilities and a clause.","Yes: it means if you don't, and it can start a new sentence with a comma after it."],"hint":"Which word means if not and can start a new sentence?","why":"Otherwise means if you don't do that. It starts a new sentence after a full stop."},{"id":"w6g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"You won't pass the swimming test unless you [[don't practise]] [[every]] week [[from]] now [[until]] June.","answer":0,"fix":"practise","fb":["Yes, this is the mistake: unless already carries the negative. Say unless you practise.","This part is fine: every week says how often.","This part is fine: from now gives the starting point.","This part is fine: until June gives the end point."],"hint":"Count the negatives after unless.","why":"Unless means except if, so the verb after it stays positive: unless you practise."},{"id":"w6g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"You may use the art room ___ up afterwards.","given":"You may use the art room, but you must tidy up afterwards.","keyword":"PROVIDING","accept":["providing you tidy","providing that you tidy"],"model":"providing you tidy","hint":"After the key word: that or nothing, then subject and verb in the present.","why":"Providing (that) means only if. It replaces but you must, and the verb stays in the present: you tidy."}]},"w7":{"id":"w7g","items":[{"id":"w7g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"If only I understood this chemistry homework!"}],"stem":"What is true?","options":["Bam doesn't understand the homework now.","Bam understood the homework last term.","Bam will understand the homework soon.","Bam understands it, but it took her a long time."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: if only and a past form make a wish about now, so the Truth is the opposite.","Understood is not past time here. It is one step back from reality, about now.","Nothing here is expected. A past form after if only means imagined.","That would be real. The step back says the understanding is not real now."],"hint":"Past form after if only: past time, or one step back from reality?","why":"If only and a past form make a wish about now, so the Truth is the opposite: Bam doesn't understand it.","truth":"Bam doesn't understand the homework — now."},{"id":"w7g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"The view from the top of the big wheel is amazing, but my phone is dead."}],"stem":"I wish I ___ a photo.","options":["can take","could take","will take","am taking"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Can is the real form. Jane's phone is dead, so step back once.","Yes: can steps back to could. Truth: she can't take a photo now.","Will never follows wish. This is an imagined now, not a real future.","A present form after wish says it is really happening. It isn't."],"hint":"What can't Jane do now? Which verb steps back?","why":"Jane can't take a photo now, so can takes one step back to could.","truth":"Jane can't take a photo — now."},{"id":"w7g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Ploy","text":"If only we hadn't missed the last BTS train!"}],"stem":"What happened?","options":["They missed the last train.","They caught the last train.","They might miss the last train.","They miss the last train every night."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: hadn't missed is two steps back, so the Truth is the opposite, in the past.","That is the wish. Flip it to find the Truth.","It is not a possibility any more. Had and V3 point to a finished past moment.","A habit would be a real present. This is one past event."],"hint":"Count the steps back, then flip the wish.","why":"Hadn't missed is two steps back and negative, so the Truth is past and positive: they missed the train.","truth":"They missed the last train — earlier tonight."},{"id":"w7g-4","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Pim wrote her whole essay without saving it. Then the laptop battery died."}],"stem":"Pim: If only I ___ (save) my essay!","accept":["had saved","'d saved"],"model":"had saved","hint":"When is the Truth dated? How many steps back?","why":"Pim didn't save it and the moment is past, so if only takes had and the past participle: had saved.","truth":"Pim didn't save her essay — before the battery died."},{"id":"w7g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence complains about something another person keeps doing?","options":["I wish you wouldn't read my messages.","I wish I hadn't read your messages.","I hope you read my messages.","I wish I could read your messages."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: wish, another person and wouldn't complain about behaviour that goes on.","Had and V3 with I is a regret about the speaker's own past action.","Hope and a present form is a real possibility the speaker wants, not a complaint.","Could is about the speaker's own missing ability now."],"hint":"Look at the subject after wish and the helper verb.","why":"Only wish with you and wouldn't is about another person's repeated behaviour that the speaker wants to stop.","truth":"The other person keeps reading her messages."},{"id":"w7g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Nan","text":"Your audition is tomorrow, isn't it? You've practised so much."}],"stem":"I ___ it goes really well!","options":["hope","wish","wished","am wishing"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: goes has not stepped back, and the audition is a real future event.","After wish the verb must step back. Goes is a present form, so the first verb cannot be wish.","A past form of wish does not help: goes is still a present form for a real possibility.","The same problem: any form of wish needs a step back in the next verb."],"hint":"Look at the verb after the gap: has it stepped back?","why":"Goes is a present form and the audition is a real future event, so the first verb is hope; wish would need a step back."},{"id":"w7g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","stem":"[[It's high time]] you [[stop]] playing that blocky game; your project [[is due]] tomorrow and you [[haven't started]] it.","answer":1,"fix":"stopped","fb":["It's high time is fine: the action is overdue.","Yes, this is the error. You may hear stop in speech, but after it's high time and a subject, exams want the past form, stopped.","Is due is a real timetable fact, so the present form is right.","Haven't started is a real fact up to now, so the present perfect is right."],"hint":"Which verb depends on the opening phrase? Has it stepped back?","why":"In exam English, it's high time and a subject take the past form, so stop steps back to stopped. The other verbs state real facts.","truth":"She hasn't stopped playing — yet."},{"id":"w7g-8","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Praew","text":"Shall I come round at seven on Saturday morning?"},{"who":"Fern","text":"I sleep until nine at weekends!"}],"stem":"I'd rather you ___ a bit later, to be honest.","options":["came","come","will come","to come"],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: would rather and a new subject take a past form. It is about Saturday, not the past.","You may hear this in speech, but exams want the step back after would rather and another subject.","Will cannot follow would rather. The preference is shown by a step back.","No to after would rather, with or without a new subject."],"hint":"The subject changes after rather. What happens to the verb?","why":"Would rather with a different subject takes a past form for a future preference: came.","truth":"Praew hasn't come yet; Fern prefers a later time — on Saturday."}]},"w8":{"id":"w8g","items":[{"id":"w8g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","stem":"Where does this sentence fit best? \"Should you wish to join the debate club, please complete the form below.\"","options":["In a voice message to your little sister","In a chat with friends at the bubble tea shop","In a notice on the school website","In a note to yourself in your diary"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["Too formal for family talk. You would simply say \"if you want to join\".","With friends, plain \"if\" sounds natural. Inversion would sound stiff.","Yes: inversion with \"should\" belongs in notices, formal emails and letters.","Nobody writes to herself this formally. Inversion is for official readers."],"hint":"Who is the reader, and how formal is the situation?","why":"Should you wish is a formal, polite way to say if you want, so it suits an official notice, not private or friendly messages."},{"id":"w8g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Travel blog","text":"Had it not been for the long delay at the airport, we would have reached Phuket before dark."}],"stem":"What really happened?","options":["There was no delay, and they reached Phuket before dark.","There was a long delay, and they reached Phuket after dark.","There was a long delay, but they still arrived before dark.","There may be a delay, so they may reach Phuket after dark."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The noun after \"had it not been for\" is real: there was a delay.","Yes: the delay is real, and arriving before dark is only imagined.","\"Would have reached\" is imagined, so the real result is the opposite.","This looks back at a finished journey. It is not a possible future."],"hint":"Is the delay real? Is the result real or imagined?","why":"Had it not been for plus a noun means if it had not been for it. The delay was real; arriving before dark is only imagined.","truth":"There was a long delay, so they reached Phuket after dark."},{"id":"w8g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Mint's message to her coach","text":"If I was impatient with the younger players yesterday, I'm sorry."}],"stem":"Leave it or fix it?","options":["Fix it: was must be were after if.","Fix it: write If I had been impatient, I would have been sorry.","Fix it: I'm sorry must be I will be sorry.","Leave it: a real past possibility, so was is right."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"Were\" is for an imagined present. Mint thinks she may really have been impatient, so \"was\" is needed.","That would mean she was not impatient and is not sorry. She is saying sorry now.","She is saying sorry as she writes, so the present is right. No prediction is being made.","Yes: maybe it really happened. A real past condition with a present result, and nothing to fix."],"hint":"Is Mint imagining being impatient, or does she think it may really have happened?","why":"Mint may really have been impatient yesterday, so the condition is a real past possibility: was, not were, and she says sorry now."},{"id":"w8g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Class essay","text":"At present, school groups visit the science museum free of charge."}],"stem":"___ the museum to charge students for entry, far fewer school groups would visit.","options":["Was","Had","Should","Were"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["\"Was\" can never open an inverted condition. Formal English uses \"were\" for every subject here.","\"Had\" needs a past participle and an imagined past. Here we have \"to charge\" and \"would visit\".","\"Should\" takes a base verb with no \"to\", and its result is real, not \"would\".","Yes: were, then the subject, then to plus a verb imagines a future change, with \"would\" in the result."],"hint":"Look at \"to charge\" and \"would visit\". Which front verb fits both?","why":"The museum is free now, so charging is imagined: were plus subject plus to plus verb, matched by would in the result.","truth":"The museum does not charge students at present."},{"id":"w8g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"B2","lines":[{"who":"Sign at the ferry pier","text":"Boats leave from three different platforms."}],"stem":"___, ask a member of staff before you board.","options":["If any","If in doubt","If so","If not"],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["\"If any\" follows few or little, between commas. It cannot start this instruction.","Yes: \"if in doubt\" means if you are not sure, a common chunk on signs.","\"If so\" points back to an idea that may be true. The line before gives a plain fact.","\"If not\" also needs an earlier idea that may be false. There is none here."],"hint":"What might a passenger feel when there are three platforms?","why":"If in doubt means if you are not sure. If so and if not need an earlier idea to point back to, and if any follows few or little."},{"id":"w8g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"My phone was at two per cent before the listening test this morning, but Jane lent me her charger."}],"stem":"___ Jane's spare charger, my phone would have died before the test.","options":["Unless","Even if","But for","In case of"],"answer":2,"near":3,"fb":["\"Unless\" needs a clause with a verb, and it is not used for this kind of imagined past.","\"Even if\" needs a clause, and it would mean the charger made no difference.","Yes: \"but for\" plus a noun means if it had not been for it.","\"In case of\" is for notices about what to do, as in \"in case of fire\". It is not an imagined past condition."],"hint":"Is the charger real? What follows the gap: a noun or a clause?","why":"The charger was real and it saved the phone. But for plus a noun means if it had not been for it; the others cannot do that job.","truth":"Jane lent her charger, so the phone did not die."},{"id":"w8g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lines":[{"who":"Fah","text":"We walked forty minutes to the pier yesterday, and it was closed."}],"stem":"[[Had we]] known [[about]] the closure, we [[would take]] the bus [[instead]].","answer":2,"fix":"would have taken","fb":["\"Had we\" is right: had moves in front of the subject for an imagined past.","\"Known about\" plus a noun is fine. Check the verbs.","No past ticket. An imagined past result needs \"have\": would have taken.","\"Instead\" is fine. Check the verb in the result."],"hint":"When is the result? Is there a past ticket?","why":"Had we known sets an imagined past, and yesterday confirms it, so the result needs would have plus the past participle.","truth":"They didn't know the pier was closed, so they didn't take the bus."},{"id":"w8g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","given":"Please send me the reading list for the summer course. (formal email)","keyword":"WOULD","stem":"I would be grateful if you ___ me the reading list for the summer course.","accept":["would send","would kindly send"],"model":"would send","hint":"A polite request in a formal email: which verb softens it after if?","why":"In a formal request, would after if is polite: it asks whether the reader is willing. It is not a future form here."}]},"w9":{"id":"w9g","items":[{"id":"w9g-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","stem":"Jane isn't in the school band because she can't read music. Which sentence means the same?","options":["If Jane can read music, she will be in the school band.","If Jane had read music, she would have been in the school band.","If Jane could read music, she would be in the school band.","Unless Jane could read music, she would be in the school band."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["This is a real possibility for the future. But sentence one says she can't read music now, so the idea is imagined.","Two steps back is for a past fact. Isn't and can't are about now.","Yes: both facts are about now, so each verb goes one step back and flips: can't becomes could, isn't becomes would be.","Unless means if not, so this says she would be in the band if she couldn't read music."],"hint":"When are the two facts true? How many steps back does that time need?","why":"The facts are present (isn't, can't), so the imagined version is one step back and flipped: could read, would be.","truth":"Jane can't read music, so she isn't in the band — now."},{"id":"w9g-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"mix","cefr":"C1","stem":"Praew's essay has three first conditionals, all correct. Her friend says: Add a third conditional and two inversions to impress the examiner. What is the best advice for Praew?","options":["Add the third conditional, even though her topic is about the future.","Keep her correct sentences and hedge one result with may.","Open every paragraph with an inversion to show her range.","Change every will to would so that the essay sounds more formal."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A third conditional is for an imagined past. Forced into a topic about the future, it will be wrong, and wrong forms lose marks.","Yes: accuracy comes first. A correct sentence with a careful hedge earns more than a rare form used badly.","One inversion in an essay is enough. Several in a row sound forced.","Would is for imagined ideas and proposals. It is not a formal will, so the meaning would change."],"hint":"What do examiners reward first: correct forms or rare forms?","why":"Accuracy beats range: correct real conditionals with a hedge earn more than a forced third conditional or several inversions."},{"id":"w9g-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence is about a real possibility, with nothing imagined?","options":["If the haze cleared, sports day would go ahead.","Had the haze cleared in time, sports day would have gone ahead.","I wish the haze would clear before sports day.","If the haze clears by Friday, sports day might go ahead."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["Cleared and would are one step back: the speaker is only imagining it.","Had the haze cleared means If the haze had cleared: an imagined past. In fact the haze stayed.","Wish with would shows the speaker wants a change that is not happening.","Yes: a present verb after if and might in the result. The question is open, and nothing is denied."],"hint":"Which sentence has no hidden Truth line?","why":"Present simple after if with might is a real, open possibility; the other three step back, so each one hides a Truth line."},{"id":"w9g-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","stem":"Key word: CASE. Sentence one: I'll write the address down because I might forget it. Sentence two: I'll write the address down ___ it.","options":["in case I will forget","in case I forget","in case I don't forget","in case I forgot"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["In case takes a present verb for the future, just like if. No will.","Yes: write it now, before you forget, with a present verb after in case.","The extra not changes the meaning. The danger is forgetting, so the verb stays positive.","A past form does not fit. You are writing the address now, to guard against a future problem."],"hint":"Which verb form follows in case when the event is in the future?","why":"In case shows a precaution and takes the present simple for a future event."},{"id":"w9g-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","stem":"Which sentence is best for an essay about phones at school?","options":["If schools ban phones. Students may talk to each other more.","If schools will ban phones, students may talk to each other more.","If schools ban phones, students'll talk to each other more.","If schools ban phones, students may talk to each other more."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["The verbs are right, but a full stop cuts the if half off from its result. It needs a comma there.","No will after if. The if half takes a present verb, even for the future.","Students'll is a short form from speech, and will is not hedged.","Yes: present after if, a comma, a careful may and no short forms."],"hint":"Check the verb after if, the comma, the hedge and short forms.","why":"It has the present simple after if, a comma after the if half, the hedge may and no contractions."},{"id":"w9g-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","stem":"Fern forgot to charge her phone last night. If she had charged it, she ___ us on LINE right now.","options":["could have messaged","can message","could message","will message"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The have ticket sends the result to the past, but right now says the result is present.","Can is for real results. Her phone is dead, so this result is imagined: one step back.","Yes: an imagined present result is could and a verb, with no have ticket, because of right now.","Will is a real promise. Her phone is dead, so the messaging is only imagined."],"hint":"When is the result? Does it need a past ticket?","why":"The condition is last night, but the result is right now, so it takes could and a verb with no have.","truth":"Fern didn't charge her phone (last night); she can't message us (now)."},{"id":"w9g-7","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","stem":"If the van driver [[would have seen]] the flooded soi [[in time]], she [[would have taken]] [[another road]] this morning.","answer":0,"fix":"had seen","fb":["Yes, this is the slip: no would in the if half. An imagined past condition is had and V3: had seen.","This part is fine: in time means early enough.","This part is fine: the result of an imagined past is would have and V3.","This part is fine: another road is correct."],"hint":"This morning is past, and it didn't happen. Check how each half is built.","why":"The if half of an imagined past takes had and V3, not would have: If the van driver had seen.","truth":"The driver didn't see the flooded soi in time, and she didn't take another road."},{"id":"w9g-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","given":"I didn't save my project last night, and I really regret it.","keyword":"WISH","stem":"I ___ my project last night.","accept":["wish I had saved","wish I'd saved","wish that I had saved","wish that I'd saved"],"model":"wish I had saved","hint":"A regret about the past: how many steps back after the key word?","why":"A regret about last night takes wish with had and V3, flipped to the positive.","truth":"She didn't save her project last night."}]}},"tests":{"placement":{"items":[{"id":"pl-1","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","lesson":"w1l1","world":"w1","stem":"Mint says: \"If I drink bubble tea after 8 p.m., I can't sleep.\" What does she mean?","options":["It happens every time she drinks it late.","She plans to drink it late tonight.","She drank it late last night, just once.","She never drinks it, so she is only imagining."],"answer":0,"near":1,"fb":["Yes: present plus present is a fact about her life, true every time.","There is no will here, so it is not a plan. Both halves are present: a general fact.","Both verbs are present, not past, so this is not the story of one night.","Nothing is imagined: no verb has stepped back. This really happens to her."],"hint":"Look at both verbs. Are they about one time or every time?","why":"Present simple in both halves gives a general truth: whenever the condition happens, the result follows."},{"id":"pl-2","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"A2","lesson":"w1l3","world":"w1","stem":"On school days, if Fah ___ the BTS before seven, she always gets a seat.","options":["take","takes","will take","taking"],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["Fah is one person (she), so the present simple needs -s.","Yes: a habit, so present simple, and with she the verb takes -s.","No will after if. And this is a habit, not a prediction about one day.","Taking alone is not a full verb. A habit needs the present simple."],"hint":"Is this a habit or one future event? And who is the subject?","why":"The word always shows a habit, so both halves are present simple, and Fah (she) needs the -s form."},{"id":"pl-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lesson":"w2l1","world":"w2","stem":"Praew says: \"If it rains this afternoon, I'll take a taxi home.\" What is true?","options":["It is raining now, so she is in a taxi.","Rain is possible, and she has a plan.","It won't rain, so she is only dreaming.","It rained, and she took a taxi home."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["The afternoon has not come yet, and I'll take is about the future, not now.","Yes: present after if plus will is a real, possible future with a decision ready.","A dream would step back: if it rained, I'd take. Here the forms are real.","Nothing here is past: rains is present and I'll is future."],"hint":"Is anything denied here? Look at the tense after if and the helping verb in the result.","why":"If plus present with will in the result is a real, possible future: she doesn't know yet, but she has decided what to do."},{"id":"pl-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B1","lesson":"w2l1","world":"w2","stem":"Tickets for the concert go on sale tomorrow. If they ___ out before Friday, we'll watch the live stream instead.","options":["will sell","sold","sell","would sell"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No will after if for a plain future condition. The prediction lives in the result.","Sold is a past form, but the tickets are not even on sale yet. A real future condition takes the present.","Yes: a real future condition takes the present simple after if.","Would never goes in a plain if half, and this future is real, not imagined."],"hint":"Which half makes the prediction? Does the if half need a future word?","why":"The sale is still in the future and really possible, so the if half takes the present simple; will belongs only in the result."},{"id":"pl-5","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B1","lesson":"w3l1","world":"w3","stem":"Nan says: \"If I had a cat, I'd call her Mochi.\" What is true now?","options":["Nan has a cat called Mochi.","Nan had a cat, but not any more.","Nan doesn't have a cat.","Nan is getting a cat soon."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["If that were true, she would not need if at all. The step back denies it.","Had is past in form only. It is not about past time here; it is about reality.","Yes: one step back from reality. The truth now is the opposite of the if half.","A real plan would be: if I get a cat, I'll call her Mochi."],"hint":"The verb is past in form. Is it about past time, or about reality?","why":"Had with would is one step back from reality, not past time: it imagines a present that is not true.","truth":"Nan doesn't have a cat (now)."},{"id":"pl-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l2","world":"w3","stem":"Fern lives two hours from school, sadly. If she ___ nearer, she'd walk there with us every morning.","options":["lives","lived","would live","has lived"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Present is for something real. She does not live nearer, so step back once.","Yes: imagined now, so one step back in the if half: the past simple.","Would goes in the result (she'd walk), not in the if half.","Has lived is real time up to now. An imagined present needs the past simple."],"hint":"Is it true now that she is near the school? How many steps back?","why":"She does not live nearer now, so the condition is imagined: one step back to the past simple, with would in the result.","truth":"Fern doesn't live near the school (now)."},{"id":"pl-7","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","world":"w4","stem":"\"If Pim had set an alarm, she wouldn't have missed the school bus.\" What really happened?","options":["She set an alarm, so she caught the bus.","She didn't set an alarm, but she still caught the bus.","She set an alarm, but she missed the bus.","She didn't set an alarm, and she missed the bus."],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["That is the imagined world. The truth is the opposite of both halves.","The if half is right, but flip the result too: wouldn't have missed means she did miss it.","Flip the if half: had set is imagined, so in fact she did not set one.","Yes: both halves are imagined past, so the truth is the opposite of each."],"hint":"Flip each half to find the truth: positive becomes negative, negative becomes positive.","why":"Both halves are two steps back, so both are imagined past. The truth is the opposite of each half.","truth":"Pim didn't set an alarm, and she missed the bus (past)."},{"id":"pl-8","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","world":"w4","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"How was the school fair on Saturday?"},{"who":"Bam","text":"Great, but our stall ran out of mango sticky rice by noon."}],"stem":"If we'd made more, we ___ twice as much.","options":["would earn","would have earned","will earn","would earned"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["No past ticket: would earn is about now or the future, but the fair was on Saturday.","Yes: an imagined past result takes would, the have ticket and the past participle.","Will is for a real future. The fair is over, and they did not make more.","The have ticket is missing: would cannot be followed directly by a past participle."],"hint":"When is the result? Does it have a past ticket?","why":"Saturday is over and they did not make more, so the result needs would have plus the past participle.","truth":"They didn't make more on Saturday, so they didn't earn twice as much."},{"id":"pl-9","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w5l1","world":"w5","stem":"\"If Mook had charged her phone last night, she'd be able to call her mum now.\" What is true?","options":["She charged it last night, and she can call now.","She didn't charge it last night, but she can call now.","She didn't charge it last night, and she can't call now.","She isn't charging it now, so she couldn't call last night."],"answer":2,"near":3,"fb":["That is the imagined world. Flip both halves to find the truth.","The first half is right, but she'd be able to is imagined too: in fact she can't.","Yes: two truths with two dates: no charging last night, no call now.","The times are the wrong way round: the condition is last night and the result is now."],"hint":"Date each half. Which half is about last night and which is about now?","why":"The condition is imagined past (had charged) and the result is imagined present (would be able), so there are two truths with two dates.","truth":"Mook didn't charge her phone (last night); she can't call (now)."},{"id":"pl-10","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w5l1","world":"w5","stem":"Fah stayed up until 2 a.m. watching a K-pop live stream. If she had gone to bed earlier, she ___ so sleepy in class right now.","options":["wouldn't have been","won't be","wouldn't be","isn't"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The have ticket makes the result past, but right now is the present.","Will is for a real future. This result is imagined: in fact she is sleepy.","Yes: a past condition with a present result: would plus the verb, no have ticket.","The present says it as a fact, but in fact she is sleepy. Step back once."],"hint":"When is the result: last night or right now? Does it need a past ticket?","why":"The condition is past, but the result is about right now, so it takes would plus the verb with no have.","truth":"Fah didn't go to bed early (last night); she is sleepy (now)."},{"id":"pl-11","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l3","world":"w6","stem":"Mum says: \"Take an umbrella in case it rains.\" When should you take the umbrella?","options":["Later, but only when the rain has started.","Now, before you know whether it will rain.","Never, because it isn't going to rain.","Only if the forecast says that rain is certain."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["That is the meaning of if. In case is a precaution, taken before the event.","Yes: in case means because it might happen, so you act first.","In case means the rain is possible, so Mum does want you to take it.","The rain does not need to be certain: maybe is enough for a precaution."],"hint":"Try the umbrella test: does the action come before the event or after it?","why":"In case means as a precaution: you act now because something might happen later. It does not mean if."},{"id":"pl-12","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l1","world":"w6","stem":"The BTS is packed after 7 a.m. You won't get a seat ___ early.","options":["unless you don't go","if you go","unless you go","in case you go"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Unless already means if not, so never add a second negative.","This says that going early stops you getting a seat: the opposite of the meaning.","Yes: unless means except if. Going early is the one thing that gets you a seat.","In case is for a precaution taken before an event, not for a condition."],"hint":"Try the except test, and count the negatives.","why":"Unless means except if, and it already holds the negative idea, so the verb after it stays positive."},{"id":"pl-13","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l1","world":"w7","stem":"Bam says: \"I wish I lived near the school.\" What do we know?","options":["She lived near the school in the past.","She doesn't live near the school now.","She will move near the school soon.","She lives near the school and likes it."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Lived is past in form only. After wish it is about now, not about the past.","Yes: wish plus a past form is one step back, so the truth now is the opposite.","A wish is not a plan. For a real future she would say: I hope to move.","Nobody wishes for what she already has. The past form shows it is not real."],"hint":"Past form, or past time? Ask what is true now.","why":"Wish plus a past form is one step back from reality: it is about now, and the truth is the opposite.","truth":"Bam doesn't live near the school (now)."},{"id":"pl-14","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l2","world":"w7","stem":"I forgot my PE kit this morning, so I had to sit and watch. If only I ___ my bag last night!","options":["packed","have packed","would have packed","had packed"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["One step back is a wish about now. Last night is past, so step back twice.","Have packed is real time up to now. A regret about last night needs had.","Would have is the result form. After if only, a past regret takes had plus V3.","Yes: a regret about a finished past time takes had plus the past participle."],"hint":"When is the wish about: now or last night? How many steps back?","why":"If only about a finished past time is a regret, so the verb goes two steps back: had plus the past participle.","truth":"She didn't pack her bag last night."},{"id":"pl-15","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l1","world":"w8","stem":"\"Had the coach known about the storm, she would have cancelled training.\" What do we learn?","options":["The coach knew, so training was cancelled.","Somebody is asking if the coach knew about the storm.","The coach didn't know, and training went ahead.","The coach may find out and cancel training later."],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["That is the imagined world. The truth is the opposite of both halves.","It looks like a question, but there is no question mark: had has replaced if.","Yes: had before the subject means if she had known, an imagined past.","Had known and would have cancelled are both about the past, not the future."],"hint":"Had comes first, but is this a question? Try putting if back.","why":"Had the coach known means if the coach had known: a formal imagined past. So she did not know, and training was not cancelled.","truth":"The coach didn't know about the storm, and training wasn't cancelled."},{"id":"pl-16","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l1","world":"w8","stem":"From a school email: \"___ any further information about the exchange programme, please contact the office.\"","options":["If should you need","Should you needed","Should you need","Would you need"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The inverted should does the job of if, so if cannot stay in the clause.","After should the verb stays in its base form.","Yes: formal inversion: should, then the subject, then the base verb, and no if.","Only should, were and had can replace if in this way; would cannot."],"hint":"Which helping verbs can stand in for if at the front of a formal sentence, and what form follows?","why":"Formal inversion replaces if with should before the subject, followed by the base verb. It means if you need."},{"id":"pl-17","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w9l1","world":"w9","stem":"\"I only got to the audition on time because my aunt drove me.\" Which sentence means the same?","options":["If my aunt had driven me, I'd have arrived at the audition on time.","Even though my aunt drove me, I was late for the audition.","If my aunt doesn't drive me, I'll be late for the audition.","If my aunt hadn't driven me, I'd have been late for the audition."],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["This imagines that she drove, so its truth is that she didn't. But she did.","The facts say she was on time, and the lift was the reason.","This is a real future. The audition is already over.","Yes: she did drive and I was on time, so we imagine the opposite of both."],"hint":"Write the truth behind each sentence and compare it with the facts given.","why":"The facts are past and real, so the same idea imagines the opposite of each: no lift from her aunt, and a late arrival.","truth":"Her aunt drove her, and she was on time (past)."},{"id":"pl-18","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w9l2","world":"w9","stem":"From an essay: \"Were governments ___ public transport free, far fewer people would drive to work.\"","options":["made","to make","make","would make"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Made fits after if (if governments made). With inverted were, the pattern needs to plus the verb.","Yes: were, subject, to plus verb is the formal twin of if governments made.","A bare verb cannot follow were plus subject here; it needs to.","Would belongs in the result half, and it is already there."],"hint":"Put if back and say it the everyday way. What does the formal pattern add after the subject?","why":"Were plus subject plus to-infinitive is the formal, inverted form of an imagined future condition; use it once in an essay.","truth":"Governments have not made public transport free."}]},"u6":{"title":"Unit 6 grammar check","sections":[{"title":"Conditionals","instructions":"Type the verb in brackets in the correct form to make a zero, first, second or third conditional. Use short forms if you like, but do not add extra words such as might or could.","items":[{"id":"u6-1","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w1l1","stem":"Look after the basil in the hot season. If it ___ (not get) enough water, the leaves turn yellow within a week.","accept":["doesn't get","does not get"],"model":"doesn't get","hint":"Look at the verb in the result. Is this one event or something that is always true?","why":"The result (turn) is present simple, so this is a general truth: present simple in the if half too, with does for it."},{"id":"u6-2","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","stem":"Mook left her umbrella at home yesterday and got soaked. If she ___ (take) it with her, she would have stayed dry.","accept":["had taken","'d taken","she'd taken"],"model":"had taken","hint":"When is the condition about? How many steps back?","why":"Yesterday is finished and she did not take it, so the if half goes two steps back: had plus the past participle.","truth":"Mook didn't take her umbrella yesterday."},{"id":"u6-3","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w2l1","stem":"If Mum ___ (say) yes tonight, I'll come to the concert with you on Saturday.","accept":["says"],"model":"says","hint":"Which half makes the prediction? Check the subject as well.","why":"A real future condition takes the present simple after if, never will; Mum (she) needs the -s form."},{"id":"u6-4","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l2","stem":"Bam lives a long way from school, so she gets up at five. If she ___ (live) nearer, she'd sleep until half past six every day.","accept":["lived"],"model":"lived","hint":"Is the condition true now? How many steps back?","why":"She does not live nearer, so the condition is imagined now: one step back to the past simple.","truth":"Bam doesn't live near the school (now)."},{"id":"u6-5","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","stem":"We got to the cinema ten minutes late last night. If we had left on time, we ___ (see) the start of the film.","accept":["would have seen","would've seen","'d have seen","'d've seen","we'd have seen","we would have seen","we would've seen"],"model":"would have seen","hint":"When is the result? Does it need a past ticket?","why":"Last night is over and they did not leave on time, so the result is imagined past: would, the have ticket and the past participle.","truth":"They didn't leave on time last night, so they missed the start."},{"id":"u6-6","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w2l1","stem":"Hurry up, Pim! If we miss the 7.15 train, we ___ (be) late for the first lesson again.","accept":["will be","'ll be","shall be","are going to be","'re going to be","we'll be"],"model":"will be","hint":"Is missing the train still possible? Which half makes the prediction?","why":"Missing the train is a real possibility this morning, so the result is a real prediction about the future."},{"id":"u6-7","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l2","stem":"Jane's phone is always on silent. If she turned the sound on, she ___ (not miss) so many calls from her mum every day.","accept":["wouldn't miss","would not miss"],"model":"wouldn't miss","hint":"Look at the tense after if. Is the result about now or about the past?","why":"Turned is one step back for an imagined present, so the result is would not plus the base verb, with no have ticket.","truth":"Jane doesn't turn the sound on, so she misses calls (now)."},{"id":"u6-8","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l2","stem":"Nan didn't see the wet-floor sign in the canteen on Monday. If she'd seen it, she ___ (not slip).","accept":["wouldn't have slipped","would not have slipped","wouldn't've slipped","would not've slipped"],"model":"wouldn't have slipped","hint":"Read the contraction after if: what follows it? Then ask when the result is.","why":"She'd seen is she had seen, an imagined past, so the result needs would not have plus the past participle.","truth":"Nan didn't see the sign on Monday, and she slipped."}]},{"title":"unless, as long as, provided, in case","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given and do not change it. Write between two and five words. Do not add might, may, could or should.","items":[{"id":"u6-9","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l1","given":"You can't join the trip if you don't hand in the form by Friday.","keyword":"UNLESS","stem":"You can't join the trip ___ in the form by Friday.","accept":["unless you hand","unless you have handed","unless you've handed"],"model":"unless you hand","hint":"The key word already holds a negative idea. What happens to the verb after it?","why":"Unless means if not, so if you don't hand becomes the positive verb after it."},{"id":"u6-10","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l2","given":"You can borrow my tablet, but you must give it back before dinner.","keyword":"LONG","stem":"You can borrow my tablet ___ it back before dinner.","accept":["as long as you give","so long as you give","as long as I get","so long as I get","as long as you bring","so long as you bring","as long as you hand","so long as you hand"],"model":"as long as you give","hint":"This is a bargain: yes, but on one condition. Which three-word linker carries that?","why":"The phrase with long means only if: it sets the one condition for the permission, and takes the present simple."},{"id":"u6-11","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l2","given":"Mum will let me go to the concert only if I finish my project first.","keyword":"PROVIDED","stem":"Mum will let me go to the concert ___ my project first.","accept":["provided I finish","provided that I finish","provided I have finished","provided I've finished","provided that I have finished","provided that I've finished","provided I complete","provided that I complete"],"model":"provided I finish","hint":"Which tense follows this linker when the condition is in the future?","why":"Provided (that) means only if. Like if, it takes the present simple for a future condition, never will."},{"id":"u6-12","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l3","given":"Take a jacket because the cinema might be cold.","keyword":"CASE","stem":"Take a jacket ___ cold in the cinema.","accept":["in case it is","in case it's","in case it gets","in case it feels","in case it becomes","in case you get","in case you are","in case you're","in case you feel"],"model":"in case it is","hint":"Is the jacket a precaution? Which tense follows the linker for a possible future?","why":"The jacket is a precaution against a possible problem, and the linker takes the present simple, not will or might."},{"id":"u6-13","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l1","given":"If it doesn't rain on Saturday, we'll have the picnic in Lumphini Park.","keyword":"UNLESS","stem":"We'll have the picnic in Lumphini Park on Saturday ___.","accept":["unless it rains","unless it is raining","unless it's raining"],"model":"unless it rains","hint":"Rain is the one thing that stops the picnic. Count the negatives in your answer.","why":"Unless means except if: the negative if-clause becomes a positive verb after it."},{"id":"u6-14","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l3","given":"Fah might get hungry on the bus, so she is taking some snacks.","keyword":"CASE","stem":"Fah is taking some snacks ___ hungry on the bus.","accept":["in case she gets","in case she is","in case she's","in case she feels","in case she becomes"],"model":"in case she gets","hint":"She packs first; the problem may or may not come later. Which tense follows the linker?","why":"The snacks are a precaution for something that might happen, so the linker is followed by the present simple."}]},{"title":"I wish / If only","instructions":"Read the situation. Then type the verb in brackets in the correct form to complete what the girl says. Use short forms if you like.","items":[{"id":"u6-15","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l1","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Mook's best friend moved to Chiang Mai last year, and Mook misses her."}],"stem":"I wish she still ___ (live) in Bangkok.","accept":["lived"],"model":"lived","hint":"Is the wish about now or about the past? How many steps back?","why":"A wish about the present takes one step back: the past simple, although the time is now.","truth":"Her friend doesn't live in Bangkok now."},{"id":"u6-16","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l1","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Nan loves swimming, but her school has no pool."}],"stem":"If only there ___ (be) a pool at my school.","accept":["were","was"],"model":"were","hint":"What is true at her school now? Step back once from that.","why":"If only about the present takes a past form of be. Write were in exams; was is also heard in speech.","truth":"There isn't a pool at Nan's school (now)."},{"id":"u6-17","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l1","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Fern would love to join the trip on Saturday, but she has an extra class that day."}],"stem":"I wish I ___ (not have) an extra class on Saturday.","accept":["didn't have","did not have"],"model":"didn't have","hint":"The class is a fact she cannot change. Which form shows distance from reality?","why":"The class is real, so the wish steps back once: a negative past simple, even though Saturday is still to come.","truth":"Fern has an extra class on Saturday."},{"id":"u6-18","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Jane spent all her pocket money on stickers on Monday. Now she can't buy a birthday present for her friend."}],"stem":"I wish I ___ (not spend) all my money on Monday.","accept":["hadn't spent","had not spent"],"model":"hadn't spent","hint":"When did it happen? How many steps back for a regret?","why":"Monday is finished, so this is a regret: two steps back, had not plus the past participle.","truth":"Jane spent all her money on Monday."},{"id":"u6-19","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l2","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Praew didn't save her essay last night, and then the laptop crashed."}],"stem":"If only I ___ (save) my essay last night!","accept":["had saved","'d saved","I'd saved"],"model":"had saved","hint":"Is this about now or about a finished time? Count the steps back.","why":"Last night is finished and she did not do it, so if only takes had plus the past participle.","truth":"Praew didn't save her essay last night."},{"id":"u6-20","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l3","lines":[{"who":"Situation","text":"Bam is trying to revise. Her neighbours are playing loud music again, and it annoys her."}],"stem":"I wish they ___ (turn) the music down!","accept":["would turn","'d turn","they'd turn"],"model":"would turn","hint":"She wants other people to act differently. Which helping verb shows that after wish?","why":"For someone else's annoying behaviour that you want to change, wish is followed by would plus the base verb.","truth":"The neighbours aren't turning the music down."}]}]},"finalB2":{"title":"Final check B2","sections":[{"title":"Choose the best answer","instructions":"Choose the one answer that fits the meaning and the grammar.","items":[{"id":"fb2-1","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"0","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w1l2","stem":"A school rule: if a student ___ late three times in one month, she must see the head of year.","options":["will arrive","arrive","arrives","would arrive"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["No will after if. A rule is always true, so the if half is present simple.","A student is one person (she), so the present simple needs -s.","Yes: a rule is a real, always-true conditional: present simple, with -s.","Would is for an imagined result. A rule is real, and would never follows a plain if."],"hint":"Is this a rule that is always true, or one imagined event? Check the subject too.","why":"A rule is a real, always-true conditional: present simple after if (with -s for a student), and must in the result."},{"id":"fb2-2","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w2l3","stem":"Which speaker is sure that her mum will come home?","options":["If Mum gets home, I'll ask her about the trip.","When Mum gets home, I'll ask her about the trip.","If Mum got home, I'd ask her about the trip.","If Mum had got home, I'd have asked her about the trip."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["If leaves it open: maybe she will come home, maybe not.","Yes: when treats the event as expected; only the time is unknown.","The step back shows the speaker is only imagining it.","Two steps back: this imagines a past that did not happen."],"hint":"Which linking word treats the event as expected, not just possible?","why":"When presents the event as expected; if leaves it open; the stepped-back forms only imagine it."},{"id":"fb2-3","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l1","stem":"Fern says: \"If I spoke Japanese, I'd watch anime without subtitles.\" What is true now?","options":["She speaks Japanese well.","She spoke Japanese when she was younger.","She is going to learn Japanese next year.","She doesn't speak Japanese."],"answer":3,"near":1,"fb":["If that were true, she would not need to imagine it.","Spoke is past in form only. Here it shows distance from reality, not past time.","The sentence makes no plan. A plan would be: if I learn Japanese, I'll watch.","Yes: one step back from reality, so the truth now is the opposite."],"hint":"The verb after if is past in form. Is it about past time, or about reality?","why":"Spoke with would is one step back from reality: it imagines a present that is not true.","truth":"Fern doesn't speak Japanese (now)."},{"id":"fb2-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l2","stem":"My laptop is at home, sadly. If I had it here, I ___ you the file right now.","options":["can send","could send","could have sent","will send"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Can is for a real result. The laptop is not here, so step back once.","Yes: imagined now, so can steps back once and takes the base verb.","The have ticket makes the result past, but the result is right now.","Will is for a real future. This situation is imagined."],"hint":"Is the result real or imagined? Is it about now or the past?","why":"The laptop is not here, so the result is imagined and present: can steps back once to could plus the base verb.","truth":"I don't have my laptop here (now)."},{"id":"fb2-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l2","stem":"Jane went to the wrong room this morning. If she ___ the LINE group last night, she'd have known about the change.","options":["checked","has checked","would have checked","had checked"],"answer":3,"near":2,"fb":["One step back is for now. Last night is past and imagined: step back twice.","Has checked is real time up to now. An imagined past needs had.","Would have is the result form. It never goes in the if half.","Yes: imagined past in the if half: had plus the past participle."],"hint":"When is the condition about? Which half takes would?","why":"Last night is finished and she did not check, so the if half needs had plus the past participle; would have stays in the result.","truth":"Jane didn't check the LINE group last night."},{"id":"fb2-6","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l3","stem":"Which sentence tells us that Praew <b>did</b> bring her umbrella?","options":["If Praew had brought her umbrella, she wouldn't have got soaked.","If Praew brings her umbrella, she won't get soaked.","If Praew hadn't brought her umbrella, she'd have got soaked.","If Praew brought her umbrella, she wouldn't get soaked."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Flip it: had brought is imagined, so in fact she did not bring it.","A real future: we don't know yet whether she will bring it.","Yes: flip the imagined negative and you get the fact: she brought it. This is relief.","This imagines now or the future; it says nothing about what she did."],"hint":"Flip each if half to find the fact behind it.","why":"An imagined past shows the opposite of the facts, so the negative if half means that she really did bring it.","truth":"Praew brought her umbrella, and she didn't get soaked (past)."},{"id":"fb2-7","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w5l3","stem":"\"If I had joined the debate club in M1, I'd be a more confident speaker now.\" What time is each half about?","options":["past condition, past result","present condition, past result","past condition, present result","present condition, present result"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The condition is past, but the result has no have ticket and says now.","The times are the wrong way round: M1 is the past and now is the present.","Yes: had joined is the past and would be is now: a mixed conditional.","Had joined with in M1 is two steps back: that half is about the past."],"hint":"Date each half with its own time word.","why":"Had joined (in M1) is an imagined past; would be (now) is an imagined present. Each half is built from its own time.","truth":"She didn't join in M1 (past); she isn't such a confident speaker (now)."},{"id":"fb2-8","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l4","stem":"We all got soaked, but nobody stayed at home. ___ it was pouring with rain yesterday, the whole team turned up for training.","options":["Even if","Provided that","Even though","Unless"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Even if is for something that may or may not be true. The rain was a fact.","Provided that sets a condition for a bargain. The rain was no condition for coming.","Yes: the rain really happened, and the result is a surprise: that is the truth test.","Unless means except if, but the team came and it did rain."],"hint":"Try the truth test: did it really rain, or is it only a maybe?","why":"The rain is a fact, not a possibility, so the linker must be the one for true things that do not stop the result."}]},{"title":"Find the mistake","instructions":"One of the four marked parts is wrong. Tap it.","items":[{"id":"fb2-9","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"1","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w2l1","stem":"If the rain [[will stop]] before four, we [[will play]] the match [[as planned]], so [[bring]] your racket.","answer":0,"fix":"stops","fb":["Yes: this is the mistake. No will after if for a plain future condition: the rain stops.","This part is fine: will belongs in the result, where the prediction is made.","This part is fine: as planned is a normal fixed phrase.","This part is fine: an imperative is a normal result in a real conditional."],"hint":"Which half takes the photo and which half makes the promise?","why":"A real future condition takes the present simple after if; will belongs only in the result."},{"id":"fb2-10","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","stem":"If Mint [[had saved]] her work last night, she [[wouldn't lose]] the whole essay [[when]] the laptop [[crashed]].","answer":1,"fix":"wouldn't have lost","fb":["This part is fine: an imagined past condition takes had plus the past participle.","Yes: this is the mistake. The result is past too, so it needs the have ticket.","This part is fine: when introduces the real past event.","This part is fine: the crash really happened, so the past simple is right."],"hint":"When did she lose the essay? Is there a past ticket in the result?","why":"Both halves are about last night, so the result needs would not have plus the past participle."},{"id":"fb2-11","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l1","stem":"You [[won't improve]] your serve unless you [[don't practise]] it [[every week]] with [[a partner]].","answer":1,"fix":"practise","fb":["This part is fine: will not is a normal real result.","Yes: this is the mistake. Unless already means if not, so the verb stays positive.","This part is fine: every week is a normal time phrase.","This part is fine: with a partner is correct."],"hint":"Count the negatives after the linker.","why":"Unless means except if. A second negative after it reverses the meaning, so the verb must be positive."},{"id":"fb2-12","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l1","stem":"I wish I [[have]] more free time [[this term]], because I [[would love]] to join the art club [[as well]].","answer":0,"fix":"had","fb":["Yes: this is the mistake. After wish, a present situation takes a past form.","This part is fine: this term tells us the wish is about now.","This part is fine: would love is a normal polite way to say what you want.","This part is fine: as well is correct at the end."],"hint":"After wish, how far does the verb step back for a wish about now?","why":"Wish about the present is one step back from reality: the past simple, although the time is now."}]},{"title":"In conversation","instructions":"Read the conversation and choose the answer that fits what the speakers mean.","items":[{"id":"fb2-13","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l3","lines":[{"who":"Mook","text":"Why are you carrying two power banks?"},{"who":"Fah","text":"The concert doesn't finish until eleven."}],"stem":"I'm taking a spare one ___ the first one runs out.","options":["unless","in case","even though","as long as"],"answer":1,"near":3,"fb":["Unless means except if: she would then leave the spare at home when it is needed.","Yes: a precaution taken now, because the problem might come later.","Even though is for a fact. The first one has not run out yet.","As long as makes a bargain, but running out is a risk, not a deal."],"hint":"Is the spare one packed before or after the problem happens?","why":"She packs the spare one now as a precaution, because the first one might run out later."},{"id":"fb2-14","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l3","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"Coach, I'm so sorry I missed the start of training yesterday."},{"who":"Coach","text":"What happened?"},{"who":"Pim","text":"My mum's car broke down on the way."}],"stem":"What does Pim say next?","options":["If it doesn't break down, I'll be here on time.","If it hadn't broken down, I'd have been here on time.","If it didn't break down, I'd be here on time.","Unless it breaks down, I'll be here on time."],"answer":1,"near":2,"fb":["A real future, but Pim is explaining yesterday, and the car did break down.","Yes: an imagined past explains what went wrong yesterday.","One step back is about now. The breakdown was yesterday: step back twice.","This is about the future. It does not explain yesterday."],"hint":"When did the problem happen? How many steps back does the reply need?","why":"The breakdown is a finished past fact, so the excuse imagines the opposite past: had not plus V3, would have plus V3.","truth":"The car broke down yesterday, and Pim wasn't on time."},{"id":"fb2-15","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l3","lines":[{"who":"Jane","text":"Good luck in the speech contest tomorrow!"},{"who":"Fern","text":"Thanks! I've practised all week."}],"stem":"What does Fern say next?","options":["I wish I didn't forget my lines.","I hope I don't forget my lines.","I wish I hadn't forgotten my lines.","If only I hadn't forgotten them."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Wish plus past is for something unreal now. Tomorrow is still open, so she hopes.","Yes: a possible future takes hope plus the present.","That is a regret about the past, but the contest has not happened yet.","If only plus had is a past regret. Nothing has gone wrong yet."],"hint":"Is the contest finished, or still to come? Is the good result possible?","why":"The contest is tomorrow and remembering is possible, so she hopes. Wish with a past form is for something unreal now or a past regret."},{"id":"fb2-16","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"m32","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w5l1","lines":[{"who":"Fern","text":"Why are you yawning so much? It's only nine o'clock."},{"who":"Jane","text":"I stayed up reading until two."}],"stem":"What does Jane say next?","options":["If I hadn't stayed up so late, I'd be very sleepy now.","If I'd stayed up until two, I wouldn't be so sleepy now.","If I'd gone to bed at ten, I wouldn't be so sleepy now.","If I stay up until two, I'm never sleepy the next day."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["The logic is reversed: staying up is the reason she is sleepy.","She did stay up until two, so she cannot imagine it as unreal.","Yes: an imagined past (bed at ten) with an imagined present result (not sleepy now).","This is a general fact, but her yawning shows the opposite."],"hint":"Write the two truths: what she did last night, and how she feels now.","why":"She did not go to bed at ten (past) and she is sleepy (now): a past condition with a present result.","truth":"Jane didn't go to bed at ten (last night); she is sleepy (now)."}]},{"title":"Rewrite it","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given and do not change it. Write between two and five words.","items":[{"id":"fb2-17","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w6l1","given":"We'll miss the start of the film if we don't leave now.","keyword":"UNLESS","stem":"We'll miss the start of the film ___ now.","accept":["unless we leave"],"model":"unless we leave","hint":"The key word already holds a negative idea. What happens to the verb?","why":"Unless means if not, so the negative verb becomes positive after it."},{"id":"fb2-18","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w7l2","given":"I didn't bring a jacket to the cinema last night, and I regret it.","keyword":"WISH","stem":"I ___ a jacket to the cinema last night.","accept":["wish I had brought","wish I'd brought","wish that I had brought","wish that I'd brought","wish I had taken","wish I'd taken","wish that I had taken","wish that I'd taken"],"model":"wish I had brought","hint":"Last night is finished. How many steps back for a regret?","why":"A regret about a finished past time takes wish plus had plus the past participle.","truth":"She didn't bring a jacket last night."},{"id":"fb2-19","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"3","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w4l1","given":"Mook didn't hear her alarm, so she missed the school bus.","keyword":"HAD","stem":"If Mook ___ her alarm, she wouldn't have missed the school bus.","accept":["had heard"],"model":"had heard","hint":"The fact is negative and past. Flip it, then step back twice.","why":"She did not hear it, so the imagined past condition is positive: the key word plus the past participle.","truth":"Mook didn't hear her alarm, and she missed the bus."},{"id":"fb2-20","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"2","cefr":"B2","lesson":"w3l2","given":"My advice is to tell the teacher today.","keyword":"WERE","stem":"If ___ tell the teacher today.","accept":["I were you, I would","I were you I would","I were you, I'd","I were you I'd"],"model":"I were you, I would","hint":"Which fixed phrase gives advice by imagining a swap of places? What follows it in the result?","why":"Advice with an imagined swap is one step back: the past form of be in the if half and would plus the base verb in the result.","truth":"The speaker isn't the other person; she is only imagining it to give advice."}]}]},"finalC1":{"title":"Final check C1","sections":[{"title":"Choose the best answer","instructions":"Choose the one answer that fits the meaning and the grammar.","items":[{"id":"fc1-1","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"conn","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w6l4","stem":"I wrote the room number on my hand; ___ I would have forgotten it before I reached the exam hall this morning.","options":["unless","otherwise","in case","even though"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Unless needs a condition after it and never takes would have.","Yes: it stands for a whole unspoken if-clause: if I had not written it down.","In case gives the reason for a precaution and takes a simple tense, not would have.","Even though introduces a fact, but forgetting the number did not happen."],"hint":"The first half is a fact. Which word can stand for 'if I had not done that'?","why":"After a semicolon, one linker can replace a whole negative if-clause; the would have result shows the imagined past.","truth":"She wrote the number down, so she didn't forget it."},{"id":"fc1-2","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"m32","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w5l1","stem":"I didn't take that IELTS course last summer. If I ___ it, I'd feel far more confident about tomorrow's test.","options":["took","would have taken","have taken","had taken"],"answer":3,"near":0,"fb":["One step back is for now or the future, but the course was last summer.","Would have is a result form. It never goes in the if half.","Have taken is real time up to now. An imagined past needs had.","Yes: the condition is an imagined past; the result (I'd feel) is about now."],"hint":"Date each half separately. Which time is the condition about?","why":"The condition is last summer (two steps back), while the result is about now (one step back): a mixed conditional.","truth":"She didn't take the course (last summer); she doesn't feel so confident (now)."},{"id":"fc1-3","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l1","stem":"You handled the judges so calmly yesterday. ___ in your place, I would have frozen completely.","options":["If had I been","Had I been","Would I have been","Have I been"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The inverted had does the job of if, so if cannot stay.","Yes: had before the subject is the formal form of if I had been.","Would cannot be inverted to make a condition; only should, were and had can.","Have cannot replace if. An imagined past needs had."],"hint":"Which helping verbs can move in front of the subject to replace if? Which one fits an imagined past?","why":"Yesterday is an imagined past, so the formal condition inverts had with the subject and drops if.","truth":"The speaker wasn't in her place yesterday."},{"id":"fc1-4","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l2","stem":"It is only a rumour, but if the school ___ phones altogether next term, most of us would complain for a week and then get used to it.","options":["would ban","were to ban","will ban","would have banned"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Would stays in the result half. A plain if half does not take it.","Yes: were to marks a future event as remote, and it matches would in the result.","Will does not follow a plain if, and it does not match would complain.","Would have is a past result form. Next term is the future."],"hint":"The result has would. Which form keeps the if half imagined and in the future?","why":"Were to plus the base verb presents a future event as remote or unlikely; the result then takes would.","truth":"There is no such plan: the speaker treats it as remote."},{"id":"fc1-5","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l2","stem":"But for ___, I would never have found the exam room in time this morning.","options":["Fah gave me directions","Fah had given me directions","Fah's directions","Fah would give me directions"],"answer":2,"near":1,"fb":["But for cannot take a clause with a subject and a verb.","The tense is right for an if-clause, but but for is never followed by a clause.","Yes: but for is followed by a noun phrase and means if it had not been for.","No clause after but for, and would never goes in the condition."],"hint":"What kind of words can follow this phrase: a clause or a noun phrase?","why":"But for means if it had not been for, and it takes a noun phrase, never a clause.","truth":"Fah gave directions, and the speaker found the room in time."},{"id":"fc1-6","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l4","stem":"It's nearly midnight and your exam starts at eight. It's high time you ___ to bed.","options":["would go","went","will go","have gone"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Would is not used after it's high time. The verb itself steps back: the past simple.","Yes: past form, present meaning: you should be in bed already and you are not.","Will does not follow it's time.","Have gone is real time up to now; the structure needs the past simple."],"hint":"Is the person in bed? Which form shows distance from reality after this phrase?","why":"It's high time plus subject takes a past form with present meaning: it should already be happening.","truth":"You are not in bed yet (now)."},{"id":"fc1-7","type":"mcq","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l4","stem":"Bam only watched the trailer, but she talks about the film as if she ___ the whole thing.","options":["sees","would see","had seen","will have seen"],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["A present form suggests it may be true, but we know she only watched the trailer.","Would is not used after as if for an unreal past event.","Yes: the unreal event is earlier than the talking, so two steps back.","A future form cannot describe an unreal event that would be in the past."],"hint":"Is the comparison real? Is the unreal event at the same time as the talking, or before it?","why":"The seeing is unreal and earlier than the talking, so as if takes had plus the past participle.","truth":"Bam didn't see the whole film."},{"id":"fc1-8","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"mix","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l3","stem":"Fah replies: \"If Ploy said she'd finished the poster, she was joking. It's still blank.\" What, if anything, needs changing in Fah's reply?","options":["Change <em>said</em> to <em>had said</em>.","Nothing: it is correct as it stands.","Change <em>was joking</em> to <em>would be joking</em>.","Change <em>was joking</em> to <em>would have been joking</em>."],"answer":1,"near":3,"fb":["Had said would imagine a past that did not happen, but Fah accepts that Ploy probably did say it.","Yes: a real past event with a conclusion about it. Nothing is imagined, so nothing steps back.","Would be imagines a present result. The speaker is stating a conclusion about the past.","That builds a third conditional, but Fah is not imagining an unreal past: she accepts what Mint heard."],"hint":"Is Fah imagining something, or working out a conclusion from a real past event?","why":"Fah accepts that Ploy said it and draws a conclusion. Real past conditionals keep normal past tenses; nothing is imagined, so nothing steps back.","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"Ploy told me she'd finished the poster for the open day."}]}]},{"title":"Find the mistake","instructions":"One of the four marked parts is wrong. Tap it.","items":[{"id":"fc1-9","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w5l2","stem":"If Fern [[weren't]] such a careful planner, she [[would forget]] to book the minibus for [[last month's]] trip, and we [[would all have stayed]] at school.","answer":1,"fix":"would have forgotten","fb":["This part is fine: being a careful planner is permanent, so one step back is right.","Yes: this is the mistake. The booking was last month, so the result needs the have ticket.","This part is fine: it tells us the result is in the past.","This part is fine: an imagined past result with would, have and the past participle."],"hint":"Date each half. Which result has no past ticket?","why":"The condition is permanent (weren't), but the result happened last month, so it needs would have plus the past participle."},{"id":"fc1-10","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l1","stem":"[[Hadn't it been]] for the delay on the MRT, we [[would have arrived]] [[in plenty of time]] for the opening ceremony [[yesterday]].","answer":0,"fix":"Had it not been","fb":["Yes: this is the mistake. In an inverted condition, not stays uncontracted after the subject.","This part is fine: an imagined past result.","This part is fine: a normal fixed phrase.","This part is fine: the time word shows the past."],"hint":"In a formal inverted condition, where does not go?","why":"Inverted conditions never contract the negative: had, then the subject, then not."},{"id":"fc1-11","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l3","stem":"I wish I [[would be]] more confident [[when I speak]] in front of the class, but I [[always freeze]] [[at the start]].","answer":0,"fix":"were","fb":["Yes: this is the mistake. Wish plus would is for other people's behaviour, not your own state.","This part is fine: a present time clause about a habit.","This part is fine: the real present habit.","This part is fine: a normal time phrase."],"hint":"Whose behaviour can follow wish plus would? What does a wish about your own state take?","why":"For your own present state, wish takes a past form. Wish plus would is kept for someone else's behaviour."},{"id":"fc1-12","type":"spot","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l2","stem":"[[Were]] the school [[to move]] the exam to Saturday, half the class [[will miss]] it [[because of]] tutoring.","answer":2,"fix":"would miss","fb":["This part is fine: were can stand at the front in place of if.","This part is fine: were plus subject plus to-infinitive is the formal pattern.","Yes: this is the mistake. A remote condition needs an imagined result, not a real one.","This part is fine: because of plus a noun is correct."],"hint":"The condition is remote. Does the result match it?","why":"Were to presents the event as remote, so the result must step back as well."}]},{"title":"In conversation","instructions":"Read the conversation and choose the answer that fits what the speakers mean.","items":[{"id":"fc1-13","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l3","lines":[{"who":"Mint","text":"Is your little sister still learning the recorder?"},{"who":"Fah","text":"Every single evening. I wish she'd give it a rest for one night."}],"stem":"What is Fah doing in her last sentence?","options":["Regretting something her sister did last night.","Complaining about a habit she wants to change.","Politely asking her sister for a favour.","Describing a plan her sister has already made."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["A past regret would need had plus a past participle; here a base verb follows the contraction.","Yes: wish plus would shows annoyance at someone else's repeated behaviour.","She is talking to Mint about her sister, not asking her sister for anything.","A wish is the opposite of a plan: the truth is that her sister does not rest."],"hint":"Read the contraction: what follows it, a base verb or a past participle?","why":"She'd give is she would give. Wish plus would shows annoyance at someone else's repeated behaviour and a desire for change.","truth":"Her sister doesn't give it a rest."},{"id":"fc1-14","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"1","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l3","lines":[{"who":"Visitor","text":"Good morning. I have an appointment with the director at ten."},{"who":"Officer","text":"She's still in a meeting, I'm afraid. If you'll take a seat, I'll let her know you're here."}],"stem":"Why does the officer use <em>will</em> after <em>if</em>?","options":["It is a mistake; it should be <em>if you take</em>.","It is a polite request: <em>will</em> means <em>are willing to</em>.","It is correct because the result also has <em>will</em>.","It is correct because the whole event is in the future."],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["The plain rule bends here: this will is not a future marker.","Yes: after if, will can mean willingness, which makes a polite request.","A will in the result never allows a future will in the if half.","Future time alone takes the present after if. Something else makes this one right."],"hint":"Try replacing the word with another phrase. Is it about time, or about being ready to do something?","why":"Will after if is fine when it means willingness: here it turns the condition into a polite request."},{"id":"fc1-15","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"conn","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w6l4","lines":[{"who":"Pim","text":"Will the parade be cancelled if it rains on sports day?"},{"who":"Coach","text":"No chance. We've waited all year for this."}],"stem":"It's going ahead ___ it rains.","options":["unless","whether or not","on condition that","in case"],"answer":1,"near":0,"fb":["Unless would mean that rain stops the parade, but the coach has just said no chance.","Yes: the result is the same either way.","That would make rain the requirement for the parade.","In case is for a precaution, not for something that makes no difference."],"hint":"Does the rain change the result at all?","why":"The coach rules out cancelling, so the rain makes no difference: the linker must mean in both cases."},{"id":"fc1-16","type":"mcq","skill":"meaning","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l4","lines":[{"who":"Bam","text":"Shall I post the group photo on the class page tonight?"},{"who":"Fern","text":"Hmm. Half of us had our eyes closed in that one."}],"stem":"What does Fern say next?","options":["I'd rather you hadn't, to be honest.","It's high time you did, to be honest.","I'd rather you didn't, to be honest.","If only you had, to be honest."],"answer":2,"near":0,"fb":["Had plus not looks back at something already done, but Bam has not posted it yet.","This urges Bam to post it, which does not fit the closed eyes.","Yes: a preference about someone else's action, now or in the future.","This regrets that Bam did not post it earlier: the opposite of what Fern wants."],"hint":"Has Bam posted it yet? Which time is Fern's preference about?","why":"The photo isn't posted yet, so the preference is about the future: would rather plus subject plus past simple.","truth":"Bam hasn't posted the photo yet."}]},{"title":"Rewrite it","instructions":"Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given and do not change it. Write between two and six words.","items":[{"id":"fc1-17","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l1","given":"If you need any more information, please email the office.","keyword":"SHOULD","stem":"___ any more information, please email the office.","accept":["Should you need","Should you require","Should you want"],"model":"Should you need","hint":"The key word takes the place of if. What word order and verb form follow?","why":"In formal inversion the key word replaces if and comes before the subject, followed by the base verb."},{"id":"fc1-18","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"formal","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w8l2","given":"If my coach hadn't encouraged me, I would have given up badminton last year.","keyword":"BUT","stem":"___ encouragement, I would have given up badminton last year.","accept":["But for my coach's"],"model":"But for my coach's","hint":"The phrase with the key word cannot take a clause. What can it take?","why":"But for means if it had not been for and is followed by a noun phrase, so the clause becomes a possessive plus noun.","truth":"Her coach encouraged her, and she didn't give up."},{"id":"fc1-19","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"m23","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w5l2","given":"Nan doesn't speak Japanese, so she didn't understand the announcement at the station.","keyword":"SPOKE","stem":"If Nan ___ understood the announcement at the station.","accept":["spoke Japanese, she would have","spoke Japanese, she would've","spoke Japanese, she'd have","spoke Japanese, she could have","spoke Japanese, she could've","spoke Japanese she would have","spoke Japanese she would've","spoke Japanese she'd have","spoke Japanese she could have","spoke Japanese she could've","spoke Japanese she'd've"],"model":"spoke Japanese, she would have","hint":"Date each half: which is always true, and which happened once in the past?","why":"Not speaking Japanese is a permanent fact (one step back); the announcement is past, so the result needs the have ticket.","truth":"Nan doesn't speak Japanese (always); she didn't understand the announcement (then)."},{"id":"fc1-20","type":"type","skill":"form","ctype":"wish","cefr":"C1","lesson":"w7l3","given":"My brother keeps borrowing my headphones without asking, and it annoys me.","keyword":"WISH","stem":"I ___ borrowing my headphones without asking.","accept":["wish my brother would stop","wish that my brother would stop","wish my brother wouldn't keep","wish that my brother wouldn't keep","wish my brother'd stop","wish he would stop","wish he'd stop","wish he wouldn't keep","wish my brother would not keep","wish he would not keep","wish that he would stop","wish that he'd stop","wish that he wouldn't keep","wish my brother didn't keep","wish my brother did not keep","wish he didn't keep","wish he did not keep","wish that my brother didn't keep","wish that he didn't keep","wish my brother wouldn't keep on","wish he wouldn't keep on"],"model":"wish my brother would stop","hint":"It is someone else's repeated behaviour. Which helping verb follows the key word?","why":"For another person's annoying habit that you want to change, wish is usually followed by would plus the base verb.","truth":"Her brother keeps borrowing them."}]}]}},"story":{"title":"What-If Worlds: Pim and the Glitch","cast":[{"name":"Pim","who":"A 13-year-old M2 student from Bangkok who loves badminton and bubble tea. She fixes broken sentences, one block at a time."},{"name":"Iffy","who":"A small blocky fox-cat and Pim's guide. She can only speak in conditional sentences, and she hums."},{"name":"The Glitch","who":"A grey fog that breaks the sentences holding each world together. Everything it says comes out wrong."},{"name":"Cog","who":"The robot forewoman of the Fact Factory. Facts only."},{"name":"Auntie Bao","who":"A bubble tea stallholder in Promise Plaza who always has a deal to offer."},{"name":"Mali","who":"The girl in the yellow hard hat who runs Dream Tycoon."},{"name":"Madam Tock","who":"The clockwork owl who keeps the yesterdays in Rewind Tower."},{"name":"The Geese","who":"Two crossing guards in orange vests at Glitch Crossroads, where Then meets Now."},{"name":"Granny Tao","who":"A block turtle who keeps the tolls on the five Connector Bridges."},{"name":"Kob","who":"The enormous frog who looks after the wells on Wish Island."},{"name":"The Herons","who":"Two stone gate guards at the Formal Citadel. They answer only formal speech."}],"chapters":[{"id":"ch0","world":null,"title":"The Rainy Evening","mission":"Follow Iffy to the nine gates and learn to set the two dials: When? Real or imagined?","after":"Pim knows the rule of the game now: set both dials before you speak. Nine worlds are waiting.","pages":[{"scene":"home","text":"Rain hammered on the window of Pim's bedroom. Badminton practice was cancelled, her bubble tea was finished, and her homework was almost done. Almost.\n\n\"[[0|If it rains this hard, our soi always floods|Zero conditional. A fact that is true every time: real, always.]],\" she told her cat, who did not care. \"[[1|If I finish this last maths question, Mum will let me play for half an hour|First conditional. A real, possible future. No will after if.]].\"\n\nShe finished it, more or less, and opened the game on her tablet. What-If Worlds. Everything in it was made of blocks: block trees, block clouds, even a block sun.\n\nBut tonight the loading screen looked wrong. A grey fog was crawling over the title, eating the letters one by one. \"Weird,\" said Pim, and she tapped the screen. Her finger went straight through the glass. Then her hand. Then, with a small pop, the rest of her.","grammar":"Two real conditionals from ordinary life: a fact (zero) and a possible future (first)."},{"scene":"factory","text":"Pim landed on a square of grass that was, frankly, too square. Her hands were blocks. Her trainers were blocks. A small orange creature, half fox and half cat, was staring at her. The tag on her collar said Iffy.\n\nBehind them, the grey fog swallowed a bridge, and the bridge fell to pieces. \"[[1|If the Glitch touches you, you will forget the way home|First conditional. A real danger in a possible future.]],\" said Iffy. \"[[0|If a sentence breaks, the thing it holds up falls down|Zero conditional. A law of this world: real, and true every time.]].\"\n\n\"Do you only talk in ifs?\" asked Pim.\n\nIffy nodded, and held up a compass with two dials. One said: When? The other said: Real or imagined? \"[[1|If you set both dials before you speak, your sentences will hold|First conditional. Real and possible, about the future.]].\"\n\n\"And how do I get home?\" asked Pim.\n\nIffy pointed at nine glowing gates. \"[[1|If you repair all nine worlds, the game will send you home|First conditional. A real promise about the future.]]. [[2|If I were you, I would start with the factory|Second conditional, for advice. Truth: Iffy is not Pim. Imagined, now.]].\"","grammar":"The two dials (When? Real or imagined?) with real conditionals and one imagined one: If I were you."}]},{"id":"ch1","world":"w1","title":"The Fact Factory","mission":"Restart the machines: every one runs on a rule that is always true.","after":"The belts are moving and the lights are on. In the Fact Factory, a true rule keeps both halves in the present.","pages":[{"scene":"factory","text":"The Fact Factory hummed like a giant fridge. Every machine had a sign on it, and every sign was a rule.\n\n\"[[0|If you press the green button, the belt moves|Zero conditional: if + present, present. It happens every time.]],\" Pim read aloud. She pressed it. The belt moved. \"OK, that's satisfying.\"\n\nA tall robot with a spanner for a hand rolled over. Her badge said Cog, Forewoman. \"Facts only in here,\" she said. \"[[0|If a rule is true every time, we write both halves in the present|Zero conditional. Real, and true always.]]. No maybes. No dreams. [[0|If you want dreams, go to World Three|Zero conditional with an imperative result: an instruction.]].\"\n\nThen Pim saw grey fog curling around the biggest machine. Its sign flickered: \"If you heat ice, it melted.\"\n\n\"That's broken,\" said Pim. \"Ice doesn't melt only in the past. It's always true.\" The machine coughed out a square of black smoke and stopped. So did the next one. And the next.","grammar":"Zero conditional: if + present, present, for rules and facts; an imperative result for instructions."},{"scene":"factory","text":"\"[[0|If one machine stops, the whole line stops|Zero conditional. A fact about the factory: true every time.]],\" said Iffy, hiding behind Pim's leg.\n\nPim climbed onto the machine and pulled the last block off the sign: \"melted\". She found a fresh block in the box below, pushed it in and read the sign aloud. \"[[0|If you heat ice, it melts|Zero conditional, repaired: both halves in the present, because it is always true.]].\" The machine purred.\n\n\"Not bad,\" said Cog. \"Next one.\" The Glitch had been busy. The second sign said: \"If water will reach 100 degrees, it boils.\"\n\n\"That one's broken too. No will after if,\" said Pim, surprising herself. She pulled out \"will\" and changed \"reach\" to \"reaches\". The fog hissed and slid away under the door.\n\nCog clapped twice, and the lights came on. \"[[0|If Cog claps twice, the lights come on|Zero conditional. Notice the -s on claps after a name, he, she or it.]],\" Pim told Iffy.\n\nCog pointed at a lever. \"[[0|If the red light flashes, don't touch that|Zero conditional with an imperative result: a safety rule.]].\" Three machines were still silent. Each one was waiting for a rule that only Pim could finish.","grammar":"Zero conditional repaired: both halves in the present, no will after if, -s after he/she/it, and an imperative result."}]},{"id":"ch2","world":"w2","title":"Promise Plaza","mission":"Reopen the market: repair the deals, offers and warnings about the possible future.","after":"The shutters are up and the promises can be kept again: present after if, and will in the promise.","pages":[{"scene":"plaza","text":"World Two smelled of grilled corn and sweet milk tea. Promise Plaza was a market of a hundred block stalls, and every stallholder was shouting an offer.\n\n\"[[1|If you buy two mango cubes, I'll give you a third one free|First conditional: if + present, will. An offer about a possible future.]]!\" called a block hen in a pink apron.\n\nPim stopped at a bubble tea stall, obviously. The owner, Auntie Bao, had pearls the size of dice. \"I need a helper,\" she said. \"[[1|If you carry these cups to the fountain, you can have a large brown sugar tea|First conditional with can: a deal. The result gives permission.]]. But be quick. [[1|If you don't hurry, the ice will melt|First conditional as a warning. Present after if, even for the future.]].\"\n\n\"Deal,\" said Pim.\n\nIffy's ears went flat. \"[[1|If the Glitch finds this market, nobody will be able to keep a promise|First conditional. A real danger in the future.]].\" Right on time, grey fog poured over the rooftops.","grammar":"First conditional: if + present, will or can, for offers, warnings and deals about a possible future."},{"scene":"plaza","text":"The fog touched the stall signs, and the promises went wrong. One sign now said: \"If you will come back tomorrow, I give you a discount.\" The stall's shutter slammed down. A promise that was built wrongly could not be kept.\n\n\"Both halves are broken,\" said Pim. \"The if half just takes a photo of the future, so it stays in the present. The will belongs in the promise.\" She moved the blocks and read the sign aloud. \"[[1|If you come back tomorrow, I'll give you a discount|First conditional, repaired: present after if, will in the promise.]].\" The shutter flew up.\n\nStall by stall, Pim swapped the blocks. Auntie Bao poured her tea with extra pearls. \"[[1|If you fix the rest, the plaza might name a drink after you|First conditional with might: a possible result, not a certain one.]].\"\n\n\"[[1|If we stay here drinking, the fog will reach the fountain first|First conditional. A warning about a real, possible future.]],\" said Iffy, tugging her sleeve. Pim sighed, took a very long sip, and ran. Three streets of broken promises were waiting.","grammar":"First conditional repaired (no will after if), plus a might result for a possible but uncertain future."}]},{"id":"ch3","world":"w3","title":"Dream Tycoon","mission":"Rebuild the dream city: imagined things only stand if you step one tense back from reality.","after":"The city is growing again. Here a past form is not about the past: it means one step away from what is real.","pages":[{"scene":"tycoon","text":"World Three was an empty field of green blocks with one sign: \"Dream Tycoon. Say it, build it.\" A girl in a yellow hard hat jumped down from a crane. \"I'm Mali. I run this place. Go on, dream something.\"\n\nPim thought. \"If I have a bubble tea shop, I will give everyone free pearls.\" Nothing happened. One sad grey block fell out of the sky.\n\n\"That sentence is a plan, not a dream,\" said Mali. \"You haven't got a shop. Here, imagined things are built one step back from reality.\"\n\nIffy nodded. \"[[2|If I were you, I would move both verbs one step back|Second conditional, for advice. Truth: Iffy is not Pim.]].\"\n\nPim tried again. \"[[2|If I had a bubble tea shop, I would give everyone free pearls|Second conditional: if + past, would. Truth: she has no shop. Imagined, now.]].\" Blocks rained down. In ten seconds a pink shop stood in front of her, with a queue of block ducks outside.\n\n\"The past form isn't about the past,\" said Mali. \"It's about distance from what's real.\"","grammar":"Second conditional: if + past, would, for an imagined present; If I were you for advice."},{"scene":"tycoon","text":"Pim could not stop. \"[[2|If this roof were flat, we could put a badminton court on it|Second conditional with could. Truth: the roof is not flat. Were is the safe form in writing.]].\" The roof went flat. A net appeared.\n\nMali whistled. \"[[2|If more players dreamed like you, my city would grow much faster|Second conditional. Truth: not many players dream like Pim. Imagined, now.]].\"\n\nThen the fog reached the rollercoaster. Its sign flickered: \"If I would have a rollercoaster, I ride it all day.\" The track began to crumble.\n\n\"Broken. No would in the if half,\" said Pim, pushing the blocks around. \"[[2|If I had a rollercoaster, I would ride it all day|Second conditional, repaired: past after if, would in the result. Truth: she has no rollercoaster.]].\" The track snapped back together and a little car rattled past.\n\n\"[[2|If we didn't have six more worlds to save, I would ask for a ride|Second conditional with a negative. Truth: they do have six more worlds to save.]],\" said Iffy sadly. Half the city was still grey, waiting to be imagined properly.","grammar":"Second conditional with could, a negative, and no would in the if half."}]},{"id":"ch4","world":"w4","title":"Rewind Tower","mission":"Climb the tower of yesterdays: you cannot change the past, only imagine it differently.","after":"Every floor is solid again. An imagined past takes two steps back: had + third form, would have + third form.","pages":[{"scene":"tower","text":"The lift in Rewind Tower had no buttons, only a clock running backwards. The doors opened on the first floor, and Pim saw herself. It was yesterday.\n\nYesterday-Pim was leaving the flat without an umbrella. \"Take it!\" Pim shouted. \"It's going to pour!\" Yesterday-Pim walked straight through her like a hologram, and missed the BTS by three seconds.\n\n\"You cannot change it,\" said a clockwork owl on the stairs. Her name plate said Madam Tock. \"The past is locked. You can only imagine it differently, two steps back.\"\n\nPim watched herself get soaked. \"[[3|If I had taken my umbrella, I wouldn't have got wet|Third conditional: had + V3, would have + V3. Truth: she didn't take it, yesterday.]].\"\n\n\"[[3|If you had left two minutes earlier, you would have caught the train|Third conditional. Truth: she left late and missed it, yesterday.]],\" said Iffy helpfully.\n\n\"Thanks, Iffy,\" said Pim. Then she smiled. \"[[3|If Fah hadn't lent me her jacket, I would have frozen in class|Third conditional for relief. Truth: Fah lent it, so Pim was fine.]].\"","grammar":"Third conditional: if + had + V3, would have + V3, for regret and relief about yesterday."},{"scene":"tower","text":"The stairs to the next floor were made of sentences, and the fog had got there first. One step said: \"If I have known about the quiz, I would studied.\" When Pim put her foot on it, it cracked.\n\n\"Broken,\" she said. \"Two steps back means had known. And the result needs its past ticket: have, plus the third form.\" She rebuilt the step block by block and read it out. \"[[3|If I had known about the quiz, I would have studied|Third conditional, repaired. Truth: she didn't know about it, yesterday.]].\" The step turned solid gold.\n\nMadam Tock flew up beside her. \"Good. [[3|If you had touched anything on that floor, the tower would have thrown you out|Third conditional. Truth: she touched nothing, so she is still inside.]].\"\n\n\"[[3|If we had climbed faster, we might have reached the top before the fog|Third conditional with might have: a possible past result. Truth: they climbed slowly.]],\" said Iffy. Above them, floor after floor of yesterdays was flickering grey. Pim started to climb.","grammar":"Third conditional repaired (had known; the have ticket), plus might have for a possible past result."}]},{"id":"ch5","world":"w5","title":"Glitch Crossroads","mission":"Untangle the signposts where THEN and NOW cross: each half of a sentence takes its own time.","after":"Both roads are lit. In a mixed conditional, each half is built from its own time: one from then, one from now.","pages":[{"scene":"crossroads","text":"World Five was a crossroads in the middle of nowhere. One road was marked Then. The other was marked Now. Two geese in orange vests stood where they met. \"Each half of a sentence takes its own road here,\" said the first goose. \"Imagine a different past, and you can get a different now.\"\n\nPim's stomach growled. \"[[m32|If I had eaten dinner before I opened the game, I wouldn't be so hungry now|Mixed 3/2: imagined past, result now. Truth: she didn't eat, so she is hungry.]].\"\n\n\"[[m32|If you hadn't touched the screen, you would be at home now|Mixed 3/2. Truth: she touched it (then), so she is not at home (now).]],\" said Iffy.\n\n\"It works the other way too,\" said the second goose. \"Take something that is true about you all the time. Imagine it differently, and you get a different past.\" She pointed at a signal lever high on a pole. Pim climbed. She slipped. She climbed again and pulled it.\n\n\"[[m23|If I were a better climber, I would have reached that the first time|Mixed 2/3: a general fact, a past result. Truth: she isn't a great climber, so she slipped.]],\" she said.","grammar":"Mixed conditionals: imagined past with a result now (3/2), and a general fact with a past result (2/3)."},{"scene":"crossroads","text":"Then the fog rolled in along both roads at once, and the signposts twisted. The big one in the middle now said: \"If I had slept well, I wouldn't have been tired now.\"\n\n\"That's broken,\" said Pim. \"The first half is last night, fine. But the second half says now, so it only needs one step back. No past ticket.\" She turned the arm of the sign until it clicked, and read it out. \"[[m32|If I had slept well, I wouldn't be tired now|Mixed 3/2, repaired. Truth: the speaker slept badly (then), so she is tired (now).]].\" The two roads lit up together, and the geese honked.\n\nIffy looked at her paws. \"[[m23|If I weren't so afraid of the fog, I would have helped you with that|Mixed 2/3: a general fact about Iffy, a past result. Truth: she is afraid, so she didn't help.]].\"\n\n\"[[m32|If you hadn't warned me about the Glitch, I wouldn't be here to fix anything|Mixed 3/2. Truth: Iffy warned her (then), so Pim is here (now).]],\" said Pim. \"So we're even.\" A dozen signposts still pointed the wrong way. Pim rolled up her sleeves.","grammar":"Mixed 3/2 repaired (would be, not would have been, for now), and both mixed types in conversation."}]},{"id":"ch6","world":"w6","title":"Connector Bridges","mission":"Cross the five bridges that will not open for if: unless, as long as, in case, even if, what if.","after":"All five bridges are open. If is not the only way to join a condition to its result.","pages":[{"scene":"bridges","text":"Five bridges crossed the canyon in World Six, and not one of them liked the word if.\n\nAt the first, a block turtle in a toll booth yawned. \"I'm Granny Tao. [[conn|This bridge won't open unless you say the password|Unless = if not. The bridge stays shut if she doesn't say it.]]. And before you ask, the password is unless.\"\n\n\"That's... fine,\" said Pim. \"Unless.\" The bridge opened.\n\nThe second bridge was made of glass. \"[[conn|You can cross as long as you don't look down|As long as = only if. Keep the condition and the deal is on.]],\" said Granny Tao, who had somehow got there first. Pim stared at Iffy's tail and got across.\n\nAt the third bridge the sky turned dark. Iffy handed her a tiny square umbrella. \"[[conn|Take this in case it starts to rain|In case = because it might happen. She takes it now, before the rain.]].\"\n\nPim opened it. Ten seconds later the sky emptied. She was very glad she had not waited to find out.","grammar":"Connectors other than if: unless (= if not), as long as (= only if), in case (= because it might happen)."},{"scene":"bridges","text":"The fourth bridge was on fire. Only slightly, but still. \"[[conn|Even if it looks scary, it is completely safe|Even if = the condition changes nothing. Scary or not, it is safe.]],\" said Granny Tao. \"The flames are decoration. Stay off the ropes. [[conn|Otherwise you will get soot on your hands|Otherwise = if you don't. If she doesn't stay off the ropes, she will get soot.]].\"\n\nThe fifth bridge was not there at all. There was only fog, and a sign the Glitch had chewed: \"Unless you don't imagine it, the bridge won't appear.\"\n\n\"Broken. Unless already means if not,\" said Pim. She pulled out \"don't\" and read the sign again. \"[[conn|Unless you imagine it, the bridge won't appear|Unless = if not, repaired: no second negative after unless.]].\"\n\n\"[[conn|What if we imagined a bridge made of giant straws|What if + past: a gentle suggestion, one step back. Truth: there is no bridge yet.]]?\" said Iffy. A bridge of giant straws appeared. It squeaked, but it held.\n\nGranny Tao was waiting on the far side. \"[[conn|You may enter World Seven provided that you repair my other signs first|Provided that = only if. A more formal cousin of as long as.]].\"","grammar":"Even if, otherwise, what if + past, provided that; and why unless never takes a second negative."}]},{"id":"ch7","world":"w7","title":"Wish Island","mission":"Clear the three wishing wells: wishes about now, regrets about then, and habits that should stop.","after":"The wells are clear. A wish shows the distance from what is real: past for now, past perfect for then, would for other people's habits.","pages":[{"scene":"island","text":"Wish Island was a ring of sand with three stone wells and one enormous frog. Her name tag said Kob. \"Welcome,\" she croaked. \"The wells don't make wishes come true. They only show how far a wish is from what's real.\"\n\nPim leaned over the first well, marked Now. \"[[wish|I wish I had my phone with me|Wish + past simple: about now. Truth: she doesn't have her phone.]].\" The water showed her phone at home on her bed, at four per cent. \"[[wish|I wish I were at home with it|Wish + were: about now. Truth: she isn't at home.]],\" she added quietly.\n\nThe second well was marked Then. Iffy dropped in a coin. \"[[wish|If only I had learned to say one sentence without an if|If only + past perfect: a regret about the past. Truth: Iffy never learned.]].\"\n\nPim laughed and dropped in her own. \"[[wish|I wish I had saved my game before I touched the screen|Wish + past perfect: a regret about the past. Truth: she didn't save it.]].\" The water showed a loading bar, stuck at zero.","grammar":"Wish / if only + past simple (about now) and + past perfect (regrets about the past)."},{"scene":"island","text":"The third well was marked Stop It. Kob looked embarrassed. \"This one is for complaints. [[wish|I wish the seagulls would stop stealing my lunch|Wish + would: someone else's annoying habit. Truth: the seagulls keep stealing it.]].\"\n\nIffy was humming again, the same four notes. \"[[wish|I wish you would hum a different song|Wish + would: about another person's habit. Truth: Iffy keeps humming the same one.]],\" said Pim. Iffy hummed louder.\n\nThen grey fog bubbled up out of all three wells, and coins flew back out. On each one was a broken wish: \"I wish I have wings.\" \"I wish I would be faster.\" \"If only I didn't forget my umbrella yesterday.\"\n\n\"None of these will go in,\" said Pim. \"After wish, the verb is never in the present. You don't use would about yourself. And yesterday needs had.\" She scratched new words onto the first coin and dropped it. \"[[wish|I wish I had wings|Wish + past simple, repaired. Truth: she has no wings. Imagined, now.]].\" Splash. The fog in that well cleared. Kob pushed a whole bucket of coins towards her.","grammar":"Wish + would for someone else's annoying habit; repairing wishes built with the wrong tense."}]},{"id":"ch8","world":"w8","title":"The Formal Citadel","mission":"Get past the gate guards, who answer only formal speech: Should you…, Were I…, Had I known…, but for.","after":"The great gate stands open. In formal English the verb can move to the front, and if disappears.","pages":[{"scene":"citadel","text":"The gate of the Formal Citadel was forty blocks high. In front of it stood two stone herons in grey uniforms, as still as statues.\n\n\"Hi,\" said Pim. \"Can you let us in if I say please?\" Nothing. Not even a blink.\n\nIffy pulled her down and whispered. \"[[formal|Should you wish to be heard, you must speak as they do|Formal: Should you wish = If you wish. Should moves to the front and if disappears.]].\"\n\nPim stood up straight and tried to sound like a school announcement. \"Good evening. [[formal|Were I to tell you our whole story, we would be here all night|Formal: Were I to tell = If I told. Imagined. Truth: she isn't going to tell it all.]].\" The first heron's eye moved, so she kept going. \"[[formal|Had I known you were so formal, I would have practised on the way|Formal third conditional: Had I known = If I had known. Truth: she didn't know.]].\"\n\n\"Acceptable,\" said the second heron. \"[[formal|Should you require assistance, ring the bell|Formal: Should you require = If you require, with an instruction as the result.]].\"\n\n\"There's a bell?\" said Pim.","grammar":"Formal inversion: Should you… (= If you…), Were I to… (= If I…), Had I known… (= If I had known…)."},{"scene":"citadel","text":"There was a bell, and beside it a brass plate. The fog was already licking at the letters: \"If should you need help, ring. Was I in your place, I would knock. Had I would known, I would have come sooner.\"\n\n\"All three are broken,\" Pim told the herons, very politely. \"When should moves to the front, if disappears. At the front it is always were, never was. And there is no would in the had half.\" She rubbed the plate with her sleeve, and the letters slid into place.\n\nThe first heron read the middle line aloud, slowly. \"[[formal|Were I in your place, I would knock|Formal second conditional: Were I = If I were. Truth: the heron is not in Pim's place.]].\" Pim knocked. The gate opened a crack.\n\n\"[[formal|But for this small fox-cat, I would never have got this far|But for + noun = if it hadn't been for. Truth: Iffy helped, so Pim got here.]],\" said Pim, and she meant it. Iffy bowed.\n\n\"Then bring her,\" said the herons. \"The inner doors are less forgiving.\"","grammar":"Inversion repaired (no if with should; were, never was; no would after had) and but for + noun."}]},{"id":"ch9","world":"w9","title":"The Final Arena","mission":"Face the Glitch with everything you know, repair the very first sentence, and send Pim home.","after":"Every world is whole again, and Pim is home. Set both dials, and any sentence will hold.","pages":[{"scene":"arena","text":"The Final Arena was a bowl of grey fog with a mouth made of broken letters. When the Glitch spoke, every word came out wrong. \"If you will challenge me, you lose,\" it roared.\n\nPim's knees were shaking, but her grammar was not. \"That's broken. [[1|If you challenge me, you will lose|First conditional, repaired: present after if, will in the result.]]. Thanks, that one works for me.\" A piece of fog turned into bright blocks and fell.\n\n\"[[0|If you repair a sentence, the fog loses a piece|Zero conditional. A law of the arena: true every time.]],\" whispered Iffy.\n\nThe Glitch tried twice more. \"If I am you, I go home. If you didn't open the game, you are safe now.\"\n\n\"Wrong dials, both times,\" said Pim. \"[[2|If I were you, I would go home|Second conditional, for advice. Truth: the Glitch is not Pim. Imagined, now.]]. And I'd love to. [[m32|If I hadn't opened the game, I would be safe now|Mixed 3/2: imagined past, result now. Truth: she opened it, so she isn't safe yet.]]. Rude, but true.\" Two more pieces fell.","grammar":"Everything together: zero, first, second and mixed 3/2, each repaired from the Glitch's broken speech."},{"scene":"arena","text":"The Glitch tried harder. \"Unless you don't stop, I will break every world.\"\n\n\"[[conn|Unless I stop, you will break every world|Unless = if not, repaired: no second negative after unless.]],\" said Pim. \"Noted. I'm not stopping.\"\n\n\"Had I would known you were coming, I built a bigger fog,\" it hissed.\n\n\"[[formal|Had you known I was coming, you would have built a bigger fog|Formal third conditional: Had you known = If you had known. Truth: it didn't know.]],\" said Pim. \"Too late.\" The fog was half its size now.\n\nIts voice was smaller too. \"I wish somebody fix me.\"\n\nPim stopped. That one did not sound like a threat. \"[[wish|I wish somebody would fix me|Wish + would: wanting someone else to act. Truth: nobody has fixed it yet.]],\" she said slowly. \"Is that what you mean?\" The fog nodded.","grammar":"Unless, formal inversion and wish + would: fixing the form to find the real meaning."},{"scene":"home","text":"Iffy crept out from behind Pim. \"[[m23|If the Glitch were really a monster, it would have stopped us in World One|Mixed 2/3: a general fact, a past result. Truth: it isn't a monster, and it didn't stop them.]].\"\n\nUnder the fog, cut into the arena floor, was the first sentence of the whole game. Somebody had built it wrongly on day one: \"If you will imagine it, you can build it.\"\n\nPim knelt down, pulled out one block, and read it. \"[[0|If you imagine it, you can build it|Zero conditional with can: a rule that is always true in this game.]].\" The last of the fog folded up into a small grey kitten with square paws. It sneezed. A door of light opened.\n\n\"[[1|If you open the game tomorrow, I will be waiting at the factory gate|First conditional. A real promise about a possible future.]],\" said Iffy.\n\n\"[[conn|I'll come back as long as you learn a new song|As long as = only if. Pim's one condition for coming back.]],\" said Pim, and she stepped through.\n\nPop. Her bedroom. The rain had stopped, and Mum was calling her for dinner. Pim picked up her cat and told her the only thing that made sense. \"[[m32|If I hadn't touched that screen, I wouldn't be this good at ifs now|Mixed 3/2: imagined past, result now. Truth: she touched it, so now she is good at ifs.]].\"","grammar":"Mixed 2/3, a real conditional with can, a first-conditional promise, as long as, and a last mixed 3/2 at home."}]}]},"games":{"sorters":[{"id":"zerofirst","name":"Always or This Time?","unlock":"w2","blurb":"Tap \"Always true\" for a fact, habit or rule, or \"One future time\" for a single possible event still to come.","cats":[{"id":"always","label":"Always true (zero)"},{"id":"once","label":"One future time (first)"}],"items":[{"t":"If I drink milk tea after six, I can't sleep.","c":"always","note":"A habit: it happens every time, so there is no will."},{"t":"Mum will be upset if I forget her birthday this Friday.","c":"once","note":"Cue: this Friday. One future day; will sits in the result half."},{"t":"Plants die if they don't get any water.","c":"always","note":"A general truth about all plants: present plus present."},{"t":"We won't go to the market this evening if it's still raining at five.","c":"once","note":"Cues: this evening, at five. One decision about one evening."},{"t":"If I finish my homework tonight, I'll watch one episode.","c":"once","note":"Cue: tonight. One evening, one plan: if plus present, then will."},{"t":"If the parcel arrives this afternoon, I'll text you a photo.","c":"once","note":"Cue: this afternoon. One possible event, one promise."},{"t":"My phone gets hot if I play games for too long.","c":"always","note":"It happens whenever she plays too long: a general fact about the phone."},{"t":"If it rains in Bangkok, the traffic always gets worse.","c":"always","note":"Cue: always. A general truth about the city."},{"t":"If you miss the 7.15 train tomorrow, you'll be late for the test.","c":"once","note":"Cue: tomorrow. A warning about one morning."},{"t":"If the BTS is crowded, I usually wait for the next train.","c":"always","note":"Cue: usually. This is what she does every time."},{"t":"If you leave ice cream in the sun, it melts in minutes.","c":"always","note":"A fact that is true every time: present plus present."},{"t":"If Ploy comes to the concert on Saturday, I'll save her a seat.","c":"once","note":"Cue: on Saturday. A promise about one event."},{"t":"I'll lend you my notes tonight if you promise to return them by Monday.","c":"once","note":"Cues: tonight, by Monday. A deal about one occasion."},{"t":"If students arrive after eight, they sign the late book.","c":"always","note":"A school rule: it is the same every day."},{"t":"If it rains tomorrow, we'll play badminton indoors.","c":"once","note":"Cue: tomorrow. One possible future event, so the result takes will."},{"t":"You can borrow three books at a time if you have a library card.","c":"always","note":"A library rule, true on any day: can gives the permission."},{"t":"If my cat hears the fridge open, she always runs to the kitchen.","c":"always","note":"Cue: always. A habit, so present plus present."},{"t":"If Mum cooks green curry, the whole flat always smells wonderful.","c":"always","note":"Cue: always. It happens every time she cooks it."},{"t":"If the haze is bad tomorrow morning, sports day might be postponed.","c":"once","note":"Cue: tomorrow morning. One day; might shows the result is only possible."},{"t":"If you mix blue and yellow, you get green.","c":"always","note":"True every time you do it: a fact, not a prediction."},{"t":"If you see Fah at lunch today, tell her about the room change.","c":"once","note":"Cue: today. One future moment; the result is an instruction for that moment."},{"t":"If Nan passes Friday's maths quiz, she'll celebrate with mango sticky rice.","c":"once","note":"Cue: Friday's quiz. One event still to come."},{"t":"If you don't charge your phone now, it'll die before we get home.","c":"once","note":"Cues: now, before we get home. A warning about this one journey."},{"t":"Water boils if you heat it to 100 degrees.","c":"always","note":"A science fact. The if half can come second: the meaning is the same."},{"t":"If you heat ice, it melts.","c":"always","note":"A science fact: present in both halves, true every time."},{"t":"If there are still tickets at ten tomorrow, I'll buy two.","c":"once","note":"Cue: at ten tomorrow. One sale, one plan."},{"t":"If the fire alarm rings, always walk to the field.","c":"always","note":"Cue: always. A standing rule: the result is an instruction for every time."},{"t":"If we win the match on Sunday, Coach will buy us all bubble tea.","c":"once","note":"Cue: on Sunday. One match that has not been played yet."}]},{"id":"real","name":"Real or Imagined?","unlock":"w3","blurb":"Tap \"Real\" if nothing is denied, \"Imagined now\" if it is not true now, or \"Imagined past\" if it did not happen.","cats":[{"id":"real","label":"Real: it can happen"},{"id":"now","label":"Imagined now"},{"id":"past","label":"Imagined past"}],"items":[{"t":"If I had left earlier this morning, I'd have caught the train.","c":"past","note":"Two steps back: had left, would have caught. Truth: I left late and missed it."},{"t":"If we leave at seven tomorrow, we might catch the first train.","c":"real","note":"Real future: it is still possible. Might means maybe."},{"t":"If I didn't have a test tomorrow, I'd come with you tonight.","c":"now","note":"Didn't have is a past form, but not past time. Truth: I have a test tomorrow."},{"t":"If we lived near the BTS, I'd sleep until seven.","c":"now","note":"Lived is a past form about now. Truth: we don't live near the BTS."},{"t":"If you heat chocolate, it melts.","c":"real","note":"Real, always: a fact. Nothing is denied, so there is no Truth line."},{"t":"I'd buy those headphones today if I had enough pocket money.","c":"now","note":"Cue: today. Truth: I don't have enough pocket money."},{"t":"You can join the trip next week if your mum signs the form.","c":"real","note":"Real future: she may sign it. Can gives the permission."},{"t":"If I were taller, I could reach the top shelf.","c":"now","note":"Were and could are one step back. Truth: I'm not tall enough to reach it."},{"t":"If you're cold, close the window.","c":"real","note":"Real now: maybe you are cold. The result is an instruction."},{"t":"If we had bought tickets last week, we'd have got front-row seats.","c":"past","note":"Cue: last week. Truth: we didn't buy them then."},{"t":"Last term, if the teacher was late, we always started reading quietly.","c":"real","note":"Real past habit: it happened. Past plus past is not imagined here."},{"t":"If you had told me on Monday, I might have helped you.","c":"past","note":"Cue: on Monday, with had told and might have. Truth: you didn't tell me then."},{"t":"If my phone had more memory, I could keep all these photos.","c":"now","note":"One step back: had, could. Truth: it doesn't have enough memory."},{"t":"If Praew were here now, she'd fix this in a minute.","c":"now","note":"Cue: now. Truth: Praew isn't here."},{"t":"If it rains tomorrow, we'll take a taxi.","c":"real","note":"Real future: present after if, will in the result. It may really rain."},{"t":"If Fah was at practice yesterday, she already knows the new plan.","c":"real","note":"Real past: maybe she was there. Nothing is denied, and there is no would."},{"t":"If Bam had saved her file, she wouldn't have lost her essay last night.","c":"past","note":"Cue: last night. Truth: she didn't save it and she lost the essay."},{"t":"I wouldn't have got wet yesterday if I'd taken my umbrella.","c":"past","note":"Here 'd is had, and would have is the past ticket. Truth: I didn't take it and I got wet."},{"t":"If I had a cat, I'd call her Mochi.","c":"now","note":"One step back: had, would. Truth: I don't have a cat now."},{"t":"We'd have won last Sunday's match if Jane hadn't hurt her ankle.","c":"past","note":"Cue: last Sunday. Truth: she hurt her ankle and we lost."},{"t":"If Fern had seen the message, she'd have come to practice on Friday.","c":"past","note":"Cue: on Friday. Truth: she didn't see it, so she didn't come."},{"t":"If Mint calls tonight, tell her I'm at badminton.","c":"real","note":"Real future: she may call. Present after if, then an instruction."},{"t":"If the cat hadn't knocked my glass over, my notes would have stayed dry.","c":"past","note":"Had plus V3, would have plus V3. Truth: she knocked it over and the notes got wet."},{"t":"If it weren't raining now, we'd be on the court.","c":"now","note":"Cue: now. Truth: it is raining, so we aren't on the court."},{"t":"If I knew the answer, I'd tell you right now.","c":"now","note":"Cue: right now. Truth: I don't know the answer."},{"t":"In primary school, if it rained at break, we played in the hall.","c":"real","note":"Real past: it really happened, many times. Past plus past, and no would."},{"t":"If my cat is hungry, she sits on my keyboard.","c":"real","note":"Real, always: a habit. Present plus present."},{"t":"If it hadn't rained yesterday, we would have had sports day.","c":"past","note":"Cue: yesterday, with had and would have. Truth: it rained, so there was no sports day."},{"t":"If Nan had charged her phone last night, she could have called us.","c":"past","note":"Cue: last night. Truth: she didn't charge it, so she couldn't call."},{"t":"If I spoke Korean, I might understand this song without subtitles.","c":"now","note":"Spoke is one step back from speak. Truth: I don't speak Korean."}]},{"id":"glitch","name":"Glitch Hunt","unlock":"w4","blurb":"Tap \"Clean\" if the sentence is correct, or \"Glitched\" if it has a grammar error.","cats":[{"id":"ok","label":"Clean"},{"id":"glitch","label":"Glitched"}],"items":[{"t":"If I would have more time now, I would learn Korean.","c":"glitch","note":"Would in the if half. Fix: If I had more time now, I would learn Korean."},{"t":"I wish I can come to your party tonight, but I have tutoring.","c":"glitch","note":"Wish plus present. Fix: I wish I could come to your party tonight, but I have tutoring."},{"t":"We'll be late unless we don't leave now.","c":"glitch","note":"Unless plus not. Fix: We'll be late unless we leave now."},{"t":"If you'll follow me, I'll show you to your seats.","c":"ok","note":"Clean, and it only looks odd: here will means are willing to. It is a polite request."},{"t":"Unless you don't hurry, you'll miss the bus.","c":"glitch","note":"Unless already means if not. Fix: Unless you hurry, you'll miss the bus."},{"t":"If I will see Mint tomorrow, I'll give her your book.","c":"glitch","note":"Will after if. Fix: If I see Mint tomorrow, I'll give her your book."},{"t":"Take an umbrella in case it rains this afternoon.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: you take it now, before any rain. That is a precaution."},{"t":"If Nan had studied, she would pass last Friday's quiz.","c":"glitch","note":"No past ticket. Fix: If Nan had studied, she would have passed last Friday's quiz."},{"t":"If it rains on Saturday, we'll watch a film at home.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: present after if, will only in the result."},{"t":"If it hadn't rained, we would have went to the market.","c":"glitch","note":"Wrong verb form after have. Fix: If it hadn't rained, we would have gone to the market."},{"t":"If Ploy had seen the message, she might have come on Friday.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: might have plus V3 is a possible past result. Truth: she didn't see it."},{"t":"If I am you, I would tell the teacher today.","c":"glitch","note":"Present for an imagined present. Fix: If I were you, I would tell the teacher today."},{"t":"If she has seen your message yesterday, she would have replied.","c":"glitch","note":"Has seen should be had seen. Fix: If she had seen your message yesterday, she would have replied."},{"t":"If I had left earlier, I would catch the 7.15 train yesterday.","c":"glitch","note":"No past ticket. Fix: If I had left earlier, I would have caught the 7.15 train yesterday."},{"t":"If I were you, I'd say sorry to her this afternoon.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: were and would are one step back. Truth: I'm not you."},{"t":"In case it rains tomorrow, we'll cancel the picnic.","c":"glitch","note":"In exam English, in case is only for a precaution you take before the event. Cancelling is a result. Fix: If it rains tomorrow, we'll cancel the picnic."},{"t":"I wish I had brought my umbrella this morning.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: wish plus had plus V3 for a past regret. Truth: I didn't bring it."},{"t":"If I had left earlier yesterday, I would have caught the train.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: had plus V3, would have plus V3. Truth: I left late and missed it."},{"t":"If you heat butter, it melts.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: a fact, present plus present."},{"t":"If I would have known about the quiz, I would have studied.","c":"glitch","note":"Would in the if half: heard in American speech, wrong in exams. Fix: If I had known about the quiz, I would have studied."},{"t":"If I have known about the traffic, I would have taken the BTS.","c":"glitch","note":"Have known should be had known. Fix: If I had known about the traffic, I would have taken the BTS."},{"t":"You can borrow my charger as long as you give it back tonight.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: as long as sets the condition for the deal."},{"t":"When we were little, if it rained, we stayed in and played cards.","c":"ok","note":"Clean, and it only looks odd: a real past habit takes past plus past, with no would."},{"t":"If I'd had my card, I would have took the BTS.","c":"glitch","note":"Wrong verb form after have. Fix: If I'd had my card, I would have taken the BTS."},{"t":"I wish I had a cat, but Mum is allergic.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: wish plus past for a wish about now. Truth: I don't have a cat."},{"t":"If I have wings, I would fly to school every day.","c":"glitch","note":"Present for an imagined present. Fix: If I had wings, I would fly to school every day."},{"t":"We would have gone to the market if it hadn't rained on Sunday.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: would have plus V3 (gone). Truth: it rained, so we didn't go."},{"t":"If we hadn't missed the BTS, we wouldn't be late now.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: a past condition with a result now. Truth: we missed it, and we are late."},{"t":"If I was rude this morning, I'm sorry.","c":"ok","note":"Clean, and it only looks odd: a real past possibility. Maybe I was rude, so was is right."},{"t":"I'll buy the tickets in case I have enough money.","c":"glitch","note":"In case is only for a precaution against a risk. Money is the condition here. Fix: I'll buy the tickets if I have enough money."},{"t":"If it will rain tomorrow, we'll stay at home.","c":"glitch","note":"Will after if. Fix: If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay at home."},{"t":"If I had more pocket money now, I could buy that sketchbook.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: had and could are one step back. Truth: I don't have enough money now."},{"t":"If I'd known about the quiz, I'd have studied last night.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: the first 'd is had, the second is would. Truth: I didn't know."},{"t":"If you see Fah at lunch, tell her I'm looking for her.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: present after if, then an instruction."},{"t":"I wish I have a cat, but Mum is allergic.","c":"glitch","note":"Wish plus present. Fix: I wish I had a cat, but Mum is allergic."},{"t":"Unless you hurry, you'll miss the bus.","c":"ok","note":"Clean: unless means if you don't, so no extra not."}]},{"id":"types","name":"Type Sorter","unlock":"w5","blurb":"Tap the type of each sentence: check the time of the if half, then the time of the result half.","cats":[{"id":"0","label":"Zero"},{"id":"1","label":"First"},{"id":"2","label":"Second"},{"id":"3","label":"Third"},{"id":"m32","label":"Mixed 3/2"},{"id":"m23","label":"Mixed 2/3"}],"items":[{"t":"If it's sunny on Saturday, we might go to the park.","c":"1","note":"First: cue on Saturday. A real future; might means maybe."},{"t":"I'd walk to school every day if I lived next door to it.","c":"2","note":"Second: lived, would plus verb. Truth: I don't live next door."},{"t":"If we had caught the 7.15 train, we would be at school by now.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: cue by now. Truth: we missed the train; we aren't at school yet."},{"t":"If Jane weren't such a calm player, she'd have lost Sunday's final.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: Jane is calm (always), so she didn't lose (Sunday)."},{"t":"I'd know the answer now if I had listened in yesterday's lesson.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: the result comes first. I didn't listen yesterday; I don't know now."},{"t":"If we don't leave in five minutes, we'll miss the train.","c":"1","note":"First: one possible future event, with will in the result."},{"t":"If I see Ploy tomorrow, I'll give her your notes.","c":"1","note":"First: cue tomorrow. Present after if, will in the result."},{"t":"If I had a bigger room, I'd keep a piano in it.","c":"2","note":"Second: had, would plus verb. Truth: my room isn't big enough now."},{"t":"If I were taller, I'd have reached the top shelf yesterday.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: a permanent condition, a past result. Truth: I'm not tall enough; I didn't reach it."},{"t":"If you tap your card at the gate, it opens.","c":"0","note":"Zero: it works like this every time."},{"t":"If Praew didn't love cats, she wouldn't have adopted three last year.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: she loves cats (always), so she adopted three (last year)."},{"t":"If I eat late at night, I always sleep badly.","c":"0","note":"Zero: cue always. A habit, present plus present."},{"t":"I'll message you tonight if my order arrives.","c":"1","note":"First: cue tonight. Will in the result, present after if."},{"t":"If it had rained on sports day, the races would have been cancelled.","c":"3","note":"Third: had rained, would have been cancelled. Truth: it didn't rain and the races went ahead."},{"t":"If the haze is bad, our school always cancels outdoor PE.","c":"0","note":"Zero: cue always. A school habit, present plus present."},{"t":"What would you do if you found a wallet outside 7-Eleven?","c":"2","note":"Second: found, would plus verb. An imagined situation: you haven't found one."},{"t":"If Mint doesn't practise this week, she won't be ready for Friday's match.","c":"1","note":"First: a warning about one future match. Won't is will not."},{"t":"If I were you, I'd say sorry to Fern today.","c":"2","note":"Second: were, would plus verb. Advice about now. Truth: I'm not you."},{"t":"If I'd eaten breakfast this morning, I wouldn't be hungry now.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: had eaten this morning, would be now. Truth: I didn't eat; I am hungry."},{"t":"Bam would have won last week's quiz if she had read the last question carefully.","c":"3","note":"Third: last week in both halves. Truth: she didn't read it carefully and didn't win."},{"t":"If it hadn't rained all night, the court would be dry now.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: it rained in the night; the court is wet now."},{"t":"If we didn't have school tomorrow, we could stay up late tonight.","c":"2","note":"Second: didn't have, could plus verb. Truth: we have school tomorrow."},{"t":"If you freeze water, it turns into ice.","c":"0","note":"Zero: a fact. Present plus present, true every time."},{"t":"If I had taken my umbrella yesterday, I wouldn't have got wet.","c":"3","note":"Third: both halves are about yesterday. Truth: I didn't take it and I got wet."},{"t":"If the order had arrived on Friday, I could have worn the shoes to the party.","c":"3","note":"Third: had arrived, could have worn. Truth: it came too late for the party."},{"t":"If my cat could talk, she would complain about dinner every day.","c":"2","note":"Second: could, would plus verb. Truth: my cat can't talk."},{"t":"If you hadn't reminded me, I would have forgotten Mum's birthday last month.","c":"3","note":"Third, for relief. Truth: you reminded me, so I didn't forget."},{"t":"If I knew her LINE ID, I'd add her right now.","c":"2","note":"Second: cue right now. Truth: I don't know her LINE ID."},{"t":"I might have passed Monday's test if I had slept more the night before.","c":"3","note":"Third: might have passed, had slept. Both are past. Truth: I didn't sleep enough."},{"t":"If I had charged my phone last night, it would still be working.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: cue still. Truth: I didn't charge it; it isn't working now."},{"t":"If you don't water basil, it dies quickly.","c":"0","note":"Zero: a fact about the plant. Present plus present."},{"t":"If I had slept earlier last night, I wouldn't be so tired now.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: past condition, result now. Truth: I slept late; I am tired now."},{"t":"My little cousin cries if she hears thunder.","c":"0","note":"Zero: it happens every time. Present plus present."},{"t":"If I spoke Japanese, I would have understood the film last night.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: spoke is about now and always, would have understood is about last night. Truth: I don't speak Japanese."},{"t":"If I knew how to swim, I would have joined the pool party last weekend.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: I can't swim (now and always), so I didn't join (last weekend)."},{"t":"Cats purr if they feel safe.","c":"0","note":"Zero: a general truth about cats. Present plus present."},{"t":"If Nan hadn't lost her card yesterday, she could be on the BTS with us now.","c":"m32","note":"Mixed 3/2: lost it yesterday, isn't with us now. Could be is a result now."},{"t":"If we'd left at six this morning, we'd have caught the first train.","c":"3","note":"Third: 'd left is had left, 'd have caught is would have caught. Truth: we left later and missed it."},{"t":"If I weren't afraid of heights, I'd have gone on the big wheel on Saturday.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: I am afraid of heights (always), so I didn't go on it (Saturday)."},{"t":"If the shop is still open at nine tonight, I'll get some milk.","c":"1","note":"First: cue tonight. Present after if, will in the result."},{"t":"If our flat were bigger, we could have had the party at home last month.","c":"m23","note":"Mixed 2/3: the flat is small (always), so the party wasn't at home (last month)."},{"t":"If you help me with maths this evening, I'll buy you a bubble tea.","c":"1","note":"First: cue this evening. A deal about one future time."}]},{"id":"conn","name":"Connector Clash","unlock":"w6","blurb":"Tap the one connector that fits the gap: unless, in case, as long as or even if.","cats":[{"id":"unless","label":"unless"},{"id":"incase","label":"in case"},{"id":"aslong","label":"as long as"},{"id":"evenif","label":"even if"}],"items":[{"t":"___ we leave right now, we'll miss the train. Come on, we can still make it!","c":"unless","note":"Except test: we'll miss it, except if we leave now. Cue: we can still make it."},{"t":"My laptop is old, so I always save my essay twice ___ it crashes.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: she saves first, because a crash might come later."},{"t":"Praew is a great sport: she smiles ___ she loses a match.","c":"evenif","note":"Losing does not change the result: she smiles either way."},{"t":"Coach lets us choose the music ___ we finish the warm-up properly.","c":"aslong","note":"A deal with Coach: music, on one condition."},{"t":"The shop will change the shoes ___ you keep your receipt.","c":"aslong","note":"A condition for a promise: receipt first, then the exchange."},{"t":"Don't wake me before nine tomorrow ___ the parcel arrives. I need to sign for it.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: don't wake me, except if the parcel comes."},{"t":"A taxi won't help: we'd still be late ___ we took one now.","c":"evenif","note":"A taxi would not change the result: late either way. Cue: a taxi won't help."},{"t":"I'll watch any film with you tonight ___ it isn't a horror film. I hate those.","c":"aslong","note":"Any film is fine on one condition: no horror."},{"t":"I'll screenshot the ticket now ___ there's no signal at the gate later.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: take the screenshot now, before the possible problem."},{"t":"Mum says I can't go to the concert ___ I finish my project first. So I'm finishing it!","c":"unless","note":"Except test: no concert, except if the project is done. Finishing it opens the door."},{"t":"Write my number on your hand ___ your phone dies at the concert.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: write it now, before anything goes wrong."},{"t":"The cat can sleep on my bed ___ she doesn't bite my toes.","c":"aslong","note":"A deal with the cat. Unless would not work here: the clause already has not."},{"t":"We'll get good seats ___ we arrive before the doors open.","c":"aslong","note":"A condition that makes the good result happen: arrive early, sit well."},{"t":"Mum gave me 100 baht extra ___ I needed a taxi home.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test in the past: the money came first, for a possible need later."},{"t":"You won't improve at badminton ___ you practise, so come to training twice a week.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: no progress, except if you practise."},{"t":"I'm going to practice tonight ___ it pours with rain. Nothing can stop me.","c":"evenif","note":"The rain changes nothing: she goes either way."},{"t":"The cat never comes when I call, ___ I shake the food box. That always works.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: she never comes, except if I shake the box."},{"t":"Mum says I can go to the concert ___ I'm home by ten.","c":"aslong","note":"A deal with Mum: permission, with one condition."},{"t":"You can sit with us ___ you promise not to spoil the ending.","c":"aslong","note":"A deal: a seat, on one condition."},{"t":"Sports day will be on Friday ___ the haze gets worse. In that case, it moves to Monday.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: Friday, except if the haze gets worse."},{"t":"My cat won't move from my chair ___ I offer her a treat. Nothing works!","c":"evenif","note":"The treat changes nothing. Cue: nothing works."},{"t":"Let's take some snacks on the trip ___ we get hungry on the bus.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: pack now, because hunger might come later."},{"t":"The train left five minutes early. I'd have missed it ___ I had run all the way, so I'm glad I walked.","c":"evenif","note":"Running would not have changed the result: missed either way. Truth: the train left early."},{"t":"I'm wearing my new trainers to the match ___ they get muddy. I don't care!","c":"evenif","note":"Mud changes nothing: she wears them either way. Cue: I don't care."},{"t":"You can borrow my charger ___ you give it back before lunch.","c":"aslong","note":"A deal: yes, but only on this condition."},{"t":"Keep your mask on outside today ___ the sky looks clear; the haze is still there.","c":"evenif","note":"A clear sky changes nothing: keep the mask on either way."},{"t":"I'm bringing a mask ___ the haze comes back this afternoon.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: she packs the mask before the haze, because it might come."},{"t":"I'll walk to school tomorrow ___ it rains. Then I'll take the bus.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: I'll walk, except if it rains. Rain is the one thing that changes the plan."},{"t":"Take an umbrella ___ it rains this afternoon; the sky looks grey.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test: you take it now, before any rain. A precaution."},{"t":"This morning we left at six ___ the traffic was bad on the way.","c":"incase","note":"Umbrella test in the past: they left early because bad traffic was possible."},{"t":"No card, no entry: you can't use the library ___ you show your student card.","c":"unless","note":"Except test: no entry, except if you show the card."},{"t":"No excuses: I'll finish this project tonight ___ I have to stay up until midnight.","c":"evenif","note":"Staying up late will not change the result: the project gets finished. Cue: no excuses."}]}],"machine":[{"id":"m1","event":"Missing the BTS","base":"Ploy left home late this morning and missed the 7.15 BTS train. Lessons have started, and she is still on her way to school.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Ploy leaves home by seven, she'll catch the 7.15 train.","truth":"Said before it happened: she can still leave on time and catch it."},"2":{"s":"If Ploy were in class now, she'd be sitting next to Fah.","truth":"She isn't in class now: she is still on her way to school."},"3":{"s":"If Ploy had left home by seven, she would have caught the 7.15 train.","truth":"She left home late this morning and missed the train."},"m32":{"s":"If Ploy had caught the 7.15 train, she would be in class by now.","truth":"She missed the train this morning, so she isn't in class now."},"m23":{"s":"If Ploy lived nearer the station, she wouldn't have missed the train this morning.","truth":"She lives a long way from the station, and she missed the train this morning."},"wish":{"s":"Ploy: \"If only I had left home ten minutes earlier!\"","truth":"Ploy didn't leave earlier: she left late."}}},{"id":"m2","event":"A rained-off sports day","base":"It rained all Friday morning, so sports day was cancelled. It is Friday afternoon now: the rain has stopped, but the field is too wet and the students are in their classrooms.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If it rains on Friday morning, sports day will be cancelled.","truth":"Said before it happened: Friday could still be dry."},"2":{"s":"If the field were dry, the students would be out there racing now.","truth":"The field isn't dry, so they are in their classrooms now."},"3":{"s":"If it hadn't rained this morning, the races would have gone ahead.","truth":"It rained this morning, so there were no races."},"m32":{"s":"If it hadn't rained all morning, the field wouldn't be too wet to run on now.","truth":"It rained all morning, and the field is too wet now."},"m23":{"s":"If the school had an indoor track, sports day wouldn't have been cancelled.","truth":"The school has no indoor track, and sports day was cancelled this morning."},"wish":{"s":"The students wish the sun would come out and dry the field.","truth":"The sun isn't coming out, and the field is still wet."}}},{"id":"m3","event":"Forgetting an umbrella","base":"Mint left her umbrella at home this morning. Now school is over, and she is standing at the bus stop in the rain, completely wet.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Mint forgets her umbrella, she'll get wet on the way home.","truth":"Said before it happened: she is still at home and can take it."},"2":{"s":"If Mint had her umbrella with her, she wouldn't be standing in the rain like this.","truth":"She doesn't have her umbrella with her now."},"3":{"s":"If Mint had taken her umbrella this morning, she wouldn't have got wet.","truth":"She didn't take it this morning, and she got wet."},"m32":{"s":"If Mint had taken her umbrella this morning, she would be dry now.","truth":"She didn't take it this morning, so she is wet now."},"m23":{"s":"If Mint checked the weather app every day, she'd have known about the rain this morning.","truth":"She doesn't check the app every day, and she didn't know about the rain this morning."},"wish":{"s":"Mint: \"If only I had my umbrella with me!\"","truth":"Mint doesn't have her umbrella with her now."}}},{"id":"m4","event":"A late online order","base":"Nan ordered new trainers online for Saturday's party, but they did not come in time. It is Sunday now, and she is still waiting for the parcel.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If the parcel arrives by Friday, Nan will wear her new trainers to the party.","truth":"Said before it happened: the parcel may still come in time."},"2":{"s":"If the trainers were here, Nan would be trying them on right now.","truth":"The trainers aren't here yet, so she isn't trying them on."},"3":{"s":"If the parcel had arrived on Friday, Nan would have worn the trainers to the party.","truth":"The parcel didn't arrive on Friday, so she wore her old shoes."},"m32":{"s":"If Nan had ordered a week earlier, she wouldn't still be waiting for the parcel.","truth":"She ordered late, and she is still waiting now."},"m23":{"s":"If the shop were in Bangkok, the parcel would have arrived before the party.","truth":"The shop isn't in Bangkok, and the parcel didn't arrive before the party."},"wish":{"s":"Nan wishes she had ordered the trainers a week earlier.","truth":"She ordered them too late."}}},{"id":"m5","event":"A badminton match","base":"Praew's team lost the school badminton final 21–19 on Saturday after she missed two serves near the end.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Praew's team wins on Saturday, they'll take the cup home.","truth":"Said before it happened: the final hasn't been played and they can still win."},"2":{"s":"If Praew had a stronger serve, she would win more points.","truth":"Her serve isn't strong enough yet."},"3":{"s":"If Praew hadn't missed those two serves, her team might have won the final.","truth":"She missed two serves on Saturday, and her team lost."},"m32":{"s":"If the team had won on Saturday, the cup would be in the school hall now.","truth":"The team lost on Saturday, so the cup isn't in the hall now."},"m23":{"s":"If Praew didn't get so nervous in finals, she wouldn't have missed those two serves.","truth":"She gets nervous in finals, and she missed two serves on Saturday."},"wish":{"s":"Praew wishes she hadn't missed those two serves.","truth":"She missed them on Saturday."}}},{"id":"m6","event":"A science project","base":"Bam started her volcano model the night before the science fair. This morning it was still wet, and it collapsed on the table. The fair starts in an hour.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Bam starts her model this weekend, it will be dry in time for the fair.","truth":"Said before it happened: she still has time to start early."},"2":{"s":"If Bam had more time, she would build the volcano again.","truth":"She doesn't have more time: the fair starts in an hour."},"3":{"s":"If Bam had started earlier, the model wouldn't have collapsed.","truth":"She started the night before, and the model collapsed this morning."},"m32":{"s":"If Bam had started a week ago, her model would be dry and ready now.","truth":"She didn't start a week ago, and the model is wet and broken now."},"m23":{"s":"If Bam used a planner, she would have started the model a week earlier.","truth":"She doesn't use a planner, and she started the model the night before."},"wish":{"s":"If only the fair were tomorrow!","truth":"The fair isn't tomorrow: it is today."}}},{"id":"m7","event":"A cat that escaped","base":"Fern left the balcony door open last night and her cat Mochi got out. Mochi is still hiding somewhere in the garden.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Fern leaves the balcony door open, Mochi will get out.","truth":"Said before it happened: the door is still shut and Mochi is indoors."},"2":{"s":"If Fern knew where Mochi was, she would carry her straight home.","truth":"Fern doesn't know where Mochi is."},"3":{"s":"If Fern had closed the balcony door, Mochi wouldn't have escaped.","truth":"Fern left the door open last night, and Mochi escaped."},"m32":{"s":"If Fern had closed the door last night, Mochi would be asleep on her bed now.","truth":"Fern didn't close the door last night, and Mochi isn't on her bed now."},"m23":{"s":"If Mochi weren't so curious, she wouldn't have run out last night.","truth":"Mochi is a curious cat, and she ran out last night."},"wish":{"s":"Fern wishes Mochi would come when she calls.","truth":"Mochi isn't coming when Fern calls: she is still hiding."}}},{"id":"m8","event":"A concert ticket sale","base":"Tickets for a K-pop concert went on sale at ten. Jane logged in at five past ten, and every ticket was already sold.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Jane logs in at ten exactly, she might get a ticket.","truth":"Said before it happened: the sale hasn't started and tickets are still there."},"2":{"s":"If Jane had a ticket, she'd be counting the days to the concert.","truth":"Jane doesn't have a ticket."},"3":{"s":"If Jane had logged in at ten, she could have bought a ticket.","truth":"She logged in at five past ten and couldn't buy one."},"m32":{"s":"If Jane had logged in on time, she would have a ticket now.","truth":"She didn't log in on time, so she has no ticket now."},"m23":{"s":"If Jane set alarms for things like this, she wouldn't have missed the sale.","truth":"She doesn't set alarms, and she missed the sale."},"wish":{"s":"Jane: \"If only I had logged in five minutes earlier!\"","truth":"Jane logged in at five past ten, which was too late."}}},{"id":"m9","event":"A maths quiz","base":"Pim watched videos instead of revising on Sunday night and did badly in Monday's maths quiz. She can retake it next week.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Pim revises on Sunday night, she'll do well in Monday's quiz.","truth":"Said before it happened: it is still the weekend and she can revise."},"2":{"s":"If Pim knew the formulas by heart, the retake would be easy.","truth":"She doesn't know the formulas by heart yet."},"3":{"s":"If Pim had revised on Sunday night, she would have got a better mark.","truth":"She didn't revise on Sunday night, and her mark was low."},"m32":{"s":"If Pim had passed on Monday, she wouldn't be revising for the retake now.","truth":"She didn't pass on Monday, so she is revising for the retake now."},"m23":{"s":"If Pim liked maths, she would have revised instead of watching videos.","truth":"She doesn't like maths much, and she watched videos on Sunday night."},"wish":{"s":"Pim wishes she had revised instead of watching videos.","truth":"She watched videos and didn't revise."}}},{"id":"m10","event":"A school trip","base":"Mook forgot to give her mum the permission form. Today her class is at the science museum, and she is at school doing worksheets.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Mook hands in the form by Thursday, she can go on the trip.","truth":"Said before it happened: there is still time to hand in the form."},"2":{"s":"If Mook were at the museum with her class, she'd be looking at the dinosaur bones now.","truth":"She isn't at the museum now: she is at school."},"3":{"s":"If Mook had handed in the form, she would have gone to the museum.","truth":"She didn't hand in the form, so she didn't go to the museum."},"m32":{"s":"If Mook had remembered the form, she wouldn't be doing worksheets at school now.","truth":"She forgot the form, so she is doing worksheets at school now."},"m23":{"s":"If the school didn't need a signed form for every trip, Mook would have gone with her class.","truth":"The school needs a signed form for every trip, and Mook didn't go."},"wish":{"s":"Mook wishes she were at the museum too.","truth":"She isn't at the museum: she is at school."}}},{"id":"m11","event":"Haze day","base":"The PM2.5 level is very high today, so the school closed this morning and Fah is having online lessons at home.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If the haze gets worse tomorrow, the school will close for the day.","truth":"Said before it happened: the air may be better or worse tomorrow."},"2":{"s":"If the air were clean today, Fah would be at school with her friends.","truth":"The air isn't clean today, so she is at home."},"3":{"s":"If the haze hadn't been so bad this morning, the school would have stayed open.","truth":"The haze was very bad this morning, and the school closed."},"m32":{"s":"If the wind had changed last night, the sky would be clear now.","truth":"The wind didn't change last night, and the sky isn't clear now."},"m23":{"s":"If Fah didn't have a laptop at home, she would have missed this morning's lessons.","truth":"She has a laptop at home, so she didn't miss this morning's lessons."},"wish":{"s":"Fah wishes she could see her friends today.","truth":"She can't see them today: the school is closed."}}},{"id":"m12","event":"A cooking class","base":"In today's cooking class, Ploy read the recipe too fast and put salt in her cake instead of sugar. The cake is out of the oven now, and it tastes of salt.","forms":{"1":{"s":"If Ploy reads the recipe carefully, her cake will taste great.","truth":"Said before it happened: she hasn't started baking, so it can still go well."},"2":{"s":"If the cake tasted better, Ploy would share it with the whole class.","truth":"The cake tastes of salt, so she isn't sharing it."},"3":{"s":"If Ploy had read the labels, she wouldn't have used salt.","truth":"She didn't read the labels, and she used salt."},"m32":{"s":"If Ploy had used sugar, everyone would be eating her cake now.","truth":"She used salt, so nobody is eating her cake now."},"m23":{"s":"If the salt and sugar jars didn't look the same, Ploy wouldn't have mixed them up.","truth":"The two jars look the same, and she mixed them up today."},"wish":{"s":"Ploy wishes she had checked the label on the jar.","truth":"She didn't check the label."}}}]}};
